var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"]},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"Home","text":""},{"location":"index.html#welcome-to-mkdocs","title":"Welcome to MkDocs","text":"<p>For full documentation visit mkdocs.org.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#commands","title":"Commands","text":"<ul> <li><code>mkdocs new [dir-name]</code> - Create a new project.</li> <li><code>mkdocs serve</code> - Start the live-reloading docs server.</li> <li><code>mkdocs build</code> - Build the documentation site.</li> <li><code>mkdocs gh-deploy</code> - Deploying to GitHub Pages.      </li> <li><code>mkdocs -h</code> - Print help message and exit.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"index.html#docker-run","title":"Docker Run","text":"<pre><code>docker build -t mkdocs-site .\ndocker-compose up\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#build-mkdocs","title":"Build Mkdocs","text":"<pre><code>1. Generate Static Files:\n    &gt; mkdocs build or  &gt; mkdocs build --site-dir site\n\n2. Install ghp-import (if not installed):\n    &gt; pip install ghp-import\n\n3. Deploy to GitHub Pages:\n    &gt; ghp-import -n -p -b gh-pages site\n\n4. Configure GitHub Pages:\n    Go to your GitHub repository.\n    Navigate to \"Settings\" &gt; \"Pages\".\n    Ensure the source branch is set to gh-pages.\n\n5. Access Your Site:\nYour site will be available at https://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/&lt;repository&gt;/.\nEx: https://dhruvish09.github.io/developer-roadmap/\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#header","title":"Header","text":"<pre><code># H1 Header\n## H2 Header\n### H3 Header\n#### H4 Header\n##### H5 Header\n###### H6 Header\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#bold","title":"Bold","text":"<pre><code>*Italic* or _Italic_\n**Bold** or __Bold__\n***Bold Italic*** or ___Bold Italic___\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#lists","title":"Lists","text":"<pre><code>## Unordered Lists:-\n    - Item 1\n    - Item 2\n      - Subitem 2.1\n      - Subitem 2.2\n    - Item 3\n\n## Ordered Lists:-\n    1. First item\n    2. Second item\n       1. Subitem 2.1\n       2. Subitem 2.2\n    3. Third item\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#links","title":"Links","text":"<pre><code>[Link Text](https://example.com \"Your Name\")\n\nFor Direct use link:\nVisit our site at https://example.com for more information.\n</code></pre> <p>Phasellus posuere in sem ut cursus</p> <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Nulla et euismod nulla. Curabitur feugiat, tortor non consequat finibus, justo purus auctor massa, nec semper lorem quam in massa.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#images","title":"Images","text":"<pre><code>![Alt Text](assets/images/logo.png)\n![Alt Text](assets/images/logo.png){ align=left }\n![Alt Text](assets/images/logo.png){ align=right }\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#code-blocks","title":"Code Blocks","text":"<pre><code>```python\ndef hello_world():\n    print(\"Hello, world!\")\n```\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#blockquotes","title":"Blockquotes","text":"<pre><code>&gt; This is a blockquote.\n&gt; It can span multiple lines.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#tables","title":"Tables","text":"<pre><code>| Header 1 | Header 2 | Header 3 |\n|----------|----------|----------|\n| Row 1    | Data     | Data     |\n| Row 2    | Data     | Data     |\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#admonitions","title":"Admonitions","text":"<p>Note Title</p> <p>This is a note.</p> <p>Warning Title</p> <p>This is a warning.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#footnotes","title":"Footnotes","text":"<pre><code>For more details, refer to the official documentation[^docs].\n\n[^docs]: [MkDocs Documentation](https://www.mkdocs.org/)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"index.html#gif-load","title":"GIF Load","text":"<p>Here is an example square GIF:</p> <p></p> Image caption"},{"location":"index.html#video-load","title":"Video Load","text":"<p>Here is an example video:</p>    Your browser does not support the video tag."},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html","title":"AI Concepts","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#wha-tis-llm","title":"Wha tis LLM","text":"<p>LLM (Large Language Model) is an AI model trained on massive text data that understands language and generates human-like responses by predicting the next word based on context.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd39 How LLM Works (Simple Flow)</p> <pre><code>User Prompt\n(Question or instruction given by user)\n   \u2193\nTokenization\n(Input text is split into small tokens)\n   \u2193\nEmbeddings (Numbers)\n(Tokens are converted into numerical representations)\n   \u2193\nTransformer (Attention + Context)\n(Model understands meaning and context)\n   \u2193\nNext Word Prediction\n(Model predicts the next best word step by step)\n   \u2193\nFinal Answer\n(Generated response shown to the user)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#traditional-ai-vs-generative-ai","title":"\ud83e\udd16 Traditional AI vs Generative AI","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd39 Traditional AI \u2014 Predict</p> <ul> <li>Uses historical data to predict or classify outcomes</li> <li>Output is fixed and predefined</li> <li>Does not create new content</li> </ul> <pre><code>Input Data \u2192 Trained Model \u2192 Prediction\n</code></pre> <p>Example: Spam Email Detection</p> <ul> <li>Input: Email content</li> <li> <p>Output:</p> </li> <li> <p>Spam</p> </li> <li>Not Spam</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd0e Key Point: Traditional AI is about decision-making, not creativity.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd39 Generative AI \u2014 Generate</p> <ul> <li>Creates new content instead of selecting from predefined labels</li> <li>Output is dynamic and context-aware</li> <li>Can generate text, code, images, audio, and more</li> </ul> <pre><code>User Prompt \u2192 Generative Model \u2192 New Content\n</code></pre> <p>Example: ChatGPT</p> <ul> <li>Input Prompt:   \u201cWrite a Python API using FastAPI\u201d</li> <li> <p>Output:</p> </li> <li> <p>Generates new FastAPI code</p> </li> <li>Provides explanation and structure</li> </ul> <p>\u2728 Key Point: Generative AI doesn\u2019t just predict\u2014it creates original content based on context.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#tokens-tokenization","title":"Tokens &amp; Tokenization","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd39 What is a Token?</p> <p>Token = Small piece of text</p> <pre><code>Text: \"I love FastAPI\"\nTokens: [\"I\", \"love\", \"Fast\", \"API\"]\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f AI does not read sentences, it reads tokens.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd39 What is Tokenization?</p> <p>Tokenization = Breaking text into tokens so the AI can understand it.</p> <pre><code>Text\n \u2193\nTokenization\n \u2193\nTokens\n</code></pre> <p>Sentence:</p> <pre><code>\"ChatGPT is awesome!\"\n</code></pre> <p>Tokenization:</p> <pre><code>[\"Chat\", \"GPT\", \"is\", \"awesome\", \"!\"]\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Model processes these tokens instead of raw text.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd39 Tokens in Generative AI Flow</p> <pre><code>User Prompt\n   \u2193\nTokenization\n   \u2193\nTokens \u2192 Numbers\n   \u2193\nModel Processing\n   \u2193\nGenerated Tokens\n   \u2193\nFinal Text\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#embeddings","title":"Embeddings","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd39 What are Embeddings?</p> <p>Embeddings = Numbers that represent meaning</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 AI cannot understand text \ud83d\udc49 So tokens are converted into numbers (vectors) \ud83d\udc49 These numbers capture semantic meaning</p> <pre><code>Text\n \u2193\nTokenization\n \u2193\nTokens\n \u2193\nEmbeddings (Numbers with meaning)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#vocabulary-context-window-super-simple","title":"Vocabulary &amp; Context Window (Super Simple)","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd39 Vocabulary</p> <p>All tokens the model knows</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 It\u2019s like the dictionary of the AI model \ud83d\udc49 Each token has a unique ID</p> <pre><code>Text\n \u2193\nTokenization\n \u2193\nTokens from Vocabulary\n</code></pre> <p>If model vocabulary contains:</p> <pre><code>[\"hello\", \"world\", \"Fast\", \"API\", \"!\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Then:</p> <pre><code>\"FastAPI!\"\n\u2192 [\"Fast\", \"API\", \"!\"]\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f If a word is unknown, it\u2019s broken into smaller tokens.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd39 Context Window</p> <p>Context Window = Maximum number of tokens a model can process at once.</p> <pre><code>Conversation Text\n \u2193\nTokens\n \u2193\nContext Window Limit\n</code></pre> <p>If context window = 4,000 tokens</p> <p>Model can:</p> <ul> <li>\u2705 Understand long conversation</li> <li>\u274c Forget messages beyond 4,000 tokens</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Old messages get dropped first.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-attention","title":"What is Attention?","text":"<p>Attention = Focus on the important words</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 When reading a sentence, the model doesn\u2019t treat all words equally \ud83d\udc49 It pays more attention to relevant words to understand meaning</p> <pre><code>Sentence\n \u2193\nTokens\n \u2193\nAttention (focus on important tokens)\n \u2193\nBetter understanding\n</code></pre> <p>Sentence:</p> <pre><code>\"Dhruvish wrote code because he loves Python\"\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Who loves Python?\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model focuses attention on:</p> <ul> <li>Dhruvish</li> <li>he</li> <li>loves Python</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Attention helps link \u201che\u201d \u2192 \u201cDhruvish\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-transformer","title":"What is Transformer?","text":"<p>Transformer = The brain that understands and generates language</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 It processes all words together (not one by one) \ud83d\udc49 Uses attention to understand context and meaning</p> <pre><code>Input Sentence\n \u2193\nTokenization\n \u2193\nEmbeddings\n \u2193\nAttention (focus on important tokens)\n \u2193\nTransformer Layers\n \u2193\nNext Token Prediction\n</code></pre> <p>Sentence:</p> <pre><code>\"Dhruvish wrote code because he loves Python\"\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Why did Dhruvish write code?\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Transformer focuses on:</p> <ul> <li>Dhruvish</li> <li>wrote code</li> <li>because</li> <li>loves Python</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Transformer understands reason + context, not just words</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-self-supervised-learning","title":"What is Self-supervised Learning?","text":"<p>Self-supervised Learning = Learning without human labels</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model creates its own labels from data \ud83d\udc49 Learns by predicting missing or next parts</p> <pre><code>Raw Data (Text)\n \u2193\nHide / Mask Part\n \u2193\nModel Predicts Missing Part\n \u2193\nLearns from Its Own Mistakes\n</code></pre> <p>Sentence:</p> <pre><code>\"Dhruvish loves ____\"\n</code></pre> <pre><code>What word comes next?\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model predicts:</p> <ul> <li>Python</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f The sentence itself provides the training signal</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-prompting-reasoning","title":"What is Prompting &amp; Reasoning?","text":"<p>Prompting = How you ask the model Reasoning = How the model thinks before answering</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Better prompt \u2192 better thinking \u2192 better output \ud83d\udc49 Model breaks the problem into logical steps</p> <pre><code>User Prompt\n \u2193\nUnderstanding Intent\n \u2193\nStep-by-step Reasoning\n \u2193\nFinal Answer\n</code></pre> <p>Prompt:</p> <pre><code>\"If a user logs in and JWT expires in 1 hour,\nwhat happens after 1 hour?\"\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Explain step by step\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model reasons:</p> <ul> <li>User logged in</li> <li>JWT has expiry time</li> <li>After 1 hour token becomes invalid</li> <li>User must re-login or refresh token</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Prompting guides how deeply the model reasons</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-prompt-engineering","title":"What is Prompt Engineering?","text":"<p>Prompt Engineering = Writing clear instructions for the model</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 How you ask decides what you get \ud83d\udc49 Clear prompt \u2192 accurate output \ud83d\udc49 Vague prompt \u2192 random output</p> <pre><code>User Prompt\n \u2193\nClear Instructions\n \u2193\nBetter Understanding\n \u2193\nBetter Output\n</code></pre> <p>Prompt:</p> <pre><code>\"Create a FastAPI login API\"\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Add JWT, input validation, and example response\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model follows:</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI</li> <li>Login endpoint</li> <li>JWT authentication</li> <li>Clean response format</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Well-engineered prompts control the model\u2019s behavior</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-few-shot-prompting","title":"What is Few-shot Prompting?","text":"<p>Few-shot Prompting = Teaching by giving examples</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model learns how to respond from examples \ud83d\udc49 No retraining needed \ud83d\udc49 Examples guide format, style, and logic</p> <pre><code>Examples\n \u2193\nPattern Understanding\n \u2193\nNew Input\n \u2193\nSimilar Output\n</code></pre> <p>Examples:</p> <pre><code>Input: Add two numbers 2 and 3\nOutput: 5\n\nInput: Add two numbers 4 and 6\nOutput: 10\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Input: Add two numbers 5 and 7\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model predicts:</p> <ul> <li>12</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Examples teach the model what pattern to follow</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-chain-of-thought-cot","title":"What is Chain of Thought (CoT)?","text":"<p>Chain of Thought = Thinking step by step before answering</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model breaks a problem into logical steps \ud83d\udc49 Improves accuracy for reasoning tasks</p> <pre><code>Question\n \u2193\nStep-by-step Thinking\n \u2193\nLogical Conclusion\n \u2193\nFinal Answer\n</code></pre> <p>Question:</p> <pre><code>A user has 3 apples and buys 2 more.\nHow many apples does the user have?\n</code></pre> <pre><code>Think step by step\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model reasons:</p> <ul> <li>User starts with 3 apples</li> <li>Buys 2 more apples</li> <li>Total = 3 + 2</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Final Answer: 5 apples</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-context-engineering","title":"What is Context Engineering?","text":"<p>Context Engineering = Supplying the right background to the model</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model answers better when it has relevant context \ud83d\udc49 Context includes instructions, data, history, examples</p> <pre><code>Context\n \u2193\nUser Prompt\n \u2193\nBetter Understanding\n \u2193\nAccurate Output\n</code></pre> <p>Context:</p> <pre><code>You are a FastAPI backend expert.\nUse JWT authentication.\nFollow REST best practices.\n</code></pre> <p>Prompt:</p> <pre><code>Create a login API\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model uses context:</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI framework</li> <li>JWT-based auth</li> <li>Clean API structure</li> <li>Best practices</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Right context leads to focused and correct answers</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-rag-retrieval","title":"What is RAG &amp; Retrieval?","text":"<p>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) = Retrieve first, then generate</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Model does not rely only on memory \ud83d\udc49 It fetches relevant data and then generates an answer \ud83d\udc49 Makes answers accurate and up-to-date</p> <pre><code>User Query\n \u2193\nConvert to Embedding\n \u2193\nSearch in Vector Database\n \u2193\nRetrieve Relevant Chunks\n \u2193\nLLM Generates Final Answer\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#vector-database","title":"Vector Database","text":"<p>Vector Database = Store embeddings for fast similarity search</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Used to find similar meaning, not exact words \ud83d\udc49 Popular tools:</p> <ul> <li>Pinecone</li> <li>FAISS</li> <li>Chroma</li> </ul> <pre><code>Documents\n \u2193\nChunking\n \u2193\nEmbeddings\n \u2193\nVector Database\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#chunking-retrieval-flow","title":"Chunking &amp; Retrieval Flow","text":"<p>Chunking = Splitting large text into small pieces</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Helps fit into context window \ud83d\udc49 Improves retrieval accuracy</p> <pre><code>Large Document\n \u2193\nChunks (small text pieces)\n \u2193\nEmbeddings\n \u2193\nStored in Vector DB\n</code></pre> <p>Query Example:</p> <pre><code>\"How does JWT authentication work?\"\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Retrieval returns chunks about:</p> <ul> <li>Token creation</li> <li>Expiry</li> <li>Validation</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f LLM uses retrieved chunks to generate a correct answer</p> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f RAG = Search + Generate (Best of both worlds)</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-are-agents","title":"What are Agents?","text":"<p>Agents = AI that can think, decide, and act</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 They don\u2019t just answer \ud83d\udc49 They plan steps, use tools, and complete tasks</p> <pre><code>Goal\n \u2193\nPlan\n \u2193\nAction\n \u2193\nObservation\n \u2193\nRepeat until goal achieved\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#tool-function-calling","title":"Tool / Function Calling","text":"<p>Tool Calling = Letting AI use external functions</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 API calls, DB queries, code execution \ud83d\udc49 Model decides when and which tool to use</p> <pre><code>User Request\n \u2193\nModel Decision\n \u2193\nTool / Function Call\n \u2193\nTool Result\n \u2193\nModel Continues\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#planning-execution-loop","title":"Planning + Execution Loop","text":"<p>Planning = Decide steps Execution = Perform steps</p> <pre><code>User Goal\n \u2193\nCreate Plan\n \u2193\nExecute Step\n \u2193\nCheck Result\n \u2193\nNext Step or Finish\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>\"Get my GitHub PRs and generate test cases\"\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Agent plan:</p> <ul> <li>Fetch PR list (GitHub API)</li> <li>Read code changes</li> <li>Generate test cases</li> <li>Return result</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Agent plans + acts + adapts, not just responds</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-fine-tuning","title":"What is Fine-tuning?","text":"<p>Fine-tuning = Teaching a model your specific style or task</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Start with a pre-trained model \ud83d\udc49 Train it further on your own data \ud83d\udc49 Improves domain-specific accuracy</p> <pre><code>Base Model\n \u2193\nYour Dataset\n \u2193\nFine-tuning\n \u2193\nCustomized Model\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Train model on:\n\u2022 Your company support tickets\n\u2022 Your API response format\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Model replies exactly in your business style</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-are-multi-modal-models","title":"What are Multi-modal Models?","text":"<p>Multi-modal Models = Models that understand more than text</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Can process text, images, audio, video \ud83d\udc49 One model, multiple input types</p> <pre><code>Text / Image / Audio\n \u2193\nShared Model\n \u2193\nUnified Understanding\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Upload image of error + ask:\n\"Why is this happening?\"\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Model understands image + text together</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-are-small-language-models-slms","title":"What are Small Language Models (SLMs)?","text":"<p>SLMs = Smaller, faster, cheaper language models</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Fewer parameters \ud83d\udc49 Optimized for specific tasks \ud83d\udc49 Ideal for production systems</p> <pre><code>Task\n \u2193\nSmall Model\n \u2193\nFast &amp; Cheap Output\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>\u2022 Classification\n\u2022 Summarization\n\u2022 Tool calling\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Use SLM instead of large LLM \u2192 cost saving</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-are-reasoning-models","title":"What are Reasoning Models?","text":"<p>Reasoning Models = Models optimized for thinking</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Strong at:</p> <ul> <li>Logic</li> <li>Math</li> <li>Multi-step problems</li> <li>Planning</li> </ul> <pre><code>Problem\n \u2193\nInternal Reasoning\n \u2193\nLogical Steps\n \u2193\nFinal Answer\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>\"Design a scalable FastAPI system\"\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Model reasons about:</p> <ul> <li>Caching</li> <li>DB</li> <li>Load balancing</li> <li>Async tasks</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#cost-performance-optimization","title":"Cost &amp; Performance Optimization","text":"<p>Optimization = Same quality, less cost</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Critical for production GenAI systems</p> <pre><code>User Request\n \u2193\nRouting Decision\n \u2193\nSmall / Large Model\n \u2193\nCached or Fresh Response\n</code></pre> <p>Key Techniques:</p> <ul> <li>Use SLMs for simple tasks</li> <li>Use RAG instead of fine-tuning</li> <li>Cache embeddings &amp; responses</li> <li>Limit context window</li> <li>Batch requests</li> <li>Stream responses</li> </ul> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>\u2022 FAQ \u2192 Cache\n\u2022 Search \u2192 RAG\n\u2022 Reasoning \u2192 Large model\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Smart routing = 10\u00d7 cost reduction</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-distillation","title":"What is Distillation?","text":"<p>Distillation = Teaching a small model using a big model</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Big model = Teacher \ud83d\udc49 Small model = Student \ud83d\udc49 Student learns to behave like teacher \ud83d\udc49 Same behavior, much cheaper</p> <pre><code>Large Model (Teacher)\n \u2193\nGenerate High-quality Outputs\n \u2193\nTrain Small Model (Student)\n \u2193\nFast &amp; Cheap Model\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Teacher model answers complex questions\nStudent model learns those answer patterns\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Small model gives near-LLM quality at lower cost</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-quantization","title":"What is Quantization?","text":"<p>Quantization = Using fewer bits to store model numbers</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Reduce precision (32-bit \u2192 8-bit / 4-bit) \ud83d\udc49 Model becomes smaller and faster \ud83d\udc49 Slight accuracy trade-off</p> <pre><code>Full Precision Model\n \u2193\nReduce Number Precision\n \u2193\nSmaller &amp; Faster Model\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>32-bit weights \u2192 8-bit weights\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Same model, less memory &amp; faster inference</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-reinforcement-learning-rlhf","title":"What is Reinforcement Learning (RLHF)?","text":"<p>RLHF = Teaching models using human feedback</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Humans rate model answers \ud83d\udc49 Model learns what is good vs bad \ud83d\udc49 Improves safety, tone, usefulness</p> <pre><code>Model Output\n \u2193\nHuman Feedback\n \u2193\nReward Signal\n \u2193\nModel Improvement\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Two answers generated\nHuman selects the better one\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Model learns preferred behavior</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#what-is-mcp-model-context-protocol","title":"What is MCP (Model Context Protocol)?","text":"<p>MCP = Standard way to give tools &amp; context to models</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Defines how models access data &amp; tools \ud83d\udc49 Useful in agent-based systems \ud83d\udc49 Project / platform specific</p> <pre><code>Model\n \u2193\nMCP Interface\n \u2193\nTools / APIs / Context\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Model needs:\n\u2022 Google Drive\n\u2022 GitHub\n\u2022 Database\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f MCP standardizes tool + context access</p> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f These techniques make GenAI production-ready, scalable, and affordable</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#1-generative-ai-genai","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Generative AI (GenAI)","text":"<p>Definition: AI systems that generate content \u2014 text, code, images, audio, video \u2014 based on input prompts. They do not act autonomously; they respond to user requests.</p> <p>Key Features:</p> <ul> <li>Input \u2192 Output (predictive/generative)</li> <li>Usually stateless</li> <li>No long-term planning or goal-oriented behavior</li> <li>Often built using LLMs, diffusion models, or GANs</li> </ul> <p>Examples:</p> <ul> <li>ChatGPT (text generation)</li> <li>DALL-E / MidJourney (image generation)</li> <li>GitHub Copilot (code generation)</li> <li>MusicLM (music generation)</li> </ul> <p>Use Cases:</p> <ul> <li>Content creation</li> <li>Summarization</li> <li>Code generation</li> <li>Data augmentation</li> </ul> <p>Limitations:</p> <ul> <li>Doesn\u2019t take initiative</li> <li>Can hallucinate or produce unsafe outputs</li> <li>No memory or long-term reasoning by default</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#2-agentic-ai","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Agentic AI","text":"<p>Definition: AI systems designed to act autonomously to achieve a goal or complete tasks. They make decisions, plan steps, and can interact with the environment. Generative AI can be a component of agentic AI.</p> <p>Key Features:</p> <ul> <li>Goal-oriented</li> <li>Can plan, reason, and act in steps</li> <li>May have memory or context</li> <li>Often combines LLMs + tools + APIs</li> </ul> <p>Examples:</p> <ul> <li>AutoGPT (autonomous agent that can perform tasks across tools)</li> <li>BabyAGI (self-prompting agent to achieve goals)</li> <li>Microsoft Copilot (AI agent assisting in workflows, sometimes autonomously)</li> </ul> <p>Use Cases:</p> <ul> <li>Task automation (e.g., booking appointments)</li> <li>Research and summarization</li> <li>Autonomous business processes</li> </ul> <p>Key Difference from GenAI: Generative AI produces content when asked, while agentic AI decides what actions to take to achieve goals.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/AI_Concepts.html#3-ai-agents","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 AI Agents","text":"<p>Definition: An AI agent is the implementation of agentic AI \u2014 a system that has:</p> <ul> <li>Observations: Input from environment or APIs</li> <li>Actions: Decisions it can take</li> <li>Goals / Rewards: What it tries to optimize</li> </ul> <p>Think of agentic AI as the concept and AI agent as the practical system implementing that concept.</p> <p>Key Features:</p> <ul> <li>Observes environment</li> <li>Acts using tools, APIs, or apps</li> <li>May use planning or reasoning loops</li> <li>Can integrate LLMs for natural language understanding</li> </ul> <p>Examples:</p> <ul> <li>LangChain agents</li> <li>AutoGPT instances</li> <li>AI trading bots</li> <li>Autonomous customer support bots</li> </ul> <p>Relation to Generative AI:</p> <ul> <li>Generative AI often powers AI agents (e.g., LLM generates actions or plans).</li> <li>But agents add autonomy, planning, tool use, which pure GenAI lacks.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Analogy:</p> <ul> <li>Generative AI: A chef who cooks whatever you ask.</li> <li>Agentic AI: A chef who decides the menu, shops, and cooks for you.</li> <li>AI Agent: That chef in a kitchen, actively managing all steps.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html","title":"Feature Flow","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#1-customer-support-automation-end-to-end-flow","title":"1. Customer Support Automation \u2013 End-to-End Flow","text":"<pre><code>User\n\u2502  User asks a customer support question\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives the request, validates input, and routes it to the AI pipeline\n\u25bc\nIntent Classification\n\u2502  Identifies the type of issue (payment, order, account, FAQ)\n\u25bc\nVector Search (RAG)\n\u2502  Retrieves relevant company documents from the knowledge base\n\u25bc\nPrompt Builder\n\u2502  Combines user query, retrieved context, and system instructions\n\u25bc\nLLM\n\u2502  Generates a clear and human-like response\n\u25bc\nConfidence Check\n\u2502  Validates accuracy, confidence, and safety of the response\n\u25bc\nAI Response\n\u2502  Sends the answer to the user if confidence is high\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u25ba Human Agent\n     Escalates the query to a support agent when AI is uncertain\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#2-document-summarization-using-generative-ai-end-to-end-flow","title":"2. Document Summarization using Generative AI \u2013 End-to-End Flow","text":"<pre><code>User\n\u2502  User uploads a document or provides a document URL\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives the request, validates file type, and handles upload\n\u25bc\nDocument Preprocessing\n\u2502  Extracts text, cleans data, and splits large documents into chunks\n\u25bc\nChunking &amp; Token Handling\n\u2502  Breaks long documents into manageable parts to fit LLM limits\n\u25bc\nPrompt Builder\n\u2502  Creates summarization instructions (short, bullet, detailed, etc.)\n\u25bc\nLLM\n\u2502  Generates summaries for each chunk or the full document\n\u25bc\nSummary Aggregation\n\u2502  Combines chunk-level summaries into a final coherent summary\n\u25bc\nQuality &amp; Length Check\n\u2502  Ensures summary is accurate, concise, and within length limits\n\u25bc\nFinal Summary\n\u2502  Returns the summarized content to the user\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#3-legal-assistant-using-generative-ai-end-to-end-flow","title":"3. Legal Assistant using Generative AI \u2013 End-to-End Flow","text":"<pre><code>User\n\u2502  Lawyer or user asks a legal question or uploads a document\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives the request, validates input, and enforces access control\n\u25bc\nUser Authentication &amp; Authorization\n\u2502  Ensures only authorized users can access legal data\n\u25bc\nLegal Intent Classification\n\u2502  Identifies task type (case law search, contract review, summarization)\n\u25bc\nDocument Preprocessing\n\u2502  Extracts and cleans text from legal documents (PDF, DOCX)\n\u25bc\nVector Search (RAG)\n\u2502  Retrieves relevant laws, case precedents, and clauses\n\u25bc\nPrompt Builder\n\u2502  Constructs a legal-safe prompt with strict instructions\n\u25bc\nLLM\n\u2502  Generates legally grounded responses using retrieved context\n\u25bc\nCitation &amp; Source Mapping\n\u2502  Attaches references to laws and case documents\n\u25bc\nConfidence &amp; Risk Check\n\u2502  Validates accuracy and flags uncertain legal advice\n\u25bc\nFinal Legal Response\n\u2502  Returns answer with citations and disclaimers\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u25ba Human Legal Expert\n     Escalates complex or high-risk cases for review\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#4-multilingual-customer-support-using-generative-ai-end-to-end-flow","title":"4. Multilingual Customer Support using Generative AI \u2013 End-to-End Flow","text":"<pre><code>User\n\u2502  User asks a question in any language\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives request, validates input, and manages routing\n\u25bc\nLanguage Detection\n\u2502  Automatically detects the user\u2019s language\n\u25bc\nTranslation (Optional)\n\u2502  Translates query to a system-preferred language (e.g., English)\n\u25bc\nIntent Classification\n\u2502  Identifies the user\u2019s issue independent of language\n\u25bc\nVector Search (RAG)\n\u2502  Retrieves relevant knowledge using multilingual embeddings\n\u25bc\nPrompt Builder\n\u2502  Builds a language-aware prompt with tone and locale rules\n\u25bc\nLLM\n\u2502  Generates a response using retrieved context\n\u25bc\nBack Translation\n\u2502  Translates response back to the user\u2019s original language\n\u25bc\nConfidence &amp; Quality Check\n\u2502  Validates accuracy, fluency, and cultural correctness\n\u25bc\nFinal Response\n\u2502  Sends the localized response to the user\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u25ba Human Agent\n     Escalates complex or low-confidence cases\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#5-ai-generated-product-design-end-to-end-flow","title":"5. AI-Generated Product Design \u2013 End-to-End Flow","text":"<pre><code>User / Product Team\n\u2502  Designer provides product idea, requirements, or reference images\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives input, validates data, and routes to design pipeline\n\u25bc\nRequirement Parsing\n\u2502  Extracts design constraints (style, size, material, brand rules)\n\u25bc\nReference Data Collection\n\u2502  Gathers existing designs, brand assets, and inspiration samples\n\u25bc\nPrompt / Design Specification Builder\n\u2502  Converts requirements into structured design prompts\n\u25bc\nGenerative Model\n\u2502  Generates product design images or 3D concepts\n\u25bc\nDesign Variations Generation\n\u2502  Produces multiple design options for comparison\n\u25bc\nQuality &amp; Brand Check\n\u2502  Ensures designs follow brand, feasibility, and safety rules\n\u25bc\nHuman Review &amp; Feedback\n\u2502  Designers review, edit, and provide feedback\n\u25bc\nIteration &amp; Refinement\n\u2502  Improves designs based on feedback\n\u25bc\nFinal Product Design\n\u2502  Outputs approved design assets for production\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#one-line-interview-explanation","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line interview explanation","text":"<p>\u201cWe convert product requirements into structured prompts, generate multiple design variations using generative models, validate them with brand rules, and refine through human feedback.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#6-ensuring-brand-voice-in-genai-marketing-content","title":"6. Ensuring Brand Voice in GenAI Marketing Content","text":"<pre><code>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Define Brand Guidelines\n- Document tone, style, vocabulary, and key messaging points\n- Example: Friendly, professional, witty, or formal\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Collect Example Content\n- Gather previous marketing content that reflects brand voice\n- Example: Blog posts, social media posts, newsletters\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Build Structured Prompts\n- Include brand instructions in the prompt\n- Example: \u201cWrite a social media post in a friendly and playful tone using these key messages\u2026\u201d\n\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 Use Fine-Tuning or Few-Shot Learning\n- Fine-tune LLM on brand-specific examples OR\n- Provide 2-3 example outputs in the prompt (few-shot prompting)\n\n5\ufe0f\u20e3 Generate Content\n- Use GenAI (LLM) to create blog posts, ads, or social media content\n\n6\ufe0f\u20e3 Quality &amp; Brand Check\n- Human-in-the-loop review to ensure tone, style, and messaging are consistent\n- Optional automated checks for brand keywords or prohibited words\n\n7\ufe0f\u20e3 Feedback &amp; Iteration\n- Collect feedback from marketing team\n- Refine prompts or model tuning to improve alignment\n\n8\ufe0f\u20e3 Approval &amp; Publishing\n- Final content is approved and scheduled for publishing\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#one-line-interview-explanation_1","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cWe guide GenAI using brand guidelines, example content, and structured prompts, then validate outputs with human review to ensure all content matches the brand voice.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#7-improving-software-development-productivity-with-generative-ai","title":"7. Improving Software Development Productivity with Generative AI","text":"<pre><code>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Code Generation &amp; Completion\n- AI suggests code snippets, functions, or full modules\n- Examples: GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT Code Interpreter\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Code Review &amp; Refactoring\n- AI detects bugs, security issues, or inefficient code\n- Suggests improvements or optimizations\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Automated Testing\n- Generate unit, integration, or API tests automatically\n- Example: AI creates test cases from function definitions or requirements\n\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 Documentation &amp; Comments\n- Auto-generate docstrings, README files, or API documentation\n- Keeps documentation consistent and up-to-date\n\n5\ufe0f\u20e3 Learning &amp; Knowledge Assistance\n- AI explains complex code, algorithms, or frameworks\n- Provides examples for faster onboarding of developers\n\n6\ufe0f\u20e3 DevOps &amp; Deployment Assistance\n- AI generates CI/CD scripts, Dockerfiles, or Kubernetes YAML\n- Helps automate deployment tasks\n\n7\ufe0f\u20e3 Debugging &amp; Issue Resolution\n- AI suggests fixes for errors and exceptions\n- Reduces time spent searching for solutions\n\n8\ufe0f\u20e3 Task Planning &amp; Project Management\n- AI generates user stories, tasks, or effort estimates\n- Helps developers focus on coding, not planning\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#one-line-interview-explanation_2","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cGenerative AI boosts developer productivity by generating code, automating testing and documentation, assisting with debugging, and speeding up onboarding and deployment tasks.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/feature_flow.html#8-generative-ai-hr-chatbot-flow","title":"8. Generative AI HR Chatbot \u2013 Flow","text":"<pre><code>Employee\n\u2502  Asks HR-related questions (leave policy, benefits, onboarding)\n\u25bc\nAPI Gateway (FastAPI)\n\u2502  Receives request, validates input, and routes it to AI pipeline\n\u25bc\nUser Authentication &amp; Role Check\n\u2502  Ensures employee identity and access permissions\n\u25bc\nIntent Classification\n\u2502  Determines the type of query (FAQ, payroll, leave, policy)\n\u25bc\nVector Search / RAG\n\u2502  Retrieves relevant HR documents, manuals, or policies\n\u25bc\nPrompt Builder\n\u2502  Constructs AI prompt with retrieved context and brand tone\n\u25bc\nLLM (Generative AI)\n\u2502  Generates professional and accurate responses\n\u25bc\nConfidence &amp; Compliance Check\n\u2502  Ensures response is accurate, safe, and HR-compliant\n\u25bc\nResponse to Employee\n\u2502  Sends AI-generated answer\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u25ba Human HR Agent\n     Escalates complex, sensitive, or low-confidence queries\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html","title":"Interview","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#1-rag-vs-fine-tuning-very-simple","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 RAG vs Fine-Tuning (Very Simple)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#rag-retrieval-augmented-generation","title":"\ud83d\udd39 RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)","text":"<p>What it means: Before answering, the model looks up relevant documents and then answers only from them.</p> <p>Think of it like: \ud83d\udcda Open book exam</p> <p>Example:</p> <ul> <li>Chatbot answering from company policies</li> <li>Support bot using internal docs</li> </ul> <p>Why use RAG</p> <ul> <li>Data changes often</li> <li>You need accurate answers</li> <li>Want to avoid hallucinations</li> </ul> <p>Pros</p> <ul> <li>More accurate</li> <li>Easy to update data</li> <li>Can show sources</li> </ul> <p>Cons</p> <ul> <li>Slightly slower</li> <li>Needs vector database</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#fine-tuning","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Fine-Tuning","text":"<p>What it means: You train the model to behave in a specific way.</p> <p>Think of it like: \ud83e\udde0 Training someone to follow a fixed pattern</p> <p>Example</p> <ul> <li>Classifying tickets</li> <li>Always returning structured JSON</li> <li>Writing in a specific tone</li> </ul> <p>Pros</p> <ul> <li>Faster responses</li> <li>Smaller prompts</li> <li>Consistent output</li> </ul> <p>Cons</p> <ul> <li>Expensive</li> <li>Hard to update</li> <li>Still hallucinates facts</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#simple-rule-very-important","title":"\u2705 Simple Rule (Very Important)","text":"<p>RAG = knowledge Fine-tuning = behavior</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#2-how-to-reduce-hallucinations-simple","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 How to Reduce Hallucinations (Simple)","text":"<p>Hallucination = model makes up answers</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#best-ways","title":"\u2705 Best Ways","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Use RAG</p> <ul> <li>Answer only from documents</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Give strict instruction</p> <pre><code>If answer is not in context, say \"I don't know\".\n</code></pre> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Lower temperature</p> <ul> <li>Use <code>0.1 \u2013 0.3</code></li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Limit context</p> <ul> <li>Too much text confuses the model</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Validate output</p> <ul> <li>Reject answers without source</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#what-doesnt-help-much","title":"\u274c What doesn\u2019t help much","text":"<ul> <li>Fine-tuning alone</li> <li>Bigger models without data</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#3-how-to-scale-genai-apis-easy-explanation","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 How to Scale GenAI APIs (Easy Explanation)","text":"<p>GenAI APIs scale like normal backend systems</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#basic-flow","title":"Basic Flow","text":"<pre><code>User \u2192 FastAPI \u2192 Queue \u2192 LLM \u2192 Response\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#key-techniques","title":"Key Techniques","text":"<p>\u2705 Async APIs</p> <ul> <li>Don\u2019t block requests</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Queue (Celery / Redis)</p> <ul> <li>Handle high load</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Caching</p> <ul> <li>Same prompt \u2192 same answer</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Rate limiting</p> <ul> <li>Prevent abuse</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Use different models</p> <ul> <li>Simple task \u2192 cheap model</li> <li>Complex task \u2192 powerful model</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Interview Line:</p> <p>\u201cWe scale GenAI using async processing, queues, and caching.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#4-handling-long-documents-very-common-question","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Handling Long Documents (Very Common Question)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#problem","title":"Problem","text":"<ul> <li>Token limits</li> <li>High cost</li> <li>Poor answers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#solution-step-by-step","title":"\u2705 Solution (Step-by-Step)","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Split document into chunks</p> <ul> <li>500\u2013800 tokens</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Store chunks in vector DB</p> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Retrieve only relevant chunks</p> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Send only those chunks to LLM</p> <p>\ud83d\udccc Never send full document directly</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#5-cost-optimization-high-impact-topic","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Cost Optimization (High Impact Topic)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#what-increases-cost","title":"What increases cost?","text":"<ul> <li>Large prompts</li> <li>Expensive models</li> <li>Repeated requests</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#how-to-reduce-cost","title":"How to reduce cost","text":"<p>\u2705 Use smaller models where possible \u2705 Cache responses &amp; embeddings \u2705 Reduce prompt size \u2705 Limit output tokens \u2705 Use RAG instead of long prompts</p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Interview Quote:</p> <p>\u201cReducing tokens is the easiest way to reduce GenAI cost.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#6-streaming-vs-normal-responses-simple","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Streaming vs Normal Responses (Simple)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#normal-response","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Normal Response","text":"<ul> <li>Wait for full answer</li> <li>Easier to implement</li> </ul> <p>Use when</p> <ul> <li>Background jobs</li> <li>APIs returning JSON</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#streaming-response","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Streaming Response","text":"<ul> <li>Answer comes word-by-word</li> <li>Feels faster to users</li> </ul> <p>Use when</p> <ul> <li>Chatbots</li> <li>Long answers</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Streaming improves user experience, not accuracy</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#7-token-limits-handling-easy","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Token Limits Handling (Easy)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#problems","title":"Problems","text":"<ul> <li>Input too long</li> <li>Model cuts answers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#solutions","title":"Solutions","text":"<p>\u2705 Count tokens before sending \u2705 Remove old messages \u2705 Summarize chat history \u2705 Store memory in vector DB</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#memory-types","title":"Memory Types","text":"Type Purpose Short-term Recent messages Summary Old chat summary Vector Important facts"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#8-popular-generative-ai-tools-frameworks","title":"8\ud83d\udd39 Popular Generative AI Tools &amp; Frameworks","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#text-language-generation","title":"\ud83d\udcdd Text / Language Generation","text":"<p>Used for chatbots, summarization, Q&amp;A, and content writing.</p> <p>Models / APIs</p> <ul> <li>OpenAI (GPT-4 / GPT-4o)</li> <li>Anthropic Claude</li> <li>Google Gemini</li> <li>Meta LLaMA</li> </ul> <p>Frameworks</p> <ul> <li>LangChain</li> <li>LlamaIndex</li> <li>Haystack</li> <li>Hugging Face Transformers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#image-generation","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Image Generation","text":"<p>Used for product design, marketing creatives, and UI mockups.</p> <p>Models / Tools</p> <ul> <li>DALL\u00b7E</li> <li>Stable Diffusion</li> <li>Midjourney</li> <li>Adobe Firefly</li> </ul> <p>Frameworks</p> <ul> <li>Diffusers (Hugging Face)</li> <li>ComfyUI</li> <li>Automatic1111</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#video-generation","title":"\ud83c\udfa5 Video Generation","text":"<p>Used for marketing videos, training, and avatars.</p> <p>Tools</p> <ul> <li>Runway</li> <li>Pika Labs</li> <li>Synthesia</li> <li>HeyGen</li> </ul> <p>Use Cases</p> <ul> <li>Text-to-video</li> <li>Avatar videos</li> <li>AI ads &amp; demos</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#audio-voice-generation","title":"\ud83d\udd0a Audio / Voice Generation","text":"<p>Used for voice bots, narration, and podcasts.</p> <p>Tools</p> <ul> <li>OpenAI TTS</li> <li>ElevenLabs</li> <li>Play.ht</li> <li>Amazon Polly</li> </ul> <p>Use Cases</p> <ul> <li>Text-to-speech</li> <li>Voice cloning</li> <li>Call center automation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#agents-autonomous-ai-systems","title":"\ud83e\udd16 Agents (Autonomous AI Systems)","text":"<p>Used for multi-step reasoning, task execution, and workflows.</p> <p>Frameworks</p> <ul> <li>LangGraph</li> <li>AutoGen</li> <li>CrewAI</li> <li>Semantic Kernel</li> </ul> <p>Capabilities</p> <ul> <li>Planning</li> <li>Tool calling</li> <li>Multi-agent collaboration</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#code-generation","title":"\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Code Generation","text":"<p>Used for developer productivity, code review, and debugging.</p> <p>Tools</p> <ul> <li>GitHub Copilot</li> <li>Amazon CodeWhisperer</li> <li>Cursor</li> <li>ChatGPT (Code Interpreter)</li> </ul> <p>Use Cases</p> <ul> <li>Code completion</li> <li>Refactoring</li> <li>Test generation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#multimodal-text-image-audio","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Multimodal (Text + Image + Audio)","text":"<p>Used for advanced AI assistants.</p> <p>Models</p> <ul> <li>GPT-4o</li> <li>Gemini Pro</li> <li>Claude 3</li> </ul> <p>Capabilities</p> <ul> <li>Image understanding</li> <li>Voice interaction</li> <li>Document + image reasoning</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#vector-databases-rag-support","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Vector Databases (RAG Support)","text":"<p>Used for knowledge retrieval.</p> <ul> <li>Pinecone</li> <li>Weaviate</li> <li>Qdrant</li> <li>FAISS</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#deployment-mlops","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Deployment &amp; MLOps","text":"<p>Used for production-ready GenAI systems.</p> <ul> <li>BentoML</li> <li>MLflow</li> <li>Weights &amp; Biases</li> <li>FastAPI (API layer)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#9-role-of-data-in-generative-ai-simple-version","title":"9\ud83d\udd39 Role of Data in Generative AI (Simple Version)","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Teaching the AI</p> <ul> <li>AI learns patterns from lots of examples (text, images, code, etc.)</li> <li>Better and more varied data \u2192 smarter AI</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Ensuring Good Output</p> <ul> <li>Clean and correct data helps AI give accurate answers</li> <li>Poor or biased data \u2192 wrong or biased outputs</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Specialized Knowledge</p> <ul> <li>Domain-specific data (like medical or legal documents) lets AI work for specific tasks</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Learning Over Time</p> <ul> <li>AI can improve by learning from user interactions and feedback</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Using Data in Practice</p> <ul> <li>AI can also use real-time data for generating answers (like FAQs, support docs)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-explanation","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cData is the fuel for Generative AI \u2013 it teaches the model, ensures accuracy, and helps it improve over time.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#10-handling-biased-or-offensive-ai-content","title":"10\ud83d\udd39 Handling Biased or Offensive AI Content","text":"<pre><code>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Prevention: Data &amp; Prompting\n- Use diverse and high-quality training data  \n- Include explicit instructions in prompts to avoid sensitive/offensive outputs  \n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Content Filtering &amp; Moderation\n- Apply automated filters to detect offensive, harmful, or biased language  \n- Example: Keyword/blocklist filters, toxicity detection models  \n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Human-in-the-Loop Review\n- Flag uncertain or sensitive outputs for human review before publishing  \n- Ensures accountability and reduces risk  \n\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 Bias &amp; Fairness Audits\n- Regularly test AI outputs for demographic, gender, or cultural biases  \n- Adjust prompts or fine-tune models to reduce bias  \n\n5\ufe0f\u20e3 Feedback &amp; Iteration\n- Collect user and stakeholder feedback on inappropriate outputs  \n- Refine prompts, data, and model behavior continuously  \n\n6\ufe0f\u20e3 Escalation &amp; Logging\n- Log biased or offensive outputs for auditing and regulatory compliance  \n- Escalate critical cases to legal or ethics teams if needed\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-interview-explanation","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cWe prevent bias through careful data and prompts, filter and moderate content, include human review for sensitive outputs, and continuously audit and improve the model.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#11-diffusion-models-simple-version","title":"11. Diffusion Models (Simple Version)","text":"<p>What it is: A diffusion model is a type of AI that creates new content by starting with random noise and slowly turning it into something meaningful, like an image or sound.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#how-it-works-simple-steps","title":"\ud83d\udd39 How It Works (Simple Steps)","text":"<ol> <li>Start with noise \u2013 like static on a TV screen</li> <li>AI gradually removes noise \u2013 step by step, it \u201ccleans\u201d it</li> <li>Final output appears \u2013 a clear image, video, or audio is generated</li> </ol>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#examples","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Examples","text":"<ul> <li>Image generation: Stable Diffusion, DALL\u00b7E</li> <li>Music or voice generation</li> <li>Video creation from text</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#key-point","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Key Point","text":"<p>\u201cDiffusion models make content by starting from randomness and learning to turn it into realistic outputs.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#12-hallucinations-in-generative-ai-easy-version","title":"12 Hallucinations in Generative AI (Easy Version)","text":"<p>What it is: A hallucination happens when AI makes up information that is wrong, even though it sounds confident.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#simple-examples","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Simple Examples","text":"<ul> <li>AI says a fake CEO name for a company</li> <li>AI writes a function or code that doesn\u2019t exist</li> <li>AI gives a wrong fact, date, or statistic</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#why-it-happens","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Why It Happens","text":"<ul> <li>AI doesn\u2019t \u201cknow\u201d facts\u2014it predicts words based on patterns it learned from data</li> <li>Sometimes it guesses and produces something false</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#how-to-fix-it","title":"\ud83d\udd39 How to Fix It","text":"<ol> <li>Give real information (documents, FAQs) to AI \u2192 RAG</li> <li>Check the answers with humans or trusted sources</li> <li>Train AI on correct data for your domain</li> </ol>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-explanation_1","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cHallucinations are when AI makes up wrong information, so we need to verify answers using documents or human review.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#13-ensuring-ethical-and-unbiased-ai","title":"13. Ensuring Ethical and Unbiased AI","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#1-diverse-high-quality-data","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Diverse &amp; High-Quality Data","text":"<ul> <li>Train AI on representative and inclusive datasets</li> <li>Avoid over-representation of a single group or perspective</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#2-bias-detection-testing","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Bias Detection &amp; Testing","text":"<ul> <li>Regularly audit AI outputs for bias or unfairness</li> <li>Test across different demographics, languages, and scenarios</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#3-transparent-design","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Transparent Design","text":"<ul> <li>Make AI decisions explainable</li> <li>Document how the model works, its limitations, and assumptions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#4-human-in-the-loop","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Human-in-the-Loop","text":"<ul> <li>Include human review for sensitive or high-impact decisions</li> <li>Escalate flagged outputs to humans</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#5-ethical-guidelines-policies","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Ethical Guidelines &amp; Policies","text":"<ul> <li>Follow industry ethics standards (like fairness, privacy, accountability)</li> <li>Ensure compliance with legal regulations (GDPR, HIPAA, etc.)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#6-continuous-monitoring-feedback","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Continuous Monitoring &amp; Feedback","text":"<ul> <li>Monitor AI in production for unexpected behavior</li> <li>Collect user feedback and update models accordingly</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-interview-explanation_1","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cEthical AI comes from using diverse data, auditing for bias, adding human oversight, following ethical guidelines, and continuously monitoring outputs.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#14-challenges-in-scaling-generative-ai-in-enterprises","title":"14. Challenges in Scaling Generative AI in Enterprises","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#1-high-computational-cost","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 High Computational Cost","text":"<ul> <li>LLMs and generative models require lots of GPU/TPU resources</li> <li>Running AI at scale can become expensive</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#2-data-privacy-security","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Data Privacy &amp; Security","text":"<ul> <li>Enterprise data often contains sensitive or confidential information</li> <li>Must ensure compliance with GDPR, HIPAA, or internal policies</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#3-model-accuracy-hallucinations","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Model Accuracy &amp; Hallucinations","text":"<ul> <li>AI can generate incorrect or misleading outputs</li> <li>Enterprises need validation layers or human-in-the-loop checks</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#4-integration-complexity","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Integration Complexity","text":"<ul> <li>Integrating AI into existing ERP, CRM, or internal tools is challenging</li> <li>Requires APIs, pipelines, and data engineering</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#5-maintaining-domain-relevance","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Maintaining Domain Relevance","text":"<ul> <li>Generic AI may not understand industry-specific terms</li> <li>Requires fine-tuning or RAG with internal knowledge bases</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#6-monitoring-governance","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Monitoring &amp; Governance","text":"<ul> <li>Need continuous monitoring of AI behavior and auditing for bias</li> <li>Must implement logging, alerts, and governance policies</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#7-scalability-latency","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Scalability &amp; Latency","text":"<ul> <li>Handling large number of users or requests in real-time</li> <li>Ensuring fast and reliable responses at scale</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#8-user-adoption-trust","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 User Adoption &amp; Trust","text":"<ul> <li>Employees may distrust AI outputs</li> <li>Need training, transparency, and clear human fallback</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-interview-explanation_2","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cScaling GenAI in enterprises is challenging due to costs, data privacy, hallucinations, integration complexity, domain relevance, monitoring, latency, and user trust.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#15-future-trends-in-generative-ai","title":"15. Future Trends in Generative AI","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#1-multi-modal-ai","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Multi-Modal AI","text":"<ul> <li>AI that can understand and generate text, images, video, and audio together</li> <li>Example: GPT-4o or Gemini Pro handling text + image reasoning</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#2-smaller-efficient-models","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Smaller &amp; Efficient Models","text":"<ul> <li>Smaller, faster models for edge devices or mobile</li> <li>Lower latency, cheaper to run, but still powerful</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#3-human-in-the-loop-systems","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Human-in-the-Loop Systems","text":"<ul> <li>AI assists humans rather than replacing them completely</li> <li>Critical for high-stakes tasks like healthcare, legal, finance</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#4-personalized-ai","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Personalized AI","text":"<ul> <li>AI will adapt to individual user preferences</li> <li>Personalized assistants, content generation, and recommendations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#5-rag-knowledge-enhanced-ai","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 RAG &amp; Knowledge-Enhanced AI","text":"<ul> <li>Retrieval-Augmented Generation for up-to-date, domain-specific knowledge</li> <li>Reduces hallucinations and improves accuracy</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#6-autonomous-ai-agents","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Autonomous AI Agents","text":"<ul> <li>AI systems that can plan, reason, and take actions autonomously</li> <li>Examples: AutoGen, LangGraph, CrewAI</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#7-ethics-governance-regulation","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Ethics, Governance &amp; Regulation","text":"<ul> <li>Growing focus on responsible AI, fairness, and compliance</li> <li>Enterprise adoption will require robust monitoring and auditing</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#8-ai-in-creativity-design","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 AI in Creativity &amp; Design","text":"<ul> <li>Generating products, marketing content, music, and videos automatically</li> <li>Accelerates creative workflows</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#9-integration-with-enterprise-workflows","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Integration with Enterprise Workflows","text":"<ul> <li>AI embedded in CRM, ERP, DevOps, HR, and customer support systems</li> <li>Enhances productivity and decision-making</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#10-ai-human-collaboration-platforms","title":"10\ufe0f\u20e3 AI + Human Collaboration Platforms","text":"<ul> <li>Platforms where humans guide, validate, and refine AI outputs in real time</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-interview-explanation_3","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cFuture trends in GenAI include multimodal models, smaller efficient models, personalized AI, RAG, autonomous agents, ethical governance, and deep integration into enterprise workflows.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#16-measuring-success-of-a-generative-ai-project","title":"16. Measuring Success of a Generative AI Project","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#1-accuracy-quality","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Accuracy &amp; Quality","text":"<ul> <li>How correct or relevant are the AI outputs?</li> <li>Metrics: human evaluation, BLEU/ROUGE scores (for text), FID/IS (for images)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#2-user-satisfaction","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 User Satisfaction","text":"<ul> <li>Are employees or customers happy with AI outputs?</li> <li>Metrics: surveys, Net Promoter Score (NPS), feedback forms</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#3-efficiency-productivity-gains","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Efficiency &amp; Productivity Gains","text":"<ul> <li>Does AI save time or reduce manual effort?</li> <li>Metrics: time saved per task, reduction in manual steps, faster response times</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#4-adoption-engagement","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Adoption &amp; Engagement","text":"<ul> <li>How widely is the AI used by the intended audience?</li> <li>Metrics: number of active users, queries handled, repeat usage</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#5-business-impact","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Business Impact","text":"<ul> <li>Does AI contribute to revenue, cost savings, or strategic goals?</li> <li>Metrics: increase in sales, reduction in support costs, improved customer retention</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#6-compliance-risk-management","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Compliance &amp; Risk Management","text":"<ul> <li>Is AI meeting ethical, legal, and regulatory standards?</li> <li>Metrics: audit logs, bias checks, privacy compliance</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#7-model-performance-over-time","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Model Performance Over Time","text":"<ul> <li>Does AI improve with usage and feedback?</li> <li>Metrics: reduction in errors, improved response confidence, fewer escalations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/interview.html#one-line-interview-explanation_4","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Explanation","text":"<p>\u201cSuccess of a GenAI project is measured by accuracy, user satisfaction, productivity gains, adoption, business impact, compliance, and continuous improvement.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/overview.html","title":"Overview","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html","title":"Agents","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#langgraph-agent","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Agent","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#what-is-a-langgraph-agent","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is a LangGraph Agent?","text":"<p>A LangGraph Agent is an intelligent node that uses a Large Language Model (LLM) to decide what to do next, choose tools, and carry out multi-step reasoning.</p> <p>It allows your AI workflow to:</p> <ul> <li>Think based on user input</li> <li>Decide which tool to use (search, summarize, etc.)</li> <li>Loop and refine results</li> <li>Return the final answer</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Think of it as the \u201cbrain\u201d of your LangGraph.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine you\u2019re at an Information Desk in a Mall.</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 You ask: \u201cWhere can I buy sports shoes?\u201d</p> <p>The assistant:</p> <ol> <li>Thinks: \"What type of store?\"</li> <li>Acts: Looks up the directory.</li> <li>Decides: \u201cNike Store on 2nd floor.\u201d</li> <li>Might follow up: \u201cSneakers or running shoes?\u201d</li> </ol> <p>This loop of thinking + action is just like a LangGraph Agent deciding what tool to use and what to say next \u2014 until the task is resolved.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udd0d AI Research Assistant Accepts a question, performs searches, summarizes findings \ud83d\udcc5 Task &amp; Calendar Manager Schedules tasks using calendar APIs \ud83e\uddea Automated QA Engineer Reads code, runs test tools, logs bugs \ud83e\udde0 Support Chatbot Agent Handles layered queries using memory + web search \u2699\ufe0f DevOps Agent Accepts commands and invokes cloud/CLI tools"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#langgraph-agent-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Agent Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \"Find latest Python news and summarize it\"\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Agent Node (LLM-Powered Decision Maker)\n   \u2022 Understands user intent\n   \u2022 Decides to use Search Tool\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd27 Search Tool\n   \u2022 Performs search\n   \u2022 Returns raw results\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Agent Node (Re-evaluate)\n   \u2022 Receives search results\n   \u2022 Decides to summarize\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u270d\ufe0f Summarizer Tool\n   \u2022 Takes raw search result\n   \u2022 Generates summary\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Agent Node (Final Step)\n   \u2022 Verifies task completion\n   \u2022 Prepares response for user\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u2705 Final Output\n   \u2022 Returns summarized Python news to user\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Agents.html#sample-agent-yaml-langgraph-syntax","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Sample Agent YAML (LangGraph Syntax)","text":"<pre><code>nodes:\n  agent:\n    type: agent\n    llm: gpt-4\n    tools: [search, summarizer]\n\nedges:\n  - source: input\n    target: agent\n  - source: agent\n    condition: needs_search\n    target: search\n  - source: search\n    target: agent\n  - source: agent\n    condition: needs_summary\n    target: summarizer\n  - source: summarizer\n    target: agent\n  - source: agent\n    condition: complete\n    target: output\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html","title":"Chains","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html#langgraph-chain","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Chain","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html#what-is-a-langgraph-chain","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is a LangGraph Chain?","text":"<p>A LangGraph Chain is a linear or branching sequence of connected steps (nodes) that process information in a structured flow \u2014 from input to output.</p> <p>Each step can be:</p> <ul> <li>A tool (e.g., search, database, web scraper)</li> <li>A model (e.g., GPT, embedding model)</li> <li>A conditional branch</li> <li>Even another sub-chain or agent</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd17 Think of it as a pipeline where data flows through modular components.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine a coffee shop assembly line:</p> <p>\u2615 You order a cappuccino.</p> <p>The barista:</p> <ol> <li>Receives the order</li> <li>Grinds the beans</li> <li>Brews espresso</li> <li>Steams milk</li> <li>Combines ingredients</li> <li>Serves the drink</li> </ol> <p>Each step depends on the previous one \u2014 this is exactly how LangGraph Chains work: each node passes its output to the next node in the sequence.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udcc4 Document Processor Load a file \u2192 Chunk text \u2192 Embed \u2192 Store in vector DB \ud83c\udf10 Web Data Summarizer Load URL \u2192 Extract text \u2192 Summarize \u2192 Output key insights \ud83d\udd27 Code Analyzer Input code \u2192 Run static checks \u2192 Suggest improvements \u2709\ufe0f Email Reply Generator Parse incoming mail \u2192 Detect intent \u2192 Generate and send a reply \ud83d\udcca Data Cleaning Pipeline Fetch CSV \u2192 Clean missing values \u2192 Normalize columns \u2192 Save to database"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Chains.html#langgraph-chain-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Chain Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \"Summarize this webpage\"\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd17 Load URL Node\n   \u2022 Fetches webpage content\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u2702\ufe0f Chunking Node\n   \u2022 Breaks text into manageable pieces\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 LLM Summarizer Node\n   \u2022 Summarizes each chunk\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcda Combine Node\n   \u2022 Merges partial summaries into a final response\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u2705 Final Output\n   \u2022 Delivers complete summary to user\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html","title":"Document Loaders","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#langgraph-document-loader","title":"\ud83d\udcc4 LangGraph: Document Loader","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#what-is-a-document-loader","title":"\ud83d\udd39 What is a Document Loader?","text":"<p>A Document Loader is used to load external data (PDFs, URLs, Notion docs, S3 files, etc.) into a format that can be chunked, embedded, or passed into an LLM or a vectorstore.</p> <p>Think of it as: \"How do I bring real-world content (files, webpages, etc.) into my AI workflow?\"</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#core-workflow-with-document-loaders","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Core Workflow with Document Loaders","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udcc1 Source (PDF / URL / Notion / S3 / Markdown)\n   \u2502\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcc4 Document Loader\n   \u2022 Loads file into standardized document format\n   \u2022 Often includes metadata (source, title, etc.)\n   \u2502\n   \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Chunker (optional)\n   \u2022 Splits large docs into chunks\n   \u2502\n   \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddf2 Embedder + VectorStore (optional)\n   \u2022 Stores chunks with embeddings for search\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#example-load-a-pdf-and-search-with-langgraph-agent","title":"\u2705 Example: Load a PDF and Search with LangGraph Agent","text":"<p>We\u2019ll use:</p> <ul> <li><code>PyMuPDFLoader</code> (PDF)</li> <li><code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> (to chunk)</li> <li><code>FAISS</code> (as vector store)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#installation","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Installation","text":"<pre><code>pip install langchain faiss-cpu pypdf\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#sample-code","title":"\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Sample Code","text":"<pre><code>from langchain.document_loaders import PyPDFLoader\nfrom langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\nfrom langchain.vectorstores import FAISS\nfrom langchain.embeddings.openai import OpenAIEmbeddings\n\n# Step 1: Load document\nloader = PyPDFLoader(\"sample.pdf\")\ndocuments = loader.load()\n\n# Step 2: Split into chunks\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=500, chunk_overlap=50)\nchunks = splitter.split_documents(documents)\n\n# Step 3: Embed and store in vector store\nvectorstore = FAISS.from_documents(chunks, OpenAIEmbeddings())\n\n# Step 4: Ask questions\nquery = \"What is the document about?\"\nretrieved_docs = vectorstore.similarity_search(query)\n\n# Preview result\nfor doc in retrieved_docs:\n    print(doc.page_content[:300])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#use-with-langgraph-agent","title":"\ud83d\udd0c Use with LangGraph Agent","text":"<p>You can add this as a custom tool inside LangGraph:</p> <pre><code>from langchain.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef search_pdf(query: str) -&gt; str:\n    docs = vectorstore.similarity_search(query)\n    return \"\\n\\n\".join(d.page_content for d in docs[:2])\n</code></pre> <p>Then include <code>search_pdf</code> as a tool in your agent\u2019s tool list.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#popular-document-loaders","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Popular Document Loaders","text":"Loader Source <code>PyPDFLoader</code> PDF files <code>WebBaseLoader</code> Web URLs <code>TextLoader</code> .txt or .md files <code>NotionDBLoader</code> Notion databases <code>UnstructuredFileLoader</code> Many formats (.docx, .ppt, .html) <code>S3FileLoader</code> AWS S3 files"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Document_Loaders.html#use-case-examples","title":"\ud83d\udcc2 Use Case Examples","text":"Use Case Loader Used Chat with your resume (PDF) PyPDFLoader Summarize research papers PyPDFLoader + Summarizer tool Ask questions over a website WebBaseLoader Build chatbot for docs folder DirectoryLoader"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html","title":"Evaluation","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#langgraph-evaluation","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Evaluation","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#what-is-langgraph-evaluation","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is LangGraph Evaluation?","text":"<p>LangGraph Evaluation is a structured way to test and assess the performance of your LangGraph workflows \u2014 especially chains and agents.</p> <p>It allows you to:</p> <ul> <li>Run test cases automatically</li> <li>Compare actual vs. expected output</li> <li>Use metrics like accuracy, similarity, BLEU, or custom functions</li> <li>Identify failures and areas for improvement</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\uddea Think of Evaluation as \u201cunit testing\u201d for your AI workflows.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine you're a teacher grading student essays.</p> <p>\ud83d\udcc4 Each student (your LangGraph) submits an answer. \u2705 You compare it to the correct answer. \ud83e\udde0 You may use rubrics (length, clarity, facts) or just judge similarity.</p> <p>This is exactly what LangGraph Evaluation does \u2014 systematically comparing output of your chain or agent to what it should have produced.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \u2705 Chain Output Validation Ensure a summarization chain gives concise and accurate results \ud83d\udcca Tool Effectiveness Testing Evaluate how well a tool-integrated agent performs (e.g., code interpreter) \ud83e\uddea A/B Model Testing Compare different LLMs in the same chain (e.g., GPT-4 vs Claude) \ud83d\udcda Curriculum QA Validate AI-generated educational content with gold-standard answers \ud83d\udcac Chatbot Behavior Check Measure if chatbot gives appropriate, polite, and accurate responses"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#evaluation-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Evaluation Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 Define Test Dataset\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; [Prompt + Expected Output]\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddea Run Graph on Prompt\n   \u2022 Executes agent or chain on input\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcca Evaluation Engine\n   \u2022 Compares actual output to expected\n   \u2022 Calculates scores (e.g., match %, similarity, custom logic)\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u2705 Report Results\n   \u2022 Shows passes, failures, metrics, diffs\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Evaluation.html#evaluation-methods-supported","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Evaluation Methods Supported","text":"Method Description \ud83d\udd0d <code>exact_match</code> Checks if actual output matches expected exactly \ud83d\udd17 <code>string_similarity</code> Measures similarity (cosine, Levenshtein, etc.) \ud83d\udcd0 <code>bleu_score</code> Useful for multi-token response evaluation \ud83d\udd27 <code>custom</code> Define your own Python function to evaluate"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html","title":"LLMs","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#langgraph-llms","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph LLMs","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#what-are-llms-in-langgraph","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What are LLMs in LangGraph?","text":"<p>LangGraph LLM nodes allow you to plug Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, or local models into your workflow. These nodes:</p> <ul> <li>Generate smart responses</li> <li>Interpret data or documents</li> <li>Decide actions when used inside agents</li> <li>Can be connected with memory, tools, or vectorstores</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Think of them as the \u201creasoning brain\u201d of your LangGraph \u2014 they interpret and act based on context.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine you're giving a smart assistant a task:</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 \u201cCan you summarize this blog post for me?\u201d</p> <p>The assistant reads it, understands it, and gives you a polished summary. That\u2019s what an LLM node does \u2014 reads your data, understands it, and responds intelligently inside a LangGraph workflow.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udcc4 Text Summarization Extract short summaries from long content \ud83e\udde0 Semantic Q\\&amp;A Use retrieved context to answer complex questions \ud83d\udcac Chatbot Reply Generator Handle multi-turn, memory-aware conversations \ud83e\uddea Code Linter Bot Analyze code and generate suggestions or fixes \u270d\ufe0f Email Generator Turn structured input into polished email drafts"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#langgraph-llm-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph LLM Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Injected into Prompt Template\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 LLM Node\n   \u2022 Processes prompt\n   \u2022 Uses memory or tool context (if configured)\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce4 Response Output\n   \u2022 Feeds into next node or returns to user\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#how-to-use-llms-in-langgraph-python","title":"\ud83d\udc0d How to Use LLMs in LangGraph (Python)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#step-1-install-langgraph","title":"\u2705 Step 1: Install LangGraph","text":"<pre><code>pip install langgraph langchain openai\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#step-2-define-your-llm-node-in-python","title":"\u2705 Step 2: Define Your LLM Node in Python","text":"<pre><code>from langgraph.graph import StateGraph\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import chat_agent_executor\nfrom langchain.chat_models import ChatOpenAI\n\n# Define the model\nllm = ChatOpenAI(model=\"gpt-4\", temperature=0.3)\n\n# Define a simple node that uses the LLM\ndef summarize_node(input_dict):\n    content = input_dict[\"input\"]\n    response = llm.predict(f\"Summarize this:\\n\\n{content}\")\n    return {\"output\": response}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#step-3-build-a-graph-with-llm-node","title":"\u2705 Step 3: Build a Graph with LLM Node","text":"<pre><code>builder = StateGraph()\n\nbuilder.add_node(\"summarize\", summarize_node)\nbuilder.set_entry_point(\"summarize\")\nbuilder.set_finish_point(\"summarize\")\n\ngraph = builder.compile()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#step-4-run-the-graph","title":"\u2705 Step 4: Run the Graph","text":"<pre><code>result = graph.invoke({\"input\": \"LangGraph is a framework for composing LLM applications...\"})\nprint(result[\"output\"])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/LLMs.html#model-configuration-tips","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Model Configuration Tips","text":"Parameter Purpose <code>temperature</code> Controls creativity (0 = precise, 1 = creative) <code>max_tokens</code> Limits output length <code>streaming</code> Stream tokens for real-time UI rendering <code>prompt</code> Customizes model instructions <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>llm = ChatOpenAI(model=\"gpt-4\", temperature=0.3, max_tokens=400)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html","title":"Memory","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#langgraph-memory","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Memory","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#what-is-langgraph-memory","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is LangGraph Memory?","text":"<p>LangGraph Memory is a mechanism that allows your LangGraph chains or agents to remember previous interactions, context, or state across multiple steps or conversations.</p> <p>It helps your workflow:</p> <ul> <li>Maintain continuity across steps</li> <li>Store and recall past user inputs or results</li> <li>Personalize responses based on history</li> <li>Enable multi-turn dialogue or task memory</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Think of Memory as the \"short-term brain\" of your AI system.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine chatting with a personal assistant:</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 \u201cRemind me to call Alice tomorrow.\u201d Later... \ud83e\uddd1 \u201cDid I set any reminders?\u201d</p> <p>The assistant:</p> <ul> <li>Remembers your past instruction</li> <li>Retrieves the reminder for you</li> <li>Responds with relevant context</li> </ul> <p>This is exactly how LangGraph Memory works \u2014 it tracks state and enables context-aware responses across multiple turns.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udcac Chatbot Conversation Maintain user history across turns to enable coherent dialogue \ud83d\udcda AI Tutor Assistant Remembers prior lessons and questions for contextual teaching \ud83e\uddea Multi-Step Reasoning Stores intermediate results used later in the chain or by the agent \ud83d\uddc2\ufe0f Task Tracker Agent Tracks tasks mentioned and completed in an ongoing session \ud83e\udde0 Persona-Based Memory Remembers user preferences, names, tone for more human-like interactions"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#langgraph-memory-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Memory Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \"What did I ask earlier?\"\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Memory Node\n   \u2022 Fetches conversation history or task state\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 LLM Node\n   \u2022 Uses memory + current input to generate response\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\u2705 Final Output\n   \u2022 Responds based on both new input and remembered context\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#sample-memory-yaml-langgraph-syntax","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Sample Memory YAML (LangGraph Syntax)","text":"<pre><code>nodes:\n  chat_agent:\n    type: agent\n    llm: gpt-4\n    tools: [search, summarizer]\n    memory:\n      type: conversation_buffer\n      key: chat_history\n\nedges:\n  - source: input\n    target: chat_agent\n  - source: chat_agent\n    target: output\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udd11 <code>memory.key</code> defines where memory is stored. <code>conversation_buffer</code> is the most common type for chat-like agents.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Memory.html#types-of-memory-in-langgraph","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Types of Memory in LangGraph","text":"Memory Type Description \ud83e\uddfe <code>conversation_buffer</code> Remembers prior messages in a session \ud83e\uddf1 <code>entity_memory</code> Tracks entities (e.g., names, dates) for better responses \ud83d\udcda <code>summary_memory</code> Summarizes previous turns to reduce token usage \ud83e\udde0 <code>custom</code> You can implement and plug in your own memory class"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html","title":"Prompting","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#langgraph-prompting","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Prompting","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#what-is-langgraph-prompting","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is LangGraph Prompting?","text":"<p>LangGraph Prompting is the practice of crafting and managing the messages given to LLMs (like GPT-4) inside LangGraph workflows. Prompts tell the model:</p> <ul> <li>What to do</li> <li>How to behave</li> <li>What tools to use</li> <li>What context or memory to consider</li> </ul> <p>LangGraph allows flexible prompting strategies, including static templates, dynamic input injection, and memory/context-aware prompts.</p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Think of prompting as writing clear instructions for your AI worker.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine giving instructions to a virtual assistant:</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 \"Act like a travel agent. Use polite language. Suggest 3 destinations in Asia.\"</p> <p>That\u2019s your prompt.</p> <p>Just like humans, AI models follow your instructions better when prompts are clear, complete, and well-structured.</p> <p>LangGraph allows you to control these instructions at every node, making your graph intelligent, reliable, and context-aware.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83c\udfeb Tutoring Assistant Prompt LLM to teach a topic in beginner-friendly language \ud83d\udcc4 Summarizer Chain Use prompts to control length, tone, or target audience of summaries \ud83e\udde0 Persona-based Agent Embed personality and behavior traits (e.g., polite, sarcastic, concise) \ud83d\udcac Chat Memory Prompts Inject conversation history into the prompt for context-aware dialogue \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Tool Selection Prompting Ask the agent to decide if it needs to use a tool or answer directly"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#langgraph-prompting-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Prompting Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Injected into Prompt Template\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Prompted LLM Node\n   \u2022 Uses system + user prompt\n   \u2022 (Optional) Injects memory, history, tool instructions\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce4 Response Output\n   \u2022 Based on prompt clarity, model reasoning, and graph context\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Prompting.html#prompt-types-in-langgraph","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Prompt Types in LangGraph","text":"Type Description \ud83d\udcdd Static Prompt Predefined message used across all calls \ud83e\udde9 Template Prompt Uses variables like <code>{input}</code> to dynamically inject data \ud83e\udde0 Memory-aware Includes conversation history or task state \ud83e\uddf0 Tool-aware Instructs the model how and when to call tools \ud83e\uddea System + User System prompt sets behavior, user prompt provides task"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html","title":"RAG","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#rag-data-retrieval-complete-directional-workflow-easy","title":"\ud83e\udde0 RAG DATA RETRIEVAL \u2013 COMPLETE DIRECTIONAL WORKFLOW (EASY)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#phase-1-data-ingestion-one-time-process","title":"\ud83d\udfe6 PHASE 1: DATA INGESTION (ONE-TIME PROCESS)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#1-raw-data-comes-in","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Raw Data Comes In","text":"<p>Examples:</p> <ul> <li>PDF</li> <li>TXT</li> <li>Website</li> <li>Database rows</li> </ul> <pre><code>PDF / Text / Website\n</code></pre> <p>\u274c LLM cannot read these directly.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#2-document-loader-langchain","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Document Loader (LangChain)","text":"<p>LangChain loads raw data and converts it into text.</p> <pre><code>Raw File\n\u2192 LangChain Document Loader\n\u2192 Plain Text\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#3-text-chunking-very-important","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Text Chunking (VERY IMPORTANT)","text":"<p>Large text is broken into small chunks</p> <p>Why?</p> <ul> <li>Vector search works best on small text</li> <li>Faster &amp; accurate retrieval</li> </ul> <pre><code>Big Text\n\u2192 Split into Chunks (300\u20131000 tokens)\n</code></pre> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>Chunk 1\nChunk 2\nChunk 3\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#4-create-embeddings","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Create Embeddings","text":"<p>Each chunk is converted into numbers (vectors) using an embedding model.</p> <pre><code>Text Chunk\n\u2192 Embedding Model\n\u2192 Vector (numbers)\n</code></pre> <p>Meaning:</p> <p>Similar meaning = vectors are closer</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#5-store-in-vector-database","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Store in Vector Database","text":"<p>All vectors are stored in a Vector DB</p> <p>Examples:</p> <ul> <li>FAISS</li> <li>Chroma</li> <li>Pinecone</li> </ul> <pre><code>Vectors\n\u2192 Vector Database\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Data ingestion is DONE \u2757 This is NOT repeated for every question</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#phase-2-user-query-flow-real-rag-happens-here","title":"\ud83d\udfe8 PHASE 2: USER QUERY FLOW (REAL RAG HAPPENS HERE)","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#6-user-asks-a-question","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 User Asks a Question","text":"<p>Example:</p> <pre><code>User: \"How does payment processing work?\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#7-convert-question-to-embedding","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Convert Question to Embedding","text":"<p>The user question is also converted into a vector.</p> <pre><code>User Question\n\u2192 Embedding Model\n\u2192 Question Vector\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#8-retriever-searches-vector-db","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Retriever Searches Vector DB","text":"<p>Retriever compares:</p> <pre><code>Question Vector\nVS\nStored Document Vectors\n</code></pre> <p>And finds TOP K similar chunks</p> <pre><code>Vector DB\n\u2192 Retriever\n\u2192 Most Relevant Chunks\n</code></pre> <p>Example result:</p> <pre><code>Chunk 5\nChunk 12\nChunk 20\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#9-build-prompt-for-llm","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Build Prompt for LLM","text":"<p>LangChain now creates a prompt:</p> <pre><code>SYSTEM PROMPT:\n\"You are an assistant. Answer using below context.\"\n\nCONTEXT:\n&lt;Retrieved Chunks&gt;\n\nQUESTION:\n&lt;User Question&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc This is the CORE of RAG</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#llm-generates-final-answer","title":"\ud83d\udd1f LLM Generates Final Answer","text":"<p>Now ONLY NOW the LLM is called.</p> <pre><code>Context + Question\n\u2192 LLM\n\u2192 Final Answer\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Answer is grounded in your data \u274c No hallucination</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#final-end-to-end-direction-flow-interview-perfect","title":"\ud83d\udd35 FINAL END-TO-END DIRECTION FLOW (INTERVIEW PERFECT)","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udcc1 FILES\n   \u2193\n\ud83d\udcc4 LOADERS\n   \u2193\n\u2702\ufe0f CHUNKS\n   \u2193\n\ud83e\udd16 EMBEDDINGS\n   \u2193\n\ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f VECTOR DB\n======================\n\ud83d\ude4b USER QUESTION\n   \u2193\n\ud83d\udd22 QUERY EMBEDDING\n   \u2193\n\ud83d\udd0e RETRIEVER\n   \u2193\n\ud83d\udcc4 RELEVANT CHUNKS\n   \u2193\n\ud83e\udde0 LLM\n   \u2193\n\u2705 ANSWER\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/RAG.html#very-simple-real-life-analogy","title":"\ud83e\udde9 VERY SIMPLE REAL-LIFE ANALOGY","text":"<p>\ud83d\udcda Library Example</p> <pre><code>Books = Your Data\nIndex = Vector DB\nLibrarian = Retriever\nReader Question = User Query\nBest Pages = Retrieved Chunks\nTeacher = LLM\n</code></pre> <p></p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html","title":"Tools","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#langgraph-tools","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Tools","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#what-are-langgraph-tools","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What are LangGraph Tools?","text":"<p>LangGraph Tools are modular, callable functions or utilities that LangGraph Agents and Chains can use to perform real-world actions \u2014 like searching the web, performing calculations, calling APIs, or manipulating data.</p> <p>They act as external helpers that your LLM can invoke when reasoning alone isn't enough.</p> <p>\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Think of tools as the \"hands and eyes\" of your LangGraph \u2014 extending the model\u2019s capabilities.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine an intelligent assistant:</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 \u201cWhat\u2019s the weather in Mumbai?\u201d</p> <p>Instead of guessing, the assistant:</p> <ol> <li>Thinks: \u201cI should look this up.\u201d</li> <li>Uses a weather API tool</li> <li>Returns accurate information</li> </ol> <p>That\u2019s what LangGraph Tools enable \u2014 letting your agent delegate specific tasks to purpose-built functions or APIs.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udd0d Web Search Tool Lets the agent search the web for up-to-date or unknown information \ud83e\uddee Calculator Tool Performs math or code-based calculations when needed \ud83c\udf10 API Tool Calls custom APIs (e.g., weather, database, services) with dynamic inputs \ud83d\udcdd Text Summarizer Tool Summarizes large texts using different logic than the LLM itself \ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f Database Tool Query MongoDB, PostgreSQL, or other databases via secure tool wrappers"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#langgraph-tool-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Tool Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc64 User Input\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Agent or Chain Node\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Decision\n   \u2022 Does this task need a tool?\n     \u2502\n     \u251c\u2500\u2500 Yes \u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udd27 Invoke Tool (e.g., search, math, API)\n     \u2502\n     \u2514\u2500\u2500 No  \u2500\u2500&gt; Respond directly with LLM\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce4 Output Result (from tool or model)\n</code></pre> <pre><code>from langgraph.tools import Tool\n\n@Tool\ndef calculate_expression(input: str) -&gt; str:\n    return str(eval(input))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Tools.html#tool-types-in-langgraph","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Tool Types in LangGraph","text":"Tool Type Description \ud83d\udd01 Function Tool A simple Python function you define and register \ud83c\udf10 API Tool Makes HTTP requests to external services (e.g., weather, finance, etc.) \ud83d\udd27 Built-in Tools Provided by LangGraph (search, summarize, etc.) \ud83d\udd17 Tool Wrappers Wraps SDKs or 3rd-party libraries (e.g., Pinecone, LangChain tools)"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html","title":"Vectorstores","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#langgraph-vectorstores","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 LangGraph Vectorstores","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#what-are-langgraph-vectorstores","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What are LangGraph Vectorstores?","text":"<p>Vectorstores in LangGraph are databases that store and retrieve high-dimensional vector embeddings of text.</p> <p>They are used to:</p> <ul> <li>Store documents as vector representations</li> <li>Search similar content using semantic similarity</li> <li>Power retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)</li> <li>Enable memory and knowledge recall within agents or chains</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Think of vectorstores as \u201csmart memory\u201d for your LLM \u2014 enabling semantic search, not just keyword match.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#real-world-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Analogy","text":"<p>Imagine a library assistant who doesn't just search book titles but understands meaning.</p> <p>\ud83e\uddd1 \u201cI\u2019m looking for documents about climate change effects.\u201d</p> <p>Instead of only matching the keyword \"climate\", they find relevant topics like:</p> <ul> <li>\u201cRising sea levels\u201d</li> <li>\u201cCarbon emissions\u201d</li> <li>\u201cWeather pattern shifts\u201d</li> </ul> <p>This is what vectorstores do: store information by meaning, so your AI can recall related knowledge, even if the user uses different words.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case Description \ud83d\udcc4 Document Q\\&amp;A User asks a question, system retrieves the most relevant documents first \ud83e\udde0 Context-Aware Agents Agent recalls relevant past knowledge from vector DB before answering \ud83d\udcda RAG Pipelines Combine retrieval with generation for more factual and grounded responses \ud83e\uddea Code Search Store and search functions, classes, snippets semantically \ud83e\uddfe Contract/Legal Analysis Embed long legal documents and search by concepts (e.g., \u201ctermination clause\u201d)"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#langgraph-vectorstore-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 LangGraph Vectorstore Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udcc4 Raw Document\n\u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83e\udde0 Embedding Model\n     \u2022 Converts text \u2192 vector\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce6 Vectorstore (e.g., Pinecone, FAISS)\n   \u2022 Stores vector + metadata\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd0d Semantic Query\n   \u2022 User prompt \u2192 embedded\n   \u2022 Finds top-k similar vectors\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce4 Retrieved Chunks \u2192 LLM\n   \u2022 Used as context to answer question\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#supported-vectorstores","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Supported Vectorstores","text":"Name Description \ud83e\udeb5 FAISS Local in-memory or disk-based vector index \ud83c\udf32 Pinecone Cloud-native vector DB with scale + speed \ud83e\uddf1 Weaviate Open-source semantic vector database \u2601\ufe0f Chroma Lightweight vectorstore, good for dev usage \ud83e\udde0 OpenAI Vector DB Coming soon to OpenAI ecosystem <p>LangGraph works with LangChain-compatible vectorstores, so you can plug in whatever you prefer.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/Vectorstores.html#summary-table","title":"\ud83d\udccc Summary Table","text":"\ud83d\udd39 Feature \u2705 Description Embedding Storage Stores document embeddings with metadata Semantic Retrieval Returns text chunks closest in meaning to a query RAG Integration Works with LLM to provide context-aware, factual answers LLM-Connected Feeds retrieved documents directly to LLM in LangGraph workflows Scalable Supports local and cloud vectorstores (e.g., Pinecone, FAISS)"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html","title":"Overview","text":""},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#langgraph-overview","title":"\ud83d\udd39 LangGraph Overview","text":"<p>LangGraph is a framework for building multi-agent workflows using LLMs (like GPT) as decision-makers. It\u2019s built on top of LangChain, so it reuses concepts like chains, tools, memory, etc., but adds graph-based state management and agent collaboration.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#agents","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Agents","text":"<p>LangGraph agents are LLM-powered nodes in a graph that can make decisions, call tools, talk to each other, or transition the workflow.</p> <ul> <li>Think of them as \"smart workers\" that handle part of the process.</li> <li>Useful in multi-step, multi-role workflows.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: ResearchAgent \u2192 SummarizerAgent \u2192 EvaluatorAgent.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#chains","title":"\ud83d\udd17 Chains","text":"<p>Chains are sequences of operations (prompt \u2192 LLM \u2192 output) that transform input to output.</p> <ul> <li>Can be used inside agents.</li> <li>Common chains: <code>LLMChain</code>, <code>RetrievalQA</code>, etc.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Prompt \u2192 GPT-4 \u2192 JSON output \u2192 Tool call.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#document-loaders","title":"\ud83d\udcc4 Document Loaders","text":"<p>These extract data from sources like PDFs, URLs, CSVs, Notion, etc.</p> <ul> <li>Used before passing content to embeddings or LLMs.</li> <li>Part of the data ingestion pipeline.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Load PDF \u2192 Split chunks \u2192 Embed \u2192 Store in vectorstore.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#evaluation","title":"\u2705 Evaluation","text":"<p>LangGraph supports evaluating LLM workflows.</p> <ul> <li>You can score agents, compare results, or log reasoning.</li> <li>Use cases: A/B testing LLM chains, agent traceability.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: HumanEval or GPT-Eval for output scoring.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#llms","title":"\ud83e\udd16 LLMs","text":"<p>LangGraph supports any LLM via LangChain \u2013 OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, etc.</p> <ul> <li>Each agent or node can use a different LLM if needed.</li> <li>You can fine-tune prompts per step.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Use GPT-4 for generation, Claude for safety filtering.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#memory","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Memory","text":"<p>Memory keeps track of previous messages/interactions.</p> <ul> <li>Important for stateful agents (like chatbots).</li> <li>LangGraph supports shared memory across steps.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Store chat history or decisions across graph nodes.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#prompting","title":"\ud83e\uddfe Prompting","text":"<p>LangGraph works well with prompt templates.</p> <ul> <li>Prompts define behavior for each node or agent.</li> <li>Prompts can be reused, parameterized, and templated.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: \u201cYou are a helpful research assistant. Summarize this document\u2026\u201d</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#tools","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Tools","text":"<p>Agents in LangGraph can call external tools (APIs, code functions, etc.).</p> <ul> <li>Supports LangChain tools and custom ones.</li> <li>Tools can be conditionally called via agent reasoning.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Agent decides to call \u201cSearchGoogle\u201d or \u201cCalculator\u201d tool.</p>"},{"location":"AI_ML/Langraph/overview.html#vectorstores","title":"\ud83d\udcda Vectorstores","text":"<p>Used to store and search embedded documents (via embeddings).</p> <ul> <li>LangGraph integrates vectorstores like Pinecone, FAISS, Chroma.</li> <li>Essential for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: Search vectorstore for similar chunks before LLM answers.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html","title":"CTO Round","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#cto-round-python-developer","title":"\ud83e\udde0 CTO Round \u2013 Python Developer","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#1-what-is-your-role-and-responsiblity-as-a-python-developer","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 What is your role and responsiblity as a Python developer","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Understand business requirements</li> <li>Estimate tasks and risks</li> <li>Design the backend solution</li> <li>Write clean, scalable Python code</li> <li>Add unit tests</li> <li>Deploy to different environments</li> <li>Support production, fix bugs, add new features</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#2-how-do-you-design-a-backend-system-before-coding","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you design a backend system before coding?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Clarify requirements</li> <li>Define APIs and data flow</li> <li>Choose architecture and tech stack</li> <li>Design database schema</li> <li>Plan for scale, security, and failures</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#3-why-python-for-backend-systems","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Why Python for backend systems?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Fast development</li> <li>Clean and readable code</li> <li>Strong ecosystem</li> <li>Excellent for APIs, automation, and AI</li> <li>FastAPI gives high performance with async support</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#4-fastapi-vs-django-when-do-you-choose-which","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 FastAPI vs Django \u2013 when do you choose which?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI: High-performance APIs, async, microservices</li> <li>Django: Full framework, admin panel, ORM, fast CRUD</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#5-how-does-fastapi-achieve-high-performance","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 How does FastAPI achieve high performance?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>ASGI-based</li> <li>Async I/O</li> <li>Uses Starlette and Pydantic</li> <li>Handles more requests with fewer resources</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#6-what-is-asyncawait-and-where-do-you-use-it","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 What is async/await and where do you use it?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Used for non-blocking tasks</li> <li>API calls, DB queries, I/O operations</li> <li>Improves concurrency and performance</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#7-how-do-you-handle-scalability","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle scalability?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Stateless services</li> <li>Horizontal scaling</li> <li>Redis caching</li> <li>Background workers</li> <li>Proper DB indexing and load balancers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#8-how-do-you-handle-background-tasks","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle background tasks?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Small tasks \u2192 FastAPI BackgroundTasks</li> <li>Heavy or long tasks \u2192 Celery + Redis/RabbitMQ</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#9-how-do-you-design-apis-for-large-systems","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you design APIs for large systems?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>REST standards</li> <li>Versioned APIs</li> <li>Clear request/response models</li> <li>Proper error handling</li> <li>OpenAPI documentation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#how-do-you-secure-apis","title":"\ud83d\udd1f How do you secure APIs?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>JWT authentication</li> <li>Role-based access control</li> <li>HTTPS</li> <li>Input validation</li> <li>Rate limiting</li> <li>Token expiry</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#11-authentication-vs-authorization","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 Authentication vs Authorization","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Authentication: Who the user is</li> <li>Authorization: What the user can access</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#12-how-do-you-handle-database-design","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle database design?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Normalize tables</li> <li>Define indexes</li> <li>Manage relationships carefully</li> <li>Optimize based on query usage</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#13-sql-vs-nosql-when-to-use-what","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 SQL vs NoSQL \u2013 when to use what?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>SQL: Structured data, transactions</li> <li>NoSQL: High scale, flexible schema, fast reads</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#14-how-do-you-improve-api-performance","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you improve API performance?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Caching</li> <li>Query optimization</li> <li>Async processing</li> <li>Pagination</li> <li>Avoid N+1 queries</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#15-what-caching-strategies-have-you-used","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 What caching strategies have you used?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li> <p>Redis for:</p> </li> <li> <p>API responses</p> </li> <li>Sessions</li> <li>Rate limiting</li> <li>Token storage (TTL-based)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#16-how-do-you-handle-production-issues","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e36\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle production issues?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Check logs and metrics</li> <li>Find root cause</li> <li>Apply hotfix or rollback</li> <li>Add monitoring to prevent recurrence</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#17-how-do-you-ensure-code-quality","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e37\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you ensure code quality?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Code reviews</li> <li>Unit tests</li> <li>Linting</li> <li>Clean documentation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#18-how-do-you-write-testable-code","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e38\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you write testable code?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Small functions</li> <li>Dependency injection</li> <li>Mock external services</li> <li>Unit and integration tests</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#19-explain-a-challenging-problem-you-solved","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e39\ufe0f\u20e3 Explain a challenging problem you solved","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>We faced high API latency due to traffic. I added Redis caching, optimized DB queries, and used async processing, which significantly reduced response time.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#20-how-do-you-handle-deployments","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e30\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle deployments?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Docker</li> <li>CI/CD pipelines</li> <li>Environment-based configs</li> <li>Automated tests</li> <li>Safe rollouts</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#21-what-system-design-concepts-do-you-know","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 What system design concepts do you know?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Load balancing</li> <li>Caching</li> <li>Rate limiting</li> <li>Queues</li> <li>Horizontal scaling</li> <li>Sharding</li> <li>Fault tolerance</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#22-how-do-you-design-for-failure","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you design for failure?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Timeouts</li> <li>Retries</li> <li>Circuit breakers</li> <li>Graceful degradation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#23-how-do-you-work-with-frontend-and-product-teams","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you work with frontend and product teams?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Clear API contracts</li> <li>Regular communication</li> <li>Proper API documentation</li> <li>Business-aligned decisions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#24-how-do-you-estimate-tasks","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you estimate tasks?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Break work into small tasks</li> <li>Identify risks</li> <li>Estimate realistically</li> <li>Add buffer</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#25-what-value-do-you-bring-as-a-senior-python-developer","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 What value do you bring as a senior Python developer?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Backend ownership</li> <li>Scalable system design</li> <li>Mentoring juniors</li> <li>Strong production support</li> <li>Business-focused technical decisions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#cto-round-closing-best-one-liner","title":"\u2b50 CTO-Round Closing (Best One-Liner)","text":"<p>\u201cI take ownership of backend systems \u2014 from design to production \u2014 and build scalable, reliable solutions.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#cto-level-system-design-questions","title":"\ud83e\udde0 CTO-LEVEL SYSTEM DESIGN QUESTIONS","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#simple-clear-answers","title":"(Simple, Clear Answers)","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#1-design-a-scalable-rest-api-system","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Design a scalable REST API system","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Stateless APIs</li> <li>Load balancer</li> <li>Horizontal scaling</li> <li>Redis caching</li> <li>Async processing</li> <li>Proper DB indexing</li> <li>Monitoring &amp; alerts</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#2-how-would-you-design-a-high-traffic-backend","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 How would you design a high-traffic backend?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Async APIs</li> <li>Cache frequently used data</li> <li>Pagination</li> <li>Optimized DB queries</li> <li>Queues for heavy tasks</li> <li>Auto-scaling services</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#3-design-authentication-for-large-systems","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Design authentication for large systems","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>JWT for stateless auth</li> <li>Short-lived access tokens</li> <li>Refresh tokens</li> <li>Role-based access control</li> <li>Central auth service</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#4-how-do-you-handle-millions-of-users","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle millions of users?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Horizontal scaling</li> <li>CDN for static content</li> <li>Redis caching</li> <li>DB read replicas</li> <li>Data partitioning if required</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#5-how-do-you-design-background-processing","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you design background processing?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Message queue (Celery + Redis/RabbitMQ)</li> <li>Retries</li> <li>Idempotent tasks</li> <li>Monitoring</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#6-how-do-you-achieve-zero-downtime-deployments","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you achieve zero-downtime deployments?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Blue-green or rolling deployments</li> <li>Health checks</li> <li>Easy rollback</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#7-how-do-you-handle-rate-limiting","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle rate limiting?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Token bucket or sliding window</li> <li>Redis-based counters</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#8-how-do-you-ensure-data-consistency","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you ensure data consistency?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>DB transactions</li> <li>Idempotent APIs</li> <li>Eventual consistency where acceptable</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#9-cap-theorem-how-do-you-apply-it","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 CAP theorem \u2014 how do you apply it?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>During network failure, choose between consistency and availability based on business need</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#how-do-you-design-observability","title":"\ud83d\udd1f How do you design observability?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Centralized logs</li> <li>Metrics</li> <li>Tracing</li> <li>Alerts</li> <li>Dashboards</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#real-production-scenarios","title":"\ud83e\udde9 REAL PRODUCTION SCENARIOS","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#ctos-care-about-these","title":"(CTOs Care About These)","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#1-api-becomes-slow-in-production","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 API becomes slow in production","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Check metrics</li> <li>Find bottleneck</li> <li>Analyze slow DB queries</li> <li>Add caching</li> <li>Scale services</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#2-database-cpu-spikes","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Database CPU spikes","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Identify slow queries</li> <li>Add indexes</li> <li>Reduce heavy joins</li> <li>Cache results</li> <li>Move heavy work to background</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#3-celery-tasks-failing-randomly","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Celery tasks failing randomly","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Add retries</li> <li>Make tasks idempotent</li> <li>Improve error logs</li> <li>Scale workers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#4-redis-goes-down","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Redis goes down","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Graceful fallback to DB</li> <li>Timeouts</li> <li>Redis replication</li> <li>Circuit breakers</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#5-memory-leak-in-python-service","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Memory leak in Python service","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Monitor memory</li> <li>Identify leaking objects</li> <li>Fix lifecycle issues</li> <li>Restart unhealthy pods</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#6-bug-after-production-deployment","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Bug after production deployment","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Rollback immediately</li> <li>Identify root cause</li> <li>Fix and add tests</li> <li>Redeploy safely</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#7-external-api-is-slow-or-failing","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 External API is slow or failing","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Timeouts</li> <li>Retries with backoff</li> <li>Circuit breaker</li> <li>Cache responses</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#8-data-inconsistency-across-services","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Data inconsistency across services","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Idempotent events</li> <li>Retries</li> <li>Compensating transactions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#9-high-error-rate-during-traffic-spikes","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 High error rate during traffic spikes","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Rate limiting</li> <li>Auto-scaling</li> <li>Queue overflow traffic</li> <li>Aggressive caching</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#security-vulnerability-found","title":"\ud83d\udd1f Security vulnerability found","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Patch immediately</li> <li>Rotate keys</li> <li>Audit logs</li> <li>Add monitoring</li> <li>Prevent recurrence</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#behavioral-cto-questions","title":"\ud83e\udde0 BEHAVIORAL CTO QUESTIONS","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#most-important","title":"(Most Important)","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#1-how-do-you-take-ownership","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you take ownership?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Own design, delivery, failures, fixes, and improvements</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#2-handling-pressure-during-incidents","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Handling pressure during incidents?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Stay calm</li> <li>Focus on impact</li> <li>Communicate clearly</li> <li>Fix safely</li> <li>Document learnings</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#3-disagreement-with-architect-or-cto","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Disagreement with architect or CTO?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Present data and trade-offs</li> <li>Align with final decision</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#4-how-do-you-mentor-juniors","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you mentor juniors?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Code reviews</li> <li>Pair programming</li> <li>Explain design choices</li> <li>Encourage ownership</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#5-speed-vs-quality-how-do-you-balance","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Speed vs quality \u2014 how do you balance?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Ship MVP fast</li> <li>Never compromise core quality and monitoring</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#6-tell-me-about-a-failure","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Tell me about a failure","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Explain issue</li> <li>Fix applied</li> <li>Preventive steps added</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#7-how-do-you-align-with-business-goals","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you align with business goals?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Understand business impact first</li> <li>Design technical solutions accordingly</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#8-what-do-ctos-expect-from-senior-engineers","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 What do CTOs expect from senior engineers?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Ownership</li> <li>Reliability</li> <li>System thinking</li> <li>Mentorship</li> <li>Business awareness</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#9-why-trust-you-with-critical-systems","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Why trust you with critical systems?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>I design for failure</li> <li>Monitor continuously</li> <li>Respond fast</li> <li>Improve reliability over time</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#how-do-you-see-your-growth","title":"\ud83d\udd1f How do you see your growth?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <ul> <li>Technical leadership</li> <li>Architecture ownership</li> <li>Building high-impact systems</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#cto-round-power-closing-line","title":"\u2b50 CTO-ROUND POWER CLOSING LINE","text":"<p>\u201cI build reliable systems, take ownership of outcomes, and align engineering decisions with business impact.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#1-how-do-you-create-system-design-for-your-work","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you create system design for your work?","text":"<p>Simple CTO-friendly answer:</p> <p>I start by understanding the business requirement and scale. Then I break the system into components like API, database, cache, background workers, and storage. I decide how data flows, where caching is needed, and which tasks should run asynchronously. Finally, I think about scalability, security, monitoring, and failure handling before writing code.</p> <p>One-line version (if CTO is in a hurry):</p> <p>Requirement \u2192 Architecture \u2192 Data flow \u2192 Scaling \u2192 Security \u2192 Monitoring.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#2-how-do-you-handle-large-data-in-a-database","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle large data in a database?","text":"<p>Easy answer:</p> <p>For large data, I focus on database optimization. I use proper indexing, pagination, and avoid heavy joins. For frequently used data, I use Redis caching. If data grows very large, I use sharding or split data into multiple tables or databases.</p> <p>Real-world touch:</p> <p>I never fetch full data at once; I always use pagination and background processing for reports.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#3-how-do-you-handle-large-file-storage","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle large file storage?","text":"<p>Clean CTO-level answer:</p> <p>I never store large files in the database. I store files in object storage like AWS S3 and keep only metadata in the database. I use pre-signed URLs for upload and download to reduce backend load. For large uploads, I use chunked or multipart upload.</p> <p>Short version:</p> <p>Database for metadata, S3 for files, CDN for fast access.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#4-how-do-you-handle-heavy-traffic","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle heavy traffic?","text":"<p>Production-ready answer:</p> <p>I design stateless APIs so they can scale horizontally. I use load balancers to distribute traffic. I cache frequently used responses using Redis. Heavy tasks are moved to background workers using Celery or queues. I also apply rate limiting to protect the system.</p> <p>CTO keyword version:</p> <p>Load balancer + caching + async processing + horizontal scaling.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#5-how-do-you-handle-a-large-number-of-users","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 How do you handle a large number of users?","text":"<p>Simple but strong answer:</p> <p>I design the system assuming users will grow. I scale APIs horizontally, cache user sessions, and optimize database queries. For authentication, I use token-based auth like JWT. For global users, I use CDN and region-based deployments.</p> <p>Real-life example line:</p> <p>Even if users increase 10x, the system should work without major code changes.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#cto-bonus-question-very-common","title":"\ud83d\udd25 CTO BONUS QUESTION (Very Common)","text":""},{"location":"Communication/CTO.html#how-do-you-ensure-system-reliability","title":"How do you ensure system reliability?","text":"<p>I use logging, monitoring, and alerts. I handle failures using retries and fallbacks. I keep deployments safe using staging environments and rollback strategies.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html","title":"Manager Round","text":"<p>Here\u2019s your Technical + HR Interview Q\\&amp;A Guide beautifully formatted in Markdown for clear understanding and practice:</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#technical-round-questions-answers","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Technical Round Questions &amp; Answers","text":""},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#1-why-should-we-hire-you","title":"1. Why should we hire you?","text":"<p>\u2705 Tip: Highlight skills + match with JD + soft skills Answer:</p> <p>From your job description, I noticed you\u2019re looking for someone skilled in Python, Django, AWS, and communication. I believe I\u2019m a perfect fit for this role.</p> <p>I have strong communication skills, both verbal and written. I\u2019ve led presentations, collaborated across departments, and managed end-to-end backend features.</p> <p>At my previous company, I participated in a hackathon where we developed a smart trolley. I managed the backend integration while coordinating with frontend and DevOps teams. Our team secured 2nd position and received recognition from the VP.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#2-why-do-you-want-to-join-this-company","title":"2. Why do you want to join this company?","text":"<p>\u2705 Tip: Talk about company values, culture, innovation, growth Answer:</p> <p>I am impressed by your company's innovative approach, strong technical culture, and consistent focus on employee growth. Your recent projects and use of modern technologies align well with my aspirations.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#3-what-do-you-know-about-this-company","title":"3. What do you know about this company?","text":"<p>\u2705 Tip: Research beforehand\u2014mention products, vision, culture Answer:</p> <p>Your company is known for delivering scalable solutions and maintaining high engineering standards. I admire your focus on AI and cloud-native applications, and your presence in the developer community is strong.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#4-what-are-your-strengths-and-weaknesses","title":"4. What are your strengths and weaknesses?","text":"<p>\u2705 Strengths:</p> <ul> <li>Flexibility</li> <li>Adaptability</li> <li>Team Player</li> <li>Motivated</li> <li>Committed</li> <li>Quick Learner</li> </ul> <p>My greatest strength is adaptability. At my last job, I quickly aligned with sprint changes and collaborated across teams to meet tight deadlines.</p> <p>\u274c Weaknesses:</p> <ul> <li>Time management</li> <li>Delegating work</li> <li>Procrastination</li> <li>Asking for help</li> </ul> <p>I\u2019ve struggled with time management earlier, but I\u2019ve improved significantly using tools like Trello and time-blocking.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#5-what-motivates-you-to-work","title":"5. What motivates you to work?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>Solving complex problems and seeing my solutions create value motivates me. I\u2019m also driven by learning opportunities and the satisfaction of contributing to team success.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#6-what-is-your-biggest-achievement-to-date","title":"6. What is your biggest achievement to date?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>Participating in a nationwide hackathon and leading our team to 2nd place with a smart trolley project. I managed cross-functional collaboration and technical execution within 24 hours.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#7-how-do-you-handle-work-pressure","title":"7. How do you handle work pressure?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>I break down tasks, prioritize, and stay calm under pressure. I believe in smart delegation and maintaining communication with stakeholders.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#8-how-do-you-train-your-juniors","title":"8. How do you train your juniors?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>I focus on mentoring through real-time project pairing, code reviews, and knowledge-sharing sessions. I also introduce them to internal documentation and best practices.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#9-what-is-your-role-and-day-to-day-responsibility","title":"9. What is your role and day-to-day responsibility?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>My daily tasks include writing scalable backend APIs, integrating third-party tools, attending stand-ups, collaborating with DevOps for deployments, and maintaining documentation.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#10-why-do-you-believe-you-are-a-good-fit-for-this-position","title":"10. Why do you believe you are a good fit for this position?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>My experience with backend frameworks, cloud services, and ability to collaborate cross-functionally matches well with the requirements of this role.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#11-can-you-explain-a-gap-in-your-resume","title":"11. Can you explain a gap in your resume?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>I took a short break for upskilling and personal development. I enrolled in advanced backend and AWS training which enhanced my practical skills.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#12-what-is-your-salary-expectation","title":"12. What is your salary expectation?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>I am looking for a package that matches my skills, experience, and industry standards. I\u2019m open to discussion based on your company\u2019s structure.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#13-why-are-you-looking-for-a-change","title":"13. Why are you looking for a change?","text":"<p>Answer:</p> <p>I\u2019m seeking new challenges and opportunities to grow technically and professionally. I want to work in an environment where I can contribute to impactful projects.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#14-what-are-your-short-term-and-long-term-goals","title":"14. What are your short-term and long-term goals?","text":"<p>Short-Term:</p> <p>Crack this interview and adapt quickly to the new environment while learning and contributing to the team.</p> <p>Long-Term:</p> <p>Grow into a leadership role with technical and managerial responsibilities and become an expert in scalable backend systems.</p>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#15-do-you-have-any-questions-for-me","title":"15. Do you have any questions for me?","text":""},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#technical-round-questions-to-ask-interviewer","title":"\ud83e\udd1d Technical Round: Questions to Ask Interviewer","text":"<ol> <li>What are the day-to-day responsibilities of this position?</li> <li>What is the primary focus area of the role?</li> <li>Are there any immediate projects I\u2019d be working on?</li> <li>Can you share examples of real projects I\u2019d be part of?</li> <li>Are there any training programs or mentorship for new hires?</li> <li>Could you tell me about the team and its working structure?</li> <li>How big is the team, and who would I collaborate with most?</li> <li>What technologies or practices do you follow for collaboration?</li> <li>Is there a long-term roadmap for this role\u2019s expansion?</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#hr-round-questions-to-ask-hr","title":"\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbc HR Round: Questions to Ask HR","text":"<ol> <li>What is the next step in the hiring process?</li> <li>How would you describe the work culture here?</li> <li>Do you have any concerns about my profile for this role?</li> <li>I\u2019ve read about the company\u2019s founding\u2014can you share more?</li> <li>Is the work environment more collaborative or independent?</li> <li>Will travel be required for this position?</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#questions-you-should-never-ask","title":"\ud83d\udeab Questions You Should Never Ask","text":"<ol> <li>How did I do in this interview?</li> <li>When will I receive an offer?</li> <li>What does your company do?</li> <li>Did I get the job?</li> <li>When can I take a vacation after joining?</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#16-what-is-your-role-and-responsiblity","title":"16. What is your role and responsiblity","text":"<ul> <li>Understand and estimate requirements and user stories</li> <li>Create technical design based on business needs</li> <li>Develop the required features and write unit test cases</li> <li>Deploy changes across different environments (Dev, QA, Production)</li> <li>Support live production systems</li> <li>Fix bugs and work on new feature development</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Communication/Manger_Round.html#even-shorter-spoken-answer-20-seconds","title":"Even shorter (spoken answer \u2013 20 seconds)","text":"<p>\u201cMy role involves understanding and estimating requirements, designing the technical solution, developing and testing the code, deploying it to different environments, and supporting production with bug fixes and new feature development.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html","title":"Redis","text":""},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-1-redis-basics-what-why-where","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Flow 1: Redis Basics \u2014 What, Why &amp; Where?","text":"<p>Goal: Understand what Redis is and why it's used.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#what-is-redis","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is Redis?","text":"<ul> <li>Full form: Remote Dictionary Server</li> <li>It\u2019s a key-value store, but more powerful than simple caching.</li> <li>Data is stored in RAM (memory) \u2192 extremely fast!</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#why-use-redis","title":"\ud83d\udca5 Why use Redis?","text":"<ul> <li>Super fast for reads/writes (in microseconds!)</li> <li> <p>Great for:</p> </li> <li> <p>Caching</p> </li> <li>Session storage</li> <li>Queues (pub/sub)</li> <li>Leaderboards</li> <li>Analytics</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#where-is-redis-used","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Where is Redis used?","text":"<ul> <li>In backend servers, APIs, real-time apps, microservices, and IoT systems.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-2-redis-data-types-what-can-it-store","title":"\ud83d\udcbe Flow 2: Redis Data Types \u2014 What Can It Store?","text":"<p>Goal: Learn how Redis is more than just key-value strings.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#data-types-supported","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Data Types Supported:","text":"Type Example Use Case String <code>user:1 \u2192 \"John\"</code> List Task queue \u2192 <code>[\"task1\", \"task2\"]</code> Set Unique tags \u2192 <code>{\"a\", \"b\"}</code> Hash User profile \u2192 <code>{name:John, age:30}</code> Sorted Set Leaderboard scores Stream Real-time event logs Geo Store location coordinates"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-3-redis-in-microservices","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Flow 3: Redis in Microservices","text":"<p>Goal: Use Redis in microservices to reduce latency and database load.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<ul> <li>Microservices need to share session data or caching.</li> <li>Database is slow for frequent reads.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution-with-redis","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Solution with Redis:","text":"<ul> <li>Use Redis as a shared in-memory cache or data layer.</li> <li> <p>Helps in:</p> </li> <li> <p>Caching user sessions</p> </li> <li>Reducing DB hits</li> <li>Queuing tasks</li> <li>Rate limiting</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-4-data-persistence-in-redis","title":"\ud83e\uddec Flow 4: Data Persistence in Redis","text":"<p>Goal: Make sure Redis doesn\u2019t lose data on restart/crash.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_1","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>RAM is volatile. What if Redis server crashes?</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solutions","title":"\u2705 Solutions:","text":"Method Description Snapshotting (<code>.rdb</code>) Saves full DB every few seconds AOF (<code>.aof</code>) Saves every operation as it happens <p>\ud83d\udc49 Both files are stored in directories like <code>/var/lib/redis</code>.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#if-redis-restarts","title":"\ud83d\udd01 If Redis Restarts:","text":"<ul> <li>It loads <code>.rdb</code> or replays <code>.aof</code> to recover all data.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-5-redis-replica-high-availability","title":"\ud83e\uddec Flow 5: Redis Replica &amp; High Availability","text":"<p>Goal: Ensure Redis is not a single point of failure.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_2","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>What if Redis server crashes? We lose data access.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution-replication","title":"\u2705 Solution: Replication","text":"<ul> <li>Set up primary (master) \u2192 read/write</li> <li>Set up replica (slave) \u2192 read-only</li> <li>Replicas auto-sync from primary</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udc65 Multiple replicas = handle more traffic &amp; failover safety</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-6-scaling-redis-clustering-sharding","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Flow 6: Scaling Redis \u2014 Clustering &amp; Sharding","text":"<p>Goal: Handle large datasets and traffic.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_3","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>Redis has memory limit. One server can't handle 100GB+.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution-sharding-via-redis-cluster","title":"\u2705 Solution: Sharding via Redis Cluster","text":"<ul> <li>Split data into multiple shards</li> <li> <p>Each shard has:</p> </li> <li> <p>One primary</p> </li> <li>One or more replicas</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#example","title":"\ud83d\uddc2 Example:","text":"<pre><code>Cluster:\n  Shard A \u2192 keys (a, b)\n  Shard B \u2192 keys (c, d)\n  Shard C \u2192 keys (e, f)\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Each shard only handles part of data \u2192 uses less memory.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-7-redis-for-global-access-active-active","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Flow 7: Redis for Global Access (Active-Active)","text":"<p>Goal: Make Redis available globally with write support.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_4","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>Users are worldwide. One Redis server causes latency.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution-active-active-deployment","title":"\u2705 Solution: Active-Active Deployment","text":"<ul> <li>Redis is deployed in multiple regions</li> <li> <p>Each region:</p> </li> <li> <p>Accepts read and write</p> </li> <li>Syncs with others using CRDT (conflict-free replicated data types)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udee0 CRDT ensures no conflict between updates from different users.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-8-redis-on-flash-memory-ssd","title":"\ud83d\udcb8 Flow 8: Redis on Flash (Memory + SSD)","text":"<p>Goal: Reduce Redis memory costs.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_5","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>RAM is expensive. What if dataset is too big?</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution-redis-on-flash","title":"\u2705 Solution: Redis on Flash","text":"<ul> <li>Hot data (used frequently) \u2192 stays in RAM</li> <li>Warm/cold data (less used) \u2192 moved to SSD</li> </ul> <p>\u27a1\ufe0f You save money while keeping fast access to important data.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-9-redis-in-kubernetes","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Flow 9: Redis in Kubernetes","text":"<p>Goal: Use Redis in cloud-native, scalable environments.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#problem_6","title":"\ud83e\uddea Problem:","text":"<p>How do we run Redis in Kubernetes?</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#solution","title":"\u2705 Solution:","text":"<ul> <li>Use Redis Helm Chart or Redis Operator</li> <li>Attach Persistent Volume (EBS, SSD) for <code>.rdb</code> and <code>.aof</code></li> <li> <p>Benefits:</p> </li> <li> <p>Autoscaling</p> </li> <li>Health checks</li> <li>Infrastructure as code</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-10-redis-modules-for-advanced-use","title":"\ud83e\uddf0 Flow 10: Redis Modules for Advanced Use","text":"<p>Goal: Extend Redis capabilities</p> Module Use Case RedisJSON Store structured JSON data RedisGraph Handle graph relationships RedisTimeSeries Time-series data (IoT, logs) RediSearch Full-text search <p>\ud83d\udc49 Just install the module and use advanced queries directly in Redis!</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-11-redis-failover-easy-flow-when-primary-crashes","title":"Flow 11: \ud83d\udd01 Redis Failover \u2014 Easy Flow (When Primary Crashes)","text":""},{"location":"Database/redis.html#scenario","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Scenario:","text":"<ul> <li>Primary A handles data for slots 0\u20135000</li> <li>Primary A crashes \u274c</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#step-by-step-process","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Step-by-Step Process:","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udce6 Primary A (Slots: 0\u20135000)\n       |\n       \ud83d\udd3b\n\ud83d\udca5 Primary A Crashes!\n       |\n       \ud83d\udc40 Redis Cluster detects failure (via heartbeat/gossip)\n       |\n       \ud83d\udccb Check available replicas of Primary A:\n            - Replica A1 (most recent data, offset: 99)\n            - Replica A2 (slightly behind, offset: 95)\n       |\n       \ud83d\uddf3 Redis starts election among replicas\n       |\n       \ud83e\udd47 Replica A1 is selected (most up-to-date)\n       |\n       \ud83d\udd04 Cluster promotes Replica A1 \u279d Becomes new **Primary A**\n       |\n       \ud83c\udf0d Cluster updates routing info (re-assigns slot ownership)\n       |\n       \ud83d\ude80 Clients now send requests to **Replica A1 (new Primary)**\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-12-redis-cluster-architecture-cluster-sharding-and-replica","title":"Flow 12:\ud83d\udd17 Redis Cluster Architecture \u2014 Cluster, Sharding, and Replica","text":"<pre><code>                         \ud83d\udd17 Redis Cluster\n        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n        \u25bc                    \u25bc                    \u25bc\n   \ud83d\udfe5 Primary Node A     \ud83d\udfe5 Primary Node B     \ud83d\udfe5 Primary Node C         \u2190 Shards (Data is split)\n (Handles Slots 0\u20135460) (Handles Slots 5461\u201310922) (Handles Slots 10923\u201316383)\n        \u2502                    \u2502                    \u2502\n        \u2502                    \u2502                    \u2502\n  \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n  \u25bc           \u25bc        \u25bc           \u25bc        \u25bc           \u25bc\n\ud83d\udfe6 Replica A1 \ud83d\udfe6 Replica A2   \ud83d\udfe6 Replica B1 \ud83d\udfe6 Replica B2   \ud83d\udfe6 Replica C1 \ud83d\udfe6 Replica C2    \u2190 Replicas (Backup of each shard)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#how-it-works","title":"\u2714\ufe0f How It Works:","text":"<ol> <li>Cluster organizes all nodes and manages the slot distribution.</li> <li>Shards (Primary Nodes) split the data across nodes using hash slots.</li> <li>Replicas are backups of each shard to ensure high availability.</li> <li>If any primary fails \u2192 a replica is promoted \u2192 no data loss or downtime.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#real-life-analogy","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Real-Life Analogy","text":"<p>Think of Redis Cluster like a global warehouse system:</p> <ul> <li>\ud83d\udce6 Cluster = Company with 3 warehouses (India, Europe, US)</li> <li>\ud83d\udcda Shards = Each warehouse stores different product categories</li> <li>\ud83d\udccb Replica = Backup warehouse in case one burns down</li> <li>\ud83d\ude9a Delivery (key) is routed based on category (hash slot) to correct warehouse</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#summary-table","title":"\u2705 Summary Table","text":"Term What It Means Purpose Cluster Group of Redis nodes Distributes load and data Shard A part of the total dataset (a primary node) Makes Redis horizontally scalable Replica Backup copy of a shard Provides fault tolerance and failover Hash Slot Bucket (0\u201316383) to map keys to shards Enables automatic key distribution"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#flow-13-python-redis-example","title":"\ud83d\udc0d Flow 13: Python + Redis Example","text":"<p>Goal: Learn how to use Redis with Python in real life.</p>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#step-1-install-redis-client","title":"\u2705 Step 1: Install Redis client","text":"<pre><code>pip install redis\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#step-2-use-in-python-code","title":"\u2705 Step 2: Use in Python code","text":"<pre><code>import redis\n\n# Connect to Redis\nr = redis.Redis(host='localhost', port=6379, db=0)\n\n# Store a value\nr.set(\"user:1\", \"Alice\")\n\n# Get the value\nprint(r.get(\"user:1\").decode())  # Output: Alice\n\n# Add items to a list\nr.rpush(\"tasks\", \"task1\", \"task2\")\n\n# Read the list\ntasks = r.lrange(\"tasks\", 0, -1)\nprint([t.decode() for t in tasks])  # ['task1', 'task2']\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/redis.html#final-summary-all-flows-at-a-glance","title":"\ud83d\udd1a Final Summary (All Flows at a Glance)","text":"Flow Title 1 Redis Basics 2 Redis Data Types 3 Redis in Microservices 4 Persistence (AOF &amp; Snapshotting) 5 Replication &amp; HA 6 Clustering &amp; Sharding 7 Global Active-Active Deployment 8 Redis on Flash 9 Kubernetes Deployment 10 Redis Modules 11 Redis Failover 12 Redis Cluster Architecture 13 Redis with Python"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html","title":"Complete Topics","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#mongodb-complete-topics-with-examples-cheat-sheet","title":"\u2705 MongoDB Complete Topics With Examples (Cheat Sheet)","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#1-basic-database-collection-commands","title":"1. Basic Database &amp; Collection Commands","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#create-switch-database","title":"Create / Switch Database","text":"<pre><code>use mydb\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#show-databases","title":"Show Databases","text":"<pre><code>show dbs\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#drop-database","title":"Drop Database","text":"<pre><code>db.dropDatabase()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#show-collections","title":"Show Collections","text":"<pre><code>show collections\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#create-collection","title":"Create Collection","text":"<pre><code>db.createCollection(\"students\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#drop-collection","title":"Drop Collection","text":"<pre><code>db.students.drop()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#2-insert-operations","title":"2. Insert Operations","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#insert-one","title":"Insert One","text":"<pre><code>db.students.insertOne({\n  name: \"Rahul\", age: 22, city: \"Mumbai\"\n})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#insert-many","title":"Insert Many","text":"<pre><code>db.students.insertMany([\n  { name: \"Amit\", age: 23 },\n  { name: \"Sneha\", age: 21 }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#3-find-select-queries","title":"3. Find / Select Queries","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#find-all","title":"Find All","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#pretty-print","title":"Pretty Print","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find().pretty()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#find-with-fields","title":"Find With Fields","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find({ city: \"Mumbai\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#find-with-selected-columns","title":"Find With Selected Columns","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find(\n  { city: \"Mumbai\" },\n  { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#4-query-operators","title":"4. Query Operators","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#comparison-operators","title":"Comparison Operators","text":"Operator Meaning Example <code>$gt</code> greater than <code>age &gt; 20</code> <code>$lt</code> less than <code>age &lt; 25</code> <code>$gte</code> \u2265 <code>age &gt;= 18</code> <code>$lte</code> \u2264 <code>age &lt;= 30</code> <code>$ne</code> not equal <code>age != 25</code> <code>$in</code> in list <code>[1,2,3]</code> <code>$nin</code> not in list <code>[1,2,3]</code> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>db.students.find({ age: { $gte: 18, $lte: 25 } })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#5-logical-operators","title":"5. Logical Operators","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#and-condition","title":"AND Condition","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find({\n  age: { $gt: 20 },\n  city: \"Delhi\"\n})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#or-condition","title":"OR Condition","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find({\n  $or: [\n    { city: \"Delhi\" },\n    { city: \"Mumbai\" }\n  ]\n})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#not-operator","title":"NOT Operator","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find({\n  age: { $not: { $gt: 25 } }\n})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#6-update-operations","title":"6. Update Operations","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#update-one","title":"Update One","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateOne(\n  { name: \"Rahul\" },\n  { $set: { city: \"Pune\" } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#update-many","title":"Update Many","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateMany(\n  { city: \"Mumbai\" },\n  { $set: { verified: true } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#increment-value","title":"Increment Value","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateOne(\n  { name: \"Rahul\" },\n  { $inc: { age: 1 } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#7-delete-operations","title":"7. Delete Operations","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#delete-one","title":"Delete One","text":"<pre><code>db.students.deleteOne({ name: \"Rahul\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#delete-many","title":"Delete Many","text":"<pre><code>db.students.deleteMany({ city: \"Delhi\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#8-sort-limit-skip","title":"8. Sort, Limit, Skip","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#sort","title":"Sort","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find().sort({ age: 1 })   // 1 = Asc, -1 = Desc\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#limit","title":"Limit","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find().limit(5)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#skip","title":"Skip","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find().skip(10)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#pagination","title":"Pagination","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find().skip(10).limit(5)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#9-indexing","title":"9. Indexing","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#create-index","title":"Create Index","text":"<pre><code>db.students.createIndex({ name: 1 })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#unique-index","title":"Unique Index","text":"<pre><code>db.students.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#show-indexes","title":"Show Indexes","text":"<pre><code>db.students.getIndexes()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#drop-index","title":"Drop Index","text":"<pre><code>db.students.dropIndex(\"name_1\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#10-aggregation-framework-most-important","title":"10. Aggregation Framework (MOST IMPORTANT)","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#basic-aggregation","title":"Basic Aggregation","text":"<pre><code>db.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { city: \"Mumbai\" } },\n  { $group: { _id: \"$city\", total: { $sum: 1 } } }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#pipeline-structure","title":"Pipeline Structure","text":"Stage Description <code>$match</code> Filter data <code>$group</code> Group and aggregate <code>$sort</code> Sort results <code>$limit</code> Limit records <code>$project</code> Select fields <code>$lookup</code> Join collections <code>$unwind</code> Flatten arrays"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#11-group-by-equivalent-of-sql-group-by","title":"11. Group By (Equivalent of SQL GROUP BY)","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#total-students-per-city","title":"Total Students Per City","text":"<pre><code>db.students.aggregate([\n  { $group: { _id: \"$city\", totalStudents: { $sum: 1 } } },\n  { $sort: { totalStudents: -1 } }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#12-mongodb-join-lookup","title":"12. MongoDB Join (lookup)","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#students-marks-join","title":"students + marks join","text":"<pre><code>db.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: \"marks\",\n      localField: \"student_id\",\n      foreignField: \"student_id\",\n      as: \"result\"\n    }\n  }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#13-projection-select-specific-columns","title":"13. Projection (Select specific columns)","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find(\n  {},\n  { _id: 0, name: 1, city: 1 }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#14-text-search","title":"14. Text Search","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#create-text-index","title":"Create Text Index","text":"<pre><code>db.products.createIndex({ name: \"text\", description: \"text\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#search","title":"Search","text":"<pre><code>db.products.find({ $text: { $search: \"mobile fast\" } })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#15-array-operators","title":"15. Array Operators","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#find-records-where-array-contains-value","title":"Find records where array contains value","text":"<pre><code>db.courses.find({ tags: \"python\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#add-value-to-array","title":"Add value to array","text":"<pre><code>db.courses.updateOne(\n  { name: \"Backend\" },\n  { $push: { tags: \"fastapi\" } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#remove-array-value","title":"Remove array value","text":"<pre><code>db.courses.updateOne(\n  { name: \"Backend\" },\n  { $pull: { tags: \"django\" } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#16-unwind-expand-array-into-multiple-documents","title":"16. $unwind (Expand array into multiple documents)","text":"<pre><code>db.orders.aggregate([\n  { $unwind: \"$items\" }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#17-distinct-values","title":"17. Distinct Values","text":"<pre><code>db.students.distinct(\"city\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#18-counting","title":"18. Counting","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#count-documents","title":"Count documents","text":"<pre><code>db.students.countDocuments({ city: \"Delhi\" })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#19-backup-restore","title":"19. Backup &amp; Restore","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#backup","title":"Backup","text":"<pre><code>mongodump --db=mydb\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#restore","title":"Restore","text":"<pre><code>mongorestore --db=mydb dump/mydb\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#20-objectid-operations","title":"20. ObjectId Operations","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#find-by-objectid","title":"Find by ObjectId","text":"<pre><code>db.students.find({ _id: ObjectId(\"65d10f3ca525d246df1257cd\") })\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#21-replace-document","title":"21. Replace Document","text":"<pre><code>db.students.replaceOne(\n  { name: \"Amit\" },\n  { name: \"Amit\", age: 25, city: \"Delhi\" }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#22-upsert-update-insert","title":"22. Upsert (Update + Insert)","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateOne(\n  { name: \"John\" },\n  { $set: { age: 24 } },\n  { upsert: true }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#23-renaming-fields","title":"23. Renaming Fields","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateMany(\n  {},\n  { $rename: { \"city\": \"location\" } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#24-drop-field","title":"24. Drop Field","text":"<pre><code>db.students.updateMany(\n  {},\n  { $unset: { age: 1 } }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#25-creating-using-views","title":"25. Creating &amp; Using Views","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#create-view","title":"Create View","text":"<pre><code>db.createView(\n  \"mumbai_students\",\n  \"students\",\n  [{ $match: { city: \"Mumbai\" } }]\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#26-bulk-write","title":"26. Bulk Write","text":"<pre><code>db.students.bulkWrite([\n  { insertOne: { document: { name: \"A\", age: 20 } } },\n  { updateOne: { filter: { name: \"A\" }, update: { $set: { age: 21 } } } },\n  { deleteOne: { filter: { name: \"A\" } } }\n])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#27-transactions-mongodb-atlas-replica-set","title":"27. Transactions (MongoDB Atlas / Replica Set)","text":"<pre><code>session = db.getMongo().startSession()\nsession.startTransaction()\n\nstudents = session.getDatabase(\"mydb\").students\n\nstudents.updateOne({ name: \"A\" }, { $set: { balance: 100 } })\nstudents.updateOne({ name: \"B\" }, { $set: { balance: 200 } })\n\nsession.commitTransaction()\nsession.endSession()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#28-schema-validation","title":"28. Schema Validation","text":"<pre><code>db.createCollection(\"employees\", {\n  validator: {\n    $jsonSchema: {\n      bsonType: \"object\",\n      required: [\"name\", \"age\"],\n      properties: {\n        name: { bsonType: \"string\" },\n        age: { bsonType: \"int\", minimum: 18 }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#29-change-streams-real-time-data-watch","title":"29. Change Streams (Real-time data watch)","text":"<pre><code>db.students.watch()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#30-ttl-index-auto-delete-data","title":"30. TTL Index (Auto delete data)","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/complete_mongodb.html#auto-delete-logs-after-2-days","title":"Auto delete logs after 2 days","text":"<pre><code>db.logs.createIndex(\n  { createdAt: 1 },\n  { expireAfterSeconds: 172800 }\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html","title":"Mongodb Practical","text":""},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#60-mongodb-practical-questions-topic-wise","title":"\u2705 60+ MongoDB Practical Questions (Topic-wise)","text":"<p>Each question forces you to write \u219d queries, aggregations, updates, joins, indexes, etc.</p>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#1-database-collection-operations","title":"1. Database &amp; Collection Operations","text":"<ol> <li>Create a database named <code>companyDB</code> and switch to it.</li> <li>Create a collection <code>employees</code> with no schema.</li> <li>Drop a collection named <code>tempBackup</code>.</li> <li>Show all collections inside your current database.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#2-insert-operations","title":"2. Insert Operations","text":"<ol> <li>Insert one employee record with name, age, city, and salary.</li> <li>Insert multiple employees with different cities and ages.</li> <li>Insert 100 sample documents using a loop (mongo shell).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#3-find-queries","title":"3. Find Queries","text":"<ol> <li>Fetch all employees who live in <code>\"Mumbai\"</code>.</li> <li>Fetch only <code>name</code> and <code>salary</code> fields for all employees.</li> <li>Find employees having salary greater than 50,000.</li> <li>Find employees who belong to either <code>\"Delhi\"</code> or <code>\"Pune\"</code>.</li> <li>Find all employees except those from <code>\"Kolkata\"</code>.</li> <li>Find employees whose age is between 25 and 35.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#4-comparison-operators","title":"4. Comparison Operators","text":"<ol> <li>Get employees whose salary is NOT equal to 30,000.</li> <li>Find employees with age in <code>[25, 28, 32]</code>.</li> <li>Find employees where city NOT IN <code>[\"Mumbai\",\"Delhi\"]</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#5-logical-operators","title":"5. Logical Operators","text":"<ol> <li>Fetch employees whose age &gt; 25 AND salary &gt; 40,000.</li> <li>Fetch employees whose city = <code>\"Surat\"</code> OR <code>\"Ahmedabad\"</code>.</li> <li>Find employees NOT older than 40.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#6-update-operations","title":"6. Update Operations","text":"<ol> <li>Update one employee\u2019s city to <code>\"Chennai\"</code>.</li> <li>Increase every employee's salary by 5%.</li> <li>Add a field <code>isActive: true</code> to all employees.</li> <li>Remove field <code>tempField</code> from all documents.</li> <li>Change all <code>\"Mumbai\"</code> employees\u2019 city to <code>\"Navi Mumbai\"</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#7-delete-operations","title":"7. Delete Operations","text":"<ol> <li>Delete all employees from <code>\"Chennai\"</code>.</li> <li>Delete only one employee with name <code>\"Rahul\"</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#8-sort-limit-skip","title":"8. Sort, Limit, Skip","text":"<ol> <li>Fetch top 5 highest-paid employees.</li> <li>Fetch next 10 employees after skipping first 10.</li> <li>Fetch employees sorted by age (desc) then name (asc).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#9-indexing","title":"9. Indexing","text":"<ol> <li>Create index on <code>email</code> (unique).</li> <li>Create compound index on <code>{ city: 1, salary: -1 }</code>.</li> <li>Drop an index from employees collection.</li> <li>Check total indexes on employees collection.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#10-aggregation-framework","title":"10. Aggregation Framework","text":"<ol> <li>Count total employees per city.</li> <li>Calculate average salary per department.</li> <li>Get min, max, average salary in the company.</li> <li>Group employees by age range (20\u201330, 30\u201340, etc.).</li> <li>Find city with highest employees.</li> <li>Sum all salaries department-wise and sort descending.</li> <li>Find total employees where salary &gt; 50,000 grouped by city.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#11-group-by","title":"11. Group By","text":"<ol> <li>Group products by category and count each.</li> <li>Group orders by customer and calculate total spend.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#12-lookup-mongodb-join","title":"12. $lookup (MongoDB JOIN)","text":"<ol> <li>Join <code>employees</code> with <code>departments</code> using <code>deptId</code>.</li> <li>Join <code>orders</code> collection with <code>products</code> collection.</li> <li>Get list of customers with their order history (lookup + unwind).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#13-projection","title":"13. Projection","text":"<ol> <li>Fetch only name, city, and salary (hide <code>_id</code>).</li> <li>Fetch only name but exclude salary &amp; age.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#14-text-search","title":"14. Text Search","text":"<ol> <li>Create a text index on <code>name</code> and <code>skills</code> in employees.</li> <li>Search employees with words \"python backend\".</li> <li>Search products that contain \"gaming\" or \"pro\" in name/description.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#15-array-query-questions","title":"15. Array Query Questions","text":"<ol> <li>Find courses that contain <code>\"python\"</code> inside tags array.</li> <li>Add <code>\"mongodb\"</code> tag to Backend course.</li> <li>Remove <code>\"django\"</code> tag from a course.</li> <li>Find documents where array has more than 3 elements.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#16-unwind","title":"16. $unwind","text":"<ol> <li>Unwind <code>items</code> array in orders and list all items individually.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#17-distinct","title":"17. Distinct","text":"<ol> <li>Get all unique cities from employees collection.</li> <li>Get unique skills from all developers.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#18-count","title":"18. Count","text":"<ol> <li>Count employees living in <code>\"Pune\"</code>.</li> <li>Count total orders in <code>orders</code> collection.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#19-backup-restore","title":"19. Backup &amp; Restore","text":"<ol> <li>Take backup of database <code>companyDB</code>.</li> <li>Restore the backup into a new database <code>companyDB_test</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#20-objectid-operations","title":"20. ObjectId Operations","text":"<ol> <li>Fetch employee using ObjectId.</li> <li>Fetch all employees created after a given ObjectId timestamp.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#21-replace-document","title":"21. Replace Document","text":"<ol> <li>Replace entire employee document with a new structure.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#22-upsert","title":"22. Upsert","text":"<ol> <li>Update employee salary or insert if not exists (by email).</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#23-rename-drop-fields","title":"23. Rename / Drop Fields","text":"<ol> <li>Rename field <code>\"dept\"</code> to <code>\"department\"</code>.</li> <li>Remove <code>oldSalary</code> field from all records.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#24-views","title":"24. Views","text":"<ol> <li>Create a view <code>puneEmployees</code> showing employees from Pune.</li> <li>Create a summary view showing total salary per department.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#25-bulk-write","title":"25. Bulk Write","text":"<ol> <li>Insert 3 new employees, update 1 employee, delete 1 employee in one bulkWrite.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#26-transactions","title":"26. Transactions","text":"<ol> <li>Transfer money from A to B with a transaction.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#27-schema-validation","title":"27. Schema Validation","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Create a collection <code>students</code> with validation:</p> </li> <li> <p>name: string</p> </li> <li>age: int &gt;= 18</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#28-change-streams","title":"28. Change Streams","text":"<ol> <li>Monitor changes to employees collection in real-time.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/MONGODB/mongodb_practical.html#29-ttl-index","title":"29. TTL Index","text":"<ol> <li>Create logs collection that auto-deletes documents after 24 hours.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html","title":"Advanced SQL","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#1-trigger","title":"\u2705 1. Trigger","text":"<p>Definition: A trigger is a piece of SQL code that automatically runs when a specific action (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) happens on a table.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE on main table ]\n          \u2193\n[ Trigger fires automatically ]\n          \u2193\n[ Custom action runs \u2192 e.g., insert log entry ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TRIGGER after_user_insert\nAFTER INSERT ON users\nFOR EACH ROW\nINSERT INTO user_logs (user_id, log_time)\nVALUES (NEW.id, NOW());\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#2-view","title":"\u2705 2. View","text":"<p>Definition: A view is a virtual table created from a SQL query \u2014 it behaves like a table but doesn't store data.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_1","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Write complex SELECT query ]\n          \u2193\n[ Create VIEW from it ]\n          \u2193\n[ SELECT * FROM view_name ] \u2190 Acts like a real table\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_1","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE VIEW active_users AS\nSELECT id, name FROM users WHERE status = 'active';\n\nSELECT * FROM active_users;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#3-stored-procedure","title":"\u2705 3. Stored Procedure","text":"<p>Definition: A stored procedure is a saved block of SQL statements that you can call anytime to run complex logic.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_2","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Create procedure with logic ]\n          \u2193\n[ CALL procedure with input ]\n          \u2193\n[ Procedure runs and returns result or modifies data ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_2","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE PROCEDURE getUser(IN uid INT)\nBEGIN\n  SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = uid;\nEND;\n\nCALL getUser(2);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#4-function","title":"\u2705 4. Function","text":"<p>Definition: A function is like a stored procedure but always returns a single value and is often used inside queries.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_3","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Call function with input ]\n          \u2193\n[ Function calculates and returns single value ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_3","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE FUNCTION getGST(price DECIMAL(10,2))\nRETURNS DECIMAL(10,2)\nRETURN price * 0.18;\n\nSELECT getGST(2000); -- returns 360.00\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#5-index","title":"\u2705 5. Index","text":"<p>Definition: An index is a performance-boosting structure that speeds up search operations on columns.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_4","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Create INDEX on column ]\n          \u2193\n[ Run SELECT with WHERE on that column ]\n          \u2193\n[ Index used \u2192 result comes fast ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_4","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_email ON users(email);\n\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'john@example.com';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#6-normalization","title":"\u2705 6. Normalization","text":"<p>Definition: Normalization is the process of organizing data to remove duplication and improve integrity.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_5","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Big table with duplicate data ]\n          \u2193\n[ Split into small tables ]\n          \u2193\n[ Connect using foreign keys ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_5","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<p>Before (Unnormalized):</p> <pre><code>| id | name | city     |\n|----|------|----------|\n| 1  | A    | Mumbai   |\n| 2  | B    | Mumbai   |\n</code></pre> <p>After Normalization:</p> <p>users</p> <pre><code>| id | name | city_id |\n</code></pre> <p>cities</p> <pre><code>| id | city   |\n|----|--------|\n| 1  | Mumbai |\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#7-join","title":"\u2705 7. Join","text":"<p>Definition: A JOIN is used to combine rows from two or more tables using a related column.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_6","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ Table 1: users ]       [ Table 2: orders ]\n          \u2193\n[ JOIN on user_id ]\n          \u2193\n[ Combined result: user + order info ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_6","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>SELECT u.name, o.product\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#8-transaction","title":"\u2705 8. Transaction","text":"<p>Definition: A transaction is a group of SQL operations that run together \u2014 either all succeed or all fail.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_7","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ START TRANSACTION ]\n          \u2193\n[ Run multiple SQL statements ]\n          \u2193\n[ If success \u2192 COMMIT | If error \u2192 ROLLBACK ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_7","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>START TRANSACTION;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#9-cursor","title":"\u2705 9. Cursor","text":"<p>Definition: A cursor lets you go through a result set row by row (like a loop), useful in procedures.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#workflow_8","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow:","text":"<pre><code>[ DECLARE cursor ]\n          \u2193\n[ OPEN cursor ]\n          \u2193\n[ FETCH next row ]\n          \u2193\n[ Do logic for that row ]\n          \u2193\n[ Repeat until end \u2192 CLOSE ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/advanced_sql_topics.html#example_8","title":"\ud83d\udccc Example:","text":"<pre><code>DECLARE user_cursor CURSOR FOR SELECT id FROM users;\nOPEN user_cursor;\nFETCH user_cursor INTO @uid;\n-- do something with @uid\nCLOSE user_cursor;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html","title":"Commands","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#mysql-commands-description-example","title":"\ud83d\udee2\ufe0f MySQL Commands \u2013 Description + Example","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> Creates a new database. <code>CREATE DATABASE school;</code> <code>DROP DATABASE</code> Deletes a database. <code>DROP DATABASE school;</code> <code>USE</code> Selects a database to work with. <code>USE school;</code> <code>SHOW DATABASES</code> Lists all databases. <code>SHOW DATABASES;</code> <code>SHOW TABLES</code> Lists all tables in the current database. <code>SHOW TABLES;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#table-management","title":"\ud83d\udcd0 Table Management","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>CREATE TABLE</code> Creates a new table. <code>CREATE TABLE students (id INT, name VARCHAR(50));</code> <code>DROP TABLE</code> Deletes a table. <code>DROP TABLE students;</code> <code>ALTER TABLE</code> Modifies a table structure. <code>ALTER TABLE students ADD email VARCHAR(100);</code> <code>RENAME TABLE</code> Renames a table. <code>RENAME TABLE students TO learners;</code> <code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> Removes all data, but keeps structure. <code>TRUNCATE TABLE students;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#data-manipulation-crud","title":"\ud83d\udcca Data Manipulation (CRUD)","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>INSERT INTO</code> Inserts data into a table. <code>INSERT INTO students (id, name) VALUES (1, 'Alice');</code> <code>SELECT</code> Retrieves data from a table. <code>SELECT * FROM students;</code> <code>UPDATE</code> Updates existing records. <code>UPDATE students SET name='Bob' WHERE id=1;</code> <code>DELETE</code> Deletes records. <code>DELETE FROM students WHERE id=1;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#query-clauses","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Query Clauses","text":"Clause Description Sample Usage <code>WHERE</code> Filters records. <code>SELECT * FROM students WHERE id = 1;</code> <code>ORDER BY</code> Sorts results. <code>SELECT * FROM students ORDER BY name ASC;</code> <code>GROUP BY</code> Groups records. <code>SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY dept;</code> <code>HAVING</code> Filters after grouping. <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2;</code> <code>LIMIT</code> Restricts number of records. <code>SELECT * FROM students LIMIT 5;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#constraints","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Constraints","text":"Constraint Description Sample Usage <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> Uniquely identifies each row. <code>id INT PRIMARY KEY</code> <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> Links to primary key in another table. <code>FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES departments(id)</code> <code>UNIQUE</code> Ensures all values in column are unique. <code>email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE</code> <code>NOT NULL</code> Column must have a value. <code>name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL</code> <code>DEFAULT</code> Sets a default value. <code>status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'active'</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#joins","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Joins","text":"Join Type Description Sample Usage <code>INNER JOIN</code> Only matching records in both tables. <code>SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>LEFT JOIN</code> All from left, matched from right. <code>SELECT * FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> All from right, matched from left. <code>SELECT * FROM A RIGHT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> All records from both sides (via <code>UNION</code>). Not native in MySQL (use <code>UNION</code> workaround)"},{"location":"Database/SQL/commands.html#functions","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Functions","text":"Function Description Sample Usage <code>COUNT()</code> Counts rows. <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;</code> <code>SUM()</code> Adds values. <code>SELECT SUM(salary) FROM employees;</code> <code>AVG()</code> Average value. <code>SELECT AVG(score) FROM tests;</code> <code>MAX()</code> Highest value. <code>SELECT MAX(score) FROM tests;</code> <code>MIN()</code> Lowest value. <code>SELECT MIN(score) FROM tests;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html","title":"Indexing","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#1-what-is-an-index-internals-perspective","title":"\ud83e\udde0 1. What Is an Index \u2013 Internals Perspective","text":"<p>An index is a separate data structure (often a B-Tree) that stores a copy of the indexed column(s) along with pointers to the actual rows in the table.</p> <p>Think of it like the index in a book:</p> <ul> <li>You want to find \"SQL Indexing\" \u2192 You go to the index page \u2192 It tells you \"Page 67\"</li> <li>Similarly, a SQL query uses the index to go directly to the rows that match.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#2-how-indexes-are-created-internally","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f 2. How Indexes Are Created Internally","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#when-you-run","title":"When you run:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_name ON employees(name);\n</code></pre> <p>This happens under the hood:</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#step-by-step","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Step-by-Step:","text":"<ol> <li>Copy of Column Values: DB engine extracts the <code>name</code> column.</li> <li>Sort the Values: The values are sorted (if using a B-Tree structure).</li> <li> <p>Build the Tree (or Hash):</p> </li> <li> <p>Nodes are created for values.</p> </li> <li>Each leaf node stores a row pointer (e.g., RowID or Primary Key).</li> <li> <p>Store the Index in Disk:</p> </li> <li> <p>Saved as a separate file in MySQL/PostgreSQL (inside <code>ibdata</code>, <code>pg_index</code>, etc.)</p> </li> <li>Indexed structures are automatically updated on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#3-most-common-index-structure-b-tree","title":"\ud83c\udf33 3. Most Common Index Structure: B+ Tree","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#why-b-tree","title":"Why B+ Tree?","text":"<ul> <li>Balanced Tree \u2192 O(log n) search</li> <li>Leaf nodes are linked \u2192 Fast range scans</li> <li>Internal nodes hold search keys, not row data</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#example-create-index-idx_name-on-employeesname","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example: <code>CREATE INDEX idx_name ON employees(name)</code>","text":"<p>If your table has:</p> <pre><code>+----+--------+\n| id | name   |\n+----+--------+\n| 1  | Alice  |\n| 2  | Bob    |\n| 3  | David  |\n| 4  | Carol  |\n+----+--------+\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#the-b-tree-looks-like","title":"The B+ Tree looks like:","text":"<pre><code>          [Bob]\n        /      \\\n   [Alice]     [Carol, David]\n</code></pre> <p>Each leaf node links to the actual row locations (row pointers or primary keys).</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#4-how-queries-use-indexes","title":"\u26a1 4. How Queries Use Indexes","text":"<p>Let\u2019s say you run:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM employees WHERE name = 'David';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#without-index","title":"Without Index:","text":"<ul> <li>Full Table Scan: Row-by-row check \u2014 very slow on large tables.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#with-index","title":"With Index:","text":"<ul> <li>Use the B+ Tree index on <code>name</code></li> <li>Traverse the tree \u2192 Locate <code>'David'</code></li> <li>Get the pointer \u2192 Jump to row ID = 3 directly!</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#5-index-maintenance-during-write-operations","title":"\ud83e\udde0 5. Index Maintenance During Write Operations","text":"<ul> <li>INSERT: DB adds the new value into the B-Tree index (keeps it sorted).</li> <li>DELETE: DB removes the value and updates tree pointers.</li> <li> <p>UPDATE: If indexed column is updated, DB:</p> </li> <li> <p>Removes old entry</p> </li> <li>Inserts the new one</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc That\u2019s why indexes slow down writes \u2013 they must be updated too.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#6-storage-internals-by-database","title":"\ud83d\udce6 6. Storage Internals by Database","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#mysql-innodb","title":"\ud83d\udc2c MySQL (InnoDB):","text":"<ul> <li>Indexes stored in <code>.ibd</code> file.</li> <li>Primary key = clustered index (rows stored in PK order).</li> <li>Secondary indexes point to primary key value.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#postgresql","title":"\ud83d\udc18 PostgreSQL:","text":"<ul> <li>Each index is a separate structure.</li> <li>Uses <code>pg_index</code>, <code>pg_class</code> system tables.</li> <li>Supports B-Tree, Hash, GIN, GiST, BRIN indexes.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#sql-server","title":"\ud83e\ude9f SQL Server:","text":"<ul> <li>One clustered index per table (actual table order).</li> <li>Non-clustered index stores index + pointer to clustered key.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#7-example-with-query-plan-explain","title":"\ud83e\uddea 7. Example with Query Plan (EXPLAIN)","text":"<pre><code>EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM employees WHERE name = 'Bob';\n</code></pre> <p>\u2192 Shows whether the query uses the index or not.</p> <p>If it uses:</p> <pre><code>Using index condition; using idx_name\n</code></pre> <p>If not:</p> <pre><code>Full table scan\n</code></pre> <p>You can also analyze index cost, rows, and usage.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#8-indexing-best-practices","title":"\ud83d\udd04 8. Indexing Best Practices","text":"Best Practice Why it helps Index columns used in WHERE, JOIN, ORDER Speeds up filtering and joining Avoid indexing low-cardinality columns Not useful (e.g., gender = M/F) Limit number of indexes Writes slow down as more indexes need updating Use composite indexes wisely Use leftmost prefix rule Monitor with <code>EXPLAIN</code> or <code>ANALYZE</code> Helps validate if index is actually used"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#9-leftmost-prefix-rule-for-composite-indexes","title":"\ud83e\udde0 9. Leftmost Prefix Rule (for composite indexes)","text":"<p>If you do:</p> <pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_name_age ON employees(name, age);\n</code></pre> <p>It can be used for:</p> <ul> <li><code>WHERE name = 'Alice'</code> \u2705</li> <li><code>WHERE name = 'Alice' AND age = 30</code> \u2705</li> <li><code>WHERE age = 30</code> \u274c (name is the first prefix)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/indexing.html#10-conclusion-summary","title":"\ud83d\udccc 10. Conclusion Summary","text":"Aspect Detail Structure Mostly B+ Tree, sometimes Hash or Bitmap Stored As Separate structure on disk Usage Helps fast lookup, sorting, joining Cost Increases write time, consumes space Maintenance Auto-updated on INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Query Visibility Use <code>EXPLAIN</code> to check if index is used"},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html","title":"Joins","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html#1-inner-join","title":"1. INNER JOIN","text":"<p>Use case: Scenario: You work at a university and want a list of students who have enrolled in at least one course (skip students not enrolled). Tables: <code>students</code> and <code>enrollments</code> Query Purpose: Find all students who are enrolled, and the courses they are taking. SQL: </p><pre><code>SELECT students.name, enrollments.course\nFROM students\nINNER JOIN enrollments\nON students.id = enrollments.student_id;\n</code></pre> Real-world example: - When generating an attendance list, you only want students present (those actually enrolled).<p></p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html#2-left-join","title":"2. LEFT JOIN","text":"<p>Use case: Scenario: You want to send emails to all registered customers, whether or not they have ever placed an order. You also want to see the order details if they exist. Tables: <code>customers</code> and <code>orders</code> Query Purpose: Show all customers, even those with no orders (order info will be NULL if none). SQL: </p><pre><code>SELECT customers.name, orders.order_id\nFROM customers\nLEFT JOIN orders\nON customers.id = orders.customer_id;\n</code></pre> Real-world example: - Sending reminders to customers who signed up but haven\u2019t bought anything yet. - Checking which customers have no orders (to target them for marketing).<p></p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html#3-right-join","title":"3. RIGHT JOIN","text":"<p>Use case: Scenario: You want to display all job positions in a company, including jobs that have not been filled yet. Tables: <code>employees</code> and <code>positions</code> Query Purpose: For each position, show the employee name if the position is filled, otherwise show the position and NULL for employee. SQL: </p><pre><code>SELECT employees.name, positions.title\nFROM employees\nRIGHT JOIN positions\nON employees.position_id = positions.id;\n</code></pre> Real-world example: - Finding job positions that are open (not filled) in your HR dashboard.<p></p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html#4-full-join","title":"4. FULL JOIN","text":"<p>Use case: Scenario: You are merging two contact lists: one from an email database and one from a phone database. Some people are only in one list. Tables: <code>email_contacts</code> and <code>phone_contacts</code> Query Purpose: List all people, with email, phone, or both (NULL where info is missing). SQL: </p><pre><code>SELECT email_contacts.name, email_contacts.email, phone_contacts.phone\nFROM email_contacts\nFULL OUTER JOIN phone_contacts\nON email_contacts.name = phone_contacts.name;\n</code></pre> Real-world example: - Creating a master contact list for your company by combining all possible sources. - Reconciling two sets of records to find total unique entries.<p></p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/joins.html#summary-table","title":"Summary Table","text":"JOIN Type When to Use (Real-world Example) INNER JOIN Find matched records in both tables (students with enrollments) LEFT JOIN List all items in the left table + matching from right (customers w/ or w/o orders) RIGHT JOIN List all items in the right table + matching from left (all job positions filled or not) FULL JOIN Bring together absolutely all rows from both tables (merge email and phone lists for full contact list)"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html","title":"Normalization","text":"<p>Normalization database ka ek process hai jisme hum data ko organize karte hain taaki redundancy (data ka duplication) aur anomalies (update, delete, insert problems) se bacha ja sake. MySQL ya kisi bhi RDBMS (Relational Database Management System) me normalization ka use table structure ko efficient banane ke liye hota hai.</p> <p>Chaliye har Normal Form ko simple Hindi me samjhte hain with examples:</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#1nf-first-normal-form","title":"\ud83d\udd34 1NF (First Normal Form)","text":"<p>Rule:</p> <ol> <li>Table ke sabhi columns atomic (indivisible) hone chahiye</li> <li>Har column me ek hi value honi chahiye (no multiple values in one column)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#not-in-1nf","title":"\u274c Not in 1NF:","text":"Student_ID Name Subjects 1 Ravi Math, Science 2 Priya English, History <p>Problem: Subjects column me multiple values hai (comma-separated), ye non-atomic hai.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#in-1nf","title":"\u2705 In 1NF:","text":"Student_ID Name Subject 1 Ravi Math 1 Ravi Science 2 Priya English 2 Priya History <p>Solution: Har row me ek hi subject rakh kar atomic bana diya.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#2nf-second-normal-form","title":"\ud83d\udfe0 2NF (Second Normal Form)","text":"<p>Rule:</p> <ol> <li>Table 1NF me hona chahiye</li> <li>Aur partial dependency nahi honi chahiye (matlab koi non-prime attribute kisi composite key ka part na ho)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#not-in-2nf-composite-primary-key","title":"\u274c Not in 2NF (Composite Primary Key):","text":"Student_ID Subject Subject_Fee 1 Math 1000 1 Science 1000 2 Math 1000 <p>Yaha primary key hai: <code>(Student_ID, Subject)</code>, lekin <code>Subject_Fee</code> sirf <code>Subject</code> pe depend karta hai. Ye partial dependency hai.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#in-2nf","title":"\u2705 In 2NF:","text":"<p>Subject Table:</p> Subject Subject_Fee Math 1000 Science 1000 <p>Student_Subject Table:</p> Student_ID Subject 1 Math 1 Science 2 Math <p>Solution: Fee ko subject table me shift karke dependency theek kiya.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#3nf-third-normal-form","title":"\ud83d\udfe2 3NF (Third Normal Form)","text":"<p>Rule:</p> <ol> <li>Table 2NF me hona chahiye</li> <li>Transitive dependency nahi honi chahiye (non-key column kisi aur non-key column pe depend na ho)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#not-in-3nf","title":"\u274c Not in 3NF:","text":"Student_ID Name ZipCode City 1 Ravi 110001 Delhi 2 Priya 400001 Mumbai <p>Yaha <code>City</code> depend karta hai <code>ZipCode</code> pe, na ki directly <code>Student_ID</code> pe \u2014 ye transitive dependency hai.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#in-3nf","title":"\u2705 In 3NF:","text":"<p>Student Table:</p> Student_ID Name ZipCode 1 Ravi 110001 2 Priya 400001 <p>ZipCode Table:</p> ZipCode City 110001 Delhi 400001 Mumbai <p>Solution: City ko alag table me le jaakar dependency clear ki.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#bcnf-boyce-codd-normal-form","title":"\ud83d\udfe3 BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form)","text":"<p>Rule:</p> <ol> <li>Table 3NF me hona chahiye</li> <li>Har determinant super key hona chahiye (har jis column pe koi aur column depend karta ho, wo unique hona chahiye)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#not-in-bcnf","title":"\u274c Not in BCNF:","text":"Professor Subject Department A Math Science A Physics Science B History Arts <p>Yaha ek Professor ek hi Department me ho sakta hai, lekin multiple subjects padha sakta hai. Toh <code>Professor \u2192 Department</code> hai, lekin <code>Professor</code> super key nahi hai (kyunki duplicate Professor aa raha hai).</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#in-bcnf","title":"\u2705 In BCNF:","text":"<p>Professor Table:</p> Professor Department A Science B Arts <p>Professor_Subject Table:</p> Professor Subject A Math A Physics B History"},{"location":"Database/SQL/normalization.html#summary-table","title":"\u2705 Summary Table:","text":"Normal Form Main Rule Problem Solved 1NF Atomic columns, no multiple values Repeating values in columns 2NF No partial dependency on composite key Redundant data from partial key 3NF No transitive dependency Indirect dependency BCNF Every determinant is a super key Advanced redundancy cases <p>Agar aap chaho to main ek MySQL schema ya <code>CREATE TABLE</code> example bhi bana ke de sakta hoon jo 1NF \u2192 BCNF tak ka transition show karta ho.</p> <p>Kya aapko chahiye wo SQL queries ke sath?</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html","title":"Query Optimization","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#what-is-query-optimization","title":"\ud83d\udd25 What is Query Optimization?","text":"<p>Making SQL queries run faster and use fewer resources.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-by-step-guide","title":"\u2705 Step-by-Step Guide:","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-1-use-only-required-columns","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 1: Use Only Required Columns","text":"<p>\u274c Bad:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM employees;\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Good:</p> <pre><code>SELECT name, salary FROM employees;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-2-use-index-on-where-column","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 2: Use Index on WHERE Column","text":"<p>\u274c Slow:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department_id = 5;\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Add Index:</p> <pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_dept ON employees(department_id);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-3-avoid-functions-in-where","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 3: Avoid Functions in WHERE","text":"<p>\u274c Slow:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE YEAR(join_date) = 2024;\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Fast:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE join_date BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-12-31';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-4-use-join-instead-of-subquery","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 4: Use JOIN Instead of Subquery","text":"<p>\u274c Slow:</p> <pre><code>SELECT name FROM employees\nWHERE department_id IN (SELECT id FROM departments WHERE name = 'Sales');\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Fast:</p> <pre><code>SELECT e.name FROM employees e\nJOIN departments d ON e.department_id = d.id\nWHERE d.name = 'Sales';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-5-use-limit-with-big-tables","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 5: Use LIMIT with Big Tables","text":"<p>\u274c Bad:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM logs ORDER BY created_at DESC;\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Good:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM logs ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 100;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#step-6-use-explain-to-debug","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 6: Use EXPLAIN to Debug","text":"<p>Check how query runs:</p> <pre><code>EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department_id = 5;\n</code></pre> <p>It tells:</p> <ul> <li>Is index used?</li> <li>Is full table scan happening?</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/query_optimization.html#in-short","title":"\u2705 In Short:","text":"Step Tip 1 Select only needed columns 2 Create index on search columns 3 Don\u2019t use functions in WHERE 4 Use JOINs instead of subqueries 5 Use LIMIT for large data 6 Use <code>EXPLAIN</code> to check query plan"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html","title":"Sharding","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#what-is-mysql-sharding","title":"\ud83e\udde9 What is MySQL Sharding?","text":"<p>Sharding is a technique to split a large database into smaller, independent parts called shards. Each shard holds a subset of your data and is usually stored on a separate MySQL server.</p> <p>\ud83d\udccc Think of it as dividing one giant table into smaller, manageable chunks \u2014 making your system faster and more scalable.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#real-life-analogy-divide-by-country","title":"\ud83c\udf70 Real-Life Analogy: Divide by Country","text":"<p>Imagine an e-commerce company with 10 million users.</p> <p>Instead of storing all users in one big database, we split them by country:</p> <ul> <li>\ud83c\uddee\ud83c\uddf3 India users \u2192 <code>mysql_india_server</code></li> <li>\ud83c\uddfa\ud83c\uddf8 USA users \u2192 <code>mysql_usa_server</code></li> <li>\ud83c\uddeb\ud83c\uddf7 France users \u2192 <code>mysql_france_server</code></li> </ul> <p>Each database has a <code>users</code> table \u2014 but only for its region.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#step-by-step-sharding-flow-user-table-by-country","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Step-by-Step Sharding Flow (User Table by Country)","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#1-original-structure-before-sharding","title":"1. Original Structure (Before Sharding)","text":"<pre><code>users table:\n+----+--------+---------+\n| id | name   | country |\n+----+--------+---------+\n| 1  | Rahul  | IN      |\n| 2  | Emily  | US      |\n| 3  | Pierre | FR      |\n+----+--------+---------+\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#2-sharding-logic","title":"2. Sharding Logic","text":"<p>We have a big <code>users</code> table. We shard based on country code.</p> <pre><code>                    \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Main Logical Table\n                     users (id, name, country)\n                              \u2502\n                              \u25bc\n                   \ud83d\udce6 Sharding Based on Country\n                              \u2502\n      \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n      \u25bc                      \u25bc                      \u25bc\n  \ud83e\uddf1 Shard 1              \ud83e\uddf1 Shard 2              \ud83e\uddf1 Shard 3\n  mysql_india            mysql_usa              mysql_france\n  users_IN table         users_US table         users_FR table\n  (IN data only)         (US data only)         (FR data only)\n\n    \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510         \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n    \u2502 id | name \u2502        \u2502 id | name  \u2502         \u2502 id | name  \u2502\n    \u2502-----------\u2502        \u2502 -----------\u2502         \u2502 -----------\u2502\n    \u2502 1  | Rahul\u2502        \u2502 1  | Emily \u2502         \u2502 1  | Pierre\u2502\n    \u2502 2  | Priya\u2502        \u2502 2  | John  \u2502         \u2502 2  | Alice \u2502\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518         \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                              \u25b2\n                              \u2502\n                   \ud83d\udd04 Application Backend\n         Uses simple routing logic like:\n         if country == 'IN' \u279c mysql_india\n         if country == 'US' \u279c mysql_usa\n         if country == 'FR' \u279c mysql_france\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#how-does-application-know-which-shard-to-use","title":"\ud83e\udde0 How Does Application Know Which Shard to Use?","text":"<p>Add shard-routing logic in your backend:</p> <pre><code>def get_user_db(country_code):\n    if country_code == 'IN':\n        return mysql_india\n    elif country_code == 'US':\n        return mysql_usa\n    elif country_code == 'FR':\n        return mysql_france\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#usage-example","title":"Usage Example:","text":"<pre><code># User is from USA\ndb = get_user_db('US')\ndb.query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=1\")\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Only the <code>mysql_usa</code> shard is queried \u2014 not all.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#types-of-sharding-simplified","title":"\ud83d\udd00 Types of Sharding (Simplified)","text":"Type Rule Applied Example 1. Geographic Sharding Based on location Users from India \u2192 <code>mysql_india</code> 2. Range-based Sharding Based on ID range or date range ID 1\u201310K \u2192 shard1, 10K\u201320K \u2192 shard2 3. Hash-based Sharding Based on hash value <code>hash(user_id) % 4</code> \u2192 4 shards 4. Table/Feature Shard Based on feature or table <code>users</code> \u2192 db1, <code>orders</code> \u2192 db2 5. Manual vs Automatic Who handles routing logic Manual: your code, Auto: Vitess/MongoDB"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#benefits-of-mysql-sharding","title":"\ud83d\udcca Benefits of MySQL Sharding","text":"\ud83d\udccc Reason \u2705 Benefit Large user base Split load across multiple servers Faster performance Smaller tables = faster queries Regional logic Store data closer to users Fault isolation One shard fails \u2260 total system failure Scalability Easy to add new servers for new shards"},{"location":"Database/SQL/sharding.html#summary","title":"\u2705 Summary","text":"Concept Details What is it? Split large DB into smaller, independent DBs Why do it? Speed, scale, and reliability How to do it? Use rules (like country or ID) to split data App changes? Add routing logic in your backend code <p>Would you like a visual ER diagram or sample codebase for this sharding approach?</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html","title":"Store Procedure","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#stored-procedure-kya-hota-hai","title":"\ud83e\udd14 Stored Procedure kya hota hai?","text":"<p>Stored Procedure ek SQL code ka block hota hai jo database me save ho jata hai, aur aap usse baar-baar CALL karke use kar sakte ho.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd01 Jaise function programming me hota hai, waise hi ye database ke liye hota hai.</p>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#stored-procedure-kyun-use-karein","title":"\u2705 Stored Procedure kyun use karein?","text":"Fayda Kya hota hai? \ud83d\udd01 Reuse Bar bar likhne ki zarurat nahi \u26a1 Fast Pehle se compiled hota hai \ud83d\udd12 Secure Limited access de sakte ho \ud83e\udde0 Logic safe Business logic DB ke andar"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#step-by-step-easy-guide","title":"\ud83d\udee0 Step-by-Step Easy Guide","text":""},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#step-1-simple-stored-procedure-banana","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 1: Simple Stored Procedure banana","text":"<pre><code>DELIMITER //\n\nCREATE PROCEDURE showEmployees()\nBEGIN\n  SELECT * FROM employees;\nEND //\n\nDELIMITER ;\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udcde Run karne ke liye:</p> <pre><code>CALL showEmployees();\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#step-2-procedure-with-input-jaise-function-me-argument","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 2: Procedure with Input (jaise function me argument)","text":"<pre><code>DELIMITER //\n\nCREATE PROCEDURE getByDepartment(IN dept_id INT)\nBEGIN\n  SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department_id = dept_id;\nEND //\n\nDELIMITER ;\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udcde Call karne ke liye:</p> <pre><code>CALL getByDepartment(3);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#step-3-procedure-with-output-jaise-return-value","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 3: Procedure with Output (jaise return value)","text":"<pre><code>DELIMITER //\n\nCREATE PROCEDURE totalEmployees(OUT total INT)\nBEGIN\n  SELECT COUNT(*) INTO total FROM employees;\nEND //\n\nDELIMITER ;\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udcde Call and fetch output:</p> <pre><code>CALL totalEmployees(@total);\nSELECT @total;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#stored-procedure-ko-delete-karna","title":"\ud83e\uddf9 Stored Procedure ko delete karna","text":"<pre><code>DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS showEmployees;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#short-summary-table","title":"\ud83d\udccc Short Summary Table","text":"Kaam SQL Syntax Example Banaana <code>CREATE PROCEDURE ... BEGIN ... END</code> Chalaana (Run) <code>CALL procedure_name();</code> Input dena <code>IN dept_id INT</code> Output lena <code>OUT total INT</code> Delete karna <code>DROP PROCEDURE procedure_name;</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#real-life-location-where-its-used","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Real-Life Location Where It\u2019s Used","text":"Location Usage Example \u2705 E-commerce Website Customer orders fetch karna (<code>getOrdersByCustomer</code>) \u2705 Admin Dashboard Monthly sales report (<code>getMonthlySales</code>) \u2705 Finance/ERP system Payroll calculation, tax report etc. \u2705 Mobile App Backend <code>CALL loginUser(email, password)</code> \u2705 Cron/Jobs Clean old logs/orders every night \u2705 Banking Systems Transaction audit trail, summaries"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#stored-procedure-parameter-types-in-mysql","title":"\u2705 Stored Procedure Parameter Types in MySQL","text":"Parameter Type Keyword Description Example Use Case 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Input <code>IN</code> Value is passed into the procedure (read-only inside procedure) Get user by ID, Filter by date 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Output <code>OUT</code> Value is set inside the procedure and passed back to caller Count total records, return total amount 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Input/Output <code>INOUT</code> Value is passed in, modified, and then returned back Add bonus to points, update balance"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#additional-concept-mysql-user-defined-variables","title":"\ud83d\udccc Additional Concept: MySQL User-defined Variables","text":"Keyword Description Example <code>@var</code> Session-level variable to hold OUT or INOUT values <code>@total</code>, <code>@amount</code>, <code>@points</code>"},{"location":"Database/SQL/store_procedure.html#what-does-this-code-mean","title":"What does this code mean?","text":"Line Meaning <code>DELIMITER //</code> Change statement end marker from <code>;</code> to <code>//</code> <code>CREATE PROCEDURE...</code> Start creating a new procedure <code>BEGIN ... END</code> Define procedure body (what it will do) <code>END //</code> End of procedure definition, using the custom delimiter <code>DELIMITER ;</code> Switch delimiter back to default <code>;</code>"},{"location":"Devops/monitoring_observability.html","title":"Metrices","text":""},{"location":"Devops/monitoring_observability.html#prometheus","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Prometheus","text":"<ul> <li>Purpose: Collects and stores metrics from services (like CPU, memory, HTTP requests).</li> <li>Key Feature: Supports powerful alert rules to notify on issues (e.g., high CPU).</li> <li>Pull-based model: It scrapes metrics from targets.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/monitoring_observability.html#grafana","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Grafana","text":"<ul> <li>Purpose: Visualizes data from Prometheus and other sources.</li> <li>Key Feature: Creates interactive dashboards and supports alerting (email, Slack, etc.).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/monitoring_observability.html#loki-promtail","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Loki + Promtail","text":"<ul> <li>Loki: A log aggregation system (by Grafana Labs), similar to Prometheus but for logs.</li> <li>Promtail: Agent that collects logs from files and pushes them to Loki.</li> <li>Query Logs: Use Grafana UI to search and visualize logs alongside metrics.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd27 Use case (combined): Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs, Grafana for dashboards + alerts \u2014 giving full observability.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html","title":"Overview","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#compute","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Compute","text":"Service Why Important EC2 Understand server provisioning, SSH, load balancers, auto-scaling. Even if using serverless now, EC2 gives core infra knowledge. Lambda Essential for serverless backends, cron jobs, webhooks, async processing. Integrates well with API Gateway &amp; S3."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#networking-api","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Networking &amp; API","text":"Service Why Important API Gateway Used with Lambda or other services to expose secure HTTP endpoints. VPC Understand how to isolate services, subnets, security groups, and how services communicate privately. Route 53 DNS management, custom domain routing, health checks."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#storage","title":"\ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Storage","text":"Service Why Important S3 Store static files, backups, logs, assets. Common with media uploads and CDN integration. EFS If your app needs shared file systems across multiple EC2s or containers."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#databases","title":"\ud83d\udee2\ufe0f Databases","text":"Service Why Important RDS (PostgreSQL/MySQL) Managed relational databases. Know about backups, read replicas, failover, etc. DynamoDB NoSQL option for highly scalable applications. Know about partition keys, indexes, and throughput. ElastiCache (Redis) For caching, session management, and improving response times. Common in high-perf backends."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#containers-if-relevant","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Containers (if relevant)","text":"Service Why Important ECS / EKS If you work with Docker, you should know ECS (Fargate) or EKS for container orchestration. Elastic Beanstalk Easy PaaS for deploying Python web apps (e.g., Flask, Django) without deep infra setup."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#security-identity","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Security &amp; Identity","text":"Service Why Important IAM Core AWS security: roles, policies, access control. A must-know. Secrets Manager Store API keys, DB passwords securely. Better than hardcoding or .env files. Cognito If your backend includes user authentication (OAuth, SSO, etc)."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#developer-tools-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Developer Tools &amp; Monitoring","text":"Service Why Important CloudWatch Logs, metrics, custom alarms, and monitoring Lambda/EC2 apps. CloudTrail Track who did what in your AWS account (audit trail). CodePipeline + CodeBuild CI/CD for automating deployment of Python apps. X-Ray Debugging performance bottlenecks in distributed applications (Lambda, API Gateway, etc)."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#others-nice-to-have","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Others (Nice to Have)","text":"Service Why Learn It Step Functions For orchestrating Lambda functions or other services (workflow-as-code). SNS &amp; SQS Event-driven systems, queues, and async processing. Common in scalable microservices. CloudFormation / CDK / Terraform Infra as Code (IaC). Automate and version your infrastructure setup."},{"location":"Devops/AWS/overview.html#focus-first-on","title":"\u2705 Focus First On:","text":"<ul> <li>Lambda</li> <li>API Gateway</li> <li>S3</li> <li>RDS</li> <li>DynamoDB</li> <li>IAM</li> <li>CloudWatch</li> <li>Secrets Manager</li> <li>SQS/SNS (event-driven)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html","title":"Deploy fastapi ec2","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#step-by-step-deploy-fastapi-on-aws-ec2-ubuntu","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Step-by-Step: Deploy FastAPI on AWS EC2 (Ubuntu)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#1-create-ec2-instance","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Create EC2 Instance","text":"<ol> <li>Login to AWS Console</li> <li>Search for EC2</li> <li>Click Launch Instance</li> <li> <p>Configure EC2:</p> </li> <li> <p>Instance Name \u2192 e.g. <code>fastapi-server</code></p> </li> <li>AMI (OS Image) \u2192 Ubuntu</li> <li>Instance Type \u2192 t2.micro (free tier)</li> <li>Key Pair \u2192 Create &amp; download <code>.pem</code> file</li> <li> <p>Network Settings</p> <ul> <li>Allow SSH (22)</li> <li>Allow HTTP (80)</li> <li>Storage \u2192 Default is fine</li> <li>Click Launch Instance</li> </ul> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#2-connect-to-ec2-instance-ssh","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Connect to EC2 Instance (SSH)","text":"<p>On your local machine:</p> <pre><code>chmod 400 Deploy-key.pem\n</code></pre> <pre><code>ssh -i \"Deploy-key.pem\" ubuntu@&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 You are now connected to the EC2 server.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#3-install-required-packages","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Install Required Packages","text":"<p>Update system and install Python + Nginx:</p> <pre><code>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install -y python3-pip python3-venv python3-full nginx\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#4-configure-nginx-as-reverse-proxy","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Configure Nginx as Reverse Proxy","text":"<p>Create Nginx config for FastAPI:</p> <pre><code>sudo vim /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/fastapi_nginx\n</code></pre> <p>Paste this configuration:</p> <pre><code>server {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name &lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n    }\n}\n</code></pre> <p>Restart Nginx:</p> <pre><code>sudo systemctl restart nginx\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#5-deploy-fastapi-application","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Deploy FastAPI Application","text":"<p>Clone FastAPI project:</p> <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/pixegami/fastapi-tutorial.git\ncd fastapi-tutorial\n</code></pre> <p>(Optional but recommended) Create virtual environment:</p> <pre><code>python3 -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate\npip install fastapi uvicorn\n</code></pre> <p>Run FastAPI app:</p> <pre><code>uvicorn main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#6-access-fastapi-from-browser","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Access FastAPI from Browser","text":"<p>Open browser and hit:</p> <pre><code>http://&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;/\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83c\udf89 FastAPI is now running on EC2 using Nginx + Uvicorn</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Deploy_fastapi_ec2.html#architecture-flow-easy-to-remember","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Architecture Flow (Easy to Remember)","text":"<pre><code>Browser\n  \u2193\nEC2 Public IP (Port 80)\n  \u2193\nNginx (Reverse Proxy)\n  \u2193\nUvicorn (Port 8000)\n  \u2193\nFastAPI App\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html","title":"EC2 Docker Compose","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#deploy-3-tier-application-on-aws-ec2-using-docker-compose","title":"\ud83d\udccc Deploy 3-Tier Application on AWS EC2 using Docker Compose","text":"<p>Project: Three-Tier Web Application Application Layers: \u2705 Frontend (UI) \u2705 Backend (API) \u2705 Database</p> <p>This guide shows how to build, configure, and deploy your 3-tier app using Docker and Docker Compose on an AWS EC2 instance.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#1-prepare-your-project","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 1. Prepare Your Project","text":"<p>Your project should have this basic structure:</p> <pre><code>three-tier-app/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 frontend/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Dockerfile\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 backend/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Dockerfile\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 database/ (optional)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 docker-compose.yml\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#2-example-docker-composeyml","title":"\ud83d\udce6 2. Example <code>docker-compose.yml</code>","text":"<p>Create a file named docker-compose.yml in the project root.</p> <pre><code>version: \"3.8\"\nservices:\n\n  frontend:\n    build: ./frontend\n    ports:\n      - \"80:80\"\n    depends_on:\n      - backend\n\n  backend:\n    build: ./backend\n    ports:\n      - \"5000:5000\"\n    depends_on:\n      - db\n    environment:\n      - DB_HOST=db\n      - DB_USER=root\n      - DB_PASS=root123\n\n  db:\n    image: mysql:8\n    environment:\n      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: root123\n      MYSQL_DATABASE: appdb\n    volumes:\n      - db_data:/var/lib/mysql\n\nvolumes:\n  db_data:\n</code></pre> <p>Customize environment variables and ports based on your app.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#3-create-aws-ec2-instance","title":"\u2601\ufe0f 3. Create AWS EC2 Instance","text":"<p>Go to the AWS Console \u2192 EC2 \u2192 Launch Instance:</p> <ul> <li>Operating System: Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS</li> <li>Instance Type: t2.micro (Free Tier)</li> <li> <p>Security Group Rules:</p> </li> <li> <p>SSH \u2192 Port 22</p> </li> <li>HTTP \u2192 Port 80</li> <li>(Optional) Custom ports if backend uses other ports</li> </ul> <p>Download the key pair (<code>.pem</code>). ([Dhruvish09.github.io][1])</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#4-connect-to-ec2-via-ssh","title":"\ud83d\udda5 4. Connect to EC2 via SSH","text":"<pre><code>chmod 400 mykey.pem\nssh -i \"mykey.pem\" ubuntu@&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>Replace <code>&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;</code> with the public IP of your EC2 instance. ([Dhruvish09.github.io][1])</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#5-install-docker-docker-compose","title":"\ud83d\udc33 5. Install Docker &amp; Docker Compose","text":"<pre><code>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install -y docker.io docker-compose\nsudo systemctl enable docker\nsudo usermod -aG docker ubuntu\n</code></pre> <p>Log out and log back in to apply Docker permissions. ([Dhruvish09.github.io][1])</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#6-upload-your-code-to-ec2","title":"\ud83d\udcc1 6. Upload Your Code to EC2","text":"<p>There are 2 common ways to upload your code:</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#option-a-clone-from-github","title":"Option A \u2014 Clone from GitHub","text":"<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/&lt;your-username&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;.git\ncd &lt;your-repo&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#option-b-using-scp","title":"Option B \u2014 Using SCP","text":"<p>From your local computer:</p> <pre><code>scp -i mykey.pem -r /local/project ubuntu@&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;:/home/ubuntu/\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#7-build-and-run-containers","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f 7. Build and Run Containers","text":"<p>Inside your project directory on the EC2 instance:</p> <pre><code>docker-compose build\ndocker-compose up -d\n</code></pre> <p>This will: \u2714 Build all service images \u2714 Create containers for frontend, backend, and database \u2714 Run everything in the background</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#8-verify-running-services","title":"\ud83d\udd0d 8. Verify Running Services","text":"<pre><code>docker ps\n</code></pre> <p>You should see all three containers up and running.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#9-access-the-application","title":"\ud83c\udf10 9. Access the Application","text":"<p>Open your browser and go to:</p> <pre><code>http://&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>You should see the frontend UI served by the frontend container.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#10-useful-commands","title":"\ud83d\udee0 10. Useful Commands","text":"Task Command View Logs <code>docker-compose logs -f</code> Stop App <code>docker-compose down</code> Restart App <code>docker-compose up -d --build</code>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#11-update-application-code-change","title":"\ud83d\udd04 11. Update Application (Code Change)","text":"<p>When you update your code:</p> <ol> <li>Pull latest changes or upload new code</li> <li>Rebuild images:</li> </ol> <pre><code>docker-compose build\ndocker-compose up -d\n</code></pre> <p>Your services will be updated with the new code.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_Compose_EC2.html#summary-deployment-flow","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Summary Deployment Flow","text":"<pre><code>Write Code\n    \u2193\nCreate Dockerfiles + docker-compose.yml\n    \u2193\nUpload Code to GitHub / EC2\n    \u2193\nSSH into EC2\n    \u2193\nInstall Docker &amp; Docker Compose\n    \u2193\ndocker-compose up -d\n    \u2193\nApplication Live on EC2 Public IP\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html","title":"EC2 Docker","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#deploy-docker-based-application-on-aws-ec2","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Deploy Docker-Based Application on AWS EC2","text":"<p>Project: Candy Store Docker Hub Repo: <code>dhruvish09/candy-store</code> Application Port: <code>3000</code></p> <p>This guide explains how to dockerize an application, push it to Docker Hub, and deploy it on an AWS EC2 instance, including how to update the application with new changes.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#1-create-dockerfile-project-root","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 1. Create Dockerfile (Project Root)","text":"<p>Create a file named <code>Dockerfile</code> in your project root.</p> <pre><code>FROM node:20-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm install\n\nCOPY . .\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"npm\", \"start\"]\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Ensure:</p> <ul> <li>App runs on port <code>3000</code></li> <li><code>npm start</code> exists in <code>package.json</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#2-build-docker-image-locally","title":"\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f 2. Build Docker Image Locally","text":"<p>Run from project root:</p> <pre><code>sudo docker build -t dhruvish09/candy-store:01 .\n</code></pre> <p>Verify:</p> <pre><code>docker images\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#3-test-docker-image-locally","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f 3. Test Docker Image Locally","text":"<pre><code>docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 dhruvish09/candy-store:01\n</code></pre> <p>Test in browser:</p> <pre><code>http://localhost:3000\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#4-push-image-to-docker-hub","title":"\ud83d\udce6 4. Push Image to Docker Hub","text":"<p>Login to Docker Hub:</p> <pre><code>docker login\n</code></pre> <p>Push image:</p> <pre><code>docker push dhruvish09/candy-store:01\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#5-create-aws-ec2-instance","title":"\u2601\ufe0f 5. Create AWS EC2 Instance","text":"<p>Recommended configuration:</p> <ul> <li>OS: Ubuntu 22.04</li> <li>Instance Type: t2.micro</li> <li> <p>Security Group:</p> </li> <li> <p>SSH \u2192 Port 22</p> </li> <li>HTTP \u2192 Port 80</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#6-connect-to-ec2-via-ssh","title":"\ud83d\udd10 6. Connect to EC2 via SSH","text":"<pre><code>ssh -i \"candy-key.pem\" ubuntu@ec2-13-201-103-68.ap-south-1.compute.amazonaws.com\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#7-install-docker-on-ec2","title":"\ud83d\udc33 7. Install Docker on EC2","text":"<pre><code>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install docker.io -y\n</code></pre> <p>Start Docker and enable on boot:</p> <pre><code>sudo systemctl start docker\nsudo systemctl enable docker\n</code></pre> <p>Allow Docker without sudo:</p> <pre><code>sudo usermod -aG docker ubuntu\nnewgrp docker\n</code></pre> <p>Verify:</p> <pre><code>docker --version\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#8-pull-docker-image-on-ec2","title":"\ud83d\udce5 8. Pull Docker Image on EC2","text":"<pre><code>docker pull dhruvish09/candy-store:01\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#9-run-application-on-ec2","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f 9. Run Application on EC2","text":"<pre><code>docker run --rm -d -p 80:3000 dhruvish09/candy-store:01\n</code></pre> <p>Verify:</p> <pre><code>docker ps\n</code></pre> <p>Access application:</p> <pre><code>http://&lt;EC2-PUBLIC-IP&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#10-update-application-new-code-changes","title":"\ud83d\udd01 10. Update Application (New Code Changes)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#rebuild-image-locally-with-new-tag","title":"Rebuild image locally with new tag","text":"<pre><code>docker build -t dhruvish09/candy-store:02 .\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#push-updated-image","title":"Push updated image","text":"<pre><code>docker push dhruvish09/candy-store:02\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#11-deploy-updated-version-on-ec2","title":"\ud83d\udd04 11. Deploy Updated Version on EC2","text":"<p>Stop old container:</p> <pre><code>docker stop &lt;container_id&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>Run updated image (Docker will auto-pull if not available):</p> <pre><code>docker run --rm -d -p 80:3000 dhruvish09/candy-store:02\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#deployment-flow","title":"\u2705 DEPLOYMENT FLOW","text":"<pre><code>Write Code\n   \u2193\nCreate Dockerfile\n   \u2193\nBuild Docker Image (Local)\n   \u2193\nRun &amp; Test Image (Local)\n   \u2193\nPush Image to Docker Hub\n   \u2193\nCreate EC2 Instance\n   \u2193\nConnect to EC2 (SSH)\n   \u2193\nInstall Docker on EC2\n   \u2193\nPull Image from Docker Hub\n   \u2193\nRun Docker Container on EC2\n   \u2193\nAccess App via EC2 Public IP\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/Docker_EC2.html#update-flow-when-code-changes","title":"\ud83d\udd01 UPDATE FLOW (WHEN CODE CHANGES)","text":"<pre><code>Make Code Changes\n   \u2193\nRebuild Docker Image (New Tag)\n   \u2193\nPush New Image to Docker Hub\n   \u2193\nStop Old Container on EC2\n   \u2193\nRun New Image on EC2\n   \u2193\nUpdated App Live\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html","title":"EC2 Github Action","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#deploy-fastapi-on-ec2-with-github-actions-cicd","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Deploy FastAPI on EC2 with GitHub Actions (CI/CD)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#1-create-ec2-one-time","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Create EC2 (One Time)","text":"<ol> <li>AWS Console \u2192 EC2</li> <li> <p>Launch Instance:</p> </li> <li> <p>OS: Ubuntu</p> </li> <li>Type: t2.micro</li> <li> <p>Allow:</p> <ul> <li>SSH (22)</li> <li>HTTP (80)</li> <li>Download key.pem</li> <li>Note Public IP</li> </ul> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#2-connect-to-ec2","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Connect to EC2","text":"<pre><code>chmod 400 deploy-key.pem\nssh -i deploy-key.pem ubuntu@&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#3-install-required-software-one-time","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Install Required Software (One Time)","text":"<pre><code>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install -y git nginx python3-pip python3-venv\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#4-clone-fastapi-project-from-github","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Clone FastAPI Project from GitHub","text":"<pre><code>cd /home/ubuntu\ngit clone https://github.com/&lt;your-username&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;.git\ncd &lt;your-repo&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#5-setup-python-environment","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Setup Python Environment","text":"<pre><code>python3 -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate\npip3 install -r requirements.txt\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#6-create-systemd-service-production-run","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Create systemd Service (Production Run)","text":"<pre><code>sudo vim /etc/systemd/system/fastapi.service\n</code></pre> <pre><code>[Unit]\nDescription=FastAPI Application\nAfter=network.target\n\n[Service]\nUser=ubuntu\nWorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/&lt;your-repo&gt;\nExecStart=/home/ubuntu/&lt;your-repo&gt;/venv/bin/gunicorn \\\n          main:app \\\n          -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker \\\n          --workers 4 \\\n          --bind 127.0.0.1:8000\nRestart=always\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n</code></pre> <p>Enable service:</p> <pre><code>sudo systemctl daemon-reload\nsudo systemctl start fastapi\nsudo systemctl enable fastapi\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#7-configure-nginx-reverse-proxy","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Configure Nginx (Reverse Proxy)","text":"<pre><code>sudo vim /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/fastapi\n</code></pre> <pre><code>server {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name &lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n    }\n}\n</code></pre> <p>Restart Nginx:</p> <pre><code>sudo systemctl restart nginx\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Visit:</p> <pre><code>http://&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#8-setup-ssh-access-for-github-actions-one-time","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Setup SSH Access for GitHub Actions (One Time)","text":"<p>On local machine:</p> <pre><code>ssh-keygen\n</code></pre> <p>Copy public key:</p> <pre><code>cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub\n</code></pre> <p>Paste into EC2:</p> <pre><code>vim ~/.ssh/authorized_keys\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#9-add-github-secrets","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Add GitHub Secrets","text":"<p>GitHub Repo \u2192 Settings \u2192 Secrets \u2192 Actions</p> <p>Add:</p> Name Value EC2_HOST Public IP EC2_USER ubuntu EC2_KEY Private SSH key"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#create-github-action-auto-deploy","title":"\ud83d\udd1f Create GitHub Action (Auto Deploy)","text":"<p>Create file:</p> <pre><code>.github/workflows/deploy.yml\n</code></pre> <pre><code>name: Deploy FastAPI to EC2\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Deploy to EC2\n        uses: appleboy/ssh-action@v1\n        with:\n          host: ${{ secrets.EC2_HOST }}\n          username: ${{ secrets.EC2_USER }}\n          key: ${{ secrets.EC2_KEY }}\n          script: |\n            cd /home/ubuntu/&lt;your-repo&gt;\n            git pull origin main\n            source venv/bin/activate\n            pip install -r requirements.txt || true\n            sudo systemctl restart fastapi\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#final-workflow-very-important","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Final Workflow (Very Important)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#developer","title":"Developer:","text":"<pre><code>git add .\ngit commit -m \"feature added\"\ngit push origin main\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Deploy_Application/EC2/EC2_github_action.html#automatically","title":"Automatically:","text":"<ul> <li>GitHub Actions triggers</li> <li>EC2 pulls code</li> <li>FastAPI restarts</li> <li>App updated \ud83d\ude80</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html","title":"Cloud_computing","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#what-is-cloud-computing-one-line","title":"\u2601\ufe0f What is Cloud Computing? (One line)","text":"<p>Cloud computing = using computers, storage, and software from the internet instead of your own machine.</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 You rent resources instead of buying them.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#simple-real-life-example","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Simple Real-Life Example","text":"<p>Think of electricity \ud83d\udca1</p> <ul> <li>You don\u2019t generate electricity at home</li> <li>You use it when needed</li> <li>You pay only for what you use</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Cloud computing works the same way.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#easy-working-flow-of-cloud-computing","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Easy Working Flow of Cloud Computing","text":"<pre><code>Your Laptop / Mobile\n        \u2193 Internet\nCloud Provider (AWS / Azure / Google)\n        \u2193\nServers, Storage, Software\n</code></pre> <p>You use \u2192 Cloud does the heavy work.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#why-cloud-is-used-very-simple","title":"\u2753 Why Cloud is Used (Very Simple)","text":"<p>\u2714 No need to buy servers \u2714 Access from anywhere \u2714 Easy to scale (increase/decrease) \u2714 Pay only for usage</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#3-types-of-cloud-services-most-important","title":"\ud83d\udce6 3 Types of Cloud Services (MOST IMPORTANT)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#1-iaas-infrastructure-as-a-service","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 IaaS \u2013 Infrastructure as a Service","text":"<p>Meaning: You rent servers, storage, network</p> <ul> <li>You manage: OS, app, data</li> <li>Cloud manages: hardware</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Example idea: \ud83d\udc49 Renting an empty house \ud83c\udfe0 (you arrange everything inside)</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#2-paas-platform-as-a-service","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 PaaS \u2013 Platform as a Service","text":"<p>Meaning: You get a ready platform to build apps</p> <ul> <li>You manage: your code</li> <li>Cloud manages: server, OS, runtime</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Example idea: \ud83d\udc49 Renting a furnished house \ud83c\udfe0\ud83d\udecb\ufe0f (just live &amp; work)</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#3-saas-software-as-a-service","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 SaaS \u2013 Software as a Service","text":"<p>Meaning: You use ready-made software</p> <ul> <li>You manage: nothing</li> <li>Cloud manages: everything</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Example idea: \ud83d\udc49 Staying in a hotel \ud83c\udfe8 (just use it)</p> <p>Examples: Gmail, Google Drive, Zoom</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Cloud_computing.html#easy-comparison-remember-this","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Easy Comparison (Remember This)","text":"<pre><code>IaaS \u2192 You control most things\nPaaS \u2192 You control only code\nSaaS \u2192 You just use software\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/IAM.html","title":"IAM","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/IAM.html#what-is-iam","title":"\ud83d\udd10 What is IAM?","text":"<p>IAM (Identity and Access Management) controls who can access what in a system.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/IAM.html#iam-3-simple-questions","title":"\ud83e\udde9 IAM = 3 Simple Questions","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Who are you? \u2192 Identity 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Can you log in? \u2192 Authentication 3\ufe0f\u20e3 What can you do? \u2192 Authorization</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/IAM.html#main-iam-parts-very-easy","title":"\ud83d\udc64 Main IAM Parts (Very Easy)","text":"<ul> <li>User \u2192 A person or app (example: you)</li> <li>Group \u2192 Collection of users (example: Dev team)</li> <li>Policy \u2192 Rules (what is allowed / denied)</li> <li>Role \u2192 Temporary permission (used by apps/services)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/IAM.html#one-line-example","title":"\ud83e\udde0 One-Line Example","text":"<p>User logs in \u2192 IAM checks policy \u2192 allows or denies action</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html","title":"Virtualization","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#what-is-virtualization-one-line-meaning","title":"\ud83c\udf0d What is Virtualization? (One-line meaning)","text":"<p>Virtualization = running multiple \u201cfake computers\u201d (virtual machines) on one real computer.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#simple-real-life-example","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Simple Real-Life Example","text":"<p>Imagine one big house \ud83c\udfe0 Virtualization turns it into multiple separate rooms \ud83d\udeaa\ud83d\udeaa\ud83d\udeaa</p> <ul> <li>House = Physical server</li> <li>Rooms = Virtual Machines (VMs)</li> <li>Each room has its own people, lights, rules   \u2192 Each VM has its own OS &amp; apps</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#without-virtualization-problem","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Without Virtualization (Problem)","text":"<pre><code>1 physical server\n\u2192 1 OS\n\u2192 1 application\n</code></pre> <p>\u274c Hardware is wasted \u274c Costly \u274c Not flexible</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#with-virtualization-solution","title":"\u2705 With Virtualization (Solution)","text":"<pre><code>1 physical server\n\u2192 Hypervisor\n\u2192 VM1 (Linux)\n\u2192 VM2 (Windows)\n\u2192 VM3 (Ubuntu)\n</code></pre> <p>\u2714 Better use of hardware \u2714 Cheaper \u2714 Flexible</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#what-is-a-hypervisor-very-easy","title":"\u2699\ufe0f What is a Hypervisor? (Very Easy)","text":"<p>Hypervisor = manager / boss</p> <p>It:</p> <ul> <li>Creates VMs</li> <li>Gives CPU, RAM, disk to each VM</li> <li>Keeps VMs separate (no fighting \ud83d\ude04)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Without hypervisor, virtualization is impossible.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#easy-flow-of-how-virtualization-works","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Easy Flow of How Virtualization Works","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#step-by-step-flow","title":"Step-by-step flow:","text":"<pre><code>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Physical Machine (CPU, RAM, Disk)\n        \u2193\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Hypervisor (Manager)\n        \u2193\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Virtual Machine 1 \u2192 OS + App\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 Virtual Machine 2 \u2192 OS + App\n5\ufe0f\u20e3 Virtual Machine 3 \u2192 OS + App\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 All VMs run at the same time on one machine.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#what-does-a-vm-think","title":"\ud83e\uddea What does a VM think?","text":"<p>Each VM thinks:</p> <p>\u201cI have my own CPU, my own RAM, my own disk\u201d</p> <p>But actually, hypervisor is sharing real hardware.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#types-of-hypervisor-very-short","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Types of Hypervisor (Very Short)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#type-1-bare-metal","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Type 1 (Bare Metal)","text":"<ul> <li>Directly on hardware</li> <li>Used in cloud, data centers</li> <li>Example: VMware ESXi</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#type-2-hosted","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Type 2 (Hosted)","text":"<ul> <li>Runs inside Windows/Linux</li> <li>Used for learning &amp; testing</li> <li>Example: VirtualBox</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/AWS/Services/Virtualization.html#why-virtualization-is-important","title":"\u2601\ufe0f Why Virtualization is Important","text":"<ul> <li>Cloud (AWS, Azure) works because of virtualization</li> <li>Saves cost</li> <li>Easy scaling</li> <li>Better performance usage</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html","title":"Docker","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#docker-full-guide-from-beginner-to-advanced-easy-practical","title":"\ud83d\udc33 Docker Full Guide \u2013 From Beginner to Advanced (Easy &amp; Practical)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#1-what-is-docker","title":"\ud83d\udd30 1. What is Docker?","text":"<p>Docker is a tool that helps you package your application and all its dependencies into one unit called a \"container\" so it runs the same everywhere \u2014 on your laptop, a test server, or in the cloud.</p> <p>\u2705 Think of it like a zipped folder that includes your code, Python/Node version, libraries, etc.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#2-why-use-docker","title":"\ud83e\udde0 2. Why Use Docker?","text":"<p>Before Docker:</p> <ul> <li>\"It works on my machine\" problem</li> <li>App crashes on production</li> <li>Complex setups and installation on every machine</li> </ul> <p>With Docker:</p> <ul> <li>Write once, run anywhere</li> <li>No setup issues</li> <li>Fast testing and deployment</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#real-life-example","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Real-Life Example:","text":"<p>You build a Flask app with Python 3.11 and <code>flask==2.3.0</code>. On your system, it works. But your friend has Python 3.6 \u2192 boom! \ud83d\udca5</p> <p>Use Docker \u2192 app runs inside a container with the exact Python version, always works \u2705</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#3-docker-vs-virtual-machine","title":"\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f 3. Docker vs Virtual Machine","text":"Feature Docker (Container) Virtual Machine (VM) Size Lightweight (MBs) Heavy (GBs) Startup Time Seconds Minutes OS Used Shares host OS Has full guest OS Use Case Microservices, CI/CD OS-level isolation, legacy apps"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#4-docker-image-vs-container","title":"\ud83d\udce6 4. Docker Image vs Container","text":"Term What it is Example Image Template of your app (read-only) <code>python:3.11</code>, <code>nginx:latest</code> Container Running instance of an image Your app running in a box"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#5-registry-vs-repository","title":"\ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f 5. Registry vs Repository","text":"<ul> <li>Registry = Storage location (e.g. Docker Hub)</li> <li>Repository = Collection of image versions (tags)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83c\udfe2 Registry = big warehouse, \ud83d\udcc1 Repository = shelf of one product (e.g. <code>nginx</code> with tags <code>1.19</code>, <code>latest</code>, etc.)</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#6-push-pull-images","title":"\ud83d\udce4 6. Push &amp; Pull Images","text":"<ul> <li>Pull images from Docker Hub</li> <li>Push your own to Docker Hub or private registry</li> </ul> <pre><code>docker pull nginx\ndocker tag my-app:v1 myuser/my-app:v1\ndocker push myuser/my-app:v1\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#7-container-ports-host-vs-container","title":"\ud83c\udf10 7. Container Ports \u2013 Host vs Container","text":"<pre><code>docker run -p 8080:80 nginx\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>8080 = Host machine port (your browser)</li> <li>80 = Container port (inside Nginx)</li> </ul> <p>You can now open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> to access Nginx.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#8-docker-logs","title":"\ud83e\udeb5 8. Docker Logs","text":"<p>Check what\u2019s happening inside a container (like app errors, server logs, etc.)</p> <pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#9-dockerfile-structure","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 9. Dockerfile Structure","text":"<pre><code>FROM python:3.11            # Use Python 3.11 as base image\nWORKDIR /app                # Set working directory inside container\nCOPY req.txt .              # Copy dependency file\nRUN pip install -r req.txt  # Install Python dependencies\nCOPY . .                    # Copy all app files\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]    # Run the Python app on container start\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#all-docker-commands","title":"\ud83d\udd27 All Docker Commands","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#install-verify-docker","title":"\u2705 Install &amp; Verify Docker","text":"<pre><code>docker --version\ndocker run hello-world\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#image-commands","title":"\ud83e\uddca Image Commands","text":"<pre><code>docker pull &lt;image&gt;               # Pull from registry\ndocker build -t my-app .          # Build image\ndocker tag my-app:v1 user/app:v1  # Add version tag\ndocker images                     # List images\ndocker rmi &lt;image_id&gt;             # Remove image\ndocker history &lt;image_id&gt;         # Show image history (layers)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#container-commands","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Container Commands","text":"<pre><code>docker run -it ubuntu bash            # Start container with interactive shell\ndocker run -d -p 3000:3000 app        # Run in detached mode with port mapping\ndocker run -it --name myapp app       # Run with custom container name\ndocker exec -it &lt;container_id&gt; bash   # Enter running container using bash\ndocker exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh               # Use `sh` if bash is not available\ndocker ps                             # Show running containers\ndocker ps -a                          # Show all containers (including stopped)\ndocker stop &lt;id&gt;                      # Stop a container\ndocker start &lt;id&gt;                     # Start a stopped container\ndocker restart &lt;id&gt;                   # Restart a container\ndocker rm &lt;id&gt;                        # Remove a container\ndocker kill &lt;id&gt;                      # Forcefully stop a container\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#logs-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\udcdc Logs &amp; Monitoring","text":"<pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_id&gt;            # View container logs\ndocker logs -f &lt;id&gt;                   # Follow (live stream) logs\ndocker top &lt;id&gt;                       # Show running processes in container\ndocker stats                          # Show live CPU, memory usage\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#push-pull","title":"\ud83d\udce4 Push &amp; Pull","text":"<pre><code>docker login                          # Log in to Docker registry\ndocker push myuser/my-app:v1         # Push image to registry\ndocker pull myuser/my-app:v1         # Pull image from registry\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#versioning","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Versioning","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#docker-tag-my-applatest-my-appv1-add-version-tag-to-image","title":"<pre><code>docker tag my-app:latest my-app:v1   # Add version tag to image\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#full-cicd-pipeline-with-docker-aws-developer-friendly-flow","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Full CI/CD Pipeline with Docker &amp; AWS (Developer-Friendly Flow)","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Push code / Open Pull Request (GitHub, CodeCommit, GitLab)\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd04 CI Workflow Triggered (GitHub Actions / CodeBuild / GitLab CI)\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udece\ufe0f Checkout code from repository\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udc33 Build Docker image\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83e\uddea Run tests and static code checks inside container\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 Report build &amp; test results (pass/fail) to team\n             \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddd0 Code Review Process (Pull Request / Merge Request)\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u274c If tests fail or review blocked \u2192 Fix code \u2192 Repeat CI\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 If approved \u2192 Merge to `main` or `release` branch\n                           \u2502\n                           \u25bc\n\ud83d\ude80 CD Workflow Triggered (on merge to main/release)\n                           \u2502\n                           \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Build &amp; tag production Docker image\n                           \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udce6 Push Docker image to AWS ECR\n                           \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f (Optional) ECR vulnerability scan\n                           \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\ude9a Deploy to AWS environment:\n                           \u2502         \u251c\u2500\u25b6 \ud83d\udd01 ECS (Fargate/EC2)\n                           \u2502         \u251c\u2500\u25b6 \u2638\ufe0f EKS (Kubernetes)\n                           \u2502         \u2514\u2500\u25b6 \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f EC2 / Elastic Beanstalk\n                           \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f Run database migrations or setup scripts\n                           \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83e\ude7a Run post-deploy health checks\n                           \u25bc\n\ud83d\udfe2 Application Live on AWS (ECS / EKS / EC2 / Beanstalk)\n                           \u2502\n                           \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udcc8 Monitor via CloudWatch Logs / Metrics / Alarms\n                                \ud83d\udd04 Auto rollback or alert on failure\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker.html#key-features-of-this-flow","title":"\ud83d\udd11 Key Features of This Flow:","text":"Stage Purpose \ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer Code changes + PR creation \ud83d\udd04 CI Code quality, security, and test validation using Docker \ud83e\uddd0 Review Team ensures correctness and readiness \ud83d\ude80 CD Auto-deploy after merge, production-grade image pushed \ud83d\udfe2 Live Monitor, rollback, or alert if needed"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html","title":"Docker Compose","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#docker-compose-complete-guide-beginner-to-advanced","title":"\ud83d\udc33 Docker Compose - Complete Guide (Beginner to Advanced)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#what-is-docker-compose","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What Is Docker Compose?","text":"<p>Docker Compose is a tool to define and run multi-container Docker applications using a file called <code>docker-compose.yml</code>.</p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Imagine your app needs:</p> <ul> <li>A backend service (like FastAPI)</li> <li>A frontend (React)</li> <li>A database (MongoDB)</li> <li>A cache (Redis)</li> </ul> <p>Normally, you'd run each container separately with <code>docker run</code>, manage ports, networks, and volumes manually. Docker Compose solves this by letting you manage everything in a single YAML file.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#why-docker-compose","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Why Docker Compose?","text":"Problem Without Compose Solved With Docker Compose Run many <code>docker run</code> commands Just run <code>docker-compose up</code> Manually link containers with ports/network Compose auto-manages network Set environment variables in terminal each time Define once in YAML or <code>.env</code> file Data lost on container restart Use <code>volumes:</code> in Compose for persistence Difficult to replicate environment Share <code>docker-compose.yml</code> with the team"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#problems-docker-compose-solves","title":"\ud83d\udea7 Problems Docker Compose Solves","text":"Problem Solution Hard to manage multiple containers Compose automates it Complex networking between services Internal auto-networking Manual data persistence setup Use named volumes Risky environment variable handling Use <code>.env</code> or Docker secrets"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#real-life-use-case-microservices-application","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Real-Life Use Case: Microservices Application","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#scenario-you-are-building-a-microservices-app-with","title":"Scenario: You are building a Microservices App with:","text":"<ul> <li><code>backend</code> \u2192 Python FastAPI</li> <li><code>frontend</code> \u2192 React</li> <li><code>mongodb</code> \u2192 Database</li> <li><code>redis</code> \u2192 Cache</li> </ul> <p>These services depend on each other, need shared network, and need to start in order.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#docker-composeyml","title":"<code>docker-compose.yml</code>","text":"<pre><code>version: '3.8'\n\nservices:\n  backend:\n    build: ./backend\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    environment:\n      MONGO_URL: mongodb://mongo:27017\n      REDIS_HOST: redis\n    depends_on:\n      - mongo\n      - redis\n    networks:\n      - app-net\n\n  frontend:\n    build: ./frontend\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    depends_on:\n      - backend\n    networks:\n      - app-net\n\n  mongo:\n    image: mongo\n    environment:\n      MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME: admin\n      MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD: secret\n    volumes:\n      - mongo_data:/data/db\n    networks:\n      - app-net\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis\n    networks:\n      - app-net\n\nvolumes:\n  mongo_data:\n\nnetworks:\n  app-net:\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#execution-flow-visual","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Execution Flow (Visual)","text":"<pre><code>docker-compose up -d\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc47 What happens:</p> <ol> <li>Docker builds <code>backend</code> and <code>frontend</code> from Dockerfiles</li> <li>Pulls <code>mongo</code> and <code>redis</code> images</li> <li>Creates shared <code>app-net</code> network</li> <li>Attaches all containers to it</li> <li>MongoDB data persists via <code>mongo_data</code> volume</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#docker-compose-structure","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Docker Compose Structure","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#key-tags-explained","title":"Key Tags Explained:","text":"Tag Description <code>services</code> Defines all containers <code>image</code> Docker image name <code>build</code> Dockerfile build path <code>ports</code> Port mapping (host:container) <code>environment</code> Env variables inside container <code>volumes</code> Data persistence <code>networks</code> Container communication <code>depends_on</code> Start order of services <code>secrets</code> Secure credentials"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#network-configuration","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Network Configuration","text":"<pre><code>networks:\n  app-net:\n    driver: bridge\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>All services in same network can use each other's service name as hostname</li> <li>e.g., <code>backend</code> connects to <code>mongo:27017</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#data-persistence-with-volumes","title":"\ud83d\udcbe Data Persistence with Volumes","text":"<pre><code>volumes:\n  mongo_data:\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>Mounted on <code>mongo</code> service as <code>/data/db</code></li> <li>Data remains even after container is deleted</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#environment-variables","title":"\ud83c\udf31 Environment Variables","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#env-file","title":"<code>.env</code> file:","text":"<pre><code>MONGO_USER=admin\nMONGO_PASS=secret\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#compose-usage","title":"Compose usage:","text":"<pre><code>environment:\n  MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_USERNAME: ${MONGO_USER}\n  MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${MONGO_PASS}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#docker-secrets-for-sensitive-data","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Docker Secrets (for sensitive data)","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#step-1-create-secret-file","title":"Step 1: Create secret file","text":"<pre><code>echo \"secretpass\" &gt; db_password.txt\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#step-2-add-to-compose","title":"Step 2: Add to Compose","text":"<pre><code>services:\n  mongo:\n    image: mongo\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\n\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    file: ./db_password.txt\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udd12 Docker secrets work best in Docker Swarm</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#private-docker-repo-access","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Private Docker Repo Access","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#step-1-authenticate","title":"Step 1: Authenticate","text":"<pre><code>docker login -u username -p password\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#step-2-use-private-image-in-compose","title":"Step 2: Use private image in Compose","text":"<pre><code>services:\n  myapp:\n    image: ghcr.io/your-org/private-image:tag\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#step-3-pull-and-run","title":"Step 3: Pull and run","text":"<pre><code>docker-compose pull\ndocker-compose up -d\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Docker Compose uses local Docker credentials to pull private images</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#limitations-of-docker-compose","title":"\ud83d\udeab Limitations of Docker Compose","text":"Limitation Notes No multi-host deployment Use Kubernetes or Swarm No readiness checks in <code>depends_on</code> Use wait-for-it script Local secrets aren't encrypted Use Swarm for real secrets No auto-scaling Use orchestrators like Swarm/K8s"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#docker-compose-cli-useful-commands","title":"\u2705 Docker Compose CLI \u2013 Useful Commands","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#start-stop-services","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Start / Stop Services","text":"<pre><code># Start all services in detached mode\ndocker-compose up -d\n\n# Start services with logs in terminal\ndocker-compose up\n\n# Stop and remove all services, networks, volumes (unless declared)\ndocker-compose down\n\n# Stop services but do not remove containers\ndocker-compose stop\n\n# Start stopped services\ndocker-compose start\n\n# Restart services\ndocker-compose restart\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#logs-and-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Logs and Monitoring","text":"<pre><code># View real-time logs for all services\ndocker-compose logs -f\n\n# View logs for a specific service\ndocker-compose logs -f &lt;service_name&gt;\n\n# Show running containers\ndocker-compose ps\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#build-image-operations","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Build &amp; Image Operations","text":"<pre><code># Build all services (from Dockerfiles)\ndocker-compose build\n\n# Build a specific service\ndocker-compose build &lt;service_name&gt;\n\n# Pull images from registry (e.g., Docker Hub)\ndocker-compose pull\n\n# Push images to registry\ndocker-compose push\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#run-exec-test","title":"\ud83e\uddea Run / Exec / Test","text":"<pre><code># Run a one-off command inside a service container\ndocker-compose run &lt;service_name&gt; &lt;command&gt;\n\n# Example: Run bash in backend service\ndocker-compose run backend bash\n\n# Execute command in a running container\ndocker-compose exec &lt;service_name&gt; &lt;command&gt;\n\n# Example: Open shell\ndocker-compose exec backend /bin/sh\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#volumes-cleanup","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Volumes / Cleanup","text":"<pre><code># Remove stopped containers\ndocker-compose rm\n\n# Remove containers, volumes, and networks\ndocker-compose down --volumes\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#configuration-and-validation","title":"\ud83e\uddf0 Configuration and Validation","text":"<pre><code># Check the configuration file for errors\ndocker-compose config\n\n# List all services\ndocker-compose config --services\n\n# Show environment variables being used\ndocker-compose config --resolve-image-digests\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/docker_compose.html#full-docker-compose-workflow-dev-to-deploy","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Full Docker Compose Workflow (Dev to Deploy)","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Write Dockerfile for each service (API, DB, Redis, Frontend)\n            \u2502\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcc4 Define docker-compose.yml\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Specify all services (app, db, cache, etc.)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Set up ports, volumes, networks, env vars\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Add secrets (.env, secrets section if needed)\n            \u2502\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddea Local Development (Single command workflow)\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose build         \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Build images\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose up -d         \ud83d\ude80 Start all services\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose logs -f       \ud83d\udcc4 Monitor logs\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose exec &lt;svc&gt;    \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f  Debug inside containers\n            \u25bc\n\u2705 Test &amp; Debug Locally\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Connect services via internal network\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Use volumes to persist DB/cache data\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Check readiness (health check scripts / logs)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd04 Push to GitHub / GitLab / CodeCommit\n            \u2502\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd01 CI Pipeline (Dockerized Testing)\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Checkout Code\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose -f docker-compose.test.yml up --build\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Run unit/integration tests inside containers\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Report results\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddd0 Review + Merge\n            \u2502\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Code approved \u2192 Merge to `main` or `release`\n                          \u2502\n                          \u25bc\n\ud83c\udfaf Optional Docker Compose for Staging\n                          \u2502\n                          \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; SSH to staging EC2 / server\n                          \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Pull latest code\n                          \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose pull / build\n                          \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose up -d\n                          \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Test app in a production-like setup\n                          \u25bc\n\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Ready for Production Docker Deployment\n      (Use ECS / EKS / Kubernetes or Convert to Helm Charts)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html","title":"Kubernetes","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-beginner-documentation","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Kubernetes Beginner Documentation","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#what-is-kubernetes","title":"\ud83c\udf10 What is Kubernetes?","text":"<p>Kubernetes (aka K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform developed by Google. It automates the:</p> <ul> <li>Deployment</li> <li>Scaling</li> <li>Management</li> <li>Load balancing</li> <li>Rollout/Rollback</li> <li>High availability of containerized applications</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#what-is-a-container","title":"\ud83d\udce6 What is a Container?","text":"<p>A container is a lightweight, standalone unit that contains an application and all its dependencies (libraries, configs, binaries). It's isolated from the host system.</p> <p>Think of it like a package that can run the same way anywhere \u2013 on your laptop, a server, or the cloud.</p> <p>Popular container tool: Docker</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#what-is-container-orchestration","title":"\ud83e\udd16 What is Container Orchestration?","text":"<p>Container Orchestration is the process of automatically managing multiple containers, including:</p> <ul> <li>Where they run (which machine)</li> <li>Restarting if they crash</li> <li>Updating with zero downtime</li> <li>Scaling up or down</li> <li>Ensuring availability</li> </ul> <p>Kubernetes is the most widely used orchestration tool.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#why-kubernetes","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Why Kubernetes?","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#old-way","title":"\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Old Way:","text":"<pre><code>Developer --&gt; Deploy on a Server\n          --&gt; Server crashes = downtime\n          --&gt; High load = failure\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-solution","title":"\u2705 Kubernetes Solution:","text":"<ul> <li>Run app on multiple servers (nodes)</li> <li>Auto-restart on failure</li> <li>Load balancing traffic</li> <li>Scaling up/down containers</li> <li>Self-healing and zero-downtime rollout</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-architecture","title":"\ud83c\udfd7 Kubernetes Architecture","text":"<p>When you install Kubernetes, it forms a Cluster.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#a-cluster-has","title":"\ud83d\udd39 A Cluster has:","text":"<ol> <li>Control Plane (Master)</li> <li>Worker Nodes (Minions)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#control-plane-master","title":"\ud83e\udded Control Plane (Master)","text":"<p>Manages the whole cluster.</p> Component Description API Server Entry point \u2013 all requests go through here Scheduler Decides which node runs the pod Controller Manager Manages state and events (e.g., restart pods) etcd Key-value store \u2013 stores cluster data"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#worker-node-minion","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Worker Node (Minion)","text":"<p>Runs the actual application containers.</p> Component Description Kubelet Talks to the API server and manages pods Kube-proxy Handles network communication Container Runtime Runs the actual containers (e.g., Docker, containerd)"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#what-is-a-pod","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 What is a Pod?","text":"<p>A Pod is the smallest unit in Kubernetes.</p> <ul> <li>It runs one or more containers (usually one)</li> <li>All containers in a Pod share network and storage</li> <li>A Pod is a single instance of your app</li> </ul> <p>Example: If your app has 3 replicas \u2192 3 pods will be created.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-yaml-file-anatomy","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Kubernetes YAML File Anatomy","text":"<p>Let\u2019s understand each keyword in a YAML file using a simple Deployment example.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#sample-deploymentyaml","title":"\u2705 Sample: <code>deployment.yaml</code>","text":"<pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1          # Version of the Kubernetes API\nkind: Deployment             # Type of Kubernetes object (Deployment, Pod, Service, etc.)\nmetadata:\n  name: my-app               # Name of the resource\n  labels:\n    app: my-app              # Labels help group and identify objects\nspec:\n  replicas: 3                # Number of pod copies to run\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: my-app            # Match pods with this label\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: my-app\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: my-app-container\n          image: nginx:latest         # Docker image to run\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 80       # Port exposed by container\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#explanation-of-yaml-tags","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Explanation of YAML Tags","text":"Tag Meaning <code>apiVersion</code> Version of the K8s API to use for this object (apps/v1 for Deployment) <code>kind</code> Type of K8s object (Deployment, Pod, Service, etc.) <code>metadata.name</code> Unique name for this resource <code>metadata.labels</code> Key-value tags used to identify and group resources <code>spec</code> Specification of what you want to run <code>replicas</code> Number of identical pods to maintain <code>selector.matchLabels</code> Links this Deployment to the correct pods <code>template.metadata.labels</code> Labels assigned to Pods created by this Deployment <code>containers</code> List of containers to run in the Pod <code>image</code> Docker image to use <code>ports.containerPort</code> Port exposed by container (e.g., 80 for HTTP)"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-service-yaml","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Kubernetes Service YAML","text":"<p>To expose the above deployment publicly:</p> <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: my-app-service\nspec:\n  type: LoadBalancer\n  selector:\n    app: my-app\n  ports:\n    - port: 80           # External port\n      targetPort: 80     # Internal container port\n</code></pre> Tag Meaning <code>type</code> Type of service: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer <code>selector</code> Connects the service to pods with matching labels <code>ports.port</code> Port accessible to users <code>ports.targetPort</code> Port exposed by container"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#how-kubernetes-controls-nodes","title":"\u2699\ufe0f How Kubernetes Controls Nodes","text":"<ul> <li>The Control Plane communicates with Nodes using Kubelet</li> <li>API Server sends instructions like: start a pod, stop a pod</li> <li>Scheduler decides which Node the Pod should run on</li> <li>Controller checks if desired Pods are running</li> <li>etcd stores the current state of the cluster</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#most-used-kubectl-commands","title":"\ud83e\uddea Most Used <code>kubectl</code> Commands","text":"Purpose Command View all pods <code>kubectl get pods</code> View deployments <code>kubectl get deployments</code> Apply config <code>kubectl apply -f filename.yaml</code> Delete config <code>kubectl delete -f filename.yaml</code> See detailed info <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code> View logs <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt;</code> Shell into a pod <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod-name&gt; -- bash</code>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#summary-tldr","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Summary (TL;DR)","text":"<ul> <li>Kubernetes = Container Orchestration</li> <li>Pod = smallest unit, runs container(s)</li> <li>Deployment = manages Pods (scaling, rollout)</li> <li>Service = exposes Pods over network</li> <li>Control Plane = API Server, Scheduler, Controller, etcd</li> <li>Worker Node = Kubelet, Kube-proxy, Container runtime</li> </ul> <p>Absolutely! Here's a super simple and clear explanation of the main components of Kubernetes, broken down so even a complete beginner can understand:</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#kubernetes-components-easy-explanation","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Kubernetes Components \u2013 Easy Explanation","text":""},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#1-control-plane-the-brain-of-kubernetes","title":"\ud83d\udea6 1. Control Plane (The Brain \ud83e\udde0 of Kubernetes)","text":"<p>This is where all the decisions are made \u2013 like what should run, where, and how.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#key-components-of-control-plane","title":"\u2705 Key Components of Control Plane:","text":"Component Simple Meaning What it Does API Server \ud83d\udcec Gatekeeper Accepts all requests (from users or internal tools) Scheduler \ud83e\uddee Planner Decides which machine (Node) should run your app Controller Manager \ud83d\udc6e Watcher Constantly checks if the system is in the desired state \u2013 restarts things if needed etcd \ud83d\udcd8 Notebook Stores all Kubernetes information (like app data, cluster settings, etc.)"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#2-worker-node-the-hands-of-kubernetes","title":"\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83c\udfed 2. Worker Node (The Hands \ud83d\udd90 of Kubernetes)","text":"<p>These are the actual machines (servers) that run your application.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#each-node-has","title":"\u2705 Each Node has:","text":"Component Simple Meaning What it Does Kubelet \ud83d\udce2 Worker Manager Talks to the control plane and makes sure the app is running correctly Kube-proxy \ud83d\udd00 Traffic Manager Manages network traffic in and out of the app Container Runtime \ud83d\udc33 Engine Actually runs your containers (Docker, containerd, etc.)"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#3-pod-box-that-runs-your-app","title":"\ud83d\udce6 3. Pod (Box that runs your App)","text":"<ul> <li>Smallest unit in Kubernetes</li> <li>It\u2019s like a box that holds one or more containers</li> <li>All containers in a pod share IP address, ports, and volume</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: If you run <code>nginx</code>, Kubernetes puts it inside a Pod.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#4-deployment-the-builder","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 4. Deployment (The Builder)","text":"<ul> <li>Makes sure the right number of Pods are running</li> <li>Helps with auto-scaling, updates, and rollbacks</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc You tell Deployment: \u201cI want 3 nginx servers running\u201d \u2192 it will create 3 Pods.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#5-service-the-door-to-your-app","title":"\ud83c\udf0d 5. Service (The Door \ud83d\udeaa to Your App)","text":"<ul> <li>Exposes Pods inside or outside the cluster</li> <li>Acts like a stable IP address or DNS name for your app</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Pods can come and go, but the Service always stays \u2013 so users can access the app reliably.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#6-configmap-secret-settings-and-passwords","title":"\ud83d\udcc1 6. ConfigMap &amp; Secret (Settings and Passwords)","text":"<ul> <li>ConfigMap = stores config like app name, port, etc.</li> <li>Secret = stores sensitive data (like passwords, tokens)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc These are injected into Pods as environment variables or files</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#7-volume-storage-for-your-app","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 7. Volume (Storage for Your App)","text":"<ul> <li>Used when containers need to save data</li> <li>Unlike container storage, volumes survive even if the container dies</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Example: A database writing files to disk.</p>"},{"location":"Devops/Docker/kubernetes.html#analogy-time-kubernetes-is-like-a-factory","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Analogy Time: Kubernetes is like a Factory \ud83c\udfed","text":"Kubernetes Part Real-World Equivalent Control Plane Factory managers Worker Node Factory workers Pod Small machine inside a worker Deployment Task scheduler Service Reception desk or gate ConfigMap App instruction sheet Secret Safe with passwords Volume Warehouse"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html","title":"Django_Framework","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#python-web-frameworks-django-interview-guide","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python Web Frameworks: Django Interview Guide","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#table-of-contents","title":"\ud83d\udccc Table of Contents","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#django","title":"Django","text":"<ol> <li>Django Basics</li> <li>Views, URLs, and Templates</li> <li>Models and ORM</li> <li>Admin &amp; Management</li> <li>Django REST Framework</li> <li>Security &amp; Middleware</li> <li>Caching &amp; Performance</li> <li>Advanced Concepts</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#django-section","title":"\ud83d\udc0d DJANGO SECTION","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#1-how-to-create-json-file-in-django","title":"1. How to Create JSON File in Django?","text":"<pre><code>import json\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\n\ndef create_json_file(request):\n    data = {'name': 'John Doe', 'age': 30, 'city': 'New York'}\n    json_data = json.dumps(data, indent=4)\n    file_path = '/path/to/your/file/data.json'\n    with open(file_path, 'w') as json_file:\n        json_file.write(json_data)\n    return JsonResponse({'message': 'JSON file created successfully'})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#2-decorators-in-django","title":"2. Decorators in Django","text":"<p>Ans: Decorators modify view behavior, e.g., for authentication:</p> <pre><code>from functools import wraps\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\n\ndef user_authenticated(view_func):\n    @wraps(view_func)\n    def _wrapped_view(request, *args, **kwargs):\n        if request.user.is_authenticated:\n            return view_func(request, *args, **kwargs)\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"You must be logged in.\")\n    return _wrapped_view\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#3-what-is-signal-in-django","title":"3. What is Signal in Django?","text":"<p>Ans: Signals allow decoupled components to notify each other.</p> <pre><code>from django.db.models.signals import post_save\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom myapp.models import MyModel\n\n@receiver(post_save, sender=MyModel)\ndef my_handler(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\n    if created:\n        print(\"New instance created:\", instance)\n    else:\n        print(\"Updated instance:\", instance)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#4-combine-multiple-querysets","title":"4. Combine Multiple Querysets","text":"<p>Ans:</p> <pre><code>from itertools import chain\ncombined = list(chain(qs1, qs2))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#5-django-architecture","title":"5. Django Architecture","text":"<p>Ans: Follows MVT (Model-View-Template).</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#6-request-lifecycle","title":"6. Request Lifecycle","text":"<pre><code>User Request \u2192 manage.py \u2192 settings.py \u2192 urls.py \u2192 views.py \u2192 models.py \u2192 template\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#7-why-is-django-loosely-coupled","title":"7. Why is Django Loosely Coupled?","text":"<p>Ans: Due to the separation in its MVT architecture.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#8-migrations","title":"8. Migrations","text":"<pre><code>makemigrations       # Create migration files\nmigrate              # Apply migrations\nshowmigrations       # Show applied migrations\nsqlmigrate app_name migration_name  # View SQL\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#9-csrf-token","title":"9. CSRF Token","text":"<p>Ans: CSRF stands for Cross Site Request Forgery. Django protects forms using <code>csrf_token</code>. Protects against cross-site request forgery attacks. Ensures request came from authenticated user.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#10-what-is-queryset","title":"10. What is QuerySet?","text":"<p>Ans: A collection of database rows represented as model instances.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#11-select_related-vs-prefetch_related","title":"11. select_related vs prefetch_related","text":"<ul> <li>select_related: Forward relationships, efficient joins.</li> <li>prefetch_related: Reverse/many-to-many, executes separate queries.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#12-django-vs-flask-vs-pyramid","title":"12. Django vs Flask vs Pyramid","text":"Framework Type Use Case Flask Micro Small apps Pyramid Flexible Medium to large apps Django Full-stack Large apps with batteries included"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#13-django-admin-vs-django-admin","title":"13. Django Admin vs django-admin","text":"<ul> <li>Django Admin Panel: GUI for managing models.</li> <li>django-admin: CLI for Django tasks.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#14-shortcut-to-render-html","title":"14. Shortcut to Render HTML","text":"<p>Ans: <code>render()</code> or <code>render_to_response()</code></p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#15-q-objects","title":"15. Q Objects","text":"<p>Ans: For complex queries with <code>OR</code>:</p> <pre><code>from django.db.models import Q\nModel.objects.filter(Q(name__startswith='A') | Q(age__gte=30))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#16-managepy","title":"16. manage.py","text":"<p>Ans: CLI tool to manage Django project.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#17-middleware","title":"17. Middleware","text":"<p>Ans: Bridge between request and response.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#18-sessions","title":"18. Sessions","text":"<p>Ans: Persist user data between requests.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#19-django-exceptions","title":"19. Django Exceptions","text":"<p>Ans: Errors in request/response, ORM, etc.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#20-onetoone-vs-foreignkey","title":"20. OneToOne vs ForeignKey","text":"<p>Ans:</p> <ul> <li>OneToOneField: 1-to-1 relationship</li> <li>ForeignKey: Many-to-1</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#21-field-classes","title":"21. Field Classes","text":"<p>Ans: Define model attributes like <code>CharField</code>, <code>IntegerField</code>, etc.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#22-what-is-jinja-templating","title":"22. What is Jinja Templating?","text":"<p>Ans: A template engine for Python-based web frameworks.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#23-serialization","title":"23. Serialization","text":"<ul> <li>Converts complex types to JSON/XML.</li> <li>ModelSerializer, HyperlinkedModelSerializer, BaseSerializer.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#24-generic-views-vs-apiview","title":"24. Generic Views vs APIView","text":"<ul> <li>Generic Views: Pre-built for CRUD.</li> <li>APIView: Custom logic for HTTP methods.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#25-mixins","title":"25. Mixins","text":"<p>Ans: Reusable logic for CBVs.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#26-caching","title":"26. Caching","text":"<p>Ans: Speeds up apps by storing frequent data.</p> <ul> <li>File-based</li> <li>In-memory</li> <li>Memcached</li> <li>DB caching</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#27-user-permissions-access-control","title":"27. User Permissions &amp; Access Control","text":"<ul> <li>Decorators: <code>@permission_required</code>, <code>@user_passes_test</code></li> <li>Mixins: <code>PermissionRequiredMixin</code></li> <li>Object-level: via <code>has_perm()</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#28-permission-classes-drf","title":"28. Permission Classes (DRF)","text":"<ul> <li><code>AllowAny</code></li> <li><code>IsAuthenticated</code></li> <li><code>IsAdminUser</code></li> <li><code>IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#29-throttling","title":"29. Throttling","text":"<ul> <li><code>AnonRateThrottle</code></li> <li><code>UserRateThrottle</code></li> <li><code>ScopedRateThrottle</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#30-values-vs-values_list","title":"30. values vs values_list","text":"<pre><code>Model.objects.values('field')        # Dict\nModel.objects.values_list('field')   # Tuple\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#31-apiview-vs-viewset","title":"31. APIView vs ViewSet","text":"<ul> <li>APIView: Custom behavior per HTTP method.</li> <li>ViewSet: Manages full CRUD.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#32-signal-vs-celery","title":"32. Signal vs Celery","text":"<ul> <li>Signal: Sync event system.</li> <li>Celery: Async task queue.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#33-celery-workers-vs-beat","title":"33. Celery Workers vs Beat","text":"<ul> <li>Worker: Executes tasks.</li> <li>Beat: Scheduler for periodic tasks.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#34-template-inheritance","title":"34. Template Inheritance","text":"<p>Ans: Avoid redundancy by extending base templates.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#35-why-django-is-preferred","title":"35. Why Django is Preferred?","text":"<ul> <li>Modular, secure, admin-ready</li> <li>Python-based</li> <li>DRY principle</li> <li>MVT architecture</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#36-django-drawbacks","title":"36. Django Drawbacks","text":"<ul> <li>Monolithic structure</li> <li>Heavy ORM dependence</li> <li>Lack of convention over configuration</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#37-companies-using-django","title":"37. Companies using Django","text":"<ul> <li>Instagram</li> <li>Pinterest</li> <li>Mozilla</li> <li>Reddit</li> <li>YouTube</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#38-advanced-topics-checklist","title":"38. Advanced Topics Checklist","text":"<ul> <li>Django Forms &amp; Validation</li> <li>Authentication Middleware</li> <li>Testing Strategies</li> <li>Performance Optimization</li> <li>Security Best Practices</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#39-url-routing-to-view-full-simple-workflow","title":"39. URL Routing to View \u2013 Full simple workflow","text":"<pre><code>Browser URL  \n   \u2193  \nProject urls.py  \n   \u2193  \nApp urls.py  \n   \u2193  \nMatched View  \n   \u2193  \nView returns response (HTML/JSON)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udfe2 Easy Interview Example Answer</p> <p>Q: How does Django map a URL to a view?</p> <p>Django uses URL routing. When a request comes in, Django checks <code>urls.py</code>. It matches the URL pattern and maps it to the corresponding view function or class. The matched view receives the request object, processes it, and returns an HttpResponse.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#40-how-a-view-interacts-with-a-model-in-django","title":"40. How a View Interacts With a Model in Django","text":"<pre><code>User Request\n      \u2193\nURL Router maps to View\n      \u2193\nView queries Model (ORM)\n      \u2193\nView gets data from database\n      \u2193\nView returns Response (HTML/JSON)\n</code></pre> <p>Easy Interview Example Answer</p> <p>A Django view interacts with a model by querying the model (using ORM) to fetch, create, update, or delete data. The view then processes that data and returns an HTTP response (HTML/JSON).</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#41-django-template-rendering-full-flow","title":"41 Django Template Rendering \u2013 Full Flow","text":"<pre><code>User Request  \n     \u2193  \nURL Router  \n     \u2193  \nView executes  \n     \u2193  \nView fetches data from model  \n     \u2193  \nrender(request, template, context)  \n     \u2193  \nTemplate engine fills placeholders  \n     \u2193  \nFinal HTML returned to browser\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83c\udfaf Interview-Ready One-Line Answer</p> <p>The view uses Django\u2019s template engine to merge HTML templates with context data using <code>render()</code>, and the resulting HTML is returned as the response.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#42-response-returned-in-django","title":"42 Response Returned in Django","text":"<p>View generates a response</p> <ul> <li><code>HttpResponse(\"Hello\")</code></li> <li><code>JsonResponse({\"status\": \"ok\"})</code></li> <li><code>render(request, \"template.html\", context)</code></li> </ul> <pre><code>View                  \u2192 returns HttpResponse\nMiddleware (reverse)  \u2192 modifies/inspects it\nWSGI Layer            \u2192 prepares final HTTP response\nBrowser               \u2192 receives content\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83c\udfaf Interview-Friendly One-Line Summary</p> <p>A \u201cresponse returned\u201d means the view has finished its logic and given Django an HttpResponse object, which is then processed by middleware and sent back to the client.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Django_Framework.html#43-abstract-user-base-in-django","title":"43 Abstract User Base in Django","text":"<p>In Django, Abstract User Base refers to creating a custom user model using <code>AbstractUser</code> or <code>AbstractBaseUser</code> to customize authentication behavior.</p> <p>\u2705 Why we use Abstract User Base?</p> <p>The default Django <code>User</code> model is limited:</p> <ul> <li>Username is mandatory</li> <li>Email is not unique</li> <li>Hard to add custom fields</li> </ul> <p>So Django provides abstract base classes to create flexible user models.</p> <p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>AbstractUser</code> (Most commonly used)</p> <p>Explanation:</p> <p><code>AbstractUser</code> is an abstract version of Django\u2019s default user model. It already includes username, email, password, permissions, and admin support. We extend it to add extra fields.</p> <p>When to use:</p> <ul> <li>Small customization needed</li> <li>Want to keep username login</li> <li>Faster and safer</li> </ul> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>class User(AbstractUser):\n    phone = models.CharField(max_length=15)\n</code></pre> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>AbstractBaseUser</code> (Advanced)</p> <p>Explanation:</p> <p><code>AbstractBaseUser</code> provides only core authentication features like password hashing and last login. We must define fields, user manager, permissions, and login logic ourselves.</p> <p>When to use:</p> <ul> <li>Email or phone based login</li> <li>Full control over authentication</li> <li>Complex business requirements</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html","title":"Fastapi_Framework","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#python-web-frameworks-fastapi-interview-guide","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python Web Frameworks: Fastapi Interview Guide","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#table-of-contents","title":"\ud83d\udccc Table of Contents","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#fastapi","title":"FastAPI","text":"<ol> <li>FastAPI Basics</li> <li>Request Handling</li> <li>Dependency Injection</li> <li>Background Tasks &amp; Middleware</li> <li>Security &amp; Validation</li> <li>Async Support &amp; Performance</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#fastapi-section","title":"\u26a1 FASTAPI SECTION","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#1-what-is-fastapi","title":"1. What is FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans: FastAPI is a modern, fast (high-performance), web framework for building APIs with Python 3.7+ based on standard Python type hints.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#2-how-to-create-a-simple-api-in-fastapi","title":"2. How to create a simple API in FastAPI?","text":"<pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(\"/\")\ndef read_root():\n    return {\"message\": \"Hello, FastAPI\"}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#3-how-does-fastapi-handle-async-and-await","title":"3. How does FastAPI handle async and await?","text":"<p>Ans: FastAPI supports asynchronous endpoints out-of-the-box using Python's <code>async</code> and <code>await</code>, allowing high-performance I/O-bound operations.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#4-what-are-pydantic-models-in-fastapi","title":"4. What are Pydantic Models in FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans: Pydantic is used in FastAPI to define request and response schemas with data validation.</p> <pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass Item(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    price: float\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#5-what-is-dependency-injection-in-fastapi","title":"5. What is Dependency Injection in FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans: FastAPI supports dependency injection for request handling, authentication, and reusable logic using the <code>Depends</code> function.</p> <pre><code>from fastapi import Depends\n\ndef get_token(token: str):\n    return token\n\n@app.get(\"/secure\")\ndef secure_endpoint(token: str = Depends(get_token)):\n    return {\"token\": token}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#6-how-to-handle-background-tasks-in-fastapi","title":"6. How to handle background tasks in FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans: Using <code>BackgroundTasks</code> for tasks that can be run after a response is sent:</p> <pre><code>from fastapi import BackgroundTasks\n\n@app.post(\"/send-email\")\ndef send_email(background_tasks: BackgroundTasks):\n    background_tasks.add_task(send_email_function)\n    return {\"message\": \"Email will be sent\"}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#7-how-does-fastapi-perform-automatic-data-validation","title":"7. How does FastAPI perform automatic data validation?","text":"<p>Ans: FastAPI uses Pydantic to validate request bodies, query parameters, and path variables automatically based on Python types.</p>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#8-how-to-use-middleware-in-fastapi","title":"8. How to use Middleware in FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans: Middleware can be added using <code>@app.middleware(\"http\")</code> decorator.</p> <pre><code>@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def log_requests(request, call_next):\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    return response\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#9-how-does-fastapi-support-openapi-swagger-docs","title":"9. How does FastAPI support OpenAPI &amp; Swagger Docs?","text":"<p>Ans: FastAPI auto-generates Swagger and ReDoc UI from route and type definitions.</p> <ul> <li>Swagger: <code>/docs</code></li> <li>ReDoc: <code>/redoc</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Fastapi_Framework.html#10-what-are-some-performance-benefits-of-fastapi","title":"10. What are some performance benefits of FastAPI?","text":"<p>Ans:</p> <ul> <li>Built on Starlette and Pydantic</li> <li>Asynchronous support with <code>async/await</code></li> <li>Automatic validation and serialization</li> <li>Auto-documentation</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html","title":"Flask_Framework","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#python-web-frameworks-flask-interview-guide","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python Web Frameworks:  Flask Interview Guide","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#table-of-contents","title":"\ud83d\udccc Table of Contents","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#flask","title":"Flask","text":"<ol> <li>Flask Basics</li> <li>Cookies &amp; URL Handling</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#flask-section","title":"\ud83e\udd84 FLASK SECTION","text":""},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#1-what-does-url_for-do-in-flask","title":"1. What does <code>url_for</code> do in Flask?","text":"<p>Ans: <code>url_for()</code> dynamically generates URLs for the specified view function. This helps avoid hardcoding URLs.</p> <pre><code>from flask import url_for\nurl_for('home')  # Outputs: '/home'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Framework/Flask_Framework.html#2-how-do-you-handle-cookies-in-flask","title":"2. How do you handle cookies in Flask?","text":"<p>Ans: Use the <code>set_cookie()</code> method on the response object:</p> <pre><code>@app.route('/setcookie')\ndef set_cookie():\n    resp = make_response(\"Setting cookie\")\n    resp.set_cookie('username', 'John')\n    return resp\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html","title":"Snippets","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#complete-python-interview-syntax-snippets","title":"\u2705 \ud83d\udd36 COMPLETE PYTHON INTERVIEW SYNTAX &amp; SNIPPETS","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#your-one-complete-document-all-combined","title":"Your One Complete Document (All Combined)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#1-python-basics","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 PYTHON BASICS","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#print","title":"Print","text":"<pre><code>print(\"Hello\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#variables-types","title":"Variables &amp; Types","text":"<pre><code>x = 10\nname = \"Dhruv\"\npi = 3.14\nflag = True\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#type-casting","title":"Type Casting","text":"<pre><code>int(\"10\")\nfloat(\"10.5\")\nstr(100)\nlist(\"abc\")  # ['a', 'b', 'c']\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#check-type","title":"Check type","text":"<pre><code>type(x)\nisinstance(x, int)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#2-conditionals","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 CONDITIONALS","text":"<pre><code>if a &gt; b:\n    pass\nelif a == b:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#inline-if","title":"Inline if","text":"<pre><code>result = \"even\" if x % 2 == 0 else \"odd\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#3-loops","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 LOOPS","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#for-loop","title":"For Loop","text":"<pre><code>for i in range(5):\n    print(i)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#while-loop","title":"While Loop","text":"<pre><code>while n &gt; 0:\n    n -= 1\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#loop-else","title":"Loop Else","text":"<pre><code>for x in items:\n    if x == 5:\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"No break executed\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#4-data-structures","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 DATA STRUCTURES","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#list","title":"\u2714 LIST","text":"<pre><code>lst = [1,2,3]\nlst.append(4)\nlst.remove(2)\nlst.pop()\nlst.sort()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#list-slicing","title":"List slicing","text":"<pre><code>lst[1:4]\nlst[::-1]  # reverse\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#tuple","title":"\u2714 TUPLE","text":"<pre><code>t = (1,2,3)\nt[0]\n</code></pre> <p>Immutable!</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#set","title":"\u2714 SET","text":"<pre><code>s = {1,2,3}\ns.add(4)\ns.remove(2)\ns.union({5})\ns.intersection({2,3})\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#dictionary","title":"\u2714 DICTIONARY","text":"<pre><code>d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nd[\"a\"]\nd.get(\"c\", 0)\nd.keys()\nd.values()\nd.items()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#5-comprehensions","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 COMPREHENSIONS","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#list_1","title":"List","text":"<pre><code>[x*x for x in range(5)]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#dict","title":"Dict","text":"<pre><code>{k: v*v for k, v in enumerate(range(4))}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#set_1","title":"Set","text":"<pre><code>{x for x in [1,1,2,3]}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#generator-expression","title":"Generator Expression","text":"<pre><code>(x*x for x in range(10))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#6-functions","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 FUNCTIONS","text":"<pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#default-args","title":"Default args","text":"<pre><code>def greet(name=\"Guest\"):\n    pass\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#args-kwargs","title":"Args / Kwargs","text":"<pre><code>def test(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args, kwargs)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#7-lambda","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 LAMBDA","text":"<pre><code>square = lambda x: x*x\n</code></pre> <p>Sorting with Lambda:</p> <pre><code>sorted_lst = sorted(data, key=lambda x: x[\"age\"])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#8-decorators","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 DECORATORS","text":"<pre><code>def my_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(\"Before\")\n        func()\n        print(\"After\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@my_decorator\ndef hello():\n    print(\"Hello\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#9-generators","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 GENERATORS","text":"<pre><code>def countdown(n):\n    while n &gt; 0:\n        yield n\n        n -= 1\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#file-handling","title":"\ud83d\udd1f FILE HANDLING","text":"<pre><code>with open(\"file.txt\", \"r\") as f:\n    data = f.read()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#11-error-handling","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 ERROR HANDLING","text":"<pre><code>try:\n    risky()\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(e)\nfinally:\n    print(\"cleanup\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#12-classes-oop","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 CLASSES &amp; OOP","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#basic","title":"Basic","text":"<pre><code>class Car:\n    def __init__(self, brand):\n        self.brand = brand\n\n    def drive(self):\n        return \"Driving\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#inheritance","title":"Inheritance","text":"<pre><code>class SportsCar(Car):\n    def speed(self):\n        return 200\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#multiple-inheritance","title":"Multiple Inheritance","text":"<pre><code>class A: pass\nclass B: pass\nclass C(A, B): pass\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#class-static-methods","title":"Class &amp; Static Methods","text":"<pre><code>class Test:\n    count = 0\n\n    @classmethod\n    def inc(cls):\n        cls.count += 1\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def info():\n        return \"Static\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#13-property-decorator","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 PROPERTY Decorator","text":"<pre><code>class User:\n    def __init__(self, age):\n        self._age = age\n\n    @property\n    def age(self):\n        return self._age\n\n    @age.setter\n    def age(self, val):\n        self._age = val\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#14-imports","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 IMPORTS","text":"<pre><code>import math\nfrom os import path\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#15-multithreading","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 MULTITHREADING","text":"<pre><code>import threading\n\ndef work():\n    print(\"thread\")\n\nt = threading.Thread(target=work)\nt.start()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#16-multiprocessing","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e36\ufe0f\u20e3 MULTIPROCESSING","text":"<pre><code>from multiprocessing import Process\n\ndef task():\n    print(\"Process\")\n\np = Process(target=task)\np.start()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#17-async-await","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e37\ufe0f\u20e3 ASYNC / AWAIT","text":"<pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def work():\n    print(\"Start\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print(\"Done\")\n\nasyncio.run(work())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#18-context-manager-with-enter-exit","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e38\ufe0f\u20e3 CONTEXT MANAGER (WITH enter &amp; exit)","text":"<pre><code>class MyCtx:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(\"Entered\")\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        print(\"Exited\")\n\nwith MyCtx():\n    pass\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#19-iterator-protocol","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e39\ufe0f\u20e3 ITERATOR PROTOCOL","text":"<pre><code>class Counter:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.n = n\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.n &lt;= 0:\n            raise StopIteration\n        val = self.n\n        self.n -= 1\n        return val\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#20-lru-cache","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e30\ufe0f\u20e3 LRU CACHE","text":"<pre><code>from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=100)\ndef api_call(x):\n    return x*x\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#21-enum","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 ENUM","text":"<pre><code>from enum import Enum\n\nclass Status(Enum):\n    ACTIVE = 1\n    BLOCKED = 2\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#22-dataclass","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 DATACLASS","text":"<pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Product:\n    name: str\n    price: float\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#23-type-hinting","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 TYPE HINTING","text":"<pre><code>def add(a: int, b: int) -&gt; int:\n    return a + b\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#24-unit-testing","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 UNIT TESTING","text":"<pre><code>import unittest\n\nclass TestMath(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self):\n        self.assertEqual(2+3, 5)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#25-json-handling","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 JSON Handling","text":"<pre><code>import json\n\njson.dumps(obj)\njson.loads(text)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#26-time-date","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e36\ufe0f\u20e3 TIME &amp; DATE","text":"<pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nnow = datetime.now()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#27-logging","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e37\ufe0f\u20e3 LOGGING","text":"<pre><code>import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogging.info(\"Info log\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#28-regex","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e38\ufe0f\u20e3 REGEX","text":"<pre><code>import re\n\nre.findall(r\"\\d+\", \"abc123\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#29-venv","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e39\ufe0f\u20e3 VENV","text":"<pre><code>python -m venv env\nsource env/bin/activate\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#30-real-scenario-tricky-questions-4-years-experience","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e30\ufe0f\u20e3 REAL SCENARIO TRICKY QUESTIONS (4 YEARS EXPERIENCE)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#1-remove-duplicates-but-keep-order","title":"1. Remove duplicates but keep order","text":"<pre><code>seen = set()\nresult = []\nfor x in lst:\n    if x not in seen:\n        seen.add(x)\n        result.append(x)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#2-flatten-list","title":"2. Flatten list","text":"<pre><code>flat = [x for sub in lst for x in sub]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#3-reverse-dict","title":"3. Reverse dict","text":"<pre><code>rev = {v: k for k, v in d.items()}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#4-merge-two-dicts","title":"4. Merge two dicts","text":"<pre><code>final = {**d1, **d2}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#5-sort-by-multiple-keys","title":"5. Sort by multiple keys","text":"<pre><code>sorted(data, key=lambda x: (x[\"age\"], x[\"name\"]))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#6-detect-duplicates-in-list","title":"6. Detect duplicates in list","text":"<pre><code>dupes = [x for x in lst if lst.count(x) &gt; 1]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#7-file-read-line-by-line-memory-safe","title":"7. File read line-by-line (memory safe)","text":"<pre><code>for line in open(\"file.txt\"):\n    print(line.strip())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#8-retry-mechanism","title":"8. Retry mechanism","text":"<pre><code>import time\nfor _ in range(3):\n    try:\n        risky()\n        break\n    except:\n        time.sleep(2)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#31-what-is-the-output-of-the-following","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 What is the output of the following:","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)\n</code></pre> <p>A. <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> B. <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code> C. Error D. <code>[4, 1, 2, 3]</code></p> <p>\u2705 Correct Answer: B</p> <pre><code>def add(num, lst=[]):\n    lst.append(num)\n    return lst\n\nprint(add(10))\nprint(add(20, []))\nprint(add(30))\n</code></pre> <p>A. <code>[10]</code>, <code>[20]</code>, <code>[30]</code> B. <code>[10]</code>, <code>[20]</code>, <code>[10, 30]</code> C. <code>[10]</code>, <code>[20]</code>, <code>[10]</code> D. Error</p> <p>\u2705 Correct Answer: B</p> <pre><code>x = [[0]] * 3\nx[0][0] = 99\nprint(x)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#correct-output","title":"Correct Output:","text":"<pre><code>[[99], [99], [99]]\n</code></pre> <p>Because all three sublists reference the same object.</p> <pre><code>def func(nums=[]):\n    nums.append(1)\n    return nums\n\nprint(func())\nprint(func())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#answer","title":"Answer:","text":"<pre><code>[1]\n[1, 1]\n</code></pre> <p>Because <code>nums</code> default list is shared across calls.</p> <pre><code>x = [10, 20, 30]\ny = x[:]\ny[0] = 99\n\nprint(x)\nprint(y)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#answer_1","title":"Answer:","text":"<pre><code>[10, 20, 30]\n[99, 20, 30]\n</code></pre> <p>Shallow copy works.</p> <pre><code>a = (1, 2, 3)\nb = (1, 2, 3)\n\nprint(a is b)\nprint(a == b)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#answer_2","title":"Answer:","text":"<pre><code>False\nTrue\n</code></pre> <p>Immutable objects can have different memory addresses but same value.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#1-list-tuple-slicing","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1. List / Tuple Slicing","text":"<pre><code>a = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]\n</code></pre> Code Explanation Output <code>a[:]</code> Full copy <code>[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]</code> <code>a[::2]</code> Every 2nd element <code>[10, 30, 50]</code> <code>a[1::2]</code> Every 2nd from index 1 <code>[20, 40, 60]</code> <code>a[::-1]</code> Reverse list <code>[60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10]</code> <code>a[1:4]</code> Index 1 \u2192 3 <code>[20, 30, 40]</code> <code>a[-3:]</code> Last 3 elements <code>[40, 50, 60]</code> <code>a[:-2]</code> All except last 2 <code>[10, 20, 30, 40]</code> <code>a[::3]</code> Every 3rd element <code>[10, 40]</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#2-set-operations-with-example","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2. Set Operations (With Example)","text":"<pre><code>a = set(\"Hello\")   # {'H','e','l','o'}\nb = set(\"World\")   # {'W','o','r','l','d'}\n</code></pre> Code Meaning Output <code>a - b</code> In <code>a</code> not in <code>b</code> <code>{'H', 'e'}</code> <code>b - a</code> In <code>b</code> not in <code>a</code> <code>{'W', 'r', 'd'}</code> <code>a &amp; b</code> Common elements <code>{'l', 'o'}</code> <code>a \\| b</code> Union (all unique) <code>{'H','e','l','o','W','r','d'}</code> <code>a ^ b</code> Symmetric difference <code>{'H','e','W','r','d'}</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#3-list-reference-vs-copy-very-important","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3. List Reference vs Copy (VERY IMPORTANT \ud83d\udd25)","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nc = a[:]\n</code></pre> Expression Result <code>a is b</code> <code>True</code> <code>a is c</code> <code>False</code> <code>a == c</code> <code>True</code> <pre><code>b.append(4)\nprint(a)   # [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(c)   # [1, 2, 3]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#4-list-methods-with-example","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4. List Methods With Example","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2, 2, 3]\n</code></pre> Code Output / Effect <code>a.count(2)</code> <code>2</code> <code>a.index(3)</code> <code>3</code> <code>a.append(5)</code> <code>[1, 2, 2, 3, 5]</code> <code>a.extend([6,7])</code> <code>[1, 2, 2, 3, 6, 7]</code> <code>a.pop()</code> Removes last element <code>a.remove(2)</code> Removes first <code>2</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#5-tuple-list-unpacking","title":"\ud83d\udd39 5. Tuple / List Unpacking","text":"<pre><code>x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n</code></pre> Code Result <code>a, b = 1, 2</code> <code>a=1, b=2</code> <code>a, *b = x</code> <code>a=1, b=[2,3,4,5]</code> <code>*a, b = x</code> <code>a=[1,2,3,4], b=5</code> <code>a, b, *c = x</code> <code>a=1, b=2, c=[3,4,5]</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#6-list-dict-set-comprehension","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6. List / Dict / Set Comprehension","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2, 3, 4]\n</code></pre> Code Output <code>[x*x for x in a]</code> <code>[1, 4, 9, 16]</code> <code>[x for x in a if x % 2 == 0]</code> <code>[2, 4]</code> <code>{x*x for x in a}</code> <code>{1, 4, 9, 16}</code> <code>{x: x*x for x in a}</code> <code>{1:1, 2:4, 3:9, 4:16}</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#7-tricky-but-common-python-snippets","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7. Tricky but Common Python Snippets \ud83d\ude08","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#mutable-default-argument","title":"\ud83d\udd38 Mutable default argument","text":"<pre><code>def add(x, lst=[]):\n    lst.append(x)\n    return lst\n\nadd(1)  # [1]\nadd(2)  # [1, 2]  \u274c unexpected\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#correct-way","title":"\u2705 Correct way","text":"<pre><code>def add(x, lst=None):\n    if lst is None:\n        lst = []\n    lst.append(x)\n    return lst\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#8-list-multiplication-trap","title":"\ud83d\udd39 8. List Multiplication Trap","text":"<pre><code>a = [[0]] * 3\na[0][0] = 1\nprint(a)  # [[1], [1], [1]] \u274c\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Correct:</p> <pre><code>a = [[0] for _ in range(3)]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#9-vs","title":"\ud83d\udd39 9. <code>+</code> vs <code>+=</code>","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2]\nb = a\n\na += [3]\nprint(b)  # [1, 2, 3]\n</code></pre> <pre><code>a = [1, 2]\nb = a\n\na = a + [3]\nprint(b)  # [1, 2]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Snippets.html#10-your-example-revisited","title":"\ud83d\udd39 10. Your Example Revisited","text":"<pre><code>a = [(1,2,3,4), (33,22,11), 0, 4, 5]\na[::3]\n</code></pre> <p>\u27a1 Output:</p> <pre><code>[(1, 2, 3, 4), 4]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/communication.html","title":"Communication","text":""},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#1-tell-me-about-your-current-role-and-responsibilities","title":"1. Tell me about your current role and responsibilities.","text":"<p>Answer format (perfect):</p>"},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#im-a-python-backend-developer-with-4-years-of-experience-in-designing-rest-apis-working-with-djangofastapi-writing-optimized-sql-queries-implementing-caching-and-integrating-third-party-services-my-work-mainly-focuses-on-performance-optimization-scalable-backend-design-and-cloud-deployment-using-docker-aws","title":"<pre><code>I\u2019m a Python backend developer with 4+ years of experience in designing REST APIs, \nworking with Django/FastAPI, writing optimized SQL queries, implementing caching, \nand integrating third-party services. \nMy work mainly focuses on performance optimization, scalable backend design, \nand cloud deployment using Docker + AWS.\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#2-which-project-are-you-most-proud-of-explain-architecture","title":"2. Which project are you most proud of? Explain architecture.","text":"<p>Explain using 4 steps:</p> <pre><code>Problem \u2192\nSolution \u2192\nTech stack \u2192\nYour exact contribution\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#3-what-challenges-did-you-face-how-did-you-solve-them","title":"3. What challenges did you face? How did you solve them?","text":"<p>Example:</p> <pre><code>We had slow API responses (2\u20133 seconds).\nI optimized using:\n\u2022 select_related/prefetch_related\n\u2022 Added Redis caching\n\u2022 Added proper DB indexing\n\u2022 Moved heavy tasks to Celery\nAPI speed improved from 2.5 sec \u2192 200 ms.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#4-why-you-chose-this-company","title":"4. why you chose this company?","text":"<pre><code>'Company Name' offers large-scale enterprise projects, stability, global delivery model, \nand structured career growth which align with my long-term goals.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#5-tell-me-a-time-when-you-solved-a-complex-problem","title":"5. Tell me a time when you solved a complex problem.","text":""},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#6-tell-me-about-a-time-you-solved-a-conflict-in-your-team","title":"6. Tell me about a time you solved a conflict in your team..","text":""},{"location":"Interview/communication.html#7-feature-list-for-weamai","title":"7 feature-list for Weam.ai:","text":"<p>Core Platform Features</p> <ul> <li>Access multiple LLMs (GPT-5, Claude, Gemini, etc.) in one interface (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Unified AI workspace for teams (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Open-source and self-hostable deployment (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Team collaboration workspaces (\u201cBrains\u201d) with access control (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Custom prompt library (create, save, share prompts) (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Fork chat (reuse and share conversations) (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Prompt enhancement (Power Prompt) (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Import chats from other platforms (docs.weam.ai)</li> </ul> <p>AI Agents &amp; Automation</p> <ul> <li>Create custom AI agents (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Pro Agents for specialized workflows (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Choose model per agent (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Agent knowledge base via uploaded documents (docs.weam.ai)</li> </ul> <p>Document &amp; Retrieval Features</p> <ul> <li>Upload and manage documents (PDF, DOCX, etc.) (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) with vector search (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>Document-grounded AI responses (docs.weam.ai)</li> </ul> <p>Modular AI Applications</p> <ul> <li>AI Chatbot (organization-aware chat) (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>AI Interview tool (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>AI Document Generator (docs.weam.ai)</li> <li>AI SEO Generator (docs.weam.ai)</li> </ul> <p>Collaboration &amp; Productivity</p> <ul> <li>Shared team chats (GitHub)</li> <li>Threaded comments (GitHub)</li> <li>Team member management and permissions (GitHub)</li> <li>Shareable links (GitHub)</li> </ul> <p>Advanced Tools</p> <ul> <li>Web scraping and web search inside chat (GitHub)</li> <li>Local model support (e.g., Ollama) (GitHub)</li> <li>Multi-workspace/enterprise support (GitHub)</li> <li>Usage analytics and admin dashboards (GitHub)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html","title":"DSA","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#dsa-for-python-developers","title":"\ud83d\ude80 DSA for Python Developers","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#list-of-dsal-interview-questions","title":"\ud83c\udfaf LIST OF DSAL INTERVIEW QUESTIONS","text":"<ol> <li>Two Sum \u2192 Hash map, O(1) lookup</li> <li>Reverse Linked List \u2192 3 pointers</li> <li>Valid Parentheses \u2192 Stack</li> <li>Merge Two Sorted Lists \u2192 Two pointers</li> <li>Binary Search \u2192 Mid logic</li> <li>BFS / DFS \u2192 Queue / Recursion</li> <li>Level Order Traversal \u2192 BFS + queue</li> <li>Longest Common Prefix \u2192 shrink prefix</li> <li>Find Missing Number \u2192 sum formula</li> <li>Move Zeroes \u2192 two pointer</li> <li>Sliding Window Max \u2192 deque</li> <li>Longest Substring Without Repeat \u2192 set + window</li> <li>Frequency Count \u2192 Counter</li> <li>Rotate Array \u2192 reverse technique</li> <li>Kth Largest Element \u2192 min-heap</li> </ol> <p>(Explanation + Practical Questions + Clean Solutions)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#1-arrays-lists","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Arrays / Lists","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#what-it-is","title":"\u2705 What it is","text":"<p>Ordered collection, fast indexing (O(1)), dynamic resizing.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#when-used","title":"\ud83c\udfaf When used","text":"<ul> <li>Sliding window</li> <li>Searching</li> <li>Two pointer problems</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-two-sum","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Two Sum","text":"<p>Find 2 numbers whose sum equals target.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#approach","title":"\u2714\ufe0f Approach","text":"<p>Use a hash map to store seen numbers.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#code","title":"\u2714\ufe0f Code","text":"<pre><code>def twoSum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}\n    for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n        if target - n in seen:\n            return [seen[target - n], i]\n        seen[n] = i\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-move-zeroes","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Move Zeroes","text":"<p>Place all zeroes at end without changing non-zero order.</p> <pre><code>def moveZeroes(nums):\n    pos = 0\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        if nums[i] != 0:\n            nums[pos] = nums[i]\n            pos += 1\n    while pos &lt; len(nums):\n        nums[pos] = 0\n        pos += 1\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-rotate-array","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Rotate Array","text":"<p>Rotate right by <code>k</code>.</p> <pre><code>def rotate(nums, k):\n    k %= len(nums)\n    nums.reverse()\n    nums[:k] = reversed(nums[:k])\n    nums[k:] = reversed(nums[k:])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#2-strings","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Strings","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#when-used_1","title":"\ud83d\ude80 When used","text":"<ul> <li>Palindromes</li> <li>Substring problems</li> <li>Pattern matching</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#approach_1","title":"\u2714\ufe0f Approach","text":"<p>Use sliding window + set.</p> <pre><code>def lengthOfLongestSubstring(s):\n    seen = set()\n    l = res = 0\n    for r in range(len(s)):\n        while s[r] in seen:\n            seen.remove(s[l])\n            l += 1\n        seen.add(s[r])\n        res = max(res, r-l+1)\n    return res\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-longest-common-prefix","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Longest Common Prefix","text":"<pre><code>def longestCommonPrefix(strs):\n    prefix = strs[0]\n    for s in strs[1:]:\n        while not s.startswith(prefix):\n            prefix = prefix[:-1]\n        if not prefix:\n            return \"\"\n    return prefix\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#3-hash-map-hashing","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Hash Map / Hashing","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-important","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why important?","text":"<p>Most asked DSA tool for Python developer (dict &amp; set) Used for fast lookup: O(1)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-count-frequency-of-elements","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Count Frequency of Elements","text":"<pre><code>from collections import Counter\nfreq = Counter([1,2,2,3,3,3])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#missing-number-0-to-n","title":"\u2b50 Missing Number (0 to n)","text":"<pre><code>def missingNumber(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    return n*(n+1)//2 - sum(nums)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#4-stack","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Stack","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#used-for","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Used for","text":"<ul> <li>Valid parentheses</li> <li>Undo/Redo</li> <li>DFS</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-valid-parentheses","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Valid Parentheses","text":"<pre><code>def isValid(s):\n    stack = []\n    pairs = {')':'(', ']':'[', '}':'{'}\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in \"([{\":\n            stack.append(ch)\n        else:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:\n                return False\n    return not stack\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#5-queue-bfs","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Queue (BFS)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#used-for_1","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Used for","text":"<ul> <li>BFS in trees &amp; graphs</li> <li>Level-order traversal</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#level-order-traversal-tree-bfs","title":"\u2b50 Level Order Traversal (Tree BFS)","text":"<pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef levelOrder(root):\n    if not root: return []\n    q = deque([root])\n    result = []\n\n    while q:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(q)):\n            node = q.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left: q.append(node.left)\n            if node.right: q.append(node.right)\n        result.append(level)\n    return result\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#6-linked-list","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Linked List","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-used","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why used?","text":"<p>Efficient insert/delete Used in interviews frequently.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#interview-question-reverse-linked-list","title":"\u2b50 Interview Question: Reverse Linked List","text":"<pre><code>def reverseList(head):\n    prev = None\n    curr = head\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev = curr\n        curr = nxt\n    return prev\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#merge-two-sorted-lists","title":"\u2b50 Merge Two Sorted Lists","text":"<pre><code>def mergeTwoLists(l1, l2):\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while l1 and l2:\n        if l1.val &lt; l2.val:\n            tail.next = l1\n            l1 = l1.next\n        else:\n            tail.next = l2\n            l2 = l2.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    tail.next = l1 or l2\n    return dummy.next\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#7-trees-dfs-bfs","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Trees (DFS + BFS)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#traversal-types","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Traversal Types","text":"<ul> <li>DFS: Preorder, Inorder, Postorder</li> <li>BFS: Level order</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#binary-tree-dfs-example","title":"\u2b50 Binary Tree DFS Example","text":"<pre><code>def dfs(root):\n    if not root:\n        return\n    dfs(root.left)\n    dfs(root.right)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#8-graphs","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Graphs","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-used_1","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why used?","text":"<p>Used to solve real-world problems:</p> <ul> <li>Routes</li> <li>Networks</li> <li>Connected components</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#graph-bfs-dfs","title":"\u2b50 Graph BFS / DFS","text":"<pre><code>def bfs(graph, start):\n    from collections import deque\n    q = deque([start])\n    visited = set([start])\n\n    while q:\n        node = q.popleft()\n        for nei in graph[node]:\n            if nei not in visited:\n                visited.add(nei)\n                q.append(nei)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#9-heaps-priority-queue","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Heaps (Priority Queue)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-used_2","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why used?","text":"<ul> <li>Kth largest element</li> <li>Scheduling</li> <li>Streams</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#kth-largest-element","title":"\u2b50 Kth Largest Element","text":"<pre><code>import heapq\n\ndef findKthLargest(nums, k):\n    heap = []\n    for n in nums:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, n)\n        if len(heap) &gt; k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n    return heap[0]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#binary-search","title":"\ud83d\udd1f Binary Search","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-used_3","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why used?","text":"<ul> <li>Fast searching in sorted array</li> <li>O(log n)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#binary-search-code","title":"\u2b50 Binary Search Code","text":"<pre><code>def binarySearch(arr, target):\n    l, r = 0, len(arr)-1\n    while l &lt;= r:\n        mid = (l+r)//2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if target &lt; arr[mid]:\n            r = mid - 1\n        else:\n            l = mid + 1\n    return -1\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#11-sliding-window","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 Sliding Window","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#why-used_4","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Why used?","text":"<p>Used for:</p> <ul> <li>sum of window</li> <li>longest substring</li> <li>max in window</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#sliding-window-maximum","title":"\u2b50 Sliding Window Maximum","text":"<pre><code>from collections import deque\n\ndef maxSlidingWindow(nums, k):\n    q = deque()\n    res = []\n\n    for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n        while q and nums[q[-1]] &lt; n:\n            q.pop()\n\n        q.append(i)\n\n        if q[0] == i-k:\n            q.popleft()\n\n        if i &gt;= k-1:\n            res.append(nums[q[0]])\n    return res\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#12-dynamic-programming-dp","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 Dynamic Programming (DP)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#key-idea","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Key idea","text":"<p>Solve small subproblems \u2192 store results \u2192 reuse \u2192 O(n)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/dsa.html#fibonacci-using-dp","title":"\u2b50 Fibonacci Using DP","text":"<pre><code>from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(None)\ndef fib(n):\n    if n &lt;= 1:\n        return n\n    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html","title":"Problem Solve","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#problem-solution-documentation-with-detailed-workflows","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Problem-Solution Documentation with Detailed Workflows","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#1-github-actions-cicd-workflow","title":"\u2705 1. GitHub Actions CI/CD Workflow","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>CI pipeline was inconsistent and didn\u2019t automatically run tests on push or PRs.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<p>Set up GitHub Actions for:</p> <ul> <li>Code checkout</li> <li>Dependency installation</li> <li>Testing and coverage reporting</li> <li>Notifications and deployment</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Push code / Open Pull Request (GitHub)\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd04 GitHub Actions Triggered\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udece\ufe0f Checkout code from repo\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udc0d Setup Python + Install dependencies\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83e\uddea Run unit tests with pytest\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udcca Generate code coverage report\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udce2 Notify developer (Slack / GitHub UI)\n             \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddd0 Code Review Process\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u274c If tests fail \u2192 Fix code \u2192 Push again\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 If all pass \u2192 Merge to `main`\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#2-unit-test-coverage-enforcement","title":"\u2705 2. Unit Test Coverage Enforcement","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_1","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Test coverage was below 30% and missing from CI checks.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_1","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Added <code>pytest-cov</code></li> <li>Configured CI to block merges if coverage &lt;90%</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_1","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Writes/Refactors Code\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddea Write Unit Tests\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\ude80 Run pytest with coverage in CI\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcca Generate Coverage Report\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udfe2 If coverage &gt;= 90% \u2192 Merge Allowed\n\ud83d\udd34 Else \u2192 Block PR + Notify Developer\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#3-playwright-for-ui-automation","title":"\u2705 3. Playwright for UI Automation","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_2","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Dynamic content made automation flaky.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_2","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Used Playwright for stable headless UI testing</li> <li>Waited for selectors and validated results</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_2","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Developer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Writes E2E Playwright Tests\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd04 CI Triggered on Commit\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83c\udf10 Launch headless browser\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83e\udded Visit dynamic page\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u23f3 Wait for selector\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83c\udfaf Interact with UI elements\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 Assert and report result\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#4-rotating-user-agents-in-scraper-crawlerai","title":"\u2705 4. Rotating User-Agents in Scraper (CrawlerAI)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_3","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Default headers blocked; scraper failed.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_3","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Rotated user agents</li> <li>Stopped on matching HTML pattern</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_3","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\udd16 Scraper\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Load User-Agent List\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd01 Loop through UAs\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Send request with UA\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Check if page structure is valid\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u274c If not \u2192 Try next UA\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 If matched \u2192 Save &amp; Exit\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#5-redis-pubsub-for-streaming","title":"\u2705 5. Redis Pub/Sub for Streaming","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_4","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Large responses caused lag and timeout.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_4","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Used Redis Pub/Sub to stream chunks</li> <li>Client received data in real time</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_4","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Client\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Requests Large Data\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Server\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Divide data into chunks\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Publish each chunk to Redis channel\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 Send metadata (e.g. done flag)\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce1 Client subscribes to channel\n             \u2502\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Receives each chunk and renders\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#6-model-wise-exception-handling-openai-gemini-anthropic","title":"\u2705 6. Model-Wise Exception Handling (OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_5","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Different models had different error structures.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_5","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Created model-specific exception classes</li> <li>Handled with nested try/except blocks</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_5","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\udde0 LLM Service\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Receive Prompt Request\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd0d Determine Model Type\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Gemini \u2192 Try/Catch GeminiError\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; OpenAI \u2192 Try/Catch OpenAIError\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Anthropic \u2192 Try/Catch AnthropicError\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcdc Log &amp; Return Unified Response\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#7-smart-token-based-api-key-selection-redis","title":"\u2705 7. Smart Token-Based API Key Selection (Redis)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_6","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Round-robin rotation used exhausted keys.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_6","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Stored token count in Redis</li> <li>Picked API key with most available tokens</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_6","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\udde0 Server API Middleware\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Receive New API Request\n             \u2502\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd01 Fetch All Keys + Token Count from Redis\n             \u2502\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Select Key with Highest Available Tokens\n                          \u2502\n                          \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Use Key to Call API\n                          \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Update Token Count in Redis\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#8-jwt-with-csrf-token-protection","title":"\u2705 8. JWT with CSRF Token Protection","text":""},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#challenge_7","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Challenge","text":"<p>Attackers used JWTs via script to spam signup.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#solution_7","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Solution","text":"<ul> <li>Added custom CSRF token header</li> <li>Matched value inside JWT</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/problem_solve.html#workflow_7","title":"\ud83e\udded Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Client\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Makes Signup API Call\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Sends JWT in Header\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Sends CSRF Token in Payload/Header\n             \u25bc\n\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Server\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Validate JWT Signature\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Validate CSRF Token Matches JWT Claim\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 Allow if Match / \u274c Reject if Not\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html","title":"Sceanio_based_Q&A","text":""},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#1-api-is-taking-5-seconds-what-will-you-check","title":"1. API is taking 5 seconds. What will you check?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Database queries</p> <ul> <li>Check slow queries</li> <li>Add proper indexes</li> <li>Avoid <code>SELECT *</code></li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Caching</p> <ul> <li>Use Redis for repeated data</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Payload size</p> <ul> <li>Reduce large request/response data</li> <li>Use pagination</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Async / Background tasks</p> <ul> <li>Move heavy work to Celery / async</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 External calls</p> <ul> <li>Check slow third-party APIs</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#2-in-production-you-see-database-load-increasing-what-will-you-check-first","title":"2. In production you see database load increasing. What will you check first?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Long-running / slow queries</p> <ul> <li>Identify queries taking most time</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Missing indexes</p> <ul> <li>Check frequent <code>WHERE</code>, <code>JOIN</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code> columns</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Too many joins</p> <ul> <li>Optimize or denormalize if needed</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 High API call volume</p> <ul> <li>Same queries executed repeatedly \u2192 add caching</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Database analysis tools</p> <ul> <li>Use <code>EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code></li> <li>Use <code>pg_stat_statements</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#3-suppose-you-get-500-internal-server-error-how-will-you-debug","title":"3. Suppose you get 500 Internal Server Error. How will you debug?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Check logs first</p> <ul> <li>Application logs</li> <li>Server logs</li> <li>Stack trace / error message</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Reproduce the issue</p> <ul> <li>Try same request in local or staging</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Validate inputs</p> <ul> <li>Check request data, headers, payload</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Exception handling</p> <ul> <li>Add proper <code>try/except</code></li> <li>Log the exact error</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Enable debug logging</p> <ul> <li>Temporarily increase log level (not in prod response)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#4-how-do-you-handle-millions-of-records-in-python","title":"4. How do you handle millions of records in Python?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Use generators</p> <ul> <li>Avoid loading everything into memory</li> <li>Process records one by one</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Batch processing</p> <ul> <li>Read and process data in chunks</li> <li>Useful for DB and file processing</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Streaming</p> <ul> <li>Stream data from files, APIs, or DB cursors</li> <li>No full data load in RAM</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Parallel processing</p> <ul> <li>Use multiprocessing for CPU-heavy tasks</li> <li>Use async for I/O-bound tasks</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Pagination</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#create-pagination-for-show-the-records","title":"* create pagination for show the records","text":""},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#5-suppose-your-api-is-failing-under-high-load-what-steps-will-you-take","title":"5. Suppose your API is failing under high load. What steps will you take?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Increase workers</p> <ul> <li>Scale app processes (Gunicorn / Uvicorn workers)</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Horizontal scaling</p> <ul> <li>Add more instances behind a load balancer</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Add caching (Redis)</p> <ul> <li>Cache frequent API responses and DB queries</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Use async / background tasks</p> <ul> <li>Move heavy work to Celery / queues</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Optimize database</p> <ul> <li>Indexing, query optimization, read replicas</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Use queues for heavy tasks</p> <ul> <li>Prevent request blocking</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#6-how-do-you-deploy-a-python-project-in-production","title":"6. How do you deploy a Python project in production?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Docker container</p> <ul> <li>Package app + dependencies for consistent environment</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Web server + app server</p> <ul> <li>Nginx as reverse proxy</li> <li>Gunicorn/Uvicorn to run Python app</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Environment variables</p> <ul> <li>Store secrets, API keys, config outside code</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 CI/CD pipeline</p> <ul> <li>Automate build, test, and deploy using GitHub Actions / Jenkins</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Logging &amp; Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Use logs (ELK, Sentry) and metrics (Prometheus, Grafana)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#7-what-major-challenges-did-you-solve-recently","title":"7. What major challenges did you solve recently?","text":"<pre><code>A key API used to take 2\u20133 seconds.  \nI optimized by:\n\u2022 Adding select_related / prefetch_related  \n\u2022 Redis caching  \n\u2022 DB indexing on frequently queried columns  \n\u2022 Moving heavy logic to Celery tasks  \nAPI time improved from 2.8s \u2192 180ms.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#8-how-do-you-handle-failures-in-microservices","title":"8. How do you handle failures in microservices?","text":"<pre><code>Retry  \nCircuit breaker  \nFallback  \nMessage queues  \n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#9-what-will-you-do-if-db-cpu-utilization-is-very-high","title":"9. What will you do if DB CPU utilization is very high?","text":"<pre><code>Check long-running queries  \nAdd indexes  \nEnable slow query log  \nSharding / read replica  \n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#10-how-do-you-expose-a-large-csv-file-via-api","title":"10. How do you expose a large CSV file via API?","text":"<p>Stream response Do not load entire file into memory  </p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#11-how-do-you-handle-sudden-traffic-spiking-10x","title":"11. How do you handle sudden traffic spiking 10x?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Horizontal Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Add more app servers behind a load balancer to distribute traffic</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Increase Workers / Threads</p> <ul> <li>Scale Gunicorn/Uvicorn workers or async workers to handle more concurrent requests</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Caching</p> <ul> <li>Use Redis / CDN to serve frequently requested data without hitting the database</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Queue Heavy Tasks</p> <ul> <li>Move non-critical or CPU-intensive tasks to Celery / message queues</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Database Optimization</p> <ul> <li>Use read replicas, indexing, and optimized queries to reduce DB load</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Rate Limiting / Throttling</p> <ul> <li>Prevent abuse and protect the system from being overwhelmed</li> </ul> <p>7\ufe0f\u20e3 Monitoring &amp; Auto-scaling</p> <ul> <li>Use monitoring tools (Prometheus/Grafana) and auto-scaling policies in cloud environments</li> </ul> <p>\ud83c\udfaf Short Interview Answer (15 sec)</p> <p>I handle sudden traffic spikes by scaling horizontally with load balancers, adding workers, caching frequently requested data, moving heavy tasks to queues, optimizing the database, and implementing rate limiting. Monitoring and auto-scaling ensure system stability under high load.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#12-how-do-you-safely-deploy-a-backend-application","title":"12. How do you safely deploy a backend application?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Use version control &amp; CI/CD</p> <ul> <li>Automated build, test, and deployment pipelines</li> <li>Run unit and integration tests before deploy</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Environment separation</p> <ul> <li>Separate dev / staging / production environments</li> <li>Deploy to staging first</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Containerization</p> <ul> <li>Use Docker for consistent and repeatable builds</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Zero-downtime deployment</p> <ul> <li>Use blue-green or rolling deployments</li> <li>Ensure health checks before routing traffic</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Environment variables &amp; secrets</p> <ul> <li>Store secrets securely (env vars, secret manager)</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Database migrations</p> <ul> <li>Apply migrations carefully and backward-compatible</li> <li>Take backups before changes</li> </ul> <p>7\ufe0f\u20e3 Monitoring &amp; rollback</p> <ul> <li>Monitor logs, metrics, and errors after deploy</li> <li>Roll back quickly if issues occur</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#short-interview-answer-1015-sec","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Short Interview Answer (10\u201315 sec)","text":"<p>I deploy backend applications safely using CI/CD pipelines, Docker, separate environments, zero-downtime deployment strategies, secure environment variables, careful database migrations, and continuous monitoring with quick rollback support.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#13-what-will-you-check-if-a-memory-leak-happens-in-python","title":"13. What will you check if a memory leak happens in Python?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Long-lived objects</p> <ul> <li>Check for objects that never get garbage collected</li> <li>Example: global lists, caches, or class-level references</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Circular references</p> <ul> <li>Objects referencing each other preventing GC</li> <li>Python usually handles with <code>gc</code> module, but some custom objects may leak</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Large data structures</p> <ul> <li>Lists, dicts, or DataFrames growing continuously</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 External resources</p> <ul> <li>Open files, sockets, or database connections not closed properly</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Profiling tools</p> <ul> <li>Use <code>tracemalloc</code> or <code>memory_profiler</code> to identify memory usage</li> <li>Example:</li> </ul> <pre><code>import tracemalloc\n\ntracemalloc.start()\n# Run your code\nprint(tracemalloc.get_traced_memory())\ntracemalloc.stop()\n</code></pre> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Third-party libraries</p> <ul> <li>Ensure they are not retaining references or leaking memory</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#14-how-to-migrate-from-monolithic-to-microservices","title":"14. How to migrate from Monolithic to Microservices?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Analyze &amp; Identify Boundaries</p> <ul> <li>Identify modules or domains that can be independent services</li> <li>Example: User Management, Payment, Notifications</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Define APIs</p> <ul> <li>Each microservice exposes well-defined APIs</li> <li>Use REST or gRPC for communication</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Database Strategy</p> <ul> <li>Decide between shared DB initially or database per service</li> <li>Gradually move to decoupled databases</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Implement Incrementally</p> <ul> <li>Start with one module \u2192 convert to microservice</li> <li>Keep the monolith running during migration</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Communication &amp; Messaging</p> <ul> <li>Use message brokers like RabbitMQ, Kafka, or Redis Pub/Sub</li> <li>For async or event-driven communication</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Service Discovery &amp; Load Balancing</p> <ul> <li>Use tools like Kubernetes, Consul, or Nginx</li> <li>Enables services to find each other dynamically</li> </ul> <p>7\ufe0f\u20e3 Monitoring &amp; Logging</p> <ul> <li>Implement centralized logging and metrics for all microservices</li> <li>Use ELK, Prometheus, Grafana</li> </ul> <p>8\ufe0f\u20e3 CI/CD &amp; Deployment</p> <ul> <li>Deploy microservices independently using Docker / Kubernetes / CI pipelines</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#15-which-logs-do-you-keep-in-production","title":"15. Which logs do you keep in production?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Application Logs</p> <ul> <li>Info, warnings, errors from your app</li> <li>Example: API request logs, exceptions, and stack traces</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Access Logs</p> <ul> <li>Track who accessed what and when</li> <li>Useful for security and auditing</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Database Logs</p> <ul> <li>Slow queries, connection issues, and errors</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Error / Exception Logs</p> <ul> <li>Capture uncaught exceptions and stack traces for debugging</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Performance / Metrics Logs</p> <ul> <li>Track latency, request count, memory/CPU usage</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Security Logs</p> <ul> <li>Authentication failures, permission issues, suspicious activity</li> </ul> <p>7\ufe0f\u20e3 Audit / Business Logs</p> <ul> <li>Important business events (e.g., user created, payment processed)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#16-how-do-you-secure-sensitive-data","title":"16. How do you secure sensitive data?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Encryption</p> <ul> <li>At rest: Encrypt databases or files (AES, TDE)</li> <li>In transit: Use TLS/HTTPS for network communication</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Hashing (for passwords)</p> <ul> <li>Use strong, salted hashes like bcrypt, Argon2</li> <li>Never store plain-text passwords</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Environment Variables / Secret Management</p> <ul> <li>Store API keys, DB credentials securely</li> <li>Use AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, or .env files</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Access Control / RBAC</p> <ul> <li>Restrict data access using roles and permissions</li> <li>Principle of least privilege</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Audit and Logging</p> <ul> <li>Log access attempts without exposing sensitive data</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Data Masking / Tokenization</p> <ul> <li>Mask sensitive fields in logs or responses</li> <li>Tokenize sensitive identifiers when needed</li> </ul> <p>7\ufe0f\u20e3 Regular Security Practices</p> <ul> <li>Keep dependencies updated, apply patches, and validate inputs</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#17-how-do-you-recover-from-a-server-crash","title":"17. How do you recover from a server crash?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Monitor &amp; Detect</p> <ul> <li>Use monitoring tools (Prometheus, Grafana, ELK) to detect crashes quickly</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Automatic Restart / Failover</p> <ul> <li>Use process managers like systemd, Supervisor, or container orchestration (Kubernetes, Docker Swarm) to auto-restart services</li> <li>Use load balancer to redirect traffic to healthy instances</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Check Logs &amp; Root Cause</p> <ul> <li>Analyze server logs, application logs, and error traces to identify cause</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Database &amp; Data Recovery</p> <ul> <li>Ensure database backups are available</li> <li>Restore from replicas or snapshots if needed</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Deploy Hotfix or Rollback</p> <ul> <li>Fix the issue and redeploy</li> <li>If necessary, rollback to the last stable version</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Prevent Future Crashes</p> <ul> <li>Implement alerts, auto-scaling, redundancy, and monitoring</li> <li>Conduct post-mortem analysis</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#18-how-will-you-preload-data-to-reduce-api-calls","title":"18. How will you preload data to reduce API calls?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Cache Frequently Used Data</p> <ul> <li>Use Redis / Memcached to store commonly accessed data</li> <li>Avoid repeated DB/API calls for the same data</li> </ul> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 Eager Loading / Preloading in ORM</p> <ul> <li>In Django: use <code>select_related</code> and <code>prefetch_related</code></li> <li>Fetch related objects in one query instead of multiple</li> </ul> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 Bulk Fetch / Batch Requests</p> <ul> <li>Fetch data in bulk before serving API requests</li> <li>Example: get all user profiles for a page in one query</li> </ul> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 Background Preloading</p> <ul> <li>Use Celery / scheduled tasks to preload data into cache periodically</li> </ul> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Front-End Preloading / CDN</p> <ul> <li>Serve static or semi-static data via CDN or prefetch in the frontend</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#19-what-is-the-n1-query-problem-how-did-you-fix-it-in-your-project","title":"19. What is the N+1 query problem? How did you fix it in your project?","text":""},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#what-is-the-n1-query-problem","title":"\u2705 What is the N+1 Query Problem?","text":"<p>N+1 problem happens when the app runs 1 query to get a list, and then N more queries to get related data for each item.</p> <p>So instead of 1 query, the database gets hit many times.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#example","title":"\u274c Example","text":"<pre><code>orders = Order.objects.all()\nfor order in orders:\n    print(order.user.name)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 If there are 10 orders \u2192 11 queries.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#how-i-fixed-it-in-my-project","title":"\u2705 How I Fixed It in My Project","text":"<p>\u201cI fixed it by fetching related data in advance using <code>select_related</code> or <code>prefetch_related</code>.\u201d</p> <pre><code>orders = Order.objects.select_related('user')\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#20-two-users-update-the-same-record-at-the-same-time-what-problems-occur-and-how-do-you-handle-it","title":"20. Two users update the same record at the same time. What problems occur and how do you handle it?","text":"<p>One user\u2019s change can overwrite the other user\u2019s change.</p> <p>So:</p> <ul> <li>Data becomes wrong</li> <li>You may lose updates</li> </ul> <p>This is called a race condition.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#how-do-we-handle-it","title":"\u2705 How do we handle it?","text":""},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#1-optimistic-locking-most-common","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Optimistic Locking (most common)","text":"<p>\u201cBefore updating, I check if someone else already changed the data.\u201d</p> <ul> <li>Use <code>version</code> or <code>updated_at</code></li> <li>If it changed \u2192 reject and ask user to retry</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udc4d Fast and safe.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#2-pessimistic-locking","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Pessimistic Locking","text":"<p>\u201cI lock the record so only one person can edit at a time.\u201d</p> <ul> <li>Others must wait</li> <li>Used for payments / inventory</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#very-short-interview-answer","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Very Short Interview Answer","text":"<p>\u201cWhen two users update the same record, one update can overwrite the other. I handle this using optimistic locking with a version check, and for critical cases, I use database locks.\u201d</p> <p>vGot you \ud83d\udc4d Here is the cleanest, easiest, no-noise version \u2014 something you can remember + say in interviews.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#21-how-to-take-database-backup","title":"21. How to take database Backup?","text":""},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#1-custom-db-in-docker-ec2","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Custom DB in Docker (EC2)","text":"<p>Steps:</p> <ol> <li>Take DB dump from container</li> <li>Save it on EC2 host</li> <li>Upload to S3</li> <li>Automate daily</li> </ol> <p>Example (MySQL):</p> <pre><code>docker exec mysql mysqldump -u root -p db &gt; backup.sql\naws s3 cp backup.sql s3://db-backup-bucket/\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Done</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#2-aws-rds-mysql-postgresql","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 AWS RDS (MySQL / PostgreSQL)","text":"<p>Steps:</p> <ol> <li>Enable Automated Backups</li> <li>Set retention (7\u201335 days)</li> <li>Take manual snapshot before releases</li> </ol> <p>\u2705 AWS handles backup &amp; restore</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#3-aws-dynamodb","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 AWS DynamoDB","text":"<p>Steps:</p> <ol> <li>Enable Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR)</li> <li>Take on-demand backup if needed</li> </ol> <p>\u2705 Fully managed, no scripts</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#one-line-interview-answer","title":"\ud83c\udfaf ONE-LINE INTERVIEW ANSWER","text":"<p>For custom databases, I take dumps and store them in S3 with automation. For AWS RDS and DynamoDB, I rely on managed backups like automated backups, snapshots, and point-in-time recovery.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#22-what-happend-when-ec2-instance-is-crash","title":"22. What happend when EC2 Instance is crash?","text":"<pre><code>User\n  \u2193\nApplication Load Balancer (ALB)\n  \u2193\nEC2 Instances (Auto Scaling Group)\n  \u2193\nEC2 Crash / Unhealthy\n  \u2193\nHealth Check Fails\n  \u2193\nAuto Scaling Terminates EC2\n  \u2193\nAuto Scaling Launches New EC2\n  \u2193\nApplication Starts\n  \u2193\nConnects to RDS &amp; S3\n  \u2193\nTraffic Continues (Minimal Downtime)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#why-no-data-loss","title":"Why no data loss?","text":"<ul> <li>Database is in RDS</li> <li>Files are in S3</li> <li>EC2 is stateless</li> <li>So EC2 can be replaced anytime</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/sceanio_based.html#one-line-explanation-to-speak-with-this-flow","title":"One-line explanation (to speak with this flow)","text":"<p>\u201cUser traffic goes to ALB, then to EC2 in an Auto Scaling Group. If an EC2 crashes, health checks fail, Auto Scaling replaces it, the app reconnects to RDS and S3, and traffic continues.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/cloud.html","title":"Cloud","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/database.html","title":"Database","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/database.html#1-what-is-composite-key","title":"1. What is Composite Key","text":"<p>Definition: A composite key is a key created using two or more columns to uniquely identify a record when a single column is not enough..</p> <p>It is used when one column is not enough to uniquely identify a record.</p> <p>\u2705 Easy Real-World Example:</p> <p>Think of students and courses:</p> <ul> <li>One student can enroll in many courses</li> <li>One course can have many students</li> </ul> <p>So, neither <code>student_id</code> nor <code>course_id</code> alone is unique.</p> <p>But together they are unique \u2705</p> <p>\u2705 SQL Example:</p> <pre><code>CREATE TABLE enrollment (\n    student_id INT,\n    course_id INT,\n    PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)\n);\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 Sample Data:</p> student_id course_id 1 101 1 102 2 101 <p>\u27a1\ufe0f Here, the combination of <code>student_id + course_id</code> is always unique.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/database.html#2-how-to-optimize-slow-sql-queries","title":"2. How to Optimize Slow SQL Queries?","text":"<ol> <li>Add indexes</li> <li>Avoid <code>SELECT *</code></li> <li>Use <code>LIMIT</code></li> <li>Normalize tables</li> <li>Avoid subqueries \u2192 use JOINs</li> <li>Use proper WHERE filters</li> <li>Check query with <code>EXPLAIN</code></li> </ol> <p>1. Add Indexes</p> <p>Indexes make searching faster. Without index \u2192 full table scan. With index \u2192 fast lookup.</p> <p>2. Avoid <code>SELECT *</code></p> <p>Only select required columns. Less data \u2192 faster query \u2192 less network load.</p> <p>3. Use <code>LIMIT</code></p> <p>Do not fetch thousands of rows if not needed. Use <code>LIMIT 50</code> or pagination. Reduces processing time.</p> <p>4. Normalize Tables</p> <p>Store data in proper related tables. Avoid repeating large data \u2192 smaller table \u2192 faster query.</p> <p>5. Avoid Subqueries \u2192 Use JOIN</p> <p>Subqueries often run multiple times. JOINs are optimized by SQL engine. Fewer scans \u2192 faster response.</p> <p>6. Use Proper WHERE Filters</p> <p>Filter early using indexed columns. SQL engine scans fewer rows.</p> <p>7. Use <code>EXPLAIN</code></p> <p>Shows how the query is executed. Helps find:</p> <ul> <li>missing indexes</li> <li>full scans</li> <li>inefficient joins</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/database.html#3-acid-properties","title":"3. ACID Properties","text":"<p>ACID ensures safe and reliable database transactions.</p> <ul> <li>Atomicity \u2192 All or nothing (no partial update)</li> <li>Consistency \u2192 Data rules must be followed</li> <li>Isolation \u2192 Transactions don\u2019t interfere with each other</li> <li>Durability \u2192 Committed data is permanently saved</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/database.html#one-line-interview-answer","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-line Interview Answer","text":"<p>ACID properties ensure that database transactions are reliable, consistent, isolated from each other, and permanently stored.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html","title":"Framework","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#1-does-uvicorn-use-multithreading-or-multiprocessing","title":"1. Does Uvicorn Use Multithreading or Multiprocessing?","text":"<p>By default</p> <ul> <li>Uvicorn runs as a single process</li> <li>It uses an async event loop, not threads</li> </ul> <p>To run multiple processes</p> <pre><code>uvicorn project.asgi:application --workers 4\n</code></pre> <p>This creates 4 worker processes.</p> <p>Simple meaning (for interview)</p> <p>More workers = more processes = more requests handled in parallel.</p> <p>\u2705 How Django Database Queries Work With Uvicorn</p> <p>Uvicorn itself is async, but Django ORM is synchronous.</p> Component How it runs Uvicorn Async (event loop, fast I/O) Django views Sync or Async Database queries Run in threads internally <p>\u2705 Interview One-Line Answers</p> <p>Q: Is Uvicorn multi-threaded? \ud83d\udc49 No. It is single-threaded and scales using multiple processes.</p> <p>Q: How many DB connections are used? \ud83d\udc49 One database connection per process (worker).</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#2-what-is-a-rest-api","title":"2. What Is a REST API?","text":"<p>A REST API is a way for applications to talk to each other using HTTP methods such as:</p> <ul> <li><code>GET</code> \u2192 Read data</li> <li><code>POST</code> \u2192 Create data</li> <li><code>PUT</code> \u2192 Update data</li> <li><code>DELETE</code> \u2192 Remove data</li> </ul> <p>Data is typically exchanged in JSON format.</p> <p>\u2705 Interview one-liner:</p> <p>A REST API allows different applications to communicate over HTTP using standard methods and JSON.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#3-how-do-you-optimize-slow-django-apis","title":"3. How do you optimize slow Django APIs?","text":"<p>How to Optimize Slow Django APIs</p> <ol> <li>Use <code>select_related</code> &amp; <code>prefetch_related</code></li> <li>Add database indexes</li> <li>Use Redis caching</li> <li>Use pagination</li> <li>Reduce number of queries</li> <li>Use asynchronous views</li> <li>Use <code>EXPLAIN()</code> to analyze slow queries</li> </ol> <p>1. <code>select_related</code> &amp; <code>prefetch_related</code></p> <p>Reduces N+1 queries. Fetch related data in fewer queries \u2192 faster API.</p> <p>2. Add Database Indexes</p> <p>Index columns used in filters. DB finds data faster \u2192 avoids full table scan.</p> <p>3. Use Redis Caching</p> <p>Store frequently used data in memory. Avoid hitting DB every time \u2192 instant response.</p> <p>4. Use Pagination</p> <p>Do not return 10,000 rows at once. Return data in chunks (page 1, page 2\u2026). Reduces load and speeds up API.</p> <p>5. Reduce Number of Queries</p> <p>Avoid loops making queries. Combine queries or use annotations.</p> <p>6. Use Asynchronous Views</p> <p>For I/O-bound tasks (API calls, file operations). Allows Django to handle more requests at once.</p> <p>7. Use <code>EXPLAIN()</code></p> <p>Check why a query is slow. Helps identify missing indexes or heavy joins.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#4-how-do-you-secure-a-rest-api","title":"4. How do you secure a REST API?","text":"<ol> <li>JWT/Auth Tokens</li> <li>HTTPS</li> <li>Rate Limiting</li> <li>Input Validation</li> <li>CORS</li> <li>Refresh Tokens</li> <li>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</li> </ol> <p>1. JWT / Auth Tokens</p> <p>Use JWT or OAuth tokens instead of sessions. Every request must include a valid token \u2192 prevents unauthorized access.</p> <p>2. HTTPS</p> <p>Encrypts all API traffic. Prevents attackers from reading or modifying data in transit.</p> <p>3. Rate Limiting</p> <p>Limits how many requests a user can make. Stops brute-force attacks and API abuse.</p> <p>4. Input Validation</p> <p>Validate all data coming from clients. Stops SQL injection, XSS, and malicious payloads.</p> <p>5. CORS</p> <p>Restricts which frontend domains can call your API. Blocks unauthorized websites from accessing API.</p> <p>6. Refresh Tokens</p> <p>Short-lived access tokens + long-lived refresh tokens. If access token is stolen, damage is limited.</p> <p>7. Role-Based Access Control</p> <p>Limit access based on user roles (admin, user, manager). Ensures only authorized people can perform sensitive operations.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#5-how-do-you-optimize-django-orm-queries","title":"5. How do you optimize Django ORM queries?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>select_related</code></p> <ul> <li>Fetch related objects in a single SQL JOIN</li> <li>Best for ForeignKey / OneToOneField</li> </ul> <pre><code>books = Book.objects.select_related('author').all()\n</code></pre> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>prefetch_related</code></p> <ul> <li>Fetch many-to-many or reverse FK relationships efficiently</li> <li>Reduces N+1 query problem</li> </ul> <pre><code>books = Book.objects.prefetch_related('tags').all()\n</code></pre> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>values</code> / <code>values_list</code></p> <ul> <li>Fetch only required fields instead of full model objects</li> <li>Reduces memory usage</li> </ul> <pre><code>Book.objects.values_list('title', 'author__name')\n</code></pre> <p>4\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>annotate</code> / <code>aggregate</code></p> <ul> <li>Perform database-side calculations</li> <li>Avoids extra Python processing</li> </ul> <pre><code>from django.db.models import Count\nauthors = Author.objects.annotate(book_count=Count('book'))\n</code></pre> <p>5\ufe0f\u20e3 Indexing</p> <ul> <li>Add database indexes on frequently queried fields</li> <li>Improves lookup and filter performance</li> </ul> <p>6\ufe0f\u20e3 Queryset caching</p> <ul> <li>Cache frequently used querysets in Redis / memcached</li> <li>Reduces repeated DB hits</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/framework.html#6-how-do-you-implement-authentication-in-djangofastapi","title":"6. How do you implement authentication in Django/FastAPI?","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Session-Based Authentication (Classic, Django default)</p> <ul> <li>Stores user session on the server and session ID in browser cookies</li> <li>Good for web apps with server-rendered pages</li> <li>Django Example:</li> </ul> <pre><code># settings.py\nMIDDLEWARE = ['django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware']\n# login\nfrom django.contrib.auth import authenticate, login\nuser = authenticate(username='user', password='pass')\nlogin(request, user)\n</code></pre> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 JWT (JSON Web Token) (Stateless, modern API)</p> <ul> <li>Token contains user info, sent with every request (Authorization header)</li> <li>Backend does not store session</li> <li>Suitable for REST APIs / mobile apps</li> <li>FastAPI Example:</li> </ul> <pre><code>from fastapi_jwt_auth import AuthJWT\ntoken = AuthJWT.create_access_token(subject=user.id)\n# send token in Authorization header\n</code></pre> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 OAuth2 / Third-Party Login</p> <ul> <li>Standard protocol for login via Google, GitHub, etc.</li> <li>Provides secure delegated access</li> <li>FastAPI Example:</li> </ul> <pre><code>from fastapi.security import OAuth2PasswordBearer\noauth2_scheme = OAuth2PasswordBearer(tokenUrl=\"token\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/python.html","title":"Python","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/system_design.html","title":"System Design","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/system_design.html#1-how-a-rate-limiter-works-simple-explanation","title":"1. How a Rate Limiter Works (Simple Explanation)","text":"<p>Step 1: Decide the Limit</p> <p>Define:</p> <ul> <li>Number of requests \u2192 e.g., 100 requests</li> <li>Time window \u2192 e.g., per 1 minute</li> </ul> <p>Step 2: Identify the Client</p> <p>Use a unique identifier:</p> <ul> <li>IP address</li> <li>User ID</li> <li>API key</li> </ul> <p>Example keys:</p> <pre><code>user:123\nip:192.168.1.1\n</code></pre> <p>Step 3: Store Request Count</p> <p>Use fast storage:</p> Storage type Use case In-memory Single server only Redis \u2705 Best for distributed systems <p>Redis structure:</p> <pre><code>key   = user:123\nvalue = request_count\nTTL   = time window\n</code></pre> <p>Step 4: Increment Per Request</p> <p>On every request:</p> <ol> <li>Increase the counter</li> <li>Check if it exceeds the defined limit</li> </ol> <p>If it exceeds: \u27a1\ufe0f Return HTTP 429 \u2013 Too Many Requests</p> <p>Step 5: Auto Reset</p> <p>Redis uses TTL (Time-To-Live) to automatically delete the key after the time window, so the counter resets automatically.</p> <p>\u2705 Perfect Interview Answer for Rate Limiter</p> <p>I implement rate limiting by defining a limit such as 100 requests per minute, identifying users by IP or user ID, storing counters in Redis, and incrementing them per request. If the limit is exceeded, I return HTTP 429. Redis TTL automatically resets the counter after the time window.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/system_design.html#2-how-do-you-scale-a-python-backend-system","title":"2. How do you scale a Python backend system?","text":"<ol> <li>Horizontal scaling with load balancer</li> <li>Caching (Redis)</li> <li>Queues (Celery / RabbitMQ)</li> <li>Database scaling (read/write replicas)</li> <li>Containerization + Kubernetes</li> <li>Pagination</li> </ol> <p>1. Horizontal Scaling With Load Balancer</p> <p>Add multiple backend servers and put a load balancer ina front. Distributes traffic \u2192 handles more users without overloading one server.</p> <p>2. Use Caching (Redis)</p> <p>Cache frequently requested API data or DB results. Reduces database load \u2192 improves speed \u2192 handles more traffic.</p> <p>3. Use Queues (Celery / RabbitMQ)</p> <p>Move heavy or long tasks (emails, reports, AI tasks) to background workers. Main API server stays fast and free to handle user requests.</p> <p>4. Split Database \u2192 Read/Write Replicas</p> <p>One DB for writes, multiple DBs for reads. Helps scale high read traffic easily.</p> <p>5. Use Docker + Kubernetes</p> <p>Run your app in containers. Kubernetes auto-scales pods based on CPU/memory \u2192 handles traffic spikes automatically.</p> <p>6. Use Pagination</p> <p>Never return huge datasets in one API call. Less data \u2192 less memory \u2192 faster API \u2192 better scalability.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Interviewer_%20Q%26A/system_design.html#3-explain-your-project-architecture","title":"3. Explain your project architecture.","text":"<p>Client \u2192 API Gateway \u2192 Django/FastAPI \u2192 Service Layer \u2192 Repository Layer \u2192 MySQL/Mongo Caching Layer: Redis Async Tasks: Celery + Redis Deployment: Docker \u2192 Nginx \u2192 Gunicorn/Uvicorn</p> <p>What major challenges did you solve recently?</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html","title":"Python","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#python-interview-qa-theory","title":"\u2705 PYTHON INTERVIEW Q\\&amp;A (THEORY)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#1-difference-between-list-and-dictionary","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1. Difference Between List and Dictionary","text":"<p>List: Ordered collection of items, indexed by integers. Dictionary: Unordered collection of key-value pairs.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#2-difference-between-append-and-extend","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2. Difference Between <code>append()</code> and <code>extend()</code>","text":"<ul> <li><code>append()</code>: Adds a single element to the end of a list.</li> <li><code>extend()</code>: Adds elements from another iterable to the end of the list.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#3-can-we-use-listtuple-as-dict-key","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3. Can We Use List/Tuple as Dict Key?","text":"<p>Only immutable types can be used as dictionary keys.</p> <ul> <li>\u2705 Tuples can be keys (if they don\u2019t contain mutable elements like lists).</li> <li>\u274c Lists can't be keys.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#4-why-python-is-dynamically-typed-duck-typing","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4. Why Python is Dynamically Typed? (Duck Typing)","text":"<ul> <li>No need to declare variable types explicitly.</li> <li>Variables take type based on the assigned value at runtime.</li> <li>\"If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck.\"</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#5-why-python-is-an-interpreted-language","title":"\ud83d\udd39 5. Why Python is an Interpreted Language?","text":"<ul> <li>Executes line-by-line using an interpreter.</li> <li>No separate compilation step.</li> <li>Errors stop execution immediately and show messages.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#6-what-is-lambda-function","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6. What is Lambda Function?","text":"<ul> <li>Anonymous function defined with <code>lambda</code> keyword.</li> <li>Syntax: <code>lambda arguments: expression</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#7-call-by-value-vs-call-by-reference-in-python","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7. Call by Value vs Call by Reference in Python","text":"<ul> <li>Python uses Call by Object Reference.</li> <li>Mutable types (like list) reflect changes, immutable types don\u2019t.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#8-what-is-mro-method-resolution-order","title":"\ud83d\udd39 8. What is MRO (Method Resolution Order)?","text":"<ul> <li>Used in multiple inheritance to determine method lookup order.</li> <li>Python uses C3 Linearization: Bottom to top, left to right.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#9-what-if-we-dont-use-with-statement-for-files","title":"\ud83d\udd39 9. What If We Don\u2019t Use <code>with</code> Statement for Files?","text":"<p>With statement handles:</p> <ul> <li>Automatic file closing via <code>__enter__()</code> and <code>__exit__()</code>.</li> <li>Without <code>with</code>, manual <code>close()</code> required.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#10-what-is-oops-in-python","title":"\ud83d\udd39 10. What is OOPS in Python?","text":"<p>Object-Oriented Programming System:</p> <ul> <li>Concepts: Class, Object, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Encapsulation, Abstraction</li> <li>Constructor: <code>__init__()</code> method auto-runs when object created.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#11-authentication-vs-authorization","title":"\ud83d\udd39 11. Authentication vs Authorization","text":"<ul> <li>Authentication: Verify identity (e.g., login)</li> <li>Authorization: Grant access (e.g., admin role)</li> <li>Tools: JWT, RBAC</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#12-how-django-migrations-work","title":"\ud83d\udd39 12. How Django Migrations Work?","text":"<ol> <li>Modify <code>models.py</code></li> <li>Run <code>makemigrations</code></li> <li>Review generated files</li> <li>Run <code>migrate</code> to apply</li> <li>Track with <code>showmigrations</code></li> <li>DB Schema Updated</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#13-django-orm-working","title":"\ud83d\udd39 13. Django ORM Working","text":"<ul> <li><code>Product.objects.all()</code> \u2192 Translates to <code>SELECT * FROM product</code></li> <li>Converts Python code to SQL behind the scenes</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#14-orm-vs-sql","title":"\ud83d\udd39 14. ORM vs SQL","text":"<p>ORM Pros:</p> <ul> <li>Easy syntax, database portability   ORM Cons:</li> <li>Slower for complex queries</li> </ul> <p>SQL Pros:</p> <ul> <li>Faster, full control   SQL Cons:</li> <li>Less portable, complex</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#15-type-of-django-orm","title":"\ud83d\udd39 15. Type of Django ORM","text":"<ul> <li>Uses Active Record Pattern</li> <li>DB rows map to Python objects</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#16-what-is-loading-in-sql","title":"\ud83d\udd39 16. What is Loading in SQL?","text":"<ul> <li>Loading = Inserting external data into tables</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#17-what-is-indexing-in-sql","title":"\ud83d\udd39 17. What is Indexing in SQL?","text":"<ul> <li>Speeds up data retrieval by indexing columns</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#18-jwt-token-what-why-how","title":"\ud83d\udd39 18. JWT Token: What, Why, How?","text":"<p>What: JSON Web Token for authentication Why: Stateless, secure, compact How it works:</p> <ol> <li>Header</li> <li>Payload</li> <li>Signature (encoded + signed)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#19-what-is-caching","title":"\ud83d\udd39 19. What is Caching?","text":"<ul> <li>Store data temporarily to avoid recalculation or re-fetching</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#20-httpresponse-vs-jsonresponse","title":"\ud83d\udd39 20. <code>HttpResponse</code> vs <code>JsonResponse</code>","text":"<ul> <li><code>HttpResponse</code>: Send HTML/Plain Text</li> <li><code>JsonResponse</code>: Send structured JSON data</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#21-what-is-middleware","title":"\ud83d\udd39 21. What is Middleware?","text":"<ul> <li>Middle layer between request &amp; response</li> <li> <p>Django Middleware Types:</p> </li> <li> <p><code>AuthenticationMiddleware</code></p> </li> <li><code>SessionMiddleware</code></li> <li><code>CsrfViewMiddleware</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#22-what-is-csrf-token","title":"\ud83d\udd39 22. What is CSRF Token?","text":"<p>CSRF = Cross Site Request Forgery</p> <ul> <li>Protects from unauthorized actions by embedding a token in forms</li> <li>Validated server-side</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#23-why-functions","title":"\ud83d\udd39 23. Why Functions?","text":"<ul> <li>Promote abstraction, reusability, and scalability</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#24-how-to-handle-db-schema-in-live-projects","title":"\ud83d\udd39 24. How to Handle DB Schema in Live Projects?","text":"<ol> <li>Use version control for schema (e.g., Flyway)</li> <li>Always take backups</li> <li>Test on staging before applying to production</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#25-code-first-vs-db-first","title":"\ud83d\udd39 25. Code First vs DB First","text":"<p>Code First:</p> <ul> <li>Write models \u2192 Generate DB via migrations</li> </ul> <p>DB First:</p> <ul> <li>Start with DB schema \u2192 Generate code (models)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#26-what-is-microservice","title":"\ud83d\udd39 26. What is Microservice?","text":"<p>[You can expand this section: A software design pattern...]</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#27-time-complexity-vs-space-complexity","title":"\ud83d\udd39 27. Time Complexity vs Space Complexity","text":"<ul> <li>Depends on project need</li> <li>Optimize for whichever is more critical</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#28-range-vs-xrange","title":"\ud83d\udd39 28. <code>range()</code> vs <code>xrange()</code>","text":"<p>range (Python 3): returns list xrange (Python 2): returns generator (lazy loading)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#29-py-vs-pyc","title":"\ud83d\udd39 29. <code>.py</code> vs <code>.pyc</code>","text":"<p>[You can expand: <code>.py</code> is source, <code>.pyc</code> is compiled bytecode]</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#30-python-interpreted-or-compiled","title":"\ud83d\udd39 30. Python: Interpreted or Compiled?","text":"<p>Python is interpreted; explain both types of execution environments.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#31-a-b-vs-a-is-b","title":"\ud83d\udd39 31. <code>a == b</code> vs <code>a is b</code>","text":"<ul> <li><code>==</code> checks value equality</li> <li><code>is</code> checks identity (same memory address)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#32-what-is-substring","title":"\ud83d\udd39 32. What is Substring?","text":"<ul> <li>A part of a string extracted using slicing</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#33-how-to-improve-api-response-speed","title":"\ud83d\udd39 33. How to Improve API Response Speed?","text":"<p>Add techniques: caching, async, DB indexing, pagination...</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#34-ist-to-utc-conversion","title":"\ud83d\udd39 34. IST to UTC Conversion","text":"<pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nimport pytz\n\nist = pytz.timezone('Asia/Kolkata')\nutc = pytz.utc\n\ndt = ist.localize(datetime.now())\nprint(\"UTC:\", dt.astimezone(utc))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/python.html#35-deep-copy-vs-shallow-copy","title":"35. Deep copy vs Shallow Copy","text":"<p>Shallow Copy</p> <ul> <li>Copies only the main object</li> <li>Nested objects are shared</li> <li>Change in nested data affects both</li> </ul> <pre><code>import copy\n\nmy_list = [1, 2, [3, 4], 5, 6]\n\nshallow_copy = copy.copy(my_list)\n\n# Change nested list\nshallow_copy[2][0] = 99\n\nprint(my_list)        # [1, 2, [99, 4], 5, 6]\nprint(shallow_copy)   # [1, 2, [99, 4], 5, 6]  \u2190 affected\n</code></pre> <p>Deep Copy</p> <ul> <li>Copies main object + nested objects</li> <li>Fully independent copy</li> <li>Changes do NOT affect each other</li> </ul> <pre><code>import copy\n\nmy_list = [1, 2, [3, 4], 5, 6]\n\ndeep_copy = copy.deepcopy(my_list)\n\n# Change nested list\ndeep_copy[2][0] = 99\n\nprint(my_list)      # [1, 2, [3, 4], 5, 6]\nprint(deep_copy)    # [1, 2, [99, 4], 5, 6]  \u2190 not affected\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html","title":"Sql","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#mysql-full-guide-beginner-to-intermediate","title":"MySQL Full Guide \u2013 Beginner to Intermediate","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#what-is-mysql","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is MySQL?","text":"<p>MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses Structured Query Language (SQL) to manage and manipulate data.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#what-is-a-database","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is a Database?","text":"<p>A database is an organized collection of structured data, generally stored and accessed electronically.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#what-is-dbms","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is DBMS?","text":"<p>DBMS (Database Management System) is software used to create and manage databases. It enables users to store, retrieve, and manipulate data efficiently.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#what-is-a-relational-database","title":"\ud83e\udde0 What is a Relational Database?","text":"<p>A Relational Database stores data in tables with rows and columns and defines relationships among them using keys.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#mysql-constraints","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 MySQL Constraints","text":"<ul> <li><code>NOT NULL</code></li> <li><code>UNIQUE</code></li> <li><code>DEFAULT</code></li> <li><code>CHECK</code></li> <li><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></li> <li><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#create-table","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Create Table","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE users (\n    id INT UNSIGNED,\n    name VARCHAR(100),\n    email VARCHAR(150),\n    password VARCHAR(100),\n    mobile VARCHAR(15),\n    gender ENUM('M', 'D', 'Y'),\n    dob DATE,\n    status BOOLEAN,\n    user_detail_id INT UNSIGNED,\n    FOREIGN KEY (user_detail_id) REFERENCES user_details(id)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE student (\n    id INT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    email VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    age TINYINT CHECK (age &gt;= 18),\n    status BOOLEAN DEFAULT 1\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#insert-data","title":"\ud83d\udce5 Insert Data","text":"<pre><code>INSERT INTO users (id, name, email, mobile, gender, dob, status)\nVALUES\n(2, 'Maitri', 'patelmaitri612000@gmail.com', '6353573222', 'F', '2000-01-09', 1),\n(3, 'Kanan', 'patelkanan@gmail.com', '8488861415', 'F', '2000-02-06', 1);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#select-data","title":"\ud83d\udce4 Select Data","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE gender = 'F';\nSELECT name AS Username, email, mobile FROM users WHERE gender = 'F';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#update-and-delete-data","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Update and Delete Data","text":"<pre><code>UPDATE student SET age = 25 WHERE id = 4;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE id = 4;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE id IN (4,5,6);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#sql-operators","title":"\ud83d\udd0e SQL Operators","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#in-not-in","title":"<code>IN</code> / <code>NOT IN</code>","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE gender IN ('M','F');\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE gender NOT IN ('M','F');\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#between-not-between","title":"<code>BETWEEN</code> / <code>NOT BETWEEN</code>","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM students WHERE age BETWEEN 10 AND 20;\nSELECT * FROM students WHERE age NOT BETWEEN 10 AND 20;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#like-operator","title":"\ud83d\udd0d LIKE Operator","text":"<ul> <li><code>%</code>: Zero or more characters</li> <li><code>_</code>: Single character</li> </ul> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE 'a%';  -- Starts with \"a\"\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE '%a';  -- Ends with \"a\"\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE '%a%'; -- Contains \"a\"\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE 'a%o'; -- Starts with \"a\" and ends with \"o\"\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#order-by-and-distinct","title":"\ud83d\udd22 ORDER BY and DISTINCT","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY name ASC;\nSELECT DISTINCT gender FROM users;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#null-and-not-null","title":"\u2753 NULL and NOT NULL","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE mobile IS NULL;\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE mobile IS NOT NULL;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#limit-and-offset","title":"\ud83d\udd22 LIMIT and OFFSET","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5;\nSELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5 OFFSET 1;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#aggregate-functions","title":"\ud83d\udd23 Aggregate Functions","text":"<pre><code>SELECT COUNT(name) FROM users;\nSELECT SUM(age) FROM student;\nSELECT AVG(age) FROM student;\nSELECT MIN(age) FROM student;\nSELECT MAX(age) FROM student;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#commit-and-rollback","title":"\ud83e\uddfe COMMIT and ROLLBACK","text":"<ul> <li><code>COMMIT</code>: Saves all changes made in the transaction.</li> <li><code>ROLLBACK</code>: Undoes all changes made in the current transaction.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#primary-and-foreign-key","title":"\ud83d\udd11 Primary and Foreign Key","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE student (\n    id INT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    city_id INT,\n    PRIMARY KEY (id),\n    FOREIGN KEY (city_id) REFERENCES city(cid)\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#joins","title":"\ud83d\udd17 Joins","text":"<ul> <li>INNER JOIN: Only matching rows</li> <li>LEFT JOIN: All from left + matches from right</li> <li>RIGHT JOIN: All from right + matches from left</li> <li>FULL JOIN: All rows from both</li> <li>SELF JOIN: Table joins with itself</li> </ul> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM students INNER JOIN city ON students.city_id = city.id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#truncate-delete-drop","title":"\u274c TRUNCATE, DELETE, DROP","text":"<pre><code>DELETE FROM Emp;\nTRUNCATE TABLE emp;\nDROP TABLE emp;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#sql-constraints","title":"\ud83d\udd12 SQL Constraints","text":"<ul> <li><code>NOT NULL</code></li> <li><code>CHECK</code></li> <li><code>DEFAULT</code></li> <li><code>INDEX</code></li> <li><code>UNIQUE</code></li> <li><code>PRIMARY</code></li> <li><code>FOREIGN</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#subsets-of-sql","title":"\ud83d\udcda Subsets of SQL","text":"<ul> <li>DDL: CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE</li> <li>DML: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</li> <li>DCL: GRANT, REVOKE</li> <li>TCL: COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#types-of-relationships","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Types of Relationships","text":"<ul> <li>One-to-One</li> <li>One-to-Many / Many-to-One</li> <li>Many-to-Many</li> <li>Self-referencing</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#rdbms-vs-dbms","title":"\ud83e\uddee RDBMS vs DBMS","text":"<ul> <li>RDBMS stores data in tables with relationships.</li> <li>DBMS does not enforce relationships.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#null-vs-0-vs-blank","title":"\ud83d\udd0d NULL vs 0 vs Blank","text":"<ul> <li><code>NULL</code>: Unknown / no value</li> <li><code>0</code>: Numeric value</li> <li><code>' '</code>: Blank string (valid string)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#normalization","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Normalization","text":"<p>Minimizes redundancy. Forms:</p> <ul> <li>1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF, etc.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#denormalization","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Denormalization","text":"<p>Adds redundancy for faster reads (opposite of normalization).</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#group-by-and-having","title":"\ud83e\uddee GROUP BY and HAVING","text":"<pre><code>SELECT customer_id, SUM(quantity) as total_quantity\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING SUM(quantity) &gt; 100;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#practice-with-multiple-tables","title":"\ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f Practice With Multiple Tables","text":"<p>Use JOINs, GROUP BY, subqueries, and filtering conditions across multiple tables to solve real-world queries.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#real-project-challenges","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Real Project Challenges","text":"<ul> <li>Explain big challenge faced in a project</li> <li>Draw implemented architecture (e.g. with Kafka)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Oral_Q_A/sql.html#why-jwt-token-is-not-secure","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Why JWT Token Is Not Secure?","text":"<ul> <li>It can be decoded (even if signed)</li> <li>If secret key is compromised, all tokens are exposed</li> <li>Long expiration tokens pose higher risks</li> <li>Doesn't support revocation (unless manually handled)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Difference Between Multiprocessing, Multithreading, and Async/Await","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#difference-between-multiprocessing-multithreading-and-asyncawait","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Difference Between Multiprocessing, Multithreading, and Async/Await","text":"Concept Runs on Best for Parallelism Type Real World Use Case Example Multiprocessing Multiple CPUs CPU-bound tasks (e.g., image processing) True Parallelism Video rendering, ML training Multithreading Single CPU I/O-bound tasks (e.g., file, network) Concurrency Web scraping, file downloads Async/Await Single CPU High I/O-bound, many tasks Cooperative Web APIs, chat apps, async scraping"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#1-multiprocessing","title":"\ud83e\udde0 1. MULTIPROCESSING","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#concept","title":"\ud83d\udd39Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Spawns separate processes, each with its own memory.</li> <li>Best for CPU-bound tasks (heavy computations).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#execution-flow","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f Execution Flow:","text":"<p>Each process runs independently and truly in parallel.</p> <pre><code>Main Process\n \u251c\u2500 Process 1: Task A (CPU-bound)\n \u251c\u2500 Process 2: Task B (CPU-bound)\n \u2514\u2500 Process 3: Task C (CPU-bound)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#python-example","title":"\ud83d\udc0d Python Example:","text":"<pre><code>import multiprocessing\nimport time\n\ndef heavy_task(name):\n    print(f\"{name} started\")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(f\"{name} finished\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    processes = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        p = multiprocessing.Process(target=heavy_task, args=(f\"Process {i+1}\",))\n        p.start()\n        processes.append(p)\n\n    for p in processes:\n        p.join()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#real-world-use-case","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Use Case:","text":"<ul> <li>Video Encoding: Each core can encode one part of a video file.</li> <li>Machine Learning: Train different models in parallel.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#2-multithreading","title":"\ud83e\uddf5 2. MULTITHREADING","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#concept_1","title":"\ud83d\udd39Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Multiple threads within the same process.</li> <li>Shares memory space.</li> <li>Ideal for I/O-bound tasks (e.g., reading files, making HTTP calls).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#execution-flow_1","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f Execution Flow:","text":"<p>Threads are concurrent, but not parallel due to GIL (Global Interpreter Lock in CPython).</p> <pre><code>Main Thread\n \u251c\u2500 Thread 1: Download file A (waits on I/O)\n \u251c\u2500 Thread 2: Download file B (waits on I/O)\n \u2514\u2500 Thread 3: Download file C (waits on I/O)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#python-example_1","title":"\ud83d\udc0d Python Example:","text":"<pre><code>import threading\nimport time\n\ndef download_file(name):\n    print(f\"{name} started\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # Simulates download\n    print(f\"{name} finished\")\n\nthreads = []\nfor i in range(3):\n    t = threading.Thread(target=download_file, args=(f\"Thread {i+1}\",))\n    t.start()\n    threads.append(t)\n\nfor t in threads:\n    t.join()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#real-world-use-case_1","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Use Case:","text":"<ul> <li>Web scraping multiple pages at once.</li> <li>Parallel file downloads in a browser.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#3-async-await-asynchronous-programming","title":"\u26a1 3. ASYNC-AWAIT (Asynchronous Programming)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#concept_2","title":"\ud83d\udd39Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Uses single-threaded event loop.</li> <li>Tasks give control back to the loop when waiting (non-blocking).</li> <li>Great for thousands of I/O-bound tasks.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#execution-flow_2","title":"\u25b6\ufe0f Execution Flow:","text":"<p>All tasks run in a non-blocking manner within a single thread.</p> <pre><code>Event Loop\n \u251c\u2500 Task A: Await response \u2192 gives control back\n \u251c\u2500 Task B: Await response \u2192 gives control back\n \u2514\u2500 Task C: Await response \u2192 gives control back\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#python-example_2","title":"\ud83d\udc0d Python Example:","text":"<pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch_data(name):\n    print(f\"{name} started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)\n    print(f\"{name} finished\")\n\nasync def main():\n    tasks = [fetch_data(f\"Task {i+1}\") for i in range(3)]\n    await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#real-world-use-case_2","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-World Use Case:","text":"<ul> <li>Async API calls in modern web frameworks (FastAPI, aiohttp).</li> <li>Chat apps, real-time dashboards, stock market feeds.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/parallel_programming.html#summary","title":"\u2705 Summary","text":"Feature Multiprocessing Multithreading Async/Await Best for CPU-bound tasks I/O-bound tasks Many I/O tasks Uses Multiple processes Threads in 1 process Event loop Parallelism True Pseudo (GIL limited) Cooperative Memory Separate Shared Shared (single loop) Example Use Video encoding File download Web API calls"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html","title":"Interview Checklist","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#1-strings","title":"1. Strings","text":"Exercise Input Output Reverse string <code>\"Python\"</code> <code>\"nohtyP\"</code> Palindrome check <code>\"level\"</code> <code>True</code> Count vowels &amp; consonants <code>\"Interview\"</code> <code>Vowels:4, Consonants:5</code> Remove duplicates <code>\"programming\"</code> <code>\"progamin\"</code> Most frequent character <code>\"mississippi\"</code> <code>'i' appears 4 times</code> Extract numbers <code>\"abc123xyz\"</code> <code>\"123\"</code> String formatting <code>\"John\"</code>, <code>30</code> <code>\"Name: John, Age:30\"</code> Replace substring <code>\"I like Java\"</code> \u2192 <code>\"Python\"</code> <code>\"I like Python\"</code> Strip whitespaces <code>\"  hello  \"</code> <code>\"hello\"</code> Convert cases <code>\"Python\"</code> <code>\"PYTHON\", \"python\"</code> Count words <code>\"This is a test\"</code> <code>4</code> Find substring index <code>\"Interview\"</code> \u2192 <code>\"view\"</code> <code>2</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#2-lists-tuples","title":"2. Lists &amp; Tuples","text":"Exercise Input Output Remove duplicates <code>[1,2,2,3,4,4,5]</code> <code>[1,2,3,4,5]</code> Sort list of tuples by 2nd element <code>[(1,3),(2,1),(4,2)]</code> <code>[(2,1),(4,2),(1,3)]</code> Flatten nested list <code>[1,[2,3],[4,[5,6]]]</code> <code>[1,2,3,4,5,6]</code> List comprehension even numbers <code>[1,2,3,4,5,6]</code> <code>[2,4,6]</code> Sum elements <code>[1,2,3,4,5]</code> <code>15</code> Max &amp; min <code>[10,20,5,40]</code> <code>Max:40, Min:5</code> Concatenate lists <code>[1,2] + [3,4]</code> <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> Tuple unpacking &amp; swap <code>a=5,b=10</code> <code>a=10,b=5</code> Reverse list <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> <code>[4,3,2,1]</code> Count occurrence <code>[1,2,2,3,2]</code> \u2192 <code>2</code> <code>3</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#3-dictionaries","title":"3. Dictionaries","text":"Exercise Input Output Count frequency in list <code>['a','b','a','c','b','a']</code> <code>{'a':3,'b':2,'c':1}</code> Merge dictionaries <code>{'a':1,'b':2}, {'b':3,'c':4}</code> <code>{'a':1,'b':3,'c':4}</code> Key with max value <code>{'a':10,'b':25,'c':15}</code> <code>'b'</code> Nested dictionary access <code>{'a':{'b':2}}</code> <code>2</code> Dictionary comprehension squares <code>[1,2,3]</code> <code>{1:1,2:4,3:9}</code> Swap keys &amp; values <code>{'x':1,'y':2}</code> <code>{1:'x',2:'y'}</code> Sort dictionary by value <code>{'a':2,'b':1,'c':3}</code> <code>{'b':1,'a':2,'c':3}</code> Get keys as list <code>{'a':1,'b':2}</code> <code>['a','b']</code> Merge &amp; sum values if duplicate <code>{'a':1}, {'a':3,'b':2}</code> <code>{'a':4,'b':2}</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#4-sets","title":"4. Sets","text":"Exercise Input Output Union &amp; Intersection <code>A={1,2,3},B={2,3,4}</code> <code>Union={1,2,3,4}, Intersection={2,3}</code> Symmetric difference <code>A={1,2,3},B={3,4,5}</code> <code>{1,2,4,5}</code> Remove duplicates from list <code>[1,2,2,3,3,3]</code> <code>{1,2,3}</code> Subset / superset <code>A={1,2},B={1,2,3}</code> <code>True</code> Add &amp; remove element <code>A={1,2}</code> After add 3 \u2192 <code>{1,2,3}</code>, remove 2 \u2192 <code>{1,3}</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#5-functions-lambda","title":"5. Functions &amp; Lambda","text":"Exercise Input Output Factorial recursive <code>5</code> <code>120</code> Fibonacci series <code>7</code> <code>[0,1,1,2,3,5,8]</code> Prime check <code>17</code> <code>True</code> Lambda filter even <code>[1,2,3,4,5,6]</code> <code>[2,4,6]</code> Map square <code>[1,2,3]</code> <code>[1,4,9]</code> Reduce sum <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> <code>10</code> Decorator logger <code>function print(\"Hi\")</code> <code>Function executed: print_hi</code> Recursive sum of list <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> <code>10</code> Palindrome function <code>\"racecar\"</code> <code>True</code> Function with args &amp; *kwargs <code>sum_all(1,2,3,x=4,y=5)</code> <code>Sum=15</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#6-file-handling-json","title":"6. File Handling &amp; JSON","text":"Exercise Input Output Read text file <code>\"sample.txt\"</code> <code>Contents of file</code> Write text file <code>\"Hello World\"</code> File contains <code>\"Hello World\"</code> Append file <code>\"New Line\"</code> File updated with <code>\"New Line\"</code> Count lines <code>\"sample.txt\"</code> <code>Lines: 10</code> Read JSON <code>'{\"name\":\"John\"}'</code> <code>{'name':'John'}</code> Write JSON <code>{'x':1}</code> File contains <code>{\"x\":1}</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#7-object-oriented-programming-oop","title":"7. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)","text":"Exercise Input Output Create class &amp; object <code>Person(\"John\",30)</code> <code>Person: John, Age:30</code> Magic method str <code>print(Person)</code> <code>\"Person: John, Age:30\"</code> Inheritance <code>class Student(Person)</code> <code>Student object inherits Person</code> Class &amp; static methods <code>Person.info()</code> <code>\"This is Person class\"</code> Property decorator <code>person.age</code> <code>30</code> Operator overloading <code>p1+p2</code> <code>Sum of ages / combined object</code> Encapsulation <code>_salary=5000</code> Access via getter/setter"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#8-iterators-generators","title":"8. Iterators &amp; Generators","text":"Exercise Input Output Iterator example <code>iter([1,2,3])</code> <code>1,2,3</code> Generator Fibonacci <code>n=5</code> <code>[0,1,1,2,3]</code> Infinite generator <code>start=0</code> <code>0,1,2,...</code> Generator expression <code>[x*x for x in range(5)]</code> <code>[0,1,4,9,16]</code> Yield squares <code>5</code> <code>0,1,4,9,16</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#9-error-handling-logging","title":"9. Error Handling &amp; Logging","text":"Exercise Input Output Try/Except <code>int(\"abc\")</code> <code>ValueError handled</code> Multiple exceptions <code>int(\"a\") / 0</code> <code>ValueError &amp; ZeroDivisionError handled</code> Custom exception <code>raise MyError()</code> <code>\"Custom Exception Raised\"</code> Assertion <code>x=5, assert x&gt;10</code> <code>AssertionError</code> Logging info <code>logging.info(\"Test\")</code> <code>\"INFO: Test\"</code> Context manager <code>with open(\"f\") as f:</code> <code>File auto-closed</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#10-modules-misc","title":"10. Modules &amp; Misc","text":"Exercise Input Output Random choice <code>[1,2,3]</code> <code>1 or 2 or 3</code> Date difference <code>'2026-01-01','2026-02-01'</code> <code>31 days</code> Collections Counter <code>[1,2,2,3,3,3]</code> <code>Counter({3:3,2:2,1:1})</code> itertools combinations <code>[1,2,3],2</code> <code>[(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)]</code> Threading example <code>function print(\"Hi\")</code> <code>\"Hi\"</code> in separate thread Timeit performance <code>sum(range(100))</code> Execution time in seconds"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Daily_Interview_Checklist.html#most-asked-practical-python-problems-interview-focused","title":"\ud83d\udd25 Most Asked Practical Python Problems (Interview-Focused)","text":"Problem Input Expected Output Move all zeros to end <code>[0,1,0,3,12]</code> <code>[1,3,12,0,0]</code> Two-pointer: Two sum sorted array <code>[1,2,3,4,6], target=6</code> <code>[1,3]</code> (indices or values adding to target) Reverse words in string <code>\"the sky is blue\"</code> <code>\"blue is sky the\"</code> Check anagram <code>\"listen\", \"silent\"</code> <code>True</code> Longest substring without repeating chars <code>\"abcabcbb\"</code> <code>3</code> (<code>\"abc\"</code>) Maximum sum subarray (Kadane) <code>[-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]</code> <code>6</code> (<code>[4,-1,2,1]</code>) Merge two sorted lists <code>[1,3,5], [2,4,6]</code> <code>[1,2,3,4,5,6]</code> Remove duplicates from sorted list <code>[1,1,2,2,3]</code> <code>[1,2,3]</code> Rotate array by k positions <code>[1,2,3,4,5], k=2</code> <code>[4,5,1,2,3]</code> Check palindrome number <code>121</code> <code>True</code> Find first non-repeating char <code>\"swiss\"</code> <code>'w'</code> Two-pointer: container with most water <code>[1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7]</code> <code>49</code> Intersection of two arrays <code>[1,2,2,1], [2,2]</code> <code>[2,2]</code> Sliding window max sum of size k <code>[1,3,2,5,1], k=3</code> <code>Max sum=10</code> (<code>[3,2,5]</code>) Square sorted array <code>[-4,-1,0,3,10]</code> <code>[0,1,9,16,100]</code> Product of array except self <code>[1,2,3,4]</code> <code>[24,12,8,6]</code> Valid parentheses <code>\"({[]})\"</code> <code>True</code> Merge intervals <code>[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10]]</code> <code>[[1,6],[8,10]]</code> Top k frequent elements <code>[1,1,1,2,2,3], k=2</code> <code>[1,2]</code> String to integer (atoi) <code>\"  -42\"</code> <code>-42</code> Longest common prefix <code>[\"flower\",\"flow\",\"flight\"]</code> <code>\"fl\"</code> Subarray with given sum <code>[1,2,3,7,5], sum=12</code> <code>[2,3,7]</code> Rotate matrix 90\u00b0 <code>[[1,2],[3,4]]</code> <code>[[3,1],[4,2]]</code> Spiral matrix traversal <code>[[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]</code> <code>[1,2,3,6,9,8,7,4,5]</code> Valid sudoku check <code>9x9 board</code> <code>True/False</code> Reverse linked list <code>1\u21922\u21923\u2192None</code> <code>3\u21922\u21921\u2192None</code> Detect cycle in linked list <code>1\u21922\u21923\u21922</code> <code>True</code> Binary search <code>[1,2,3,4,5], target=3</code> <code>2</code> (index) Find missing number in array <code>[3,0,1]</code> <code>2</code> Find duplicates <code>[1,3,4,2,2]</code> <code>[2]</code> Minimum window substring <code>\"ADOBECODEBANC\", \"ABC\"</code> <code>\"BANC\"</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/Dict/methods.html","title":"Methods","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>clear()</code> Removes all items from the dictionary. <code>d.clear()</code> \u2192 <code>{}</code> <code>copy()</code> Returns a shallow copy of the dictionary. <code>d2 = d.copy()</code> \u2192 <code>{'a': 1, 'b': 2}</code> <code>fromkeys()</code> Creates a new dictionary with keys from a sequence and a set value. <code>dict.fromkeys(['x', 'y'], 0)</code> \u2192 <code>{'x': 0, 'y': 0}</code> <code>get()</code> Returns the value for the specified key if key is in dictionary. <code>d.get('a')</code> \u2192 <code>1</code> <code>items()</code> Returns a view object of key-value pairs. <code>list(d.items())</code> \u2192 <code>[('a', 1), ('b', 2)]</code> <code>keys()</code> Returns a view object of keys. <code>list(d.keys())</code> \u2192 <code>['a', 'b']</code> <code>values()</code> Returns a view object of values. <code>list(d.values())</code> \u2192 <code>[1, 2]</code> <code>pop()</code> Removes specified key and returns the corresponding value. <code>d.pop('b')</code> \u2192 removes <code>'b'</code> and returns its value <code>popitem()</code> Removes and returns the last inserted key-value pair (in Python 3.7+). <code>d.popitem()</code> \u2192 <code>('b', 2)</code> <code>setdefault()</code> Returns the value of a key if it exists. If not, inserts the key with a value. <code>d.setdefault('c', 99)</code> \u2192 adds <code>'c': 99</code> if missing <code>update()</code> Updates dictionary with key-value pairs from another dictionary or iterable. <code>d.update({'d': 4})</code> \u2192 adds/updates <code>'d': 4</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html","title":"Practical","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#python-list-practicals-for-interview","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python List Practicals for Interview","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#practical-list-overview","title":"\ud83d\udccb Practical List Overview","text":"<ol> <li>Modify List by Inserting/Removing Elements</li> <li>Add Elements to a List at Specific Indices</li> <li>Pythonic Element Exclusion \u2013 List Comprehension vs Iterative Removal</li> <li>Swap Two Elements Using <code>enumerate</code></li> <li>Extract Every 3rd Element from a List</li> <li>Get Sum of Even and Odd Numbers in a List</li> <li>Find the Smallest Positive Number in a List</li> <li>Find Maximum and Minimum Values in a List</li> <li>Remove Duplicate Values from a List</li> <li>Remove Duplicates Using List Comprehension</li> <li>Find Non-Repetitive Elements (Without Using <code>count</code>)</li> <li>List Element Rotation Program (By Index)</li> <li>List Comprehension Examples</li> <li>Flatten a Nested List (Two Approaches)</li> <li>Sort List Without Using <code>sort()</code> (Quicksort)</li> <li>Sort Elements in List Using Decorator</li> <li>Generate Fibonacci Series Using List</li> <li>Implement Binary Search Algorithm</li> <li>Seprate 3 list based on proper index vise 20.Find missing number from a list</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#1-modify-list-by-insertingremoving-elements","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Modify List by Inserting/Removing Elements","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nmy_list.insert(2, 10)\nmy_list.remove(3)\ndel my_list[0]\nprint(\"List after modifications:\", my_list)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#2-add-elements-to-a-list-at-specific-indices","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Add Elements to a List at Specific Indices","text":"<pre><code>original_list = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nelements_to_add = [15, 25]\nindices_to_add = [2, 4]\n\nfor index, element in zip(indices_to_add, elements_to_add):\n    original_list.insert(index, element)\n\nprint(\"List after adding elements:\", original_list)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#3-pythonic-element-exclusion-list-comprehension-vs-iterative-removal","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Pythonic Element Exclusion \u2013 List Comprehension vs Iterative Removal","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [32, 4, 12, 5, 7, 6, 8, 9]\nnew_list = [4, 6]\n\n# Using list comprehension\nresult = [value for value in my_list if value not in new_list]\nprint(\"Result using List Comprehension:\", result)\n\n# Iterative removal\nfor i in new_list:\n    my_list.remove(i)\n\nprint(\"Result after Iterative Removal:\", my_list)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#4-swap-two-elements-using-enumerate","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Swap Two Elements Using <code>enumerate</code>","text":"<pre><code>def swapvalue(lst, pos1, pos2):\n    for i, x in enumerate(lst):\n        if i == pos1:\n            element1 = x\n        if i == pos2:\n            element2 = x\n    lst[pos1] = element2\n    lst[pos2] = element1\n    return lst\n\nmy_list = [61, 24, 12, 16]\nprint(swapvalue(my_list, 0, 1))  # Swap first and second element\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#5-extract-every-3rd-element-from-a-list","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Extract Every 3rd Element from a List","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [1, 2, 54, 22, 44, 5, 13, 23, 3]\nresult = [val for i, val in enumerate(my_list, start=1) if i % 3 == 0]\nprint(\"Every 3rd element:\", result)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#6-get-sum-of-even-and-odd-numbers-in-a-list","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Get Sum of Even and Odd Numbers in a List","text":"<pre><code>def odd_even_sum(lst):\n    even_sum = sum(i for i in lst if i % 2 == 0)\n    odd_sum = sum(i for i in lst if i % 2 != 0)\n    return f\"Even Sum: {even_sum}\", f\"Odd Sum: {odd_sum}\"\n\noriginal_list = [10, 19, 30, 40, 53, 60, 70, 80]\neven, odd = odd_even_sum(original_list)\nprint(even, odd)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#7-find-the-smallest-positive-number-in-a-list","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Find the Smallest Positive Number in a List","text":"<pre><code>a = [2, 3, 4, 1, 32, 31]\nsmall = None\nfor num in a:\n    if num &gt; 0 and (small is None or num &lt; small):\n        small = num\nprint(\"Smallest positive number:\", small)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#8-find-maximum-and-minimum-values-in-a-list","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Find Maximum and Minimum Values in a List","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [1, 2, 4, 12, 45, 32, 4, 36, 6]\nmaximum = minimum = my_list[0]\nfor i in my_list:\n    if i &gt; maximum:\n        maximum = i\n    if i &lt; minimum:\n        minimum = i\nprint(f\"Max: {maximum}, Min: {minimum}\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#9-remove-duplicate-values-from-a-list","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Remove Duplicate Values from a List","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [1, 2, 4, 3, 5, 6, 2, 9, 9, 4, 2, 5, 7, 8]\nnew_list = []\nfor i in my_list:\n    if i not in new_list:\n        new_list.append(i)\nprint(\"List without duplicates:\", new_list)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#remove-duplicates-using-list-comprehension","title":"\ud83d\udd1f Remove Duplicates Using List Comprehension","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [2, 3, 36, 6, 3, 6, 8, 9, 8, 5, 9, 6, 9, 3]\nnew_list = []\n[new_list.append(x) for x in my_list if x not in new_list]\nprint(\"Clean list:\", new_list)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#11-find-non-repetitive-elements-without-using-count","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 Find Non-Repetitive Elements (Without Using <code>count</code>)","text":"<pre><code>def find_unique(lst):\n    count = {}\n    for i in lst:\n        count[i] = count.get(i, 0) + 1\n    return [k for k, v in count.items() if v == 1]\n\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8]\nprint(\"Non-repetitive elements:\", find_unique(my_list))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#12-list-element-rotation-program-by-index","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 List Element Rotation Program (By Index)","text":"<pre><code>def rotate_list(lst, n):\n    return lst[-n:] + lst[:-n]\n\nmy_list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nprint(rotate_list(my_list, 2))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#13-list-comprehension-examples","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 List Comprehension Examples","text":"<pre><code>my_list = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]\nprint([x**2 for x in my_list])           # Squares\nprint([x for x in my_list if x % 2 == 0])  # Even\nprint([x for x in my_list if x % 2 != 0])  # Odd\nprint([x for x in my_list if x % 3 == 0])  # Div by 3\n\nnested = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\nprint([x for sub in nested for x in sub])\n\nwords = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\nprint([w[0] for w in words])  # First letters\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#14-flatten-a-nested-list-two-approaches","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 Flatten a Nested List (Two Approaches)","text":"<pre><code># Manual flatten\ndef manual_flatten(lst):\n    flat = []\n    for i in lst:\n        if isinstance(i, list):\n            for j in i:\n                if isinstance(j, list):\n                    flat.extend(j)\n                else:\n                    flat.append(j)\n        else:\n            flat.append(i)\n    return flat\n\n# Recursive flatten\ndef flatten_recursive(lst):\n    flat = []\n    for i in lst:\n        if isinstance(i, list):\n            flat.extend(flatten_recursive(i))\n        else:\n            flat.append(i)\n    return flat\n\nmy_list = [10, 20, [30, 40, [50, 60, 70], 80], 50, 60, [12, 13], 70]\nprint(manual_flatten(my_list))\nprint(flatten_recursive(my_list))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#15-sort-list-without-using-sort-quicksort","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 Sort List Without Using <code>sort()</code> (Quicksort)","text":"<pre><code>def quicksort(arr):\n    if len(arr) &lt;= 1:\n        return arr\n    pivot = arr[len(arr)//2]\n    left = [x for x in arr if x &lt; pivot]\n    middle = [x for x in arr if x == pivot]\n    right = [x for x in arr if x &gt; pivot]\n    return quicksort(left) + middle + quicksort(right)\n\nunsorted = [3, 6, 8, 10, 1, 2, 1]\nprint(\"Sorted:\", quicksort(unsorted))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#16-sort-elements-in-list-using-decorator","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e36\ufe0f\u20e3 Sort Elements in List Using Decorator","text":"<pre><code>def sort_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        return sorted(result) if isinstance(result, list) else result\n    return wrapper\n\n@sort_decorator\ndef get_numbers(lst):\n    return lst\n\nprint(get_numbers([3, 2, 4, 6, 3, 56, 7, 8, 5, 3]))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#17-generate-fibonacci-series-using-list","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e37\ufe0f\u20e3 Generate Fibonacci Series Using List","text":"<pre><code>def fibbo(n):\n    series = [0, 1]\n    for _ in range(2, n):\n        series.append(series[-1] + series[-2])\n    return series\n\nprint(\"Fibonacci Series:\", fibbo(5))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#18-implement-binary-search-algorithm","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e38\ufe0f\u20e3 Implement Binary Search Algorithm","text":"<pre><code>def binary_search(arr, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n    while left &lt;= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] &lt; target:\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n    return -1\n\nsorted_list = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]\nprint(\"Target found at index:\", binary_search(sorted_list, 7))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#19-seprate-3-list-based-on-proper-index-vise","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e39 Seprate 3 list based on proper index vise","text":"<p>my_list = [[1,2,3],[4,5,6]] new_list = [] for i in zip(*my_list):     new_list.append(list(i)) print(new_list) # [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/list_practical.html#20-find-missing-number-from-a-list","title":"20. Find missing number from a list","text":"<p>lst = [1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 10] new_list = [] for i in rang e(1,max(lst)+1):     if i not in lst:         new_list.append(i) print(new_list)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/methods.html","title":"Methods","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/methods.html#python-list-methods-reference-practice","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python List Methods Reference &amp; Practice","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/methods.html#common-list-methods","title":"\ud83d\udccb Common List Methods","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>append()</code> Adds a single item to the end of the list. <code>lst.append(4)</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4]</code> <code>extend()</code> Adds all elements of an iterable to the end. <code>lst.extend([5, 6])</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code> <code>insert()</code> Inserts an item at a specific index. <code>lst.insert(1, 10)</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 10, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code> <code>remove()</code> Removes the first matching element. <code>lst.remove(10)</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code> <code>pop()</code> Removes and returns the item at the given index (default last). <code>lst.pop()</code> \u2192 removes <code>6</code>, list becomes <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5]</code> <code>index()</code> Returns the index of the first matching value. <code>lst.index(4)</code> \u2192 <code>3</code> <code>count()</code> Returns the count of the value. <code>lst.count(2)</code> \u2192 <code>1</code> <code>sort()</code> Sorts the list in ascending order (in-place). <code>lst.sort()</code> \u2192 <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> <code>reverse()</code> Reverses the list in-place. <code>lst.reverse()</code> \u2192 <code>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code> <code>copy()</code> Returns a shallow copy of the list. <code>copy_lst = lst.copy()</code> \u2192 <code>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code> <code>clear()</code> Removes all items from the list. <code>lst.clear()</code> \u2192 <code>[]</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/methods.html#python-list-method-practice-guess-the-method","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Python List Method Practice (Guess the Method)","text":"<p>Try to guess which method solves each task below!</p> <ol> <li> <p>Add the number <code>4</code> to the end of the list <code>[3, 1, 2]</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>append()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Add the elements <code>[5, 6]</code> to <code>[3, 1, 2, 4]</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>extend()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Insert <code>10</code> at index <code>1</code> in <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 10, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>insert()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Remove <code>10</code> from <code>[3, 10, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code> \u2192 <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>remove()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Remove and return the last element from <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>pop()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Get the index of element <code>4</code> in <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>index()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Count how many times <code>2</code> appears in <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>count()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Sort <code>[3, 1, 2, 4, 5]</code> in ascending order \u2192 <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>sort()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Reverse <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> \u2192 <code>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code>    \u27a4 Method: <code>reverse()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Make a shallow copy of <code>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code>     \u27a4 Method: <code>copy()</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Clear all elements from <code>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code>     \u27a4 Method: <code>clear()</code></p> </li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/List/methods.html#12-remove-the-prefix-un-from-the-string-unhappy-happy","title":"12. Remove the prefix <code>\"un\"</code> from the string <code>\"unhappy\"</code> \u2192 <code>\"happy\"</code>","text":"<p>\u27a4 Method: <code>removeprefix()</code> (Python 3.9+)</p> <pre><code>print(\"unhappy\".removeprefix(\"un\"))  # Output: 'happy'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html","title":"Methods","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#python-string-methods-cheat-sheet","title":"\ud83e\uddf5 Python String Methods Cheat Sheet","text":"<p>A practical guide to Python\u2019s built-in string methods with examples and outputs.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#modify-and-clean","title":"\ud83e\uddfc Modify and Clean","text":"<pre><code>s = \"  Hello, World!  \"\n\nprint(s.strip())                   # 'Hello, World!'\nprint(s.lstrip())                  # 'Hello, World!  '\nprint(s.rstrip())                  # '  Hello, World!'\nprint(s.replace(\"World\", \"Python\"))  # '  Hello, Python!  '\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#change-case","title":"\ud83d\udd20 Change Case","text":"<pre><code>print(s.lower())           # '  hello, world!  '\nprint(s.upper())           # '  HELLO, WORLD!  '\nprint(s.capitalize())      # '  hello, world!  '\nprint(s.title())           # '  Hello, World!  '\nprint(s.swapcase())        # '  hELLO, wORLD!  '\n\n# Advanced case folding (useful for comparisons)\nprint(\"Stra\u00dfe\".casefold()) # 'strasse'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#find-and-search","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Find and Search","text":"<pre><code>print(s.find(\"o\"))          # 6\nprint(s.rfind(\"o\"))         # 9\nprint(s.index(\"W\"))         # 8\nprint(s.rindex(\"l\"))        # 10\nprint(s.count(\"l\"))         # 3\n\nprint(s.startswith(\"  He\"))  # True\nprint(s.endswith(\"!  \"))     # True\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#check-string-types","title":"\u2705 Check String Types","text":"<pre><code>print(\"Hello123\".isalnum())     # True\nprint(\"Hello\".isalpha())        # True\nprint(\"123\".isdigit())          # True\nprint(\"\u2163\".isnumeric())          # True\nprint(\"123\".isdecimal())        # True\n\nprint(\"hello\".islower())        # True\nprint(\"HELLO\".isupper())        # True\nprint(\"Hello World\".istitle())  # True\n\nprint(\"   \".isspace())          # True\nprint(\"name_1\".isidentifier())  # True\nprint(\"hello\\n\".isprintable())  # False\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#join-split-partition","title":"\ud83d\udd17 Join, Split &amp; Partition","text":"<pre><code>print(\", \".join([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]))         # 'a, b, c'\nprint(\"one,two,three\".split(\",\"))         # ['one', 'two', 'three']\nprint(\"line1\\nline2\".splitlines())        # ['line1', 'line2']\n\nprint(\"a=b\".partition(\"=\"))               # ('a', '=', 'b')\nprint(\"a=b=c\".rpartition(\"=\"))            # ('a=b', '=', 'c')\nprint(\"key=value=again\".split(\"=\", 1))    # ['key', 'value=again']\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#align-and-pad","title":"\ud83d\udcd0 Align and Pad","text":"<pre><code>print(\"42\".zfill(5))       # '00042'\nprint(\"hi\".center(6))      # '  hi  '\nprint(\"hi\".ljust(6))       # 'hi    '\nprint(\"hi\".rjust(6))       # '    hi'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#format-strings","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Format Strings","text":"<pre><code>print(\"Hello, {}\".format(\"Alice\"))                 # 'Hello, Alice'\nprint(\"Name: {name}\".format_map({'name': 'Bob'}))  # 'Name: Bob'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/methods.html#prefixsuffix-removal-python-39","title":"\ud83e\uddf9 Prefix/Suffix Removal (Python 3.9+)","text":"<pre><code>print(\"unhappy\".removeprefix(\"un\"))         # 'happy'\nprint(\"filename.txt\".removesuffix(\".txt\"))  # 'filename'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html","title":"Practical","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#python-string-practicals-interview-friendly","title":"\ud83e\uddf5 Python String Practicals \u2013 Interview Friendly","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#1-reverse-a-string","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Reverse a String","text":"<pre><code>def reverse_string(my_string):\n    res = \"\"\n    for i in my_string:\n        res = i + res\n    return res\n\nprint(\"Reversed String:\", reverse_string(\"Dhruvish patel\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#2-count-the-number-of-vowels-in-a-string","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Count the Number of Vowels in a String","text":"<pre><code>def count_vowels(input_string):\n    vowels = \"aeiouAEIOU\"\n    return sum(1 for char in input_string if char in vowels)\n\nprint(\"Count of vowels:\", count_vowels(\"Dhruvish patel\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#3-find-the-most-frequent-character-in-a-string","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Find the Most Frequent Character in a String","text":"<pre><code>def most_frequent(my_string):\n    freq = {}\n    for char in my_string.replace(\" \", \"\"):\n        freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n    return max(freq, key=freq.get)\n\nprint(\"Most frequent character:\", most_frequent(\"DDhruvish patel\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#4-find-all-most-frequent-characters","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Find All Most Frequent Characters","text":"<pre><code>def all_max_frequent(input_string):\n    freq = {}\n    max_freq = 0\n    for char in input_string:\n        freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n        max_freq = max(max_freq, freq[char])\n    return [k for k, v in freq.items() if v == max_freq]\n\nprint(\"All most frequent characters:\", all_max_frequent(\"Dhruvish from ahmedabad\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#5-remove-all-duplicates-from-a-string","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Remove All Duplicates from a String","text":"<pre><code>def remove_duplicate(my_string):\n    result = \"\"\n    for char in my_string:\n        if char not in result:\n            result += char\n    return result\n\nprint(\"Without duplicates:\", remove_duplicate(\"Dhruvish pattel\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#6-remove-first-duplicate-only","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Remove First Duplicate Only","text":"<pre><code>def remove_first_duplicate(string):\n    seen = set()\n    result = ''\n    duplicate_skipped = False\n    for char in string:\n        if char not in seen:\n            seen.add(char)\n            result += char\n        elif not duplicate_skipped:\n            duplicate_skipped = True\n        else:\n            result += char\n    return result\n\nprint(remove_first_duplicate(\"abcddefgghijjd\"))  # 'abcdefgghijjd'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#7-capitalize-first-letter-in-each-word","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Capitalize First Letter in Each Word","text":"<pre><code>def capitalize_words(sentence):\n    return ' '.join(word.capitalize() for word in sentence.split())\n\nprint(capitalize_words(\"dhruvish Patel from ahmedabad\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#alternate-method","title":"\ud83c\udf00 Alternate Method","text":"<pre><code>print(\"dhruvish Patel from ahmedabad\".title())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#8-count-occurrences-of-a-specific-substring","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Count Occurrences of a Specific Substring","text":"<pre><code>def count_substring(string, substring):\n    return string.count(substring)\n\nprint(count_substring(\"Hello, world! The world is round.\", \"world\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#9-first-non-repeated-character","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 First Non-Repeated Character","text":"<pre><code>def first_non_repeated_char(s):\n    freq = {}\n    for char in s:\n        freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n    for char in s:\n        if freq[char] == 1:\n            return char\n    return None\n\nprint(\"First non-repeated character:\", first_non_repeated_char(\"Dhruvish Patel\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#first-2-and-last-2-characters-of-a-string","title":"\ud83d\udd1f First 2 and Last 2 Characters of a String","text":"<pre><code>def format_string(s):\n    return s[:2] + s[-2:] if len(s) &gt;= 2 else ''\n\nprint(format_string(\"w3resource\"))  # 'w3ce'\nprint(format_string(\"w3\"))         # 'w3w3'\nprint(format_string(\" w\"))         # ''\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#11-swap-first-two-characters-of-two-strings","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e31\ufe0f\u20e3 Swap First Two Characters of Two Strings","text":"<pre><code>def chars_mix_up(a, b):\n    return b[:2] + a[2:] + \" \" + a[:2] + b[2:]\n\nprint(chars_mix_up('abc', 'xyz'))  # 'xyc abz'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#12-append-ing-or-ly-based-on-conditions","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e32\ufe0f\u20e3 Append 'ing' or 'ly' Based on Conditions","text":"<pre><code>def modify_string(s):\n    if len(s) &lt; 3:\n        return s\n    if s.endswith(\"ing\"):\n        return s + \"ly\"\n    return s + \"ing\"\n\nprint(modify_string(\"abc\"))     # abcing\nprint(modify_string(\"string\"))  # stringly\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#13-remove-character-at-specific-index","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e33\ufe0f\u20e3 Remove Character at Specific Index","text":"<pre><code>def remove_char_at_index(s, n):\n    return s[:n] + s[n+1:]\n\nprint(remove_char_at_index(\"Dhruvish\", 2))  # 'Dhrvvish'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#14-swap-first-and-last-characters","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e34\ufe0f\u20e3 Swap First and Last Characters","text":"<pre><code>def swap_first_last(s):\n    return s[-1] + s[1:-1] + s[0] if len(s) &gt; 1 else s\n\nprint(swap_first_last(\"Dhruvish\"))  # 'hhruvisD'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#15-remove-characters-at-odd-indexes","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e35\ufe0f\u20e3 Remove Characters at Odd Indexes","text":"<pre><code>def remove_odd_indices(s):\n    return ''.join([char for i, char in enumerate(s) if i % 2 == 0])\n\nprint(remove_odd_indices(\"Dhruvish\"))  # 'Druvh'\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Data_type/String/string_practical.html#16-write-a-function-that-returns-the-longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e36 Write a function that returns the longest substring without repeating characters.","text":"<pre><code>def longest_unique_substring(s):\n    char_set = set()\n    left = 0\n    max_sub = \"\"\n\n    for right in range(len(s)):\n        while s[right] in char_set:\n            char_set.remove(s[left])\n            left += 1\n\n        char_set.add(s[right])\n\n        if right - left + 1 &gt; len(max_sub):\n            max_sub = s[left:right + 1]\n\n    return max_sub\n\nprint(longest_unique_substring(\"abcabcbb\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html","title":"Methods","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#mongodb-commands-description-example","title":"MongoDB Commands \u2013 Description + Example","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#database-management","title":"\ud83d\udee2\ufe0f Database Management","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>use</code> Switches to specified database. <code>use school</code> <code>db.createCollection()</code> Creates a new collection. <code>db.createCollection(\"students\")</code> <code>db.dropDatabase()</code> Deletes the current database. <code>db.dropDatabase()</code> <code>show dbs</code> Lists all databases. <code>show dbs</code> <code>show collections</code> Lists all collections in the DB. <code>show collections</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#collection-management","title":"\ud83d\udcd0 Collection Management","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.collection.drop()</code> Deletes a collection. <code>db.students.drop()</code> <code>db.renameCollection()</code> Renames a collection. <code>db.oldName.renameCollection(\"newName\")</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#crud-operations","title":"\ud83d\udcca CRUD Operations","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.collection.insertOne()</code> Inserts a single document. <code>db.students.insertOne({name: \"Alice\", age: 20})</code> <code>db.collection.insertMany()</code> Inserts multiple documents. <code>db.students.insertMany([{name: \"Bob\"}, {name: \"Eve\"}])</code> <code>db.collection.find()</code> Retrieves documents. <code>db.students.find({age: 20})</code> <code>db.collection.findOne()</code> Retrieves a single document. <code>db.students.findOne({name: \"Alice\"})</code> <code>db.collection.updateOne()</code> Updates first matching document. <code>db.students.updateOne({name: \"Alice\"}, {$set: {age: 21}})</code> <code>db.collection.updateMany()</code> Updates all matching documents. <code>db.students.updateMany({age: 20}, {$set: {grade: \"B\"}})</code> <code>db.collection.replaceOne()</code> Replaces the whole document. <code>db.students.replaceOne({name: \"Bob\"}, {name: \"Robert\", age: 25})</code> <code>db.collection.deleteOne()</code> Deletes first matching document. <code>db.students.deleteOne({name: \"Alice\"})</code> <code>db.collection.deleteMany()</code> Deletes all matching documents. <code>db.students.deleteMany({age: 20})</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#query-modifiers","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Query Modifiers","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>.sort()</code> Sorts query results. <code>db.students.find().sort({age: -1})</code> <code>.limit()</code> Limits number of results. <code>db.students.find().limit(5)</code> <code>.skip()</code> Skips specified number of results. <code>db.students.find().skip(10)</code> <code>.countDocuments()</code> Counts matching documents. <code>db.students.countDocuments({age: 20})</code> <code>.distinct()</code> Gets distinct values of a field. <code>db.students.distinct(\"age\")</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#indexing","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Indexing","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.collection.createIndex()</code> Creates an index. <code>db.students.createIndex({name: 1})</code> <code>db.collection.dropIndex()</code> Drops an index. <code>db.students.dropIndex({name: 1})</code> <code>db.collection.getIndexes()</code> Lists all indexes. <code>db.students.getIndexes()</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#aggregation-grouping","title":"\ud83d\udcca Aggregation &amp; Grouping","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.collection.aggregate()</code> Performs aggregation pipeline. <code>db.students.aggregate([{$group: {_id: \"$age\", count: {$sum: 1}}}])</code> <code>$match</code> Filters documents (like <code>WHERE</code>). <code>{ $match: {age: 20} }</code> <code>$group</code> Groups documents. <code>{ $group: {_id: \"$grade\", total: {$sum: 1}} }</code> <code>$project</code> Shapes the output. <code>{ $project: {name: 1, age: 1} }</code> <code>$sort</code> Sorts results. <code>{ $sort: {age: -1} }</code> <code>$limit</code> Limits the results. <code>{ $limit: 5 }</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#validation-schema","title":"\ud83e\uddea Validation &amp; Schema","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.createCollection()</code> With schema validation. <code>db.createCollection(\"students\", {validator: { $jsonSchema: {bsonType: \"object\", required: [\"name\"]}}})</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#user-role-management","title":"\ud83d\udd10 User &amp; Role Management","text":"Method Description Sample Usage <code>db.createUser()</code> Creates a new user. <code>db.createUser({user: \"admin\", pwd: \"pass\", roles: [\"readWrite\"]})</code> <code>db.updateUser()</code> Updates an existing user. <code>db.updateUser(\"admin\", {pwd: \"newpass\"})</code> <code>db.dropUser()</code> Removes a user. <code>db.dropUser(\"admin\")</code> <code>db.getUsers()</code> Lists users. <code>db.getUsers()</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/Mongodb/methods.html#utility-commands","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Utility Commands","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>db.stats()</code> Shows database statistics. <code>db.stats()</code> <code>db.collection.stats()</code> Shows collection statistics. <code>db.students.stats()</code> <code>db.version()</code> Returns MongoDB version. <code>db.version()</code> <code>db.serverStatus()</code> Returns server info and health. <code>db.serverStatus()</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html","title":"Commands","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#mysql-commands-description-example","title":"\ud83d\udee2\ufe0f MySQL Commands \u2013 Description + Example","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> Creates a new database. <code>CREATE DATABASE school;</code> <code>DROP DATABASE</code> Deletes a database. <code>DROP DATABASE school;</code> <code>USE</code> Selects a database to work with. <code>USE school;</code> <code>SHOW DATABASES</code> Lists all databases. <code>SHOW DATABASES;</code> <code>SHOW TABLES</code> Lists all tables in the current database. <code>SHOW TABLES;</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#table-management","title":"\ud83d\udcd0 Table Management","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>CREATE TABLE</code> Creates a new table. <code>CREATE TABLE students (id INT, name VARCHAR(50));</code> <code>DROP TABLE</code> Deletes a table. <code>DROP TABLE students;</code> <code>ALTER TABLE</code> Modifies a table structure. <code>ALTER TABLE students ADD email VARCHAR(100);</code> <code>RENAME TABLE</code> Renames a table. <code>RENAME TABLE students TO learners;</code> <code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> Removes all data, but keeps structure. <code>TRUNCATE TABLE students;</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#data-manipulation-crud","title":"\ud83d\udcca Data Manipulation (CRUD)","text":"Command Description Sample Usage <code>INSERT INTO</code> Inserts data into a table. <code>INSERT INTO students (id, name) VALUES (1, 'Alice');</code> <code>SELECT</code> Retrieves data from a table. <code>SELECT * FROM students;</code> <code>UPDATE</code> Updates existing records. <code>UPDATE students SET name='Bob' WHERE id=1;</code> <code>DELETE</code> Deletes records. <code>DELETE FROM students WHERE id=1;</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#query-clauses","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Query Clauses","text":"Clause Description Sample Usage <code>WHERE</code> Filters records. <code>SELECT * FROM students WHERE id = 1;</code> <code>ORDER BY</code> Sorts results. <code>SELECT * FROM students ORDER BY name ASC;</code> <code>GROUP BY</code> Groups records. <code>SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY dept;</code> <code>HAVING</code> Filters after grouping. <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2;</code> <code>LIMIT</code> Restricts number of records. <code>SELECT * FROM students LIMIT 5;</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#constraints","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Constraints","text":"Constraint Description Sample Usage <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> Uniquely identifies each row. <code>id INT PRIMARY KEY</code> <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> Links to primary key in another table. <code>FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES departments(id)</code> <code>UNIQUE</code> Ensures all values in column are unique. <code>email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE</code> <code>NOT NULL</code> Column must have a value. <code>name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL</code> <code>DEFAULT</code> Sets a default value. <code>status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'active'</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#joins","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Joins","text":"Join Type Description Sample Usage <code>INNER JOIN</code> Only matching records in both tables. <code>SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>LEFT JOIN</code> All from left, matched from right. <code>SELECT * FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> All from right, matched from left. <code>SELECT * FROM A RIGHT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id;</code> <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> All records from both sides (via <code>UNION</code>). Not native in MySQL (use <code>UNION</code> workaround)"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/commands.html#functions","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Functions","text":"Function Description Sample Usage <code>COUNT()</code> Counts rows. <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;</code> <code>SUM()</code> Adds values. <code>SELECT SUM(salary) FROM employees;</code> <code>AVG()</code> Average value. <code>SELECT AVG(score) FROM tests;</code> <code>MAX()</code> Highest value. <code>SELECT MAX(score) FROM tests;</code> <code>MIN()</code> Lowest value. <code>SELECT MIN(score) FROM tests;</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html","title":"SQL List","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#sql-practical-questions-based-on-your-database","title":"\ud83e\udde0 SQL PRACTICAL QUESTIONS (BASED ON YOUR DATABASE)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#tables-you-have","title":"\ud83d\udce6 TABLES YOU HAVE","text":"<p><code>users, addresses, categories, products, orders, order_items, payments, employees, departments</code></p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#basic-queries-foundation-must-be-fast","title":"\ud83d\udfe2 BASIC QUERIES (FOUNDATION \u2013 MUST BE FAST)","text":"<ol> <li>Get all users sorted by <code>created_at</code> (latest first)</li> <li>Find users created after <code>2023-02-01</code></li> <li>Count total users</li> <li>List all unique cities from addresses</li> <li>Find products priced above 2000</li> <li>Get all orders placed in June 2023</li> <li>Find all payments with status <code>FAILED</code></li> <li>List employees with salary greater than 60,000</li> <li>Count number of departments</li> <li>Find users whose name starts with <code>A</code></li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#joins-most-frequently-asked","title":"\ud83d\udfe1 JOINS (MOST FREQUENTLY ASKED)","text":"<ol> <li>Show user name with their city</li> <li>Show users and number of addresses they have</li> <li>List orders with user name and email</li> <li>Show order id with product name and quantity</li> <li>List products with their category name</li> <li>Show users and their total orders (include zero orders)</li> <li>Find orders that do not have any payment</li> <li>Get payments with user name and order date</li> <li>Show employees with department name</li> <li>Show employees with their manager name</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#group-by-having-interview-favorite","title":"\ud83d\udfe0 GROUP BY &amp; HAVING (INTERVIEW FAVORITE)","text":"<ol> <li>Find users who placed more than 2 orders</li> <li>Get category-wise product count</li> <li>Find total orders per day</li> <li>Get total revenue per order</li> <li>Find users whose total spend is more than 30,000</li> <li>Find products sold more than 2 times</li> <li>Get department-wise average salary</li> <li>Find managers having more than 1 employee</li> <li>Count payments by status</li> <li>Find users having more than one address</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#subqueries-senior-level-thinking","title":"\ud83d\udd35 SUBQUERIES (SENIOR LEVEL THINKING)","text":"<ol> <li>Find users who placed the highest number of orders</li> <li>Get products that were never ordered</li> <li>Find users who never made a successful payment</li> <li>Get second highest salary among employees</li> <li>Find employees earning more than their manager</li> <li>Get highest paid employee in each department</li> <li>Find users whose total spending is above average</li> <li>Find orders whose total value is above average order value</li> <li>Get users who placed orders on the latest order date</li> <li>Find products priced higher than category average</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#window-functions-very-important-for-senior","title":"\ud83d\udfe3 WINDOW FUNCTIONS (VERY IMPORTANT FOR SENIOR)","text":"<ol> <li>Rank users based on total spending</li> <li>Show running total of revenue by order date</li> <li>Find top 3 most expensive products per category</li> <li>Assign row numbers to orders per user</li> <li>Find duplicate emails using ROW_NUMBER</li> <li>Compare employee salary with department average</li> <li>Show first and last order date per user</li> <li>Implement pagination (page 2, size 5)</li> <li>Rank employees by salary within department</li> <li>Month-wise revenue growth</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#real-backend-scenarios-api-sql","title":"\ud83d\udd34 REAL BACKEND SCENARIOS (API + SQL)","text":"<ol> <li>Write query for paginated user list (LIMIT/OFFSET)</li> <li>Fetch order history API for a user</li> <li>Dashboard query: total users, orders, revenue</li> <li>Admin report: failed vs successful payments</li> <li>Find inactive users (no orders in last 60 days)</li> <li>Find top 5 customers by spending</li> <li>Detect orphan records (orders without users)</li> <li>Insert order and payment in a transaction</li> <li>Soft delete users and exclude them from queries</li> <li>Find users with inconsistent payment data</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#advanced-edge-cases-what-seniors-are-judged-on","title":"\u26ab ADVANCED &amp; EDGE CASES (WHAT SENIORS ARE JUDGED ON)","text":"<ol> <li>Optimize revenue query using indexes</li> <li>Rewrite subquery using JOIN</li> <li>Explain EXPLAIN plan for heavy query</li> <li>Handle NULL values in joins properly</li> <li>Avoid double counting in revenue queries</li> <li>Find users who ordered from multiple categories</li> <li>Detect sudden spikes in daily orders</li> <li>Find employees under same manager</li> <li>Idempotent insert for users</li> <li>Lock rows while processing payment</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#most-asked-sql-practical-problems-interview-focused","title":"\u2705 Most Asked SQL Practical Problems (Interview-Focused)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#users-addresses","title":"Users &amp; Addresses","text":"Problem Tables Description / Input Expected Output Find all users with their addresses <code>users, addresses</code> Join users and addresses by <code>user_id</code> <code>user_id, name, address_line, city, zip</code> Find users without addresses <code>users, addresses</code> Left join to find NULL addresses Users who don\u2019t have an address Users with multiple shipping addresses <code>users, addresses</code> Group by <code>user_id</code> count&gt;1 <code>user_id, address_count</code> Users who never placed an order <code>users, orders</code> Left join orders NULL <code>user_id, name</code> Users who purchased all products in a category <code>users, orders, order_items, products, categories</code> Set division problem <code>user_id, category_name</code> Users who spent more than average spending <code>users, orders, order_items, products</code> Calculate avg spending \u2192 filter <code>user_id, total_spent</code> Users who ordered in last 30 days <code>users, orders</code> Filter order_date <code>user_id, order_id</code> Users with repeated purchases of same product <code>users, orders, order_items</code> Count&gt;1 per product per user <code>user_id, product_id, purchase_count</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#orders-order_items","title":"Orders &amp; Order_Items","text":"Problem Tables Description / Input Expected Output Count number of orders per user <code>users, orders</code> Group by <code>user_id</code> <code>user_id, total_orders</code> Total amount spent by each user <code>users, orders, order_items, products</code> Sum of <code>order_items.quantity*products.price</code> per user <code>user_id, total_spent</code> Orders without payments <code>orders, payments</code> Left join <code>payments</code> NULL <code>order_id, user_id</code> Orders with multiple products <code>orders, order_items</code> Count products per order &gt; 1 <code>order_id, product_count</code> Orders with only one product <code>orders, order_items</code> Count items=1 <code>order_id, user_id</code> Most recent order per user <code>orders</code> Max(order_date) group by user <code>user_id, order_id, order_date</code> Orders where shipping address differs from billing address <code>orders, addresses</code> Compare addresses <code>order_id, user_id</code> Subtotal / total per order <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Sum(quantity*price) per order <code>order_id, total_amount</code> Orders containing a specific category <code>orders, order_items, products, categories</code> Filter by <code>category_id</code> <code>order_id, user_id</code> Orders including most expensive product <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Filter product with max(price) <code>order_id, product_id</code> Total orders per day <code>orders</code> Group by order_date <code>order_date, total_orders</code> Average products per order <code>orders, order_items</code> Avg(count items per order) <code>avg_items_per_order</code> Total revenue per day <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Sum(quantity*price) per day <code>order_date, total_revenue</code> Monthly sales trend <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Group by month <code>month, total_sales</code> Running total per day / order <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Cumulative sum of revenue <code>order_date, cumulative_total</code> Orders with partial payments <code>orders, payments</code> Order total &gt; sum(payment.amount) <code>order_id, amount_due</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#products-categories","title":"Products &amp; Categories","text":"Problem Tables Description / Input Expected Output Top 5 products by sales <code>products, order_items</code> Sum quantity sold per product <code>product_id, total_quantity_sold</code> Products never ordered <code>products, order_items</code> Left join <code>order_items</code> NULL <code>product_id, name</code> Most expensive product in each category <code>categories, products</code> Max price per category <code>category_name, product_name, max_price</code> Total sales per category <code>categories, products, order_items</code> Join \u2192 sum sales <code>category_name, total_sales</code> Top selling category per month <code>categories, products, order_items, orders</code> Group by month+category <code>month, category_name, total_sales</code> Products with price &gt; avg price <code>products</code> Filter <code>product_id, name, price</code> Pivot / string aggregation by category <code>categories, products</code> List products per category <code>category_name, product_list</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#employees-departments","title":"Employees &amp; Departments","text":"Problem Tables Description / Input Expected Output Employees in each department <code>employees, departments</code> Group by dept <code>department_id, employee_count</code> Employee with highest salary per department <code>employees</code> Partition by dept <code>department_id, employee_name, max_salary</code> Employees with salary above dept average <code>employees</code> Partition by dept <code>employee_id, name, salary</code> Employees with no manager <code>employees</code> <code>manager_id IS NULL</code> <code>employee_id, name</code> Employees reporting to a manager <code>employees</code> <code>manager_id=101</code> <code>employee_id, name</code> Departments with more than N employees <code>employees, departments</code> Group by dept having count&gt;5 <code>department_id, employee_count</code> Second highest salary employee <code>employees</code> Using <code>RANK()</code> or <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> <code>employee_id, name, salary</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/list_practical.html#miscellaneous-combined-queries","title":"Miscellaneous / Combined Queries","text":"Problem Tables Description / Input Expected Output Users who ordered all products in a category <code>users, orders, order_items, products, categories</code> Set division <code>user_id, category_name</code> Identify inactive customers <code>users, orders</code> Last order &gt; 6 months ago <code>user_id, last_order_date</code> Find missing numbers (e.g., order_id gaps) <code>orders</code> Identify missing consecutive IDs <code>missing_order_id</code> Find overlapping intervals (bookings/orders) <code>orders(start_date,end_date)</code> Overlapping date ranges <code>order_id1, order_id2</code> Top N users by total spending <code>users, orders, order_items, products</code> Sum spending \u2192 top N <code>user_id, total_spent</code> Average order value per user <code>users, orders, order_items, products</code> Sum per order \u2192 avg per user <code>user_id, avg_order_value</code> Running total of revenue <code>orders, order_items, products</code> Cumulative sum <code>order_date, cumulative_revenue</code>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html","title":"practical","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#mysql-interview-practice-real-world-questions-answers","title":"\ud83d\udcbe MySQL Interview Practice \u2013 Real-World Questions &amp; Answers","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#1-what-is-the-sql-query-used-for-creating-a-database-and-a-table","title":"\u2705 1. What is the SQL query used for creating a database and a table?","text":"<pre><code>CREATE DATABASE company;\nUSE company;\n\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100),\n    department_id INT,\n    salary DECIMAL\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#2-create-a-table-with-the-same-structure-of-another-table-no-data","title":"\u2705 2. Create a table with the same structure of another table (no data)?","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE department_copy AS\nSELECT * FROM department WHERE 1=2;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#3-create-a-table-with-the-same-structure-and-data-of-another-table","title":"\u2705 3. Create a table with the same structure and data of another table?","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE Emp\nSELECT * FROM Employee;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#4-find-the-2nd-3rd-nth-highest-salary","title":"\u2705 4. Find the 2nd / 3rd / nth highest salary?","text":"<pre><code>-- Option 1: Nested MAX\nSELECT MAX(Salary) FROM Employee\nWHERE Salary &lt; (\n    SELECT MAX(Salary) FROM Employee\n    WHERE Salary &lt; (\n        SELECT MAX(Salary) FROM Employee\n    )\n);\n\n-- Option 2: OFFSET\nSELECT Salary FROM Employee \nORDER BY Salary DESC\nLIMIT 1 OFFSET 2; -- 3rd highest (OFFSET is 0-based)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#5-find-employees-who-also-hold-managerial-positions","title":"\u2705 5. Find employees who also hold managerial positions?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employees \nWHERE Employee_ID IN (SELECT Manager_ID FROM Employees);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#6-find-names-of-employees-that-begin-with-a","title":"\u2705 6. Find names of employees that begin with \u2018A\u2019?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT Name FROM Employee WHERE Name LIKE 'A%';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#7-display-the-current-date","title":"\u2705 7. Display the current date?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT CURRENT_DATE();\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#8-fetch-alternate-records-from-a-table","title":"\u2705 8. Fetch alternate records from a table?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE id % 2 = 0;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#9-fetch-common-records-from-two-tables","title":"\u2705 9. Fetch common records from two tables?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee\nINNER JOIN Skill ON Employee.Skill_ID = Skill.ID;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#10-remove-duplicate-rows-in-a-table","title":"\u2705 10. Remove duplicate rows in a table?","text":"<pre><code>DELETE E1 FROM Employee E1\nINNER JOIN Employee E2 \nWHERE \n    E1.id &lt; E2.id AND \n    E1.Name = E2.Name;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#11-find-the-nth-record-from-a-table","title":"\u2705 11. Find the nth record from a table?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee LIMIT 1 OFFSET 10;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#12-find-the-first-5-records-from-a-table","title":"\u2705 12. Find the first 5 records from a table?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee ORDER BY ID LIMIT 5;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#13-find-the-last-5-records-from-a-table","title":"\u2705 13. Find the last 5 records from a table?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee ORDER BY ID DESC LIMIT 5;\n\n-- Or sorted in ascending order\nSELECT * FROM (\n    SELECT * FROM Employee ORDER BY ID DESC LIMIT 5\n) AS last5\nORDER BY ID;\n\n-- Or using MAX\nSELECT * FROM Employee \nWHERE ID &gt; (SELECT MAX(ID) - 5 FROM Employee);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#14-find-the-first-or-last-record-from-a-table","title":"\u2705 14. Find the first or last record from a table?","text":"<p>First Record:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee LIMIT 1;\nSELECT * FROM Employee WHERE ID = (SELECT MIN(ID) FROM Employee);\n</code></pre> <p>Last Record:</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM Employee ORDER BY ID DESC LIMIT 1;\nSELECT * FROM Employee WHERE ID = (SELECT MAX(ID) FROM Employee);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#15-find-distinct-records-without-using-distinct-keyword","title":"\u2705 15. Find distinct records without using <code>DISTINCT</code> keyword?","text":"<p>Using GROUP BY:</p> <pre><code>SELECT dept_id FROM Employee GROUP BY dept_id;\n</code></pre> <p>Using UNION:</p> <pre><code>SELECT dept_id FROM Employee\nUNION\nSELECT dept_id FROM Employee;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#16-maximum-salary-of-each-department","title":"\u2705 16. Maximum salary of each department?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT dept_id, MAX(salary)\nFROM Employee\nGROUP BY dept_id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#17-department-wise-employee-count-sorted","title":"\u2705 17. Department-wise employee count (sorted)?","text":"<pre><code>SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS Count\nFROM Employee\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY Count ASC;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#18-change-the-datatype-of-a-column","title":"\u2705 18. Change the datatype of a column?","text":"<pre><code>ALTER TABLE Employee MODIFY manager_id BIGINT;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#19-difference-between-unique-key-primary-key-and-foreign-key","title":"\u2705 19. Difference between Unique Key, Primary Key and Foreign Key?","text":"<ul> <li> <p>Unique Key   Ensures uniqueness for a column. Allows one <code>NULL</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Primary Key   Combination of <code>UNIQUE + NOT NULL</code>. Only one per table.</p> </li> <li> <p>Foreign Key   References a key in another table to enforce referential integrity.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#20-union-vs-union-all","title":"\u2705 20. <code>UNION</code> vs <code>UNION ALL</code>","text":"<ul> <li><code>UNION</code> removes duplicates.</li> <li><code>UNION ALL</code> keeps all rows (including duplicates).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#21-cartesian-join-cross-join","title":"\u2705 21. Cartesian Join (Cross Join)","text":"<p>Returns every combination between two tables.</p> <pre><code>SELECT * FROM table1\nCROSS JOIN table2;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#22-difference-full-outer-join-vs-union-all","title":"\u2705 22. Difference: Full Outer Join vs UNION ALL","text":"<ul> <li> <p>Full Outer Join   Returns all rows from both tables with <code>NULL</code>s where no match.</p> </li> <li> <p>UNION ALL   Combines result sets with duplicates, doesn\u2019t match rows.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#23-get-only-customers-who-made-transactions-in-both-2013-and-2023","title":"\u2705 23. Get only customers who made transactions in both 2013 and 2023","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#select-customer_id-from-transactions-where-yeartransaction_date-in-2013-2023-group-by-customer_id-having-countdistinct-yeartransaction_date-2","title":"<pre><code>SELECT customer_id\nFROM transactions\nWHERE YEAR(transaction_date) IN (2013, 2023)\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT YEAR(transaction_date)) = 2;\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#24-write-a-query-to-get-2nd-highest-salary","title":"24. Write a query to get 2nd highest salary.","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#select-salary-from-employee-order-by-salary-desc-limit-1-offset-1-offset-1-skips-the-highest-gives-2nd-highest","title":"<pre><code>SELECT salary\nFROM employee\nORDER BY salary DESC\nLIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;  -- OFFSET 1 skips the highest, gives 2nd highest\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#25-find-duplicates-records-count","title":"25. Find Duplicates records count","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#select-name-count-as-cnt-from-employees-group-by-name-having-count-1","title":"<pre><code>SELECT name, COUNT(*) AS cnt\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY name\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practical.html#26-get-department-wise-highest-salary-employee","title":"26. Get department-wise highest salary employee","text":"<p>Tables:</p> <p><code>employees(emp_id, emp_name, salary, dept_id)</code> <code>departments(dept_id, dept_name)</code></p> <pre><code>SELECT d.dept_name, e.emp_name, e.salary\nFROM employees e\nJOIN departments d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id\nWHERE e.salary = (\n    SELECT MAX(salary)\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id = e.dept_id\n);\n```ING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html","title":"\ud83e\udde0 MySQL Interview Practice \u2013 Real-World Practical Tasks","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#mysql-interview-practice-real-world-practical-tasks","title":"\ud83e\udde0 MySQL Interview Practice \u2013 Real-World Practical Tasks","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#database-table-setup","title":"\ud83d\udcc1 Database &amp; Table Setup","text":"<ol> <li>Create a database called <code>\"company\"</code> and switch to it.</li> <li> <p>Create a table <code>\"employees\"</code> with columns:</p> </li> <li> <p><code>id</code> (INT, PRIMARY KEY)</p> </li> <li><code>name</code> (VARCHAR)</li> <li><code>department_id</code> (INT)</li> <li><code>salary</code> (DECIMAL)</li> <li>Add a new column <code>email</code> (VARCHAR) to the <code>\"employees\"</code> table.</li> <li>Rename the <code>\"employees\"</code> table to <code>\"staff\"</code>.</li> <li>Remove all records from the <code>\"staff\"</code> table without deleting the table itself.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#data-manipulation","title":"\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbc Data Manipulation","text":"<ol> <li>Insert 3 staff records into the <code>\"staff\"</code> table with different departments and salaries.</li> <li>Select all records from the <code>\"staff\"</code> table where <code>department_id = 2</code>.</li> <li>Update salary by 10% for employees in <code>department_id = 3</code>.</li> <li>Delete a staff record where <code>name = \"John Doe\"</code>.</li> <li>Retrieve staff names sorted by salary in descending order.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#aggregations-filtering","title":"\ud83d\udcca Aggregations &amp; Filtering","text":"<ol> <li>Get the total number of staff per department.</li> <li>Get the average salary for <code>department_id = 1</code>, but only show if it's above <code>50000</code>.</li> <li>Limit the result to top 5 highest earning staff.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#table-relationships-constraints","title":"\ud83c\udfe2 Table Relationships &amp; Constraints","text":"<ol> <li> <p>Create a <code>departments</code> table with:</p> </li> <li> <p><code>id</code> (INT, PRIMARY KEY)</p> </li> <li> <p><code>name</code> (VARCHAR)</p> </li> <li> <p>Add a foreign key from <code>staff.department_id</code> \u2192 <code>departments.id</code>.</p> </li> <li>Ensure the <code>email</code> column in <code>staff</code> is unique.</li> <li>Ensure the <code>name</code> column in <code>staff</code> cannot be NULL.</li> <li>Set default value of a new <code>status</code> column to <code>\"active\"</code> in <code>staff</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_practice.html#joins-advanced-queries","title":"\ud83d\udd17 Joins &amp; Advanced Queries","text":"<ol> <li>Join <code>staff</code> and <code>departments</code> to display employee name with their department name.</li> <li>Show all departments, even those without any employees (LEFT JOIN).</li> <li>Count how many employees are in each department.</li> <li>Show the total salary payout for each department.</li> <li>Find the highest salary in the <code>staff</code> table.</li> <li>Show names of employees with the lowest salary.</li> <li>Use <code>UNION</code> to simulate FULL OUTER JOIN between <code>projects</code> and <code>staff_projects</code>.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html","title":"oral","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#mysql-sql-interview-guide-markdown","title":"\u2705 MySQL &amp; SQL Interview Guide (Markdown)","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#1-what-is-mysql","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1. What is MySQL?","text":"<p>MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses Structured Query Language (SQL) for managing and manipulating databases.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#2-what-is-a-database","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2. What is a Database?","text":"<p>A database is an organized collection of structured information or data, typically stored electronically in a computer system.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#3-what-is-dbms","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3. What is DBMS?","text":"<p>DBMS (Database Management System) is software that allows users to define, create, maintain, and control access to databases.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#4-what-is-a-relational-database","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4. What is a Relational Database?","text":"<p>A relational database stores data in tables (rows and columns) and defines relationships between those tables using keys.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#5-sql-table-creation-examples","title":"\ud83d\udd39 5. SQL Table Creation Examples","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#without-constraints","title":"Without Constraints:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE users (\n  id INT UNSIGNED,\n  name VARCHAR(100),\n  email VARCHAR(150),\n  password VARCHAR(100),\n  mobile VARCHAR(15),\n  gender ENUM('M','D','Y'),\n  dob DATE,\n  status BOOLEAN,\n  user_detail_id INT UNSIGNED,\n  FOREIGN KEY (user_detail_id) REFERENCES user_details(id)\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#with-constraints","title":"With Constraints:","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE student (\n  id INT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n  email VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  age TINYINT CHECK (age &gt;= 18),\n  status BOOLEAN DEFAULT 1\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#6-insert-data","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6. Insert Data","text":"<pre><code>INSERT INTO users (id, name, email, mobile, gender, dob, status)\nVALUES\n(2, 'Maitri', 'patelmaitri612000@gmail.com', '6353573222', 'F', '2000-01-09', 1),\n(3, 'Kanan', 'patelkanan@gmail.com', '8488861415', 'F', '2000-02-06', 1);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#7-select-data","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7. Select Data","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE gender = 'F';\nSELECT name AS 'Username', email, mobile FROM users WHERE gender = 'F';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#8-update-delete-data","title":"\ud83d\udd39 8. Update &amp; Delete Data","text":"<pre><code>UPDATE student SET age = 25 WHERE id = 4;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE id = 4;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE id IN (4,5,6);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#9-sql-operators","title":"\ud83d\udd39 9. SQL Operators","text":"<pre><code>-- IN\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE gender IN ('M','F');\n-- NOT IN\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE gender NOT IN ('M','F');\n-- BETWEEN\nSELECT * FROM students WHERE age BETWEEN 10 AND 20;\n-- NOT BETWEEN\nSELECT * FROM students WHERE age NOT BETWEEN 10 AND 20;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#10-like-operator","title":"\ud83d\udd39 10. LIKE Operator","text":"<pre><code>-- % Wildcard\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE 'a%';   -- starts with 'a'\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE '%a';   -- ends with 'a'\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE '%a%';  -- contains 'a'\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE 'a%o';  -- starts with 'a' and ends with 'o'\n\n-- _ Wildcard\nSELECT * FROM Customers WHERE city LIKE '_n%';\nSELECT * FROM Customers WHERE city LIKE 'n_%';\nSELECT * FROM Customers WHERE city LIKE 'n__%';\nSELECT * FROM Customers WHERE city LIKE 'L_nd__';\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#11-order-by-distinct","title":"\ud83d\udd39 11. ORDER BY &amp; DISTINCT","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY name ASC;\nSELECT DISTINCT gender FROM users;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#12-null-and-not-null","title":"\ud83d\udd39 12. NULL and NOT NULL","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE mobile IS NULL;\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE mobile IS NOT NULL;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#13-limit-and-offset","title":"\ud83d\udd39 13. LIMIT and OFFSET","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5;\nSELECT * FROM users ORDER BY name DESC LIMIT 5;\nSELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5 OFFSET 0;\nSELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5 OFFSET 1;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#14-aggregate-functions","title":"\ud83d\udd39 14. Aggregate Functions","text":"<pre><code>SELECT COUNT(name) FROM users;\nSELECT SUM(age) FROM student;\nSELECT AVG(age) FROM student;\nSELECT MIN(age) FROM student;\nSELECT MAX(age) FROM student;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#15-commit-and-rollback","title":"\ud83d\udd39 15. COMMIT and ROLLBACK","text":"<ul> <li>COMMIT saves changes permanently.</li> <li>ROLLBACK undoes changes since the last commit.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#16-primary-key-foreign-key-example","title":"\ud83d\udd39 16. Primary Key &amp; Foreign Key Example","text":"<pre><code>CREATE TABLE student (\n  id INT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n  city_id INT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (id),\n  FOREIGN KEY (city_id) REFERENCES city(cid)\n);\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#17-sql-joins","title":"\ud83d\udd39 17. SQL Joins","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#inner-join","title":"INNER JOIN","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM students\nINNER JOIN city ON students.city_id = city.id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#left-join","title":"LEFT JOIN","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM students\nLEFT JOIN city ON students.city_id = city.id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#right-join","title":"RIGHT JOIN","text":"<pre><code>SELECT * FROM students\nRIGHT JOIN city ON students.city_id = city.id;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#full-join","title":"FULL JOIN","text":"<p>(Not supported in MySQL, use UNION workaround)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#self-join","title":"SELF JOIN","text":"<p>Used when a table references itself.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#18-truncate-vs-delete-vs-drop","title":"\ud83d\udd39 18. TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP","text":"<ul> <li>DELETE: Deletes rows (can rollback)</li> <li>TRUNCATE: Deletes all rows (no rollback)</li> <li>DROP: Deletes table structure</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#19-sql-constraints","title":"\ud83d\udd39 19. SQL Constraints","text":"<ul> <li>NOT NULL</li> <li>UNIQUE</li> <li>CHECK</li> <li>DEFAULT</li> <li>PRIMARY KEY</li> <li>FOREIGN KEY</li> <li>INDEX</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#20-sql-language-subsets","title":"\ud83d\udd39 20. SQL Language Subsets","text":""},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#ddl-data-definition-language","title":"DDL \u2013 Data Definition Language:","text":"<ul> <li>CREATE, DROP, ALTER, TRUNCATE</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#dml-data-manipulation-language","title":"DML \u2013 Data Manipulation Language:","text":"<ul> <li>SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#dcl-data-control-language","title":"DCL \u2013 Data Control Language:","text":"<ul> <li>GRANT, REVOKE</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#tcl-transaction-control-language","title":"TCL \u2013 Transaction Control Language:","text":"<ul> <li>COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#21-types-of-relationships-in-sql","title":"\ud83d\udd39 21. Types of Relationships in SQL","text":"<ul> <li>One-to-One</li> <li>One-to-Many</li> <li>Many-to-One</li> <li>Many-to-Many</li> <li>Self-Referencing</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#22-rdbms-vs-dbms","title":"\ud83d\udd39 22. RDBMS vs DBMS","text":"<ul> <li>DBMS: No relational model (e.g., file systems)</li> <li>RDBMS: Uses relational model with tables and keys (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#23-null-vs-zero-vs-blank-space","title":"\ud83d\udd39 23. NULL vs Zero vs Blank Space","text":"<ul> <li>NULL: Unknown or missing value</li> <li>Zero: Numeric value</li> <li>Blank space: Textual/character field</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#24-what-is-normalization","title":"\ud83d\udd39 24. What is Normalization?","text":"<p>Normalization is the process of organizing data to reduce redundancy and improve integrity.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#25-what-is-denormalization","title":"\ud83d\udd39 25. What is Denormalization?","text":"<p>Denormalization is the process of introducing redundancy for better performance and reduced complexity.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#26-what-is-acid-property","title":"\ud83d\udd39 26. What is ACID Property?","text":"<p>(Expand if needed)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#27-group-by-vs-having","title":"\ud83d\udd39 27. GROUP BY vs HAVING","text":"<pre><code>SELECT customer_id, SUM(quantity) AS total_quantity\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING SUM(quantity) &gt; 100;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#28-multi-table-sql-examples","title":"\ud83d\udd39 28. Multi-table SQL Examples","text":"<p>(Create examples of multi-table queries using JOINs, subqueries, etc.)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#29-real-world-challenge-in-a-project","title":"\ud83d\udd39 29. Real-World Challenge in a Project","text":"<p>Describe a specific SQL or backend challenge, and how you solved it (e.g., optimizing complex JOINs, building materialized views, partitioning, etc.)</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#30-project-architecture-explanation","title":"\ud83d\udd39 30. Project Architecture Explanation","text":"<p>Add a diagram or markdown layout for how components like Kafka, databases, microservices, and caching systems work together in your backend setup.</p>"},{"location":"Interview/Practical/SQL/sql_questions.html#31-why-jwt-token-is-not-fully-secure","title":"\ud83d\udd39 31. Why JWT Token is Not Fully Secure?","text":"<ul> <li>Stored in localStorage \u2013 vulnerable to XSS attacks</li> <li>No built-in token revocation mechanism</li> <li>Exposed in every request</li> </ul> <p>Use HTTPS, short expiration, refresh tokens, and avoid storing sensitive data inside JWT payload.</p>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html","title":"Django","text":""},{"location":"ORM/django.html#1-create-insert","title":"\ud83d\udfe2 1. CREATE (INSERT)","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.create(name=\"John\", age=25)\n\nu = User(name=\"John\", age=25)\nu.save()\n\nUser.objects.bulk_create([\n    User(name=\"A\"),\n    User(name=\"B\")\n])\n\nOrder.objects.create(user=user_obj, total=100)\n\nuser.groups.add(group_obj)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#2-read-select","title":"\ud83d\udd35 2. READ (SELECT)","text":""},{"location":"ORM/django.html#21-basic-select","title":"2.1 Basic Select","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.all()\n\nUser.objects.get(id=1)\n\nUser.objects.filter(age=25)\n\nUser.objects.exclude(age=25)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#22-conditions","title":"2.2 Conditions","text":""},{"location":"ORM/django.html#and","title":"AND","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(age=25, is_active=True)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#or","title":"OR","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(Q(age=20) | Q(age=30))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#in","title":"IN","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(id__in=[1, 2, 3])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#not-in","title":"NOT IN","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.exclude(id__in=[1, 2, 3])\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#23-like-search","title":"2.3 LIKE / SEARCH","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(name__icontains=\"john\")\n\nUser.objects.filter(name__startswith=\"A\")\n\nUser.objects.filter(name__endswith=\"Z\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#24-null-checks","title":"2.4 NULL Checks","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(email__isnull=True)\n\nUser.objects.filter(email__isnull=False)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#25-order-limit","title":"2.5 ORDER / LIMIT","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.order_by(\"name\")\n\nUser.objects.order_by(\"-name\")\n\nUser.objects.all()[:5]\n\nUser.objects.all()[5:10]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#26-values-optimization","title":"2.6 Values / Optimization","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.values(\"id\", \"name\")\n\nUser.objects.values_list(\"name\", flat=True)\n\nUser.objects.distinct()\n\nUser.objects.count()\n\nUser.objects.exists()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#27-joins","title":"2.7 Joins","text":"<pre><code>Order.objects.select_related(\"user\")\n\nUser.objects.prefetch_related(\"groups\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#3-update","title":"\ud83d\udfe1 3. UPDATE","text":""},{"location":"ORM/django.html#31-single-record","title":"3.1 Single Record","text":"<pre><code>u = User.objects.get(id=1)\nu.age = 30\nu.save()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#32-multiple-records","title":"3.2 Multiple Records","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(age=20).update(age=21)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#33-using-f-expressions","title":"3.3 Using F Expressions","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.update(age=F(\"age\") + 1)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#34-relations","title":"3.4 Relations","text":"<pre><code>user.groups.remove(group_obj)\n\nuser.groups.clear()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#4-delete","title":"\ud83d\udd34 4. DELETE","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.get(id=1).delete()\n\nUser.objects.filter(age__lt=18).delete()\n\nUser.objects.all().delete()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#5-aggregation-group-by-having","title":"\ud83d\udfe3 5. AGGREGATION (GROUP BY / HAVING)","text":""},{"location":"ORM/django.html#group-by","title":"GROUP BY","text":"<pre><code>Order.objects.values(\"user_id\")\n     .annotate(total=Sum(\"amount\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#having","title":"HAVING","text":"<pre><code>Order.objects.values(\"user_id\")\n     .annotate(total=Sum(\"amount\"))\n     .filter(total__gt=100)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#aggregates","title":"Aggregates","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.aggregate(Max(\"age\"))\n\nUser.objects.aggregate(Min(\"age\"))\n\nUser.objects.aggregate(Avg(\"age\"))\n\nOrder.objects.aggregate(Sum(\"amount\"))\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#6-date-range-filters","title":"\u26a1 6. DATE / RANGE FILTERS","text":"<pre><code>User.objects.filter(age__range=(18, 30))\n\nUser.objects.filter(created_at__date=\"2025-01-01\")\n\nUser.objects.filter(created_at__year=2025)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/django.html#how-to-remember-interview-tip","title":"\u2705 HOW TO REMEMBER (INTERVIEW TIP)","text":"<pre><code>CREATE  \u2192 create(), save(), bulk_create()\nREAD    \u2192 filter(), get(), exclude(), order_by()\nUPDATE  \u2192 update(), save(), F()\nDELETE  \u2192 delete()\nGROUP   \u2192 values() + annotate() + filter()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html","title":"Flask","text":""},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#flask-orm","title":"Flask ORM","text":""},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#1-create-table","title":"1. Create Table","text":"<pre><code>from flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy\n\ndb = SQLAlchemy()\n\nclass User(db.Model):\n    __tablename__ = 'users'\n\n    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)\n    name = db.Column(db.String(100))\n    email = db.Column(db.String(150))\n    password = db.Column(db.String(100))\n    mobile = db.Column(db.String(15))\n    gender = db.Column(db.Enum('M', 'F', 'O'))\n    dob = db.Column(db.Date)\n    status = db.Column(db.Boolean)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#1-select-data","title":"1. Select data","text":"<pre><code>User.query.filter_by(gender='F').all()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#2-operators","title":"2. Operators","text":"<ul> <li> <p>IN Operator:   The <code>IN</code> operator is used to filter querysets based on whether a field's value is within a given list of values.     </p><pre><code>    User.query.filter(User.gender.in_(['M', 'F'])).all()\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> <li> <p>LIKE Operator: </p><pre><code>    matching_users = User.query.filter(or_(User.name.ilike('%John%'), User.email.ilike('%example.com%'))).all()\n    for user in matching_users:\n        print(user.name, user.email)  # Accessing user attributes\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#3-order-by","title":"3. ORDER BY","text":"<pre><code>User.query.order_by(User.name.asc()).all() # Return asceding order record\nUser.query.order_by(User.name.desc()).all() # Return descending order record\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#4-is-null-and-is-not-null","title":"4. IS NULL and IS NOT NULL","text":"<ul> <li> <p>IS NULL: </p><pre><code>    User.query.filter(User.mobile == null()).all()\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> <li> <p>IS NOT NULL: </p><pre><code>    User.query.filter(User.mobile.isnot(None)).all()\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#4-update-and-delete","title":"4. Update and Delete","text":"<ul> <li> <p>Update: </p><pre><code>    # Update the age for the student with id=4\n    student = Student.query.filter_by(id=4).first()\n    student.age = 25\n    db.session.commit()\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> <li> <p>Delete: </p><pre><code>    # Delete the student with id=4\n    student = Student.query.filter_by(id=4).first()\n    db.session.delete(student)\n    db.session.commit()\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"ORM/flask.html#4-group-by-and-having","title":"4. GROUP BY and HAVING","text":"<ul> <li> <p>GROUP BY:     GROUP BY in SQLAlchemy ORM is achieved using the group_by() method to specify the fields you want to group your query results by. It organizes the results into groups based on one or more columns.   </p><pre><code>    from sqlalchemy import func\n\n    query = db.session.query(\n        Order.customer_id, func.sum(Order.quantity).label('total_quantity')\n    ).group_by(Order.customer_id)\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> <li> <p>HAVING:     HAVING in SQLAlchemy ORM is performed using the having() method to apply conditions on the aggregated results, similar to the SQL HAVING clause.     </p><pre><code>    from sqlalchemy import func\n    query = db.session.query(Order.customer_id, func.sum(Order.quantity).label('total_quantity'))\n            .group_by(Order.customer_id).having(func.sum(Order.quantity) &gt; 100)\n</code></pre><p></p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html","title":"Async Programming","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#1-subroutine-normal-function-one-road-one-car","title":"\ud83d\udea6 1. SUBROUTINE (Normal Function) \u2014 One Road, One Car","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#flow","title":"Flow","text":"<pre><code>Main Program\n   |\n   --&gt; call function A\n           |\n           --&gt; function B\n                |\n                return to A\n           |\n       return to Main\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#meaning","title":"Meaning","text":"<ul> <li>Executes top \u2192 bottom.</li> <li>Only one function runs at a time.</li> <li>Cannot pause in the middle.</li> <li>No switching between functions.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#example","title":"Example","text":"<pre><code>def sub():\n    print(\"Run completely\")\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Mental model: \u27a1\ufe0f One straight road. One car. No overtaking.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#2-coroutine-function-that-can-pause-resume","title":"\ud83c\udf00 2. COROUTINE \u2014 Function That Can PAUSE &amp; RESUME","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#flow_1","title":"Flow","text":"<pre><code>Main Program\n   |\n   --&gt; coroutine A starts\n           |\n           yield (pause here)\n           |\n   (control returns to Main)\n   |\nMain resumes coroutine A later\n           |\n           continues from where paused\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#meaning_1","title":"Meaning","text":"<ul> <li>Provides cooperative multitasking.</li> <li>Can pause (yield) and resume.</li> <li>Not automatic; you must resume manually.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#example_1","title":"Example","text":"<pre><code>def coroutine():\n    yield \"Paused here\"\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Mental model: \u27a1\ufe0f A car stops on the road, waits, then continues later.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#3-concurrency-switching-tasks-without-waiting","title":"\u2699\ufe0f 3. CONCURRENCY \u2014 Switching Tasks Without Waiting","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#flow_2","title":"Flow","text":"<pre><code>Task A runs\n    |\n    (task waits for I/O)\n    |\nSwitch to Task B\n    |\nTask B waits\n    |\nSwitch back to Task A\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#meaning_2","title":"Meaning","text":"<ul> <li>Helps when tasks wait for I/O (API calls, DB, file read).</li> <li>Python can interleave tasks.</li> <li>Doesn\u2019t run two CPU-heavy tasks at the same time \u2014 that\u2019s parallelism.</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc Mental model: \u27a1\ufe0f One worker doing many tasks by switching whenever one is waiting.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#4-async-await-keywords-to-create-manage-coroutines","title":"\ud83d\udd11 4. async / await \u2014 Keywords to Create &amp; Manage Coroutines","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#flow_3","title":"Flow","text":"<pre><code>async def taskA():\n   await something  --&gt; pause here\n                      |\nMain loop moves to taskB\n                      |\nTaskA resumes when result is ready\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#meaning_3","title":"Meaning","text":"<ul> <li><code>async</code> creates a coroutine function.</li> <li><code>await</code> pauses execution until awaited work completes.</li> <li>Python switches to another task while waiting.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#example_2","title":"Example","text":"<pre><code>async def download():\n    await network_call()\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Mental model: \u27a1\ufe0f \u201cawait\u201d = pause and give chance to others.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#5-asyncio-module-the-manager-event-loop","title":"\ud83e\udde0 5. asyncio Module \u2014 The Manager (Event Loop)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#flow_4","title":"Flow","text":"<pre><code>Event Loop\n    |\n    |-- schedules coroutine A\n    |\n    |-- coroutine A hits await -&gt; paused\n    |\n    |-- schedules coroutine B\n    |\n    |-- coroutine B completes\n    |\n    |-- returns to coroutine A\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#meaning_4","title":"Meaning","text":"<ul> <li>Central brain that manages all async coroutines.</li> <li>Switches tasks when they are waiting.</li> <li> <p>Provides:</p> </li> <li> <p><code>asyncio.create_task()</code></p> </li> <li><code>asyncio.run()</code></li> <li>Event loop</li> <li>Awaitable I/O functions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#example_3","title":"Example","text":"<pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    task1 = asyncio.create_task(job1())\n    task2 = asyncio.create_task(job2())\n    await task1\n    await task2\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Mental model: \u27a1\ufe0f Event loop = traffic controller that decides which task runs next.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Async_Programming.html#all-concepts-in-one-flow","title":"\ud83c\udfaf ALL CONCEPTS IN ONE FLOW","text":"<pre><code>Subroutine \u2192 runs one function fully.\n\nCoroutine \u2192 can pause and resume manually.\n\nConcurrency \u2192 multiple tasks switch when waiting (not at same time).\n\nasync/await \u2192 special syntax to write coroutines easily.\n\nasyncio \u2192 event loop manages async switching automatically.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html","title":"Celery","text":""},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#what-is-celery","title":"\ud83c\udf1f What is Celery?","text":"<p>Celery is a background task queue system. It allows you to run tasks asynchronously, in the background, or on a schedule.</p> <p>In simple words:</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Your API / App sends work to Celery \ud83d\udc49 Celery runs it separately in background \ud83d\udc49 Your API remains fast and free</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#why-do-we-need-celery","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Why do we need Celery?","text":"<p>Without Celery, long tasks will block API and make users wait.</p> <p>Example long tasks:</p> <ul> <li>Sending Emails</li> <li>Processing files</li> <li>Generating reports</li> <li>Database cleanup</li> <li>Machine learning jobs</li> <li>API scheduling</li> <li>Video/Image processing</li> </ul> <p>Celery moves these tasks out of your API, so the app stays fast.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#how-celery-works-full-flow-explained","title":"\ud83c\udf1f How Celery Works (Full Flow Explained)","text":"<p>Here is the exact flow you should say in an interview:</p> <pre><code>Step 1: User makes API request\nStep 2: API sends a task to Message Broker (Redis/RabbitMQ)\nStep 3: Celery Worker listens to the broker and picks task\nStep 4: Worker executes the task in background\nStep 5: Worker sends task result to Result Backend (Redis/DB)\nStep 6: API can show result later (optional)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#architecture-diagram-very-easy","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Architecture Diagram (Very Easy)","text":"<pre><code>              \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n              \u2502          FastAPI / Django    \u2502\n              \u2502        (Your Main App)       \u2502\n              \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                             \u2502 Sends Task\n                             \u25bc\n                 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                 \u2502     Message Broker      \u2502\n                 \u2502   Redis / RabbitMQ      \u2502\n                 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                              \u2502\n                    Worker Reads Task\n                              \u25bc\n              \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n              \u2502         Celery Worker         \u2502\n              \u2502    (Executes Tasks in BG)     \u2502\n              \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                              \u2502 Stores Result\n                              \u25bc\n                 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                 \u2502      Result Backend      \u2502\n                 \u2502 Redis / DB / S3 / File   \u2502\n                 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#key-components-of-celery-interview-explanation","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Key Components of Celery (Interview Explanation)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#1-task","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Task","text":"<p>A Python function executed in background.</p> <pre><code>@app.task\ndef send_email():\n    ...\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#2-broker-redisrabbitmq","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Broker (Redis/RabbitMQ)","text":"<p>Stores tasks temporarily like a queue.</p> <p>Think of it as a delivery boy standing with tasks.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#3-worker","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Worker","text":"<p>Celery worker is the background executor. It takes tasks from broker and runs them.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#4-result-backend","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Result Backend","text":"<p>Stores results so you can check task status.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#one-line-explanation-for-interview","title":"\ud83c\udf1f One-Line Explanation for Interview","text":"<p>Celery = Task Queue + Background Worker + Scheduling system.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#when-to-use-celery","title":"\ud83c\udf1f When to Use Celery?","text":"<p>Use Celery when:</p> <p>\u2714 You have long-running tasks \u2714 You want async processing \u2714 You want scheduled jobs \u2714 You want retry logic \u2714 You want distributed workers \u2714 You want scalable background processing</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#where-not-to-use-celery","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Where NOT to use Celery?","text":"<p>\u274c Small apps with tiny background tasks \u274c CPU-heavy tasks without multiprocessing \u274c If tasks need extremely high throughput \u2192 use Kafka/Streaming</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#real-life-example-explain-to-interviewer","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Real-Life Example (Explain to Interviewer)","text":"<p>Example: User uploads large video to your FastAPI app.</p> <p>Without Celery:</p> <ul> <li>API hangs for 2 minutes</li> <li>User gets bad experience</li> </ul> <p>With Celery:</p> <ol> <li>API uploads file</li> <li>API creates Celery task: \u201cProcess video\u201d</li> <li>Returns response instantly:    \u201cYour video is being processed\u201d</li> <li>Celery worker processes video in background</li> <li>Backend saves result</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#celery-task-flow-simple-example","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Celery Task Flow (Simple Example)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#1-fastapi-receives-request","title":"1. FastAPI receives request","text":"<pre><code>POST /process-video\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#2-fastapi-sends-celery-task","title":"2. FastAPI sends Celery task","text":"<pre><code>task_id = process_video.delay(file_path)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#3-celery-worker-picks-task","title":"3. Celery Worker picks task","text":"<pre><code>Working on task process_video\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#4-worker-finishes-task","title":"4. Worker finishes task","text":"<pre><code>Task Completed.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#5-fastapi-can-check-status","title":"5. FastAPI can check status","text":"<pre><code>GET /task-status/{task_id}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#celery-features-simple-points","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Celery Features (Simple Points)","text":"<p>\u2714 Asynchronous Tasks \u2714 Scheduled Tasks (Cron jobs) \u2714 Retries if task fails \u2714 Timeouts \u2714 Priority queues \u2714 Multiple workers \u2714 Monitoring with Flower</p>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#simple-celery-fastapi-example","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Simple Celery + FastAPI Example","text":""},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#celery_apppy","title":"celery_app.py","text":"<pre><code>from celery import Celery\n\ncelery_app = Celery(\n    'worker',\n    broker='redis://localhost:6379/0',\n    backend='redis://localhost:6379/1'\n)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#taskspy","title":"tasks.py","text":"<pre><code>from .celery_app import celery_app\nimport time\n\n@celery_app.task\ndef add(a, b):\n    time.sleep(5)\n    return a + b\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#mainpy-fastapi","title":"main.py (FastAPI)","text":"<pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom .tasks import add\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(\"/add\")\ndef add_numbers(a: int, b: int):\n    task = add.delay(a, b)\n    return {\"task_id\": task.id}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#how-to-run-celery","title":"\ud83c\udf1f How to Run Celery","text":""},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#run-celery-worker","title":"Run Celery worker:","text":"<pre><code>celery -A tasks worker --loglevel=info\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#how-to-track-task-status","title":"\ud83c\udf1f How to Track Task Status","text":"<pre><code>@app.get(\"/status/{task_id}\")\ndef get_status(task_id):\n    result = add.AsyncResult(task_id)\n    return {\"status\": result.status, \"result\": result.result}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Celery.html#perfect-interview-answer-say-this","title":"\ud83c\udf1f Perfect Interview Answer (Say This):","text":"<p>Celery is a distributed task queue used to run background jobs asynchronously. It works using four components: the main application produces tasks, the message broker stores them, Celery workers execute them, and the result backend stores the results. It\u2019s ideal for long-running or heavy tasks such as sending emails, processing files, reports, ML tasks, and scheduled jobs. Celery helps keep APIs responsive because heavy tasks move out of the request cycle and run independently in the background.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html","title":"Decorators","text":""},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#python-decorators-simple-guide","title":"\ud83c\udf80 Python Decorators \u2013 Simple Guide","text":""},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#definition","title":"\u2705 Definition","text":"<p>A decorator is a function that adds extra functionality to another function without changing its code.</p> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Think of a decorator like adding toppings to a pizza \u2014 the base remains the same, but you add flavor (extra behavior)!</p>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#basic-syntax","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Basic Syntax","text":"<pre><code>def decorator_function(original_function):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(\"Extra code before\")\n        original_function()\n        print(\"Extra code after\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@decorator_function\ndef say_hello():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n\nsay_hello()\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udd38 Output:</p> <pre><code>Extra code before\nHello!\nExtra code after\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#real-world-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Real-World Use Cases","text":"Use Case What Decorator Does \u2705 Logging Logs function calls \u23f1\ufe0f Timing Measures function execution time \ud83d\udd12 Authentication Checks user login before running a function \u274c Error Handling Catches and handles exceptions automatically"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#example-1-logging-decorator","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Example 1: Logging Decorator","text":"<pre><code>def log_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(f\"Calling {func.__name__}\")\n        func()\n        print(f\"Finished {func.__name__}\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@log_decorator\ndef greet():\n    print(\"Hi there!\")\n\ngreet()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#example-2-timing-decorator","title":"\u23f1\ufe0f Example 2: Timing Decorator","text":"<pre><code>import time\n\ndef timer_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        start = time.time()\n        func()\n        end = time.time()\n        print(f\"Took {end - start:.2f} seconds\")\n    return wrapper\n\n@timer_decorator\ndef slow_function():\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(\"Done\")\n\nslow_function()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#example-3-decorator-with-arguments","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Example 3: Decorator with Arguments","text":"<pre><code>def repeat(n):\n    def decorator(func):\n        def wrapper():\n            for _ in range(n):\n                func()\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@repeat(3)\ndef hello():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n\nhello()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#example-decorator-for-rate-limit-using-redis","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Example : Decorator for Rate limit using redis","text":"<pre><code>import time\nimport redis\n\n# Connect to Redis (Make sure your Redis server is running!)\ndb = redis.Redis(host='localhost', port=6379, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef rate_limit(max_calls, timespan):\n    def decorator(func):\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            key = f\"limit:{func.__name__}\"\n            now = time.time()\n\n            # 1. Delete timestamps older than the window (e.g., older than 60s)\n            db.zremrangebyscore(key, 0, now - timespan)\n\n            # 2. Check if we are still under the limit\n            if db.zcard(key) &lt; max_calls:\n                db.zadd(key, {str(now): now}) # Log this call\n                return func(*args, **kwargs)\n\n            # 3. Otherwise, block\n            print(f\"\u274c Limit reached! (Max {max_calls} calls per {timespan}s)\")\n\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n# --- Usage ---\n@rate_limit(max_calls=5, timespan=60)\ndef access_system():\n    print(\"\u2705 Success: System accessed.\")\n\n# Test it\naccess_system()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#example-decorator-for-rate-limit-using-local-system","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Example : Decorator for Rate limit using local system","text":""},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#import-time-def-rate_limitmax_calls-window-users-def-decoratorfunc-def-wrapperuser_id-now-inttimetime-use-integer-seconds-count-start-usersgetuser_id-0-now-reset-window-if-expired-if-now-start-window-count-start-0-now-check-limit-if-count-max_calls-printf-user-user_id-limit-exceeded-return-update-count-usersuser_id-count-1-start-return-funcuser_id-return-wrapper-return-decorator-rate_limitmax_calls2-window10-max-2-calls-per-10-seconds-def-hellouser_id-printf-user-user_id-access-allowed-hellouser1-hellouser1-hellouser1-blocked-hellouser1-blocked","title":"<pre><code>import time\n\ndef rate_limit(max_calls, window):\n    users = {}\n\n    def decorator(func):\n        def wrapper(user_id):\n            now = int(time.time())  # use integer seconds\n            count, start = users.get(user_id, (0, now))\n\n            # reset window if expired\n            if now - start &gt;= window:\n                count, start = 0, now\n\n            # check limit\n            if count &gt;= max_calls:\n                print(f\"\u274c User {user_id}: limit exceeded\")\n                return\n\n            # update count\n            users[user_id] = (count + 1, start)\n            return func(user_id)\n\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@rate_limit(max_calls=2, window=10)  # max 2 calls per 10 seconds\ndef hello(user_id):\n    print(f\"\u2705 User {user_id}: access allowed\")\n\nhello(\"user1\")\nhello(\"user1\")\nhello(\"user1\")  # blocked\nhello(\"user1\")  # blocked\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"Python/Decorators.html#summary","title":"\u2705 Summary","text":"Concept Description <code>@decorator</code> Adds functionality to a function <code>def wrapper()</code> Wraps the original function <code>return wrapper</code> Returns the modified behavior"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html","title":"File Handling","text":""},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#topic-json-csv-xml-yaml","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Topic: JSON / CSV / XML / YAML","text":"Format What it is Use Case Example JSON Data format (JavaScript Object Notation) APIs, config files <code>json.load()</code>, <code>json.dump()</code> CSV Comma-separated values Excel exports, flat databases <code>csv.reader()</code>, <code>csv.writer()</code> XML Markup language Data from legacy APIs or configs <code>xml.etree.ElementTree</code> YAML Human-friendly config format Docker, Kubernetes, CI/CD configs <code>yaml.safe_load()</code> (with PyYAML)"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#json-example","title":"\u2705 JSON Example:","text":"<pre><code>import json\n\ndata = {\"name\": \"Alice\", \"age\": 25}\nwith open(\"data.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(data, f)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#topic-log-file-processing","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Topic: Log File Processing","text":"Description Use Case Example Reading <code>.log</code> or <code>.txt</code> files Monitor, parse, or extract logs Find error lines or user activity"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#example","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>with open(\"app.log\") as log:\n    for line in log:\n        if \"ERROR\" in line:\n            print(line)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#topic-file-compression","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Topic: File Compression","text":"Method Description Use Case Example <code>.zip</code>, <code>.gz</code>, <code>.tar</code> Compress or decompress files Backup, send, extract files <code>zipfile</code>, <code>gzip</code>, <code>tarfile</code> modules"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#zip-example","title":"\u2705 Zip Example:","text":"<pre><code>import zipfile\n\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(\"archive.zip\", \"w\") as zipf:\n    zipf.write(\"data.txt\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#topic-path-handling","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Topic: Path Handling","text":"Tool Description Use Case Example <code>os.path</code>, <code>pathlib</code> Handle file and folder paths Cross-platform path building, checking <code>Path.exists()</code>, <code>os.path.join()</code>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#example-with-pathlib","title":"\u2705 Example with <code>pathlib</code>:","text":"<pre><code>from pathlib import Path\n\nfile = Path(\"data.txt\")\nif file.exists():\n    print(\"File found!\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#topic-binary-files-buffers","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Topic: Binary Files / Buffers","text":"Description Use Case Example Read/write non-text files (images, audio, etc.) Work with low-level data or byte streams Use <code>'rb'</code> / <code>'wb'</code> modes in <code>open()</code>"},{"location":"Python/File_Handling.html#example_1","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>with open(\"image.png\", \"rb\") as img:\n    content = img.read()\n    print(len(content))  # Bytes read\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html","title":"File I/O","text":""},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#python-file-io-summary","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Python File I/O \u2013 Summary","text":""},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#definition","title":"\u2705 Definition","text":"<p>File I/O (Input/Output) in Python is the process of reading from and writing to files like <code>.txt</code>, <code>.csv</code>, <code>.xlsx</code>, and <code>.pdf</code>.</p>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#common-methods","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Common Methods","text":"Method / Function Purpose <code>open(filename, mode)</code> Opens a file (e.g. <code>r</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>a</code>) <code>file.read()</code> Reads entire content <code>file.readline()</code> Reads a single line <code>file.readlines()</code> Reads all lines into a list <code>file.write(data)</code> Writes data <code>file.close()</code> Closes the file <code>with open(...)</code> Safely opens and auto-closes file"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#use-cases-sample-examples","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Use Cases + Sample Examples","text":""},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#1-txt-file-notes-logs","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>.txt</code> File \u2013 Notes, Logs","text":"<p>\ud83d\udccc Use Case: Store or read a to-do list</p> <pre><code># Write to a .txt file\nwith open(\"todo.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"1. Buy milk\\n2. Call friend\")\n\n# Read from a .txt file\nwith open(\"todo.txt\", \"r\") as f:\n    print(f.read())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#2-csv-file-tabular-data-eg-marks-sales","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>.csv</code> File \u2013 Tabular Data (e.g. marks, sales)","text":"<p>\ud83d\udccc Use Case: Store student scores</p> <pre><code>import csv\n\n# Write CSV\nwith open(\"students.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\") as f:\n    writer = csv.writer(f)\n    writer.writerow([\"Name\", \"Score\"])\n    writer.writerow([\"Alice\", 90])\n    writer.writerow([\"Bob\", 85])\n\n# Read CSV\nwith open(\"students.csv\", \"r\") as f:\n    reader = csv.reader(f)\n    for row in reader:\n        print(row)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#3-xlsx-file-excel-reports","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>.xlsx</code> File \u2013 Excel Reports","text":"<p>\ud83d\udccc Use Case: Generate report card</p> <pre><code>import openpyxl\n\n# Write Excel\nwb = openpyxl.Workbook()\nws = wb.active\nws.append([\"Name\", \"Math\"])\nws.append([\"Tom\", 88])\nwb.save(\"report.xlsx\")\n\n# Read Excel\nwb = openpyxl.load_workbook(\"report.xlsx\")\nws = wb.active\nfor row in ws.iter_rows(values_only=True):\n    print(row)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Install with: <code>pip install openpyxl</code></p>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#4-pdf-file-read-documents","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 <code>.pdf</code> File \u2013 Read Documents","text":"<p>\ud83d\udccc Use Case: Extract text from a PDF</p> <pre><code>import PyPDF2\n\nwith open(\"sample.pdf\", \"rb\") as f:\n    reader = PyPDF2.PdfReader(f)\n    for page in reader.pages:\n        print(page.extract_text())\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Install with: <code>pip install PyPDF2</code></p>"},{"location":"Python/File_I_O.html#summary-table","title":"\ud83e\uddfe Summary Table","text":"File Type Use Case Python Tools <code>.txt</code> Notes, logs Built-in <code>open</code> <code>.csv</code> Data tables <code>csv</code> module <code>.xlsx</code> Excel reports <code>openpyxl</code> <code>.pdf</code> Read documents <code>PyPDF2</code>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html","title":"GIL","text":""},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#python-gil-flow-wise-super-easy-explanation","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python GIL \u2014 Flow Wise, Super Easy Explanation","text":""},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#1-python-runs-using-cpython","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Python runs using CPython","text":"<ul> <li>Most Python you write runs on CPython \u2192 the default Python.</li> <li>CPython has a special lock inside it \u2192 GIL.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#2-what-is-gil","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 What is GIL?","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 GIL = Global Interpreter Lock \ud83d\udc49 It is a big lock that allows ONLY ONE THREAD to run Python code at a time.</p> <p>Imagine: You have 4 workers (threads) but only 1 key (GIL). Only the worker with the key can work. Others wait.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#3-why-does-python-need-this-lock","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Why does Python need this lock?","text":"<p>Because Python uses reference counting for memory. Whenever a variable is created, changed, or deleted \u2192 Python updates its memory count.</p> <p>If multiple threads update that memory at the same time, it can break the program.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd12 So Python created GIL = one-at-a-time safety.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#4-how-gil-works-internally","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 How GIL works internally","text":"<p>Flow:</p> <pre><code>Thread wants to run code \u2192 Tries to take GIL\nIf it gets GIL \u2192 Runs code\nAfter some time \u2192 Releases GIL\nNext thread gets GIL \u2192 Runs\nNext \u2192 Runs\n</code></pre> <p>\u26a0\ufe0f No two threads can run Python code together.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#5-when-is-gil-a-problem","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 When is GIL a problem?","text":"<p>When you do CPU-heavy work, like:</p> <ul> <li>image processing</li> <li>huge calculations</li> <li>loops with heavy logic</li> <li>compression</li> <li>encryption</li> </ul> <p>Even if you create 10 threads, only 1 thread runs at a time.</p> <p>\u274c CPU-heavy + multithreading \u2260 Faster in Python \u2714\ufe0f They wait for GIL, so speed stays the same.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#6-when-gil-is-not-a-problem","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 When GIL is NOT a problem?","text":"<p>When your program does I/O work, like:</p> <ul> <li>database calls</li> <li>file read/write</li> <li>network requests</li> <li>API calls</li> <li>waiting for data</li> </ul> <p>During I/O \u2192 Python releases GIL automatically. So other threads can run.</p> <p>\u2714\ufe0f I/O-heavy + threads = Good \u2714\ufe0f Example: FastAPI, Django, Scraping, Bots</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#7-simple-example-to-visualize","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Simple Example to Visualize","text":""},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#without-gil-ideal-world","title":"Without GIL (Ideal World)","text":"<pre><code>Thread 1 \u2192 Core 1  (runs)\nThread 2 \u2192 Core 2  (runs)\nThread 3 \u2192 Core 3  (runs)\nThread 4 \u2192 Core 4  (runs)\n</code></pre> <p>All run in parallel.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#with-python-gil","title":"With Python GIL","text":"<pre><code>Thread 1 \u2192 gets GIL \u2192 runs\nThread 2 \u2192 waits\nThread 3 \u2192 waits\nThread 4 \u2192 waits\n</code></pre> <p>One-at-a-time behavior.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#8-how-to-bypass-gil","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 How to bypass GIL?","text":"<p>To use all CPU cores:</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#use-multiprocessing","title":"\u2714 Use Multiprocessing","text":"<p>Each process \u2192 has its own GIL So processes run in parallel.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#use-c-libraries-numpy-opencv","title":"\u2714 Use C libraries (NumPy, OpenCV)","text":"<p>They release GIL internally.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#use-asyncawait-for-io","title":"\u2714 Use async/await for I/O","text":"<p>No blocking, no extra threads needed.</p>"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#9-super-simple-summary","title":"9\ufe0f\u20e3 Super Simple Summary","text":"Topic Easy Explanation GIL Only 1 thread can run Python code at a time Why To protect memory from corruption Good for I/O tasks (APIs, DB, file, network) Bad for CPU-heavy + threads Solution Use multiprocessing / C extensions / async"},{"location":"Python/GIL_Lock.html#final-flow-diagram-like-story","title":"\ud83d\udd1f Final Flow Diagram (Like Story)","text":"<pre><code>Python program starts\n      \u2193\nInterpreter (CPython) loads\n      \u2193\nGIL activated (single lock)\n      \u2193\nThread tries to run code\n      \u2193\nDoes it have GIL?\n      \u2193       \u2193\n   YES        NO\n Runs code   Wait\n      \u2193\nDone or yield\n      \u2193\nRelease GIL\n      \u2193\nNext thread continues\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html","title":"Generators & Iterators","text":""},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#python-generators-iterators","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Python Generators &amp; Iterators","text":""},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#definition","title":"\u2705 Definition","text":""},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#iterator","title":"\ud83d\udccc Iterator:","text":"<p>An iterator is an object that can be looped through, one element at a time.</p> <p>Think of it like a TV remote \u2014 you press \"next\" to go to the next channel.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#generator","title":"\ud83d\udccc Generator:","text":"<p>A generator is a special function that remembers its state and gives you one value at a time using the <code>yield</code> keyword.</p> <p>Think of it like a water tap \u2014 it gives water (data) only when asked.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#common-methods","title":"\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Common Methods","text":"Method / Concept Description <code>iter(obj)</code> Turns an iterable into an iterator <code>next(iterator)</code> Gets the next value from an iterator <code>yield</code> Used in a generator function to return a value"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#iterator-example","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Iterator \u2013 Example","text":"<pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 3]\nit = iter(nums)\n\nprint(next(it))  # 1\nprint(next(it))  # 2\nprint(next(it))  # 3\n# print(next(it))  # Error: StopIteration\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#generator-example","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Generator \u2013 Example","text":"<pre><code>def count_up_to(max):\n    count = 1\n    while count &lt;= max:\n        yield count\n        count += 1\n\ngen = count_up_to(3)\n\nprint(next(gen))  # 1\nprint(next(gen))  # 2\nprint(next(gen))  # 3\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#use-cases","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Use Cases","text":"Concept Real-World Use Case Iterator Looping over items like list, tuple, file lines Generator Large data processing, streaming, lazy loading"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#generator-use-case-reading-a-large-file-line-by-line","title":"\u2705 Generator Use Case: Reading a large file line by line","text":"<pre><code>def read_large_file(file_path):\n    with open(file_path, \"r\") as file:\n        for line in file:\n            yield line\n\nfor line in read_large_file(\"bigfile.txt\"):\n    print(line.strip())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Generators_Iterators.html#differences-between-iterator-generator","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Differences Between Iterator &amp; Generator","text":"Feature Iterator Generator How to create Using <code>iter()</code> Using <code>def</code> + <code>yield</code> Memory usage Can be high (stores data) Low (generates one at a time) State saving Manual Automatic (resumes from last <code>yield</code>)"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html","title":"JWT Token","text":""},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#what-is-jwt-in-simple-words","title":"\ud83d\udd10 What is JWT (in simple words)?","text":"<p>JWT (JSON Web Token) is a digital ID card issued by the server after login.</p> <p>Once issued:</p> <ul> <li>Client shows this ID card on every request</li> <li>Server verifies it</li> <li>No need to log in again</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#real-life-analogy","title":"\ud83c\udf0d Real-life analogy","text":"<p>Think of JWT like a metro card \ud83c\udfab</p> <ol> <li>You login \u2192 you get a metro card</li> <li>Every station \u2192 you show the card</li> <li> <p>Station checks:</p> </li> <li> <p>Is it valid?</p> </li> <li>Is it expired?</li> <li>If yes \u2192 allow entry</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#jwt-structure-dont-memorize-just-understand","title":"\ud83e\udde0 JWT structure (don\u2019t memorize, just understand)","text":"<p>JWT = <code>HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE</code></p> <p>Example:</p> <pre><code>eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.\neyJ1c2VyX2lkIjoxLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4iLCJleHAiOjE3MDAwMDAwMDB9.\nxYz...\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#1-header","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Header","text":"<ul> <li>Token type (JWT)</li> <li>Algorithm (HS256 / RS256)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#2-payload","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Payload","text":"<ul> <li>User data (not secret!)</li> </ul> <pre><code>{\n  \"user_id\": 1,\n  \"role\": \"admin\",\n  \"exp\": 1700000000\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#3-signature-most-important","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Signature (MOST IMPORTANT)","text":"<p>Created using:</p> <pre><code>Header + Payload + Secret Key\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Prevents tampering</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#jwt-flow-in-a-live-application","title":"\ud83d\udd01 JWT flow in a LIVE application","text":""},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#step-1-login","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 1: Login","text":"<p>User sends:</p> <pre><code>POST /login\nemail + password\n</code></pre> <p>Server:</p> <ul> <li>Validates credentials</li> <li>Creates JWT</li> <li>Sends it back</li> </ul> <pre><code>{\n  \"access_token\": \"jwt_token_here\"\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#step-2-store-token-client-side","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 2: Store token (Client side)","text":"<ul> <li>Web \u2192 LocalStorage / HttpOnly Cookie</li> <li>Mobile \u2192 Secure storage</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#step-3-use-token-on-every-request","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 3: Use token on every request","text":"<p>Client sends token in Authorization header:</p> <pre><code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;JWT_TOKEN&gt;\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#step-4-server-validates-token","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Step 4: Server validates token","text":"<p>Server checks:</p> <ol> <li>\u2705 Signature is valid</li> <li>\u2705 Token not expired</li> <li>\u2705 Token not altered</li> <li>\u2705 User exists</li> <li>\u2705 Role/permissions allowed</li> </ol> <p>If all pass \u2192 request allowed</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#how-jwt-provides-security","title":"\ud83d\udd10 How JWT provides SECURITY","text":""},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#1-no-password-sent-again","title":"\u2705 1. No password sent again","text":"<p>Only token is sent \u2192 safer</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#2-signature-prevents-tampering","title":"\u2705 2. Signature prevents tampering","text":"<p>If attacker changes:</p> <pre><code>\"role\": \"admin\"\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Signature becomes invalid \u2192 request rejected</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#3-expiry-time","title":"\u2705 3. Expiry time","text":"<p>Token auto-expires:</p> <pre><code>\"exp\": 1700000000\n</code></pre> <p>Even if stolen \u2192 usable only for short time</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#4-stateless-authentication","title":"\u2705 4. Stateless authentication","text":"<ul> <li>Server doesn\u2019t store sessions</li> <li>Easy to scale (microservices)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#access-token-vs-refresh-token-live-systems","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Access Token vs Refresh Token (LIVE systems)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#access-token","title":"Access Token","text":"<ul> <li>Short life (5\u201315 minutes)</li> <li>Used for API calls</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#refresh-token","title":"Refresh Token","text":"<ul> <li>Long life (7\u201330 days)</li> <li>Used to generate new access token</li> </ul> <p>Flow:</p> <pre><code>Access token expired \u2192 send refresh token \u2192 get new access token\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#extra-production-security-interview-gold","title":"\ud83d\udd12 Extra production security (interview gold \u2b50)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#use-https","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Use HTTPS","text":"<p>JWT must never go over HTTP</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#use-httponly-cookies-web","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Use HttpOnly Cookies (Web)","text":"<p>Prevents XSS attacks</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#token-blacklisting-logout","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Token Blacklisting (Logout)","text":"<p>Store refresh tokens in DB/Redis Invalidate on logout</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#role-based-access","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Role-based access","text":"<pre><code>if user.role != \"admin\":\n    deny access\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#common-mistakes-mention-in-interview","title":"\u26a0\ufe0f Common mistakes (mention in interview)","text":"<p>\u274c Storing secrets in payload \u274c Long-lived access tokens \u274c No refresh token \u274c Sending JWT in query params</p>"},{"location":"Python/Jwt_token.html#mini-fastapi-example-simple","title":"\ud83e\uddea Mini FastAPI example (simple)","text":"<pre><code>from fastapi import Depends, HTTPException\nfrom jose import jwt, JWTError\n\ndef verify_token(token: str):\n    try:\n        payload = jwt.decode(token, SECRET_KEY, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n        return payload\n    except JWTError:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html","title":"Lambda & Functional","text":""},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#lambda-functional-programming-easy-guide","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Lambda &amp; Functional Programming \u2013 Easy Guide","text":""},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#what-is-a-lambda-function","title":"\u2705 What is a Lambda Function?","text":"<p>A lambda is a small, one-line anonymous function (no name) used when you need a quick function for a short task.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#syntax","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Syntax:","text":"<pre><code>lambda arguments: expression\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#example","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>add = lambda x, y: x + y\nprint(add(2, 3))  # Output: 5\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#when-to-use-lambda","title":"\ud83c\udfaf When to Use Lambda?","text":"<ul> <li>With functions like <code>map()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>sorted()</code>, etc.</li> <li>When you need a function for one-time use</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#functional-programming","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Functional Programming","text":"<p>Python supports functional programming using:</p> Function Purpose <code>map()</code> Apply a function to every item in a list <code>filter()</code> Keep items that return <code>True</code> <code>reduce()</code> Combine all items to a single value <code>lambda</code> Create quick functions"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#examples","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Examples","text":""},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#1-map-lambda","title":"\u2705 1. <code>map()</code> + lambda","text":"<pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 3]\nsquared = list(map(lambda x: x*x, nums))\nprint(squared)  # [1, 4, 9]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#2-filter-lambda","title":"\u2705 2. <code>filter()</code> + lambda","text":"<pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\neven = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, nums))\nprint(even)  # [2, 4]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#3-reduce-lambda","title":"\u2705 3. <code>reduce()</code> + lambda","text":"<pre><code>from functools import reduce\n\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ntotal = reduce(lambda x, y: x + y, nums)\nprint(total)  # 10\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Lambda_Functional_Programming.html#summary","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Summary","text":"Concept Description <code>lambda</code> Short one-line function <code>map()</code> Apply function to all items <code>filter()</code> Filter items using a condition <code>reduce()</code> Reduce list to a single value"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html","title":"Memory Management","text":""},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#python-memory-management-made-simple","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python Memory Management \u2013 Made Simple","text":""},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#definition","title":"\u2705 Definition","text":"<p>Memory Management in Python is the process of:</p> <p>Allocating, tracking, and freeing memory used by variables and objects in your program.</p> <p>Python handles this automatically using:</p> <ul> <li>Reference counting</li> <li>Garbage collection</li> <li>Private memory manager (PyMalloc)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow","text":""},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#how-memory-is-managed","title":"\ud83d\udce6 How memory is managed:","text":"<ol> <li>You create an object \u2192 Python assigns memory</li> <li>Multiple variables can point to the same object</li> <li>When no variable points to the object, Python deletes it (Garbage Collection)</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#key-concepts","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Key Concepts","text":"Term Meaning Reference Count Number of variables referring to an object Garbage Collection Automatic cleanup of unused objects PyMalloc Python\u2019s built-in allocator (manages memory blocks internally)"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Use Cases","text":"Use Case Memory Feature Used Avoid memory leaks Use <code>del</code>, avoid circular references Handle large data files Use generators, lazy loading Release memory early Use <code>del</code>, context managers Debug memory issues Use <code>gc</code> module"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#examples","title":"\ud83e\uddea Examples","text":""},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#1-reference-counting","title":"\u2705 1. Reference Counting","text":"<pre><code>a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a  # Now ref count is 2\ndel a  # Ref count is 1\ndel b  # Ref count is 0 \u2192 garbage collected\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#2-checking-reference-count","title":"\u2705 2. Checking Reference Count","text":"<pre><code>import sys\n\nx = \"hello\"\nprint(sys.getrefcount(x))  # Shows number of references\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#3-manual-garbage-collection","title":"\u2705 3. Manual Garbage Collection","text":"<pre><code>import gc\n\ngc.collect()  # Manually run garbage collection\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#4-memory-efficient-generator","title":"\u2705 4. Memory Efficient Generator","text":"<pre><code>def generate_numbers():\n    for i in range(1000000):\n        yield i\n\nnums = generate_numbers()  # Uses very little memory\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#best-practices","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Best Practices","text":"Task Best Practice Avoid memory leaks Break circular references Handle large data Use generators (<code>yield</code>) Release memory early Use <code>with open(...)</code> (context manager) Analyze memory Use tools like <code>tracemalloc</code>, <code>gc</code>"},{"location":"Python/Memory_Management.html#summary","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Summary","text":"Concept What It Does Reference Counting Tracks how many variables use an object Garbage Collector Frees memory when ref count = 0 Generators Save memory by yielding one value at a time PyMalloc Internal memory allocator used by Python"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html","title":"MetaProgramming","text":""},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#python-metaprogramming-simplified-guide","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python Metaprogramming \u2013 Simplified Guide","text":""},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#definition","title":"\u2705 Definition","text":"<p>Metaprogramming means:</p> <p>Writing code that modifies or generates other code dynamically \u2014 often at runtime.</p> <p>In Python, this includes:</p> <ul> <li>Introspection (checking types, attributes)</li> <li>Decorators</li> <li>Metaclasses</li> <li>Dynamic attribute handling</li> </ul> <p>Think of it like: \"Code that writes or tweaks code.\"</p>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 Workflow","text":"<ol> <li>\ud83d\udc40 Inspect: Use functions like <code>type()</code>, <code>dir()</code>, <code>hasattr()</code> to examine objects</li> <li>\u270d\ufe0f Modify: Add or change functions/attributes dynamically</li> <li>\ud83e\uddf1 Wrap: Use decorators to wrap or extend function behavior</li> <li>\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f Control creation: Use metaclasses to customize class behavior</li> </ol>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc Use Cases","text":"Use Case Technique Add behavior to functions Decorators Validate class definitions Metaclasses Log method calls dynamically Class decorators Build API clients on the fly Dynamic class creation Frameworks like Django, FastAPI Use metaclasses and decorators under the hood"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#examples","title":"\ud83e\uddea Examples","text":""},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#1-introspection","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Introspection","text":"<pre><code>x = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(type(x))         # &lt;class 'list'&gt;\nprint(dir(x))          # Lists all methods/attributes of list\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#2-function-decorator-metaprogramming","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Function Decorator (Metaprogramming)","text":"<pre><code>def logger(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        print(f\"Calling {func.__name__}\")\n        func()\n    return wrapper\n\n@logger\ndef greet():\n    print(\"Hello!\")\n\ngreet()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#3-dynamic-attribute-addition","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Dynamic Attribute Addition","text":"<pre><code>class Person:\n    pass\n\np = Person()\np.name = \"Alice\"  # Dynamically added\nprint(p.name)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#4-metaclass-example-advanced","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Metaclass Example (Advanced)","text":"<pre><code>class Meta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        print(f\"Creating class {name}\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n\nclass MyClass(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#when-to-use-metaprogramming","title":"\ud83c\udfaf When to Use Metaprogramming?","text":"Situation Use Metaprogramming? You want to reduce repetitive code \u2705 Yes (use decorators) You\u2019re building a framework or library \u2705 Yes (use metaclasses) You need dynamic, flexible behavior \u2705 Yes You're building simple scripts \u274c No (overkill)"},{"location":"Python/MetaProgramming.html#summary","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Summary","text":"Feature Tool / Concept Introspection <code>type()</code>, <code>dir()</code>, <code>getattr()</code> Code wrapping <code>@decorators</code> Dynamic edits <code>setattr()</code>, <code>__getattr__()</code> Class control <code>metaclasses</code>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html","title":"Multithreading & Multiprocessing","text":""},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#1-multithreading-perfect-interview-explanation","title":"\u2705 1. MULTITHREADING (Perfect Interview Explanation)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#simple-definition","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Simple Definition","text":"<p>Multithreading means running multiple threads inside the same process to perform tasks \u201ctogether\u201d, mainly useful when tasks spend most of their time waiting (I/O bound).</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#key-point","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Key Point","text":"<p>Python has GIL (Global Interpreter Lock) \u2192 Only one thread can execute Python code at a time. So multithreading in Python does NOT give real parallel CPU execution.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#best-use-cases","title":"\u2714 Best Use-Cases","text":"<p>Use multithreading for tasks that wait for external response:</p> <ul> <li>API calls</li> <li>File download/upload</li> <li>Database queries</li> <li>Web scraping</li> <li>Reading/writing files</li> <li>Sleep timers</li> </ul> <p>Because when one thread waits, another runs \u2192 leading to speed-up.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#flow-explanation-very-simple","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Flow Explanation (Very Simple)","text":"<pre><code>Main Program\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Thread 1 (download file)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Thread 2 (call API)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Thread 3 (save data)\n</code></pre> <p>All 3 threads share:</p> <ul> <li>Same memory</li> <li>Same process</li> </ul> <p>But only 1 thread executes Python code at a time (due to GIL).</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#interview-one-line","title":"\u2714 Interview One-Line","text":"<p>\u201cMultithreading improves performance for I/O-bound tasks because threads release the GIL when waiting.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#example","title":"\ud83e\uddea Example","text":"<pre><code>import threading\nimport time\n\ndef task(name):\n    print(f\"{name} started\")\n    time.sleep(2)  # I/O wait\n    print(f\"{name} finished\")\n\nt1 = threading.Thread(target=task, args=(\"Thread 1\",))\nt2 = threading.Thread(target=task, args=(\"Thread 2\",))\n\nt1.start()\nt2.start()\nt1.join()\nt2.join()\n</code></pre> <pre><code>import time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef task(n):\n    print(f\"Task {n} started\")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(f\"Task {n} finished\")\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=5) as executor:\n    for i in range(10):\n        executor.submit(task, i)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#2-multiprocessing-perfect-interview-explanation","title":"\u2705 2. MULTIPROCESSING (Perfect Interview Explanation)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#simple-definition_1","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Simple Definition","text":"<p>Multiprocessing means running multiple processes, each with its own memory and Python interpreter.</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 No GIL \ud83d\udc49 True parallel execution on multiple CPU cores</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#best-use-cases_1","title":"\u2714 Best Use-Cases","text":"<p>Use multiprocessing for CPU-heavy tasks:</p> <ul> <li>ML model training</li> <li>Image processing</li> <li>Video encoding</li> <li>Mathematical calculations</li> <li>Data transformations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#flow-explanation-very-simple_1","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Flow Explanation (Very Simple)","text":"<pre><code>Main Program\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Process A \u2192 uses CPU Core 1\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Process B \u2192 uses CPU Core 2\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Process C \u2192 uses CPU Core 3\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Process D \u2192 uses CPU Core 4\n</code></pre> <p>Each process: \u2714 Has its own memory \u2714 Has its own GIL \u2714 Runs truly parallel</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#interview-one-liner","title":"\u2714 Interview One-Liner","text":"<p>\u201cMultiprocessing bypasses the GIL and provides real parallelism, so it\u2019s ideal for CPU-bound workloads.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#example_1","title":"\ud83e\uddea Example","text":"<pre><code>from multiprocessing import Process\nimport time\n\ndef task(n):\n    print(f\"Processing {n}\")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(f\"Done {n}\")\n\np1 = Process(target=task, args=(1,))\np2 = Process(target=task, args=(2,))\n\np1.start()\np2.start()\np1.join()\np2.join()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#3-async-await-perfect-interview-explanation","title":"\u2705 3. ASYNC / AWAIT (Perfect Interview Explanation)","text":""},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#simple-definition_2","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Simple Definition","text":"<p>Async/await is single-threaded concurrency. It does NOT use multiple threads or multiple processes.</p> <p>It uses an event loop to switch between tasks when one is waiting.</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#best-use-cases_2","title":"\u2714 Best Use-Cases","text":"<p>Perfect for high-performance I/O operations:</p> <ul> <li>Calling APIs in parallel</li> <li>FastAPI / aiohttp servers</li> <li>WebSockets</li> <li>Database operations</li> <li>Background tasks</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#flow-explanation-very-simple_2","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Flow Explanation (Very Simple)","text":"<pre><code>Event Loop\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Task 1 (waiting for API response)\n   \u2502       \u21b3 Switch to Task 2\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Task 2 (waiting for DB)\n   \u2502       \u21b3 Switch to Task 3\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Task 3 (waiting for file I/O)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 No blocking \ud83d\udc49 Everything runs smoothly on a single thread \ud83d\udc49 No GIL problems</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#interview-one-liner_1","title":"\u2714 Interview One-Liner","text":"<p>\u201cAsync allows concurrency without threads or processes by switching tasks during I/O wait time.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#example_2","title":"\ud83e\uddea Example","text":"<pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def task(name):\n    print(f\"{name} started\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)  # Non-blocking wait\n    print(f\"{name} finished\")\n\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.gather(\n        task(\"Task 1\"),\n        task(\"Task 2\")\n    )\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Multi_processing_threading.html#final-10-second-comparison-say-this-in-interview","title":"\ud83c\udf1f FINAL 10-SECOND COMPARISON (Say This in Interview)","text":"Feature Multithreading Multiprocessing Async/Await Type Concurrency Parallelism Concurrency Works On I/O-bound CPU-bound I/O-bound GIL Blocks true parallel No GIL issue No GIL issue Memory Low High Very low Speed Medium Fast for CPU Fastest for I/O Execution Multiple threads Multiple processes Single thread (event loop)"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html","title":"OAuth Token","text":""},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#oauth2-login-googlefacebook","title":"OAuth2 Login (Google/Facebook)","text":""},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#high-level-model","title":"\ud83e\udde0 High-Level Model","text":"<pre><code>Google/Facebook  \u2192  Confirms Identity\nFastAPI          \u2192  Creates User + Issues JWT\nClient           \u2192  Uses JWT to call APIs\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#complete-directional-flow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 COMPLETE DIRECTIONAL FLOW","text":""},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-1-user-clicks-login-with-google","title":"STEP 1\ufe0f\u20e3 User clicks \u201cLogin with Google\u201d","text":"<pre><code>User \u2192 Frontend\n</code></pre> <p>Frontend redirects browser to OAuth provider:</p> <pre><code>GET https://accounts.google.com/authorize\n    ?client_id=XXX\n    &amp;redirect_uri=/callback\n    &amp;response_type=code\n    &amp;scope=openid email profile\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Backend NOT involved yet \ud83d\udccc No user exists in DB yet</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-2-oauth-provider-authenticates-user","title":"STEP 2\ufe0f\u20e3 OAuth Provider authenticates user","text":"<pre><code>User \u2192 Google/Facebook\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>User logs in</li> <li>User gives consent</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-3-authorization-server-redirects-back-with-code","title":"STEP 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Authorization Server redirects back with CODE","text":"<pre><code>Google \u2192 Browser \u2192 FastAPI\nGET /callback?code=AUTH_CODE\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc Authorization Code \u2260 Token \ud83d\udccc Code is short-lived and one-time</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-4-backend-exchanges-code-for-tokens","title":"STEP 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Backend exchanges code for tokens","text":"<pre><code>FastAPI \u2192 Google Token Endpoint\nPOST /token\n</code></pre> <p>Backend sends:</p> <ul> <li>authorization code</li> <li>client_id</li> <li>client_secret</li> <li>redirect_uri</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-5-oauth-provider-returns-tokens","title":"STEP 5\ufe0f\u20e3 OAuth Provider returns tokens","text":"<pre><code>{\n  \"id_token\": \"...\",\n  \"access_token\": \"...\",\n  \"refresh_token\": \"...\",\n  \"expires_in\": 3600\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#token-meaning","title":"Token Meaning:","text":"Token Purpose id_token Identity proof (MOST IMPORTANT) access_token Call Google/Facebook APIs refresh_token Get new provider tokens"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-6-backend-verifies-id_token","title":"STEP 6\ufe0f\u20e3 Backend verifies <code>id_token</code>","text":"<p>FastAPI verifies:</p> <ul> <li>Signature</li> <li>Issuer</li> <li>Audience</li> <li>Expiry</li> </ul> <p>Extracts user identity:</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"sub\": \"google-unique-id\",\n  \"email\": \"user@gmail.com\",\n  \"name\": \"User Name\"\n}\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc This proves who the user is</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-7-just-in-time-user-creation-no-registration","title":"STEP 7\ufe0f\u20e3 Just-In-Time User Creation (NO REGISTRATION)","text":"<pre><code>user = find_user(provider=\"google\", provider_id=sub)\n\nif not user:\n    user = create_user(\n        email=email,\n        name=name,\n        provider=\"google\",\n        provider_user_id=sub\n    )\n</code></pre> <p>\u2705 First OAuth login = Registration \u274c No password \u274c No signup form</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-8-backend-issues-its-own-jwt","title":"STEP 8\ufe0f\u20e3 Backend issues its OWN JWT \ud83d\udd10","text":"<p>Backend creates JWT:</p> <pre><code>{\n  \"user_id\": 42,\n  \"email\": \"user@gmail.com\",\n  \"role\": \"user\"\n}\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udccc This JWT represents:</p> <p>\u201cUser is authenticated in MY SYSTEM\u201d</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-9-client-uses-backend-jwt-for-all-requests","title":"STEP 9\ufe0f\u20e3 Client uses Backend JWT for all requests","text":"<pre><code>Client \u2192 FastAPI\nAuthorization: Bearer &lt;YOUR_JWT&gt;\n</code></pre> <p>FastAPI verifies JWT locally:</p> <ul> <li>No Google calls</li> <li>Fast</li> <li>Stateless</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#step-token-refresh-backend-controlled","title":"STEP \ud83d\udd01 Token Refresh (Backend Controlled)","text":"<p>When JWT expires:</p> <pre><code>Client \u2192 /refresh\n</code></pre> <p>Backend:</p> <ul> <li>validates refresh token</li> <li>issues new JWT</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udccc OAuth provider NOT involved</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#when-to-use-oauth-provider-access-token","title":"\u2705 WHEN TO USE OAUTH PROVIDER ACCESS TOKEN","text":"<p>Use it ONLY IF your backend needs provider APIs:</p> <ul> <li>Google Calendar</li> <li>Google Drive</li> <li>Facebook Graph API</li> </ul> <p>Otherwise: \u274c Don\u2019t store it \u274c Don\u2019t refresh it</p> <p>\ud83d\udccc No password field \ud83d\udccc Supports multi-provider login</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#ultra-simple-analogy-memorable","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Ultra-Simple Analogy (Memorable)","text":"<ul> <li>OAuth Login \u2192 Passport (Identity)</li> <li>JWT \u2192 Boarding Pass (Access)</li> </ul> <p>You don\u2019t show your passport at every gate \u2708\ufe0f</p>"},{"location":"Python/OAuth_token.html#final-answer","title":"\ud83c\udfc6 FINAL ANSWER","text":"<p>OAuth2 is used to authenticate users via trusted providers. After verifying the identity using the ID token, the backend performs just-in-time user creation and issues its own JWT, which is then used to authorize all API requests.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html","title":"Parallel Programming","text":""},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#multithreading-vs-multiprocessing-vs-asynchronous-programming","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Multithreading vs \ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing vs \u26a1 Asynchronous Programming","text":""},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#1-definitions","title":"\u2705 1. DEFINITIONS","text":"Concept Simple Definition \ud83e\uddf5 Multithreading Run multiple tasks (threads) at the same time in 1 process (one core) \ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing Run multiple processes on multiple CPU cores \u2014 true parallel execution \u26a1 Asynchronous Programming Run tasks non-blocking using <code>async/await</code> \u2014 great for I/O without using threads"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#2-workflow","title":"\ud83d\udd01 2. WORKFLOW","text":""},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#multithreading-flow","title":"\ud83e\uddf5 Multithreading Flow","text":"<pre><code>1 Process\n \u251c\u2500\u2500 Thread 1: Download image\n \u251c\u2500\u2500 Thread 2: Save to disk\n \u2514\u2500\u2500 Thread 3: Show progress\n\n\u2705 Shares memory  \n\u274c Not truly parallel (limited by GIL)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#multiprocessing-flow","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing Flow","text":"<pre><code>CPU 1 \u2192 Process A: Resize image  \nCPU 2 \u2192 Process B: Compress file  \nCPU 3 \u2192 Process C: Upload image\n\n\u2705 Truly parallel  \n\u274c Separate memory\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#async-programming-flow","title":"\u26a1 Async Programming Flow","text":"<pre><code>Main Program\n \u251c\u2500\u2500 Await: API Call\n \u251c\u2500\u2500 Await: File Write\n \u2514\u2500\u2500 Await: DB Query\n\n\u2705 Super fast for I/O  \n\u2705 No threads or processes  \n\u274c Not for CPU-heavy tasks\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#3-use-cases","title":"\ud83d\udcbc 3. USE CASES","text":"Task Type Multithreading \ud83e\uddf5 Multiprocessing \ud83e\udde0 Async Programming \u26a1 Downloading files \u2705 \u274c \u2705 API / Web calls \u2705 \u274c \u2705 Image processing \u274c \u2705 \u274c Machine Learning \u274c \u2705 \u274c Realtime apps \u274c \u274c \u2705 (best)"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#4-examples","title":"\ud83e\uddea 4. EXAMPLES","text":""},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#multithreading-example","title":"\ud83e\uddf5 Multithreading Example","text":"<pre><code>import threading\nimport time\n\ndef task():\n    print(\"Start thread task\")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(\"Thread task done\")\n\nt = threading.Thread(target=task)\nt.start()\n\nprint(\"Main continues...\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#multiprocessing-example","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing Example","text":"<pre><code>import multiprocessing\nimport time\n\ndef task():\n    print(\"Start process task\")\n    time.sleep(2)\n    print(\"Process task done\")\n\np = multiprocessing.Process(target=task)\np.start()\n\nprint(\"Main continues...\")\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#async-programming-example","title":"\u26a1 Async Programming Example","text":"<pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def task():\n    print(\"Start async task\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(2)\n    print(\"Async task done\")\n\nasync def main():\n    await task()\n    print(\"Main continues...\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#5-when-to-use-what","title":"\ud83c\udfaf 5. WHEN TO USE WHAT?","text":"Situation Use Many API calls, file or DB reads \u26a1 Async Programming Parallel file downloads \ud83e\uddf5 Multithreading Heavy CPU computation (e.g., image processing) \ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing Real-time chat apps, fast I/O \u26a1 Async (best performance)"},{"location":"Python/Parallel_Programming.html#summary-table","title":"\ud83e\udde0 SUMMARY TABLE","text":"Feature \ud83e\uddf5 Multithreading \ud83e\udde0 Multiprocessing \u26a1 Async Programming True parallelism \u274c (GIL limits it) \u2705 Yes \u274c No (single-thread) Good for I/O tasks \u2705 \u274c \u2705 (best) Good for CPU tasks \u274c \u2705 \u274c Memory sharing \u2705 Shared \u274c Separate \u2705 Shared (single thread) Easy to write \u2705 \u2705 \u26a0\ufe0f Needs async syntax"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html","title":"Inheritance","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#python-inheritance-full-guide-easy-to-understand","title":"\ud83e\uddec Python Inheritance \u2013 Full Guide (Easy to Understand)","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#what-is-inheritance","title":"\ud83d\udd30 What is Inheritance?","text":"<p>Inheritance allows a class (child) to use the properties and methods of another class (parent). This promotes code reuse and helps build relationships between classes.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#why-use-inheritance","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Why Use Inheritance?","text":"<p>\u2705 Avoid code duplication \u2705 Reuse existing logic \u2705 Create class hierarchies \u2705 Easy to update and maintain</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#syntax-of-inheritance","title":"\ud83d\udd11 Syntax of Inheritance","text":"<pre><code>class Parent:\n    # code\n\nclass Child(Parent):\n    # inherits Parent's properties/methods\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#types-of-inheritance-in-python","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Types of Inheritance in Python","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#1-single-inheritance","title":"1. Single Inheritance","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 One child class inherits from one parent class.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Animal:\n    def sound(self):\n        print(\"Animal makes sound\")\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def bark(self):\n        print(\"Dog barks\")\n\nd = Dog()\nd.sound()  # From Animal\nd.bark()   # From Dog\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#concept-diagram","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Concept Diagram:","text":"<pre><code>Animal \u2500\u2500\u2500\u25b6 Dog\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can the child class override a parent method?   A: Yes, using the same method name.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can the child use all methods of the parent?   A: Yes, unless overridden.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What happens if the parent changes?   A: All child classes will reflect the change if they don\u2019t override it.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#2-multiple-inheritance","title":"2. Multiple Inheritance","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 A child class inherits from more than one parent.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example_1","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Father:\n    def skills(self):\n        print(\"Programming\")\n\nclass Mother:\n    def cooking(self):\n        print(\"Cooking\")\n\nclass Child(Father, Mother):\n    def sports(self):\n        print(\"Playing football\")\n\nc = Child()\nc.skills()\nc.cooking()\nc.sports()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#concept-diagram_1","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Concept Diagram:","text":"<pre><code>Father \u2500\u2510\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u25b6 Child\nMother \u2500\u2518\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_1","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What if both parents have the same method?   A: Python follows Method Resolution Order (MRO) \u2014 left to right.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Does Python support multiple inheritance directly?   A: Yes.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: How to resolve conflicts in method names?   A: Use <code>super()</code> or explicit class call.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#3-multilevel-inheritance","title":"3. Multilevel Inheritance","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 A child class inherits from a class that is already derived from another class.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example_2","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Grandparent:\n    def origin(self):\n        print(\"India\")\n\nclass Parent(Grandparent):\n    def surname(self):\n        print(\"Patel\")\n\nclass Child(Parent):\n    def name(self):\n        print(\"Dhruvish\")\n\nc = Child()\nc.origin()\nc.surname()\nc.name()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#concept-diagram_2","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Concept Diagram:","text":"<pre><code>Grandparent \u2500\u25b6 Parent \u2500\u25b6 Child\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_2","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: How many levels of inheritance can Python handle?   A: Technically unlimited, but usually 2\u20133 levels are practical.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can the child access grandparent methods?   A: Yes, unless overridden or hidden.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What happens if intermediate class overrides the method?   A: The overridden method is used, unless the child overrides again.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#4-hierarchical-inheritance","title":"4. Hierarchical Inheritance","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example_3","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Vehicle:\n    def start(self):\n        print(\"Engine started\")\n\nclass Car(Vehicle):\n    def drive(self):\n        print(\"Driving a car\")\n\nclass Bike(Vehicle):\n    def ride(self):\n        print(\"Riding a bike\")\n\nc = Car()\nb = Bike()\nc.start()\nb.start()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#concept-diagram_3","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Concept Diagram:","text":"<pre><code>       Vehicle\n        /   \\\n     Car    Bike\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_3","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can both child classes use the same parent method?   A: Yes.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What if one child overrides the method?   A: Only that child\u2019s version will change.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can you create an object of the parent class?   A: Yes, unless it's abstract (using <code>abc</code> module).</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#5-hybrid-inheritance","title":"5. Hybrid Inheritance","text":"<p>\ud83d\udc49 A combination of more than one type of inheritance.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example-multiple-multilevel","title":"\u2705 Example (Multiple + Multilevel):","text":"<pre><code>class A:\n    def method_a(self):\n        print(\"A\")\n\nclass B(A):\n    def method_b(self):\n        print(\"B\")\n\nclass C:\n    def method_c(self):\n        print(\"C\")\n\nclass D(B, C):\n    def method_d(self):\n        print(\"D\")\n\nobj = D()\nobj.method_a()\nobj.method_b()\nobj.method_c()\nobj.method_d()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#concept-diagram_4","title":"\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Concept Diagram:","text":"<pre><code>   A \u2500\u25b6 B \u2500\u2510\n           \u251c\u2500\u2500\u25b6 D\n       C \u2500\u2500\u2518\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_4","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Does Python handle hybrid inheritance?   A: Yes, using MRO.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What\u2019s the risk with hybrid inheritance?   A: Method conflicts if multiple parents have the same method.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: How can you resolve method conflicts?   A: Using <code>super()</code> and MRO.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#super-keyword","title":"\u2699\ufe0f <code>super()</code> Keyword","text":"<p>Used to call methods from the parent class.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#example_4","title":"\u2705 Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Parent:\n    def show(self):\n        print(\"Parent class\")\n\nclass Child(Parent):\n    def show(self):\n        super().show()   # Call parent method\n        print(\"Child class\")\n\nc = Child()\nc.show()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_5","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What if the parent method doesn't exist?   A: <code>AttributeError</code> will be raised.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Is <code>super()</code> limited to only immediate parent?   A: No, it follows the MRO chain.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#method-overriding","title":"\ud83d\udccc Method Overriding","text":"<p>Child class overrides a method from the parent with the same name.</p> <pre><code>class Animal:\n    def sound(self):\n        print(\"Some sound\")\n\nclass Cat(Animal):\n    def sound(self):   # Overriding\n        print(\"Meow\")\n\nc = Cat()\nc.sound()  # Meow\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#cross-questions_6","title":"\u2753 Cross Questions:","text":"<ul> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: How do you access the parent\u2019s version of the method?   A: Use <code>super().method()</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: What happens if child doesn\u2019t override?   A: The parent\u2019s method is used.</p> </li> <li> <p>\u2705 Q: Can you override constructors (<code>__init__</code>)?   A: Yes, and call parent constructor via <code>super().__init__()</code>.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#useful-functions","title":"\ud83d\udd0e Useful Functions","text":"Function Description <code>super()</code> Call parent class methods <code>isinstance(obj, Class)</code> Check if object is an instance of class <code>issubclass(Class1, Class2)</code> Check if Class1 is subclass of Class2"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Inheritance.html#summary-table","title":"\ud83d\udcd6 Summary Table","text":"Type Description Example Classes Single One child inherits one parent <code>Dog(**Animal**)</code> Multiple One child, multiple parents <code>Child(Father, Mother)</code> Multilevel Chain of inheritance <code>Child(Parent(Grandparent))</code> Hierarchical Multiple children, one parent <code>Car(Vehicle), Bike(Vehicle)</code> Hybrid Mix of above types <code>D(B(A), C)</code>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html","title":"Opps","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#python-oop-concepts-explained-simply-with-examples","title":"\ud83e\udde0 Python OOP Concepts \u2013 Explained Simply with Examples","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#1-class-object","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 1. Class &amp; Object","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>A class is a blueprint.</li> <li>An object is an actual thing created from the blueprint.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Car:\n    def start(self):\n        print(\"Car started\")\n\n# Object creation\nmy_car = Car()\nmy_car.start()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Car started\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#2-constructor-__init__","title":"\u2699\ufe0f 2. Constructor (<code>__init__</code>)","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept_1","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Special method called automatically when an object is created.</li> <li>Used to initialize values.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example_1","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def greet(self):\n        print(f\"Hello, I\u2019m {self.name}\")\n\np = Person(\"Alice\")\np.greet()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_1","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Hello, I\u2019m Alice\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#problem","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Problem:","text":"<p>Create a <code>Book</code> class that stores title and author and prints them.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#solution","title":"\u2705 Solution:","text":"<pre><code>class Book:\n    def __init__(self, title, author):\n        self.title = title\n        self.author = author\n\n    def info(self):\n        print(f\"{self.title} by {self.author}\")\n\nb = Book(\"1984\", \"George Orwell\")\nb.info()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_2","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>1984 by George Orwell\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#3-encapsulation","title":"\ud83d\udd10 3. Encapsulation","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept_2","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Hiding internal details using private variables/methods.</li> <li>Achieved using <code>self.__var</code> (double underscore).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example_2","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example:","text":"<pre><code>class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__balance = 0  # private\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.__balance += amount\n\n    def get_balance(self):\n        return self.__balance\n\nacct = BankAccount()\nacct.deposit(500)\nprint(acct.get_balance())  # 500\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_3","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>500\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#problem_1","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Problem:","text":"<p>Restrict direct access to a student's marks and allow only through method.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#solution_1","title":"\u2705 Solution:","text":"<pre><code>class Student:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__marks = 0\n\n    def set_marks(self, m):\n        self.__marks = m\n\n    def get_marks(self):\n        return self.__marks\n\ns = Student()\ns.set_marks(90)\nprint(s.get_marks())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_4","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>90\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#4-abstraction","title":"\ud83c\udfad 4. Abstraction","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept_3","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Hiding complex logic; showing only what's necessary.</li> <li>Done using abstract classes (<code>abc</code> module).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example_3","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example:","text":"<pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, r):\n        self.r = r\n\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14 * self.r * self.r\n\nc = Circle(5)\nprint(c.area())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_5","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>78.5\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#problem_2","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Problem:","text":"<p>Create an abstract <code>Appliance</code> class with method <code>turn_on()</code>.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#solution_2","title":"\u2705 Solution:","text":"<pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Appliance(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def turn_on(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Fan(Appliance):\n    def turn_on(self):\n        print(\"Fan is ON\")\n\nf = Fan()\nf.turn_on()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_6","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Fan is ON\n</code></pre> <p>We can implement the class directly without <code>ABC</code>, but <code>ABC</code> and <code>@abstractmethod</code> are used to enforce a contract. They ensure that every subclass must implement required methods. This prevents silent bugs and improves code safety in large-scale applications.</p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#5-inheritance","title":"\ud83d\udd04 5. Inheritance","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept_4","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>A child class inherits properties from a parent class.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example_4","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example:","text":"<pre><code>class Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        print(\"Animal speaks\")\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        print(\"Dog barks\")\n\nd = Dog()\nd.speak()\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_7","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Dog barks\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#6-polymorphism","title":"\ud83e\uddec 6. Polymorphism","text":""},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#concept_5","title":"\u2705 Concept:","text":"<ul> <li>Same method name, different behavior.</li> <li> <p>Achieved via:</p> </li> <li> <p>Method Overriding</p> </li> <li>Duck Typing</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example-overriding","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example (Overriding):","text":"<pre><code>class Bird:\n    def sound(self):\n        print(\"Chirp\")\n\nclass Duck(Bird):\n    def sound(self):\n        print(\"Quack\")\n\nb = Duck()\nb.sound()  # Quack\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_8","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Quack\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#example-duck-typing","title":"\ud83d\udd0d Example (Duck Typing):","text":"<pre><code>class Cat:\n    def speak(self):\n        print(\"Meow\")\n\nclass Dog:\n    def speak(self):\n        print(\"Bark\")\n\ndef animal_sound(animal):\n    animal.speak()\n\nanimal_sound(Cat())\nanimal_sound(Dog())\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#output_9","title":"\u2705 Output:","text":"<pre><code>Meow\nBark\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#summary-table","title":"\ud83d\udcda Summary Table","text":"Concept Purpose Keyword/Tool Example Class Class/Object Blueprint vs Instance <code>class</code>, <code>object</code> <code>Car</code>, <code>Person</code> Constructor Init values on creation <code>__init__()</code> <code>Person(\"Bob\")</code> Encapsulation Hide sensitive data <code>__var</code>, methods <code>BankAccount</code> Abstraction Show essential, hide details <code>ABC</code>, <code>@abstractmethod</code> <code>Shape</code> Inheritance Reuse behavior from parent <code>class Child(Parent)</code> <code>Dog(Animal)</code> Polymorphism Same interface, different use Override, Duck Typing <code>speak()</code>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#7-instance-method-vs-classmethod-vs-staticmethod","title":"7. Instance Method vs <code>@classmethod</code> vs <code>@staticmethod</code>","text":"<p>1\ufe0f\u20e3 Instance Method</p> <p>Why / When to use :--</p> <ul> <li>When you need to work with object (instance) data</li> <li>Uses <code>self</code></li> </ul> <pre><code>class User:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def greet(self):\n        return f\"Hello {self.name}\"\n</code></pre> <p>2\ufe0f\u20e3 @classmethod</p> <p>Why / When to use:-- </p> <ul> <li>When logic is related to the class, not a single object</li> <li> <p>Used for:</p> </li> <li> <p>Factory methods</p> </li> <li>Modifying class variables</li> <li>Uses <code>cls</code></li> </ul> <pre><code>class User:\n    user_count = 0\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        User.user_count += 1\n\n    @classmethod\n    def total_users(cls):\n        return cls.user_count\n</code></pre> <p>\u2714 Can access class variables \u2714 Cannot access instance data directly</p> <p>3\ufe0f\u20e3 @staticmethod</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Utility function inside a class</p> <p>Why / When to use:--</p> <ul> <li> <p>When method:</p> </li> <li> <p>Does NOT need <code>self</code></p> </li> <li>Does NOT need <code>cls</code></li> <li>Just logically belongs to the class</li> </ul> <pre><code>class MathUtils:\n    @staticmethod\n    def add(a, b):\n        return a + b\n</code></pre> <p>\u2714 No access to instance or class data \u2714 Just a helper function</p> <p>\ud83d\udd25 What if I don\u2019t use <code>@staticmethod</code> or <code>@classmethod</code>?</p> <p>\u274c Case 1: Not using <code>@staticmethod</code></p> <pre><code>class Math:\n    def add(a, b):   # \u274c error\n        return a + b\n\nMath.add(2, 3)\n</code></pre> <p>\u274c Error because Python expects <code>self</code></p> <p>\u2714 Fix \u2192 use <code>@staticmethod</code></p> <p>\u274c Case 2: Not using <code>@classmethod</code></p> <pre><code>class User:\n    count = 0\n\n    def total(cls):   # \u274c wrong\n        return cls.count\n</code></pre> <p>\u274c <code>cls</code> is not automatically passed</p> <p>\u2714 Fix \u2192 use <code>@classmethod</code></p>"},{"location":"Python/OOPS/Opps.html#8-metaclass","title":"8. Metaclass","text":"<p>Step 1: Create Metaclass</p> <pre><code>class CheckRoleMeta(type):\n    def __new__(cls, name, bases, dct):\n        if 'role' not in dct:\n            raise TypeError(\"Class must define 'role'\")\n        return super().__new__(cls, name, bases, dct)\n</code></pre> <p>Step 2: Use Metaclass</p> <pre><code>class User(metaclass=CheckRoleMeta):\n    role = \"admin\"\n</code></pre> <p>\u2714 Class created successfully \u2714 Rule enforced at class creation</p> <p>\u274c Without role (Error)</p> <pre><code>class Employee(metaclass=CheckRoleMeta):\n    pass\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udeab Error: <code>Class must define 'role'</code></p> <p>\ud83e\udde0 USE CASE</p> <p>\ud83d\udc49 Without metaclasses, classes are created normally. With metaclasses, we can add validation or modify classes at creation time. This is useful in frameworks like Django to enforce rules automatically.</p>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html","title":"Developer roadmap","text":""},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#core-tech-stack","title":"\u2705 Core Tech Stack","text":""},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#programming-language","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Programming Language","text":"<ul> <li>Python: Data structures, OOP, decorators, generators, type hints, exception handling, file I/O</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#backend-frameworks","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Backend Frameworks","text":"<ul> <li>FastAPI: Request/response lifecycle, async support, dependency injection, Pydantic models, JWT auth</li> <li>Flask: Blueprints, middleware, request/response handling, RESTful API design</li> <li>Django (Basic to Intermediate): ORM, views, templates, authentication</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#databases","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Databases","text":"<ul> <li>MongoDB: NoSQL concepts, schema design, indexing, aggregation</li> <li>MySQL: Joins, indexing, normalization, query optimization, stored procedures</li> <li>Redis: Caching, pub/sub, Celery broker</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#cloud-serverless","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Cloud &amp; Serverless","text":"<ul> <li>AWS Lambda: Stateless functions, IAM roles, deployment</li> <li>AWS S3: File storage, permissions, lifecycle rules</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#asynchronous-processing-queues","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Asynchronous Processing &amp; Queues","text":"<ul> <li>Celery: Task queues, retry logic, scheduling, worker setup</li> <li>RabbitMQ / Redis as broker: Know basic concepts even if using Redis</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#advanced-tools","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Advanced Tools","text":""},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#llm-ai-ecosystem","title":"\ud83d\udd39 LLM &amp; AI Ecosystem","text":"<ul> <li>OpenAI / Anthropic / Gemini APIs: API design, limitations, pricing, streaming</li> <li>LangChain: Agents, tools, memory, chaining, prompt templates</li> <li>LangGraph: Node-based LLM workflow orchestration, conditional flow, persistent agent state</li> <li>Pinecone: Vector database usage, upserts, similarity search, namespace handling</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#web-crawling-content-extraction","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Web Crawling &amp; Content Extraction","text":"<ul> <li>Playwright: Browser automation, scraping, headless mode</li> <li>BeautifulSoup: HTML parsing and DOM traversal</li> <li>Crawler4AI (custom): Know your own architecture, when and how it scrapes, integrates with LLMs</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#devops","title":"\ud83d\udcc8 DevOps","text":""},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#testing-cicd","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Testing &amp; CI/CD","text":"<ul> <li>Pytest: Fixtures, mocks, assertions, coverage</li> <li>Nose: Basic understanding</li> <li>Locust: Load testing basics</li> <li>Jenkins: Pipelines, CI/CD basics</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#docker-deployment","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Docker &amp; Deployment","text":"<ul> <li>Docker: Dockerfiles, volumes, environment variables, image layers</li> <li>Docker Compose: Multi-container setups, networks, service linking</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#monitoring-observability","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Monitoring &amp; Observability","text":"<ul> <li>Prometheus: Metrics collection and alert rules</li> <li>Grafana: Dashboard creation, alerting</li> <li>Loki + Promtail: Log aggregation and querying</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#security-concepts","title":"\ud83d\udd10 Security Concepts","text":"<ul> <li>JWT (JSON Web Tokens): Auth flow, token expiry, validation</li> <li>CSRF Protection: How it works and how to implement</li> <li>Cryptography in Python: Encryption/decryption basics (use of <code>cryptography</code> or <code>pycrypto</code>)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#frontend","title":"\ud83e\uddea Frontend","text":"<ul> <li>HTML, CSS, Bootstrap: Form handling, responsive design basics</li> <li>ReactJS (team): Understand props, state, API integration \u2013 not in-depth but enough to collaborate</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#notifications-messaging","title":"\u2709\ufe0f Notifications &amp; Messaging","text":"<ul> <li>SMTP / SES: Sending HTML emails, auth config</li> <li>FCM (Firebase Cloud Messaging): For push notifications (optional unless you're applying for mobile-integrated systems)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"Roadmap/Python/developer_roadmap.html#summary-your-interview-prep-checklist","title":"Summary \u2013 Your Interview Prep Checklist \u2705","text":"Area Key Technologies / Concepts Language &amp; Frameworks Python, FastAPI, Flask, Django Databases MongoDB, MySQL, Redis Async &amp; Background Tasks Celery, Redis, S3, AWS Lambda DevOps &amp; Deployment Docker, Docker Compose, Jenkins AI &amp; LLM Integration OpenAI, LangChain, Pinecone, Anthropic, Gemini Monitoring &amp; Logging Prometheus, Grafana, Loki, Promtail (log shipping) Testing Pytest, Nose, Locust Web Scraping Playwright, BeautifulSoup, Crawler4AI Security JWT, CSRF, Cryptography (Python-based) Notifications SMTP, SES, FCM Frontend (Basic) HTML, CSS, Bootstrap"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html","title":"Overview","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#system-design-core-knowledge-map","title":"\ud83e\udde0 System Design \u2013 Core Knowledge Map","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#1-core-concepts","title":"\u2705 1. Core Concepts","text":"Concept Description Client-Server Model Communication model between clients and centralized servers REST vs. RPC vs. GraphQL API communication styles and trade-offs Microservices vs. Monoliths Architecture patterns and scalability implications Sync vs. Async Communication Blocking vs. non-blocking data flows Stateless vs. Stateful Session management and implications on scaling Service Discovery Dynamic detection of service locations (e.g., Consul, Eureka) Load Balancing Distributes traffic across services (e.g., Round-robin, Least Connections)"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#2-scalability","title":"\u2705 2. Scalability","text":"Concept Description Horizontal vs. Vertical Scaling Scaling out vs. scaling up Load Balancers Tools like NGINX, HAProxy for traffic distribution Sticky Sessions Maintain session affinity with specific servers Sharding &amp; Partitioning Split data across databases/servers CDNs Cache static assets near the user for faster access"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#3-data-management","title":"\u2705 3. Data Management","text":"Concept Description SQL vs. NoSQL Relational (MySQL/PostgreSQL) vs. Document/Key-Value (MongoDB, DynamoDB) CAP Theorem Trade-off between Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance Replication Copying data across nodes for fault tolerance Eventual Consistency Relaxed consistency model in distributed systems Indexing &amp; Query Optimization Improve DB performance through optimized indexing Caching Strategies Read-through, Write-through, Write-back strategies"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#4-caching","title":"\u2705 4. Caching","text":"Concept Description Caching Tools Redis, Memcached Cache Invalidation Strategies to keep cache fresh Eviction Policies TTL, LFU, LRU, FIFO CDN Edge Caching Cache content at the network edge"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#5-message-queues-async-processing","title":"\u2705 5. Message Queues &amp; Async Processing","text":"Concept Description Message Brokers RabbitMQ, Kafka, AWS SQS Pub/Sub Pattern Publisher-subscriber model for events Task Queues Tools like Celery, Dramatiq for background jobs Dead Letter Queues (DLQ) Handle failed or undelivered messages"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#6-high-availability-fault-tolerance","title":"\u2705 6. High Availability &amp; Fault Tolerance","text":"Concept Description Redundancy Multiple instances to eliminate single points of failure Failover Mechanisms Auto-switch to backup services Health Checks Monitor service uptime Auto Healing Restart failed services automatically Graceful Degradation Maintain partial functionality on failure"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#7-system-performance-optimization","title":"\u2705 7. System Performance &amp; Optimization","text":"Concept Description Rate Limiting / Throttling Control API usage per user/client Bulkhead Pattern Isolate failures in a subset of the system Backpressure Handling Prevent overload by signaling producers API Profiling / Benchmarking Measure performance using tools like Postman, Apache Benchmark"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#8-security","title":"\u2705 8. Security","text":"Concept Description Authentication OAuth2, JWT, API Keys Authorization RBAC, ABAC models Transport Security HTTPS, TLS Common Threats SQL Injection, CSRF, XSS DDoS Protection Rate limiting, Web Application Firewall (WAF) Encryption Data encryption at rest &amp; in transit"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#9-api-design-gateways","title":"\u2705 9. API Design &amp; Gateways","text":"Concept Description API Specs OpenAPI / Swagger API Versioning URI-based or Header-based version control API Gateway Kong, AWS API Gateway for routing and protection Rate Limiting &amp; Auth Centralized control at gateway level Circuit Breakers Prevent cascading failures (e.g., Netflix Hystrix)"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#10-observability","title":"\u2705 10. Observability","text":"Concept Description Monitoring Prometheus, Grafana, CloudWatch Logging ELK Stack, Loki Tracing Jaeger, Zipkin, OpenTelemetry Alerting On-call alerts with PagerDuty, Opsgenie, or Prometheus AlertManager"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#11-design-patterns-principles","title":"\u2705 11. Design Patterns &amp; Principles","text":"Concept Description 12-Factor App Modern web app development principles Repository Pattern Abstract DB access Dependency Injection Decouple component dependencies Classic Patterns Singleton, Factory, Adapter, etc. Event-driven Architecture Reactive systems powered by events CQRS Command Query Responsibility Segregation Saga Pattern Handle distributed transactions reliably"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#12-distributed-systems-concepts","title":"\u2705 12. Distributed Systems Concepts","text":"Concept Description Consensus Algorithms Raft, Paxos for leader election Leader Election Ensures coordination in clusters Distributed Locks Redis, ZooKeeper for critical section control Quorum Minimum required nodes for consistency Logical Clocks Vector Clocks, Lamport Timestamps for event ordering"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#13-cicd-deployment","title":"\u2705 13. CI/CD &amp; Deployment","text":"Concept Description Docker &amp; Compose Containerize and manage services Kubernetes Orchestrate and scale containers Helm K8s package manager CI/CD Tools GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins Deployment Strategies Blue-Green, Canary Deployments IaC Terraform, CloudFormation for reproducible infra"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#14-cloud-services-knowledge","title":"\u2705 14. Cloud Services Knowledge","text":"Concept Description Cloud Platforms AWS, GCP, Azure Core Services EC2, S3, RDS, Lambda, API Gateway, SQS, SNS Networking VPC, Subnetting, Security Groups Serverless Design Pay-per-execution via Lambda/Firebase Functions"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#1-client-server-architecture","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 1. Client-Server Architecture","text":"<ul> <li>Mobile app or browser acts as the client.</li> <li>Backend service (Node.js, Django, FastAPI) is the server.</li> <li>When a user logs in, the client sends a request to <code>/login</code>, and the server processes credentials and returns a session token.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#2-ip-address","title":"\ud83c\udf10 2. IP Address","text":"<ul> <li>Every server is hosted on a unique IP (e.g., <code>18.162.0.1</code> for AWS).</li> <li>DNS (like Route53) maps <code>www.example.com</code> to this IP so users don\u2019t need to remember it.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#3-dns-domain-name-system","title":"\ud83c\udf0d 3. DNS (Domain Name System)","text":"<p>It\u2019s like your phone\u2019s contact list, but for websites.</p> <p>Instead of remembering IP numbers like <code>123.45.67.89</code>, you just type a name like <code>google.com</code>.</p> <ol> <li>You type: <code>algomaster.io</code></li> <li>Computer asks DNS: \u201cWhat\u2019s the IP of algomaster.io?\u201d</li> <li>DNS replies: \u201cIt\u2019s 123.45.67.89\u201d</li> <li>Browser connects to that IP and opens the website.</li> </ol> <p>Open terminal and type:</p> <p><code>ping algomaster.io</code></p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#4-proxy-reverse-proxy","title":"\ud83e\uddf0 4. Proxy / Reverse Proxy","text":"<p>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb What is a Proxy?</p> <p></p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Real-Life Example:</p> <p>Imagine you want to ask a question in class but feel shy \ud83d\ude05</p> <p>So you tell your friend to ask it for you. Your friend is the proxy \u2014 asking on your behalf.</p> <p>\ud83c\udf10 Internet Version:</p> <p>You \u2192 Proxy Server \u2192 Website</p> <ul> <li>You tell the proxy: \"Get me <code>google.com</code>\"</li> <li>The proxy goes to <code>google.com</code>, gets the data</li> <li>Then it brings it back to you</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Why use it?</p> <ul> <li>Hides your identity/IP</li> <li>Bypasses blocks (e.g., accessing blocked sites)</li> <li>Used in schools/offices to monitor or restrict websites</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd01 What is a Reverse Proxy?</p> <p></p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Real-Life Example:</p> <p>Imagine a company has many departments: Support, Sales, HR.</p> <p>Visitors don\u2019t know which department to go to \u2014 they talk to the receptionist.</p> <p>The receptionist (reverse proxy) listens and sends them to the right department.</p> <p>\ud83c\udf10 Internet Version:</p> <p>Visitor \u2192 Reverse Proxy \u2192 Backend Server</p> <ul> <li>You open <code>amazon.com</code></li> <li>The reverse proxy receives your request</li> <li> <p>It decides:</p> </li> <li> <p>Which server to send you to</p> </li> <li>Whether to allow you or block you</li> <li>Gets the response and sends it back to you</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f Why use it?</p> <ul> <li>Protects real servers (you can\u2019t see them)</li> <li>Blocks bad traffic (like hackers or bots)</li> <li>Balances traffic (if 1 server is busy, sends to another)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#5-latency","title":"\ud83d\udd50 5. Latency","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd52 What is Latency? </p> <p>Latency = Delay It\u2019s the time it takes for data to go from you (client) to a server, and come back.</p> <p>\ud83d\udcac Real-Life Example</p> <p>Imagine shouting to a friend across the street:</p> <ul> <li>You yell: \u201cHey!\u201d</li> <li>Your friend hears it and yells back: \u201cWhat?\u201d</li> </ul> <p>\u23f1\ufe0f The time between you yelling and hearing the reply \u2014 that\u2019s latency.</p> <p>If your friend is far away (or on the other side of the world), it takes longer. That\u2019s higher latency.</p> <p>\ud83c\udf0d Internet Example</p> <p>Let\u2019s say:</p> <ul> <li>\ud83e\uddd1 You are in India</li> <li>\ud83c\udf10 The server is in New York</li> </ul> <p>What happens:</p> <pre><code>You \u2192 Request \u2192 New York Server\nNew York Server \u2192 Response \u2192 You\n</code></pre> <p>This round-trip takes time because data travels through cables, satellites, routers... over thousands of kilometers!</p> <p>That delay is called latency.</p> <p>\ud83d\udc22 Why High Latency Feels Bad</p> <ul> <li>Slow website loading</li> <li>Delays in video calls or online games</li> <li>Lag in live apps like stock trading or chatting</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\ude80 How to Reduce Latency</p> <p>\u2705 Use CDNs (Content Delivery Networks) \u2705 Deploy servers in multiple locations (data centers) \u2705 Let users connect to the nearest server</p> <p>\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f Flow: High vs Low Latency</p> <p>\ud83c\udf0d Without nearby server (High Latency):</p> <pre><code>[ User in India ]\n       \u2193\n[ Server in New York ]\n       \u2193\n   (Long trip = Slow)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83c\udfe2 With nearby server (Low Latency):</p> <pre><code>[ User in India ]\n       \u2193\n[ Server in Mumbai ]\n       \u2193\n   (Short trip = Fast)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#6-httphttps","title":"\ud83d\udd10 6. HTTP/HTTPS","text":"<p>\ud83c\udf10 What is HTTP?</p> <ul> <li>HTTP stands for HyperText Transfer Protocol</li> <li>It\u2019s how your browser talks to websites</li> <li>Every time you open a website, your browser sends a request, and the server sends back a response</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udcac Real-Life Example:</p> <p>Imagine sending a letter to a website asking for a page, and it sends back a reply with that page.</p> <p>\u274c Problem with HTTP</p> <ul> <li>HTTP sends data as plain text</li> <li>Anyone in the middle (like a hacker) can see or steal your data   (like passwords or credit card info)</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 What is HTTPS?</p> <ul> <li>HTTPS is secure HTTP</li> <li>It uses encryption (SSL/TLS) to protect your data</li> <li>Even if someone sees the data, they can\u2019t read it</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd12 That\u2019s why you see a lock symbol in your browser bar for secure websites</p> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Simple Comparison</p> HTTP HTTPS (Secure) \ud83d\udd10 Security \u274c Not secure \u2705 Encrypted \ud83d\udc40 Safe to use No (for sensitive info) Yes (safe for logins, payments) \ud83c\udf0d Example http://example.com https://example.com"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#7-apis","title":"\ud83d\udce1 7. APIs","text":"<p>\ud83e\udd16 What is an API?</p> <p></p> <p>API = Application Programming Interface</p> <p>It\u2019s like a messenger or waiter between:</p> <ul> <li>\ud83c\udf7d\ufe0f You (the app/user)</li> <li>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83c\udf73 The kitchen (the server)</li> </ul> <p>You don\u2019t go into the kitchen \u2014 you tell the waiter (API) what you want. The waiter brings it back for you.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd04 How APIs Work (Simple Flow)</p> <pre><code>[ Client (App) ]\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nSends Request (e.g. \"Get user info\")\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n[ API Server ]\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nFetches from Database or another service\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nSends back Response (usually in JSON)\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n[ Client ]\nShows data to user (like profile, product, map, etc.)\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udcac Real-Life Example</p> <p>You open a food delivery app and search for \"Pizza\":</p> <ul> <li>App sends a request to the API \u2192 <code>\"GET /search?item=pizza\"</code></li> <li>API checks in the restaurant database</li> <li>Sends back the results:</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#8-rest-api","title":"\ud83d\udd04 8. REST API","text":"<p>\ud83c\udf10 What is a REST API?</p> <p></p> <p>REST = Representational State Transfer</p> <p>It\u2019s the most popular style of building APIs.</p> <p>REST APIs help clients (like apps) talk to servers using HTTP \u2014 just like how your browser loads web pages.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd04 Example Flow</p> <pre><code>[ Client App ]\n    \u2502\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 GET /users/123         \u2192 get user info\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 POST /users            \u2192 create new user\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 PUT /users/123         \u2192 update full user\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 PATCH /users/123/email \u2192 update only email\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 DELETE /users/123      \u2192 delete user\n        \u2193\n[ Server Responds with JSON ]\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#9-graphql","title":"\ud83e\uddec 9. GraphQL","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd0d What is GraphQL?</p> <p>GraphQL = A smarter way to get data from the server. It lets you ask for exactly what you need \u2014 nothing more, nothing less.</p> <p>\u2699\ufe0f REST vs GraphQL \u2014 Real-Life Analogy</p> <p>Imagine you're ordering at a restaurant:</p> <ul> <li> <p>REST: You must order fixed combo meals.   \u2192 You may get extra stuff you don\u2019t want, or miss something you need.</p> </li> <li> <p>GraphQL: You tell the waiter exactly what you want on your plate.   \u2192 Custom order = no waste, no missing items.</p> </li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd04 REST Example (Multiple Requests Needed)</p> <p>To get full user info in REST, you might call:</p> <pre><code>GET /api/users/123           \u2192 basic user\nGET /api/users/123/profile   \u2192 user profile\nGET /api/users/123/posts     \u2192 user posts\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 That\u2019s 3 different requests</p> <p>\u2705 GraphQL Example (One Request Does It All)</p> <p>With GraphQL, you can send one smart query:</p> <pre><code>{\n  user(id: 123) {\n    name\n    profile {\n      bio\n      avatar\n    }\n    posts {\n      title\n      createdAt\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83d\udc49 You get everything you need in one shot \u2014 and only that.</p> <p>\ud83d\udca1 Benefits of GraphQL</p> Benefit Explanation \ud83c\udfaf Precise Data Ask for exactly what you want \ud83d\udd01 Fewer Requests One request instead of many \u26a1 Faster on Slow Networks Smaller payload, better for mobile \ud83d\udcd0 Strong Typing You know the structure of the data up front <p>\ud83d\udcca REST vs GraphQL \u2014 Quick Comparison</p> Feature REST GraphQL \ud83d\udd17 Calls Multiple endpoints Single endpoint \ud83d\udce6 Data returned Fixed shape (often too much/little) Exactly what you ask for \ud83d\ude80 Performance May send extra data Sends minimal, precise data \ud83e\udde0 Complexity Easier to cache Harder to cache"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#10-databases","title":"\ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f 10. Databases","text":"<p>\ud83d\udce6 What is a Database?</p> <p>A database is a dedicated system for:</p> <ul> <li>\ud83d\udcdd Storing data (like users, products, orders)</li> <li>\ud83d\udd0d Fetching data (like search, filter, sort)</li> <li>\u270f\ufe0f Updating data</li> <li>\u274c Deleting data</li> </ul> <p>All done in a safe, consistent, and fast way.</p> <p>\ud83d\udd04 How It Works (Simple Flow)</p> <pre><code>[ Client (App) ]\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nSends request to server (e.g., \"Get my profile\")\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\n[ Server ]\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nTalks to [ Database ] \u2192 fetches or stores data\n     \u2502\n     \u25bc\nSends result back to client\n</code></pre> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Different Types of Databases</p> <p>Depending on the app\u2019s needs, you choose different types:</p> Type Use Case Example \ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f SQL (Relational) Bank systems, e-commerce (MySQL, PostgreSQL) \ud83d\udce6 NoSQL (Document/Key-Value) Social media, real-time apps (MongoDB, Redis) \ud83e\udeb5 Time Series IoT, sensor data (InfluxDB) \ud83e\udde0 Graph Social networks (Neo4j)"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#11-sql-vs-nosql","title":"\u2696\ufe0f 11. SQL vs NoSQL","text":"<p>\ud83d\udd37 SQL (Relational Databases)</p> <ul> <li>Stores data in tables (like Excel sheets)</li> <li>Has a fixed schema \u2014 structure is predefined</li> <li>Ensures data integrity and consistency</li> <li>Follows ACID rules (safe transactions)</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Best for:</p> <ul> <li>Structured data</li> <li>Relationships (like users \u2192 orders)</li> <li>Apps that require accuracy and consistency (e.g., banks, inventory)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Examples:</p> <ul> <li>MySQL</li> <li>PostgreSQL</li> <li>SQLite</li> </ul> <p>\ud83d\udd36 NoSQL (Non-Relational Databases)</p> <ul> <li>Stores data in flexible formats (documents, key-values, graphs)</li> <li>No fixed schema \u2014 you can change structure anytime</li> <li>Designed for high performance and scalability</li> <li>Follows BASE principles (more flexible)</li> </ul> <p>\u2705 Best for:</p> <ul> <li>Big data</li> <li>Fast-changing or unstructured data</li> <li>Apps that need speed and scale (e.g., chat apps, recommendation engines)</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde0 Examples:</p> Type Description Example \ud83d\udd11 Key-Value Simple lookup by key Redis \ud83d\udcc4 Document JSON-like documents MongoDB \ud83c\udf10 Graph Complex relationships Neo4j \ud83d\udcda Wide-Column Big, distributed, column-based Cassandra <p>\ud83e\udd14 Which Should You Use?</p> Need Choose \u2705 Structured data SQL \u2705 Strong consistency SQL \u2705 Complex relationships SQL \u26a1 High speed &amp; flexibility NoSQL \ud83d\udd04 Massive scale &amp; updates NoSQL \ud83e\uddea Need both? Use both together!"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#12-vertical-scaling","title":"\ud83d\udcc8 12. Vertical Scaling","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Dev team increases EC2 instance size from <code>t3.medium</code> to <code>m5.large</code> to handle Black Friday traffic.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#13-horizontal-scaling","title":"\ud83e\udde9 13. Horizontal Scaling","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>More containers/pods deployed behind a load balancer.</li> <li>Kubernetes <code>HorizontalPodAutoscaler</code> adds pods when CPU &gt; 80%.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#14-load-balancers","title":"\u2696\ufe0f 14. Load Balancers","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>User hits <code>LoadBalancer</code> (like AWS ALB) which distributes to available backend pods.</li> <li>Ensures zero downtime even if one node fails.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#15-database-indexing","title":"\ud83d\uddc3\ufe0f 15. Database Indexing","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Indexes added to <code>user_id</code>, <code>product_name</code> fields to speed up search and sort.</li> <li>Slow query logs are monitored to add/remove indexes.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#16-replication","title":"\ud83d\udd01 16. Replication","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Writes go to master DB, reads served from multiple replicas.</li> <li>Improves performance and read scalability.</li> <li>Example: Amazon Aurora or MongoDB Replica Sets.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#17-sharding","title":"\ud83c\udf70 17. Sharding","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>100 million users \u2192 split across <code>user_shard_1</code>, <code>user_shard_2</code>...</li> <li>Based on region or user ID modulus.</li> <li>Helps in horizontal scaling of DB.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#18-vertical-partitioning","title":"\ud83e\uddee 18. Vertical Partitioning","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li><code>user_core</code> (id, email), <code>user_preferences</code> (dark_mode, language) stored separately.</li> <li>Reduces read payloads.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#19-caching","title":"\ud83d\ude80 19. Caching","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Redis/Memcached used to store recent products or user sessions.\\n- Prevents DB overload.\\n- API checks cache before hitting DB.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#20-denormalization","title":"\ud83d\udce6 20. Denormalization","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Store username in <code>comments</code> collection to avoid join with <code>users</code>.</li> <li>Speeds up reads at the cost of write duplication.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#21-cap-theorem","title":"\u2696\ufe0f 21. CAP Theorem","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Choose between Consistency (banking), Availability (social feeds), or Partition tolerance.</li> <li>Real life: DynamoDB = AP, RDBMS = CP.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#22-blob-storage","title":"\ud83e\uddfe 22. Blob Storage","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>User uploads invoice PDF \u2192 goes to AWS S3.\\n- Returns a public URL stored in DB.\\n- Used for storing images, audio, large files.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#23-cdn","title":"\ud83c\udf0d 23. CDN","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Static files like images, CSS served from CDN edge locations (Cloudflare/Akamai).</li> <li>Reduces latency, offloads origin servers.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#24-websockets","title":"\ud83d\udd04 24. WebSockets","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Real-time stock price updates or chat apps.\\n- Server pushes updates without client polling.\\n- Used via libraries like Socket.IO or Django Channels.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#25-webhooks","title":"\ud83d\udd14 25. Webhooks","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Stripe charges user \u2192 sends <code>POST /payment-success</code> to your server.\\n- Server logs payment and updates order status.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#26-microservices","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 26. Microservices","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Cart, Payment, Inventory are separate services.\\n- Communicate via REST, message queues.\\n- Deployed individually via Docker/Kubernetes.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#27-message-queues","title":"\ud83d\udce9 27. Message Queues","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>User places order \u2192 published to RabbitMQ \u2192 <code>order_processor</code> service consumes and processes it.\\n- Ensures decoupling and async flow.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#28-rate-limiting","title":"\ud83d\udea6 28. Rate Limiting","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Protect APIs from abuse using token buckets.\\n- NGINX or API Gateway returns 429 Too Many Requests if limit exceeded.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#29-api-gateways","title":"\ud83d\udee3\ufe0f 29. API Gateways","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>Central entry point like Kong or AWS API Gateway.\\n- Handles auth, logging, routing to microservices.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#30-idempotency","title":"\u2705 30. Idempotency","text":"<p>Execution:</p> <ul> <li>User refreshes payment screen \u2192 same <code>idempotency_key</code> ensures payment isn't duplicated.\\n- Stripe and Razorpay support it natively.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/overview.html#how-all-these-work-together-in-a-real-app-e-commerce","title":"\u2705 How All These Work Together in a Real App (E-Commerce):","text":"<ol> <li>User visits site \u2192 DNS resolves domain \u2192 reaches Load Balancer.</li> <li>Load Balancer sends traffic to available backend server.</li> <li>Backend fetches data from cache or DB (with index).</li> <li>Orders handled via REST APIs \u2192 stored in SQL \u2192 replicated to read replicas.</li> <li>Product info fetched from NoSQL \u2192 cached \u2192 served via CDN.</li> <li>Payments go via APIs (Stripe) \u2192 Webhook triggers status update.</li> <li>Background tasks (emails, invoices) handled via message queue.</li> <li>Scaling and fault tolerance handled via horizontal scaling, reverse proxies, rate limiting, and microservices architecture.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"System%20Design/API%20Design%20%26%20Gateways/API%20Gateway.html","title":"API Gateway","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/API%20Design%20%26%20Gateways/API%20Specs.html","title":"API Specs","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/API%20Design%20%26%20Gateways/API%20Versioning.html","title":"API Versioning","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/API%20Design%20%26%20Gateways/Circuit%20Breakers.html","title":"Circuit Breakers","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/API%20Design%20%26%20Gateways/Rate%20Limiting_Auth.html","title":"Rate Limiting Auth","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html","title":"Order Payment","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#order-payment-system","title":"\u2705 Order &amp; Payment System","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#overall-flow-one-look","title":"\ud83e\udde9 Overall Flow (One Look)","text":"<pre><code>User\n \u2193\nOrder Service\n \u2193\nInventory Service (Reserve)\n \u2193\nPayment Service\n \u2193\nPayment Gateway\n \u2193\nWebhook\n \u2193\nOrder Confirmation\n \u2193\nInventory Deduction\n \u2193\nNotification\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#1-order-creation-inventory-first","title":"1\ufe0f\u20e3 Order Creation (Inventory First)","text":"<p>Purpose: Create order safely without charging user</p> <pre><code>User \u2192 Order Service \u2192 Inventory Service\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>User places order</li> <li>Order Service validates items &amp; price</li> <li>Inventory Service reserves stock</li> <li>Order saved with status CREATED</li> <li>Order ID returned</li> </ol> <p>\u2705 No payment yet \u2705 Stock is locked</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#2-payment-initiation","title":"2\ufe0f\u20e3 Payment Initiation","text":"<p>Purpose: Start payment securely</p> <pre><code>User \u2192 Payment Service \u2192 Payment Gateway\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps_1","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>User clicks Pay</li> <li>Payment Service creates payment request</li> <li>Payment Gateway checkout opens</li> <li>Order updated to PAYMENT_PENDING</li> </ol> <p>\u2705 Card handled by gateway \u2705 Backend owns payment intent</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#3-payment-result-webhook-driven","title":"3\ufe0f\u20e3 Payment Result (Webhook Driven)","text":"<p>Purpose: Avoid fake success</p> <pre><code>Payment Gateway \u2192 Payment Service (Webhook)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps_2","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Gateway sends webhook (success / failure)</li> <li>Payment Service verifies signature</li> <li>Payment status saved</li> <li>Payment event published</li> </ol> <p>\ud83d\udea8 Frontend response is ignored</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#4-order-finalization","title":"4\ufe0f\u20e3 Order Finalization","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#if-payment-success","title":"\u2705 If Payment SUCCESS","text":"<pre><code>Payment Event \u2192 Order Service \u2192 Inventory Service\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps_3","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Order marked PAID</li> <li>Inventory stock deducted</li> <li>Order marked CONFIRMED</li> <li>Notification sent</li> </ol> <p>\ud83c\udfaf Money captured \ud83c\udfaf Order confirmed</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#if-payment-failure","title":"\u274c If Payment FAILURE","text":"<pre><code>Payment Event \u2192 Order Service \u2192 Inventory Service\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps_4","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>Order marked FAILED</li> <li>Inventory released</li> <li>User notified</li> </ol> <p>\ud83c\udfaf No money deducted \ud83c\udfaf Stock restored</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#5-refund-flow-optional","title":"5\ufe0f\u20e3 Refund Flow (Optional)","text":"<pre><code>User \u2192 Order Service \u2192 Payment Service \u2192 Gateway\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#steps_5","title":"Steps","text":"<ol> <li>User requests refund</li> <li>Payment Service initiates refund</li> <li>Gateway confirms refund</li> <li>Order \u2192 REFUNDED</li> <li>Inventory optionally restored</li> </ol>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#6-idempotency-flow-critical","title":"6\ufe0f\u20e3 Idempotency Flow (Critical)","text":"<p>Why: Prevent double charge</p> <pre><code>Client \u2192 Payment Service\n        (Idempotency-Key)\n</code></pre> <ul> <li>Same key = same response</li> <li>Stored in Redis / DB</li> <li>Safe retries</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#7-failure-recovery","title":"7\ufe0f\u20e3 Failure Recovery","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#payment-success-but-order-not-confirmed","title":"Payment success but order not confirmed","text":"<ul> <li>Event retry</li> <li>Reconciliation job fixes state</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#inventory-locked-too-long","title":"Inventory locked too long","text":"<ul> <li>Auto-release after timeout</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#8-order-state-machine-final","title":"8\ufe0f\u20e3 Order State Machine (Final)","text":"<pre><code>CREATED\n  \u2193\nPAYMENT_PENDING\n  \u2193\nPAID\n  \u2193\nCONFIRMED\n\nFAILED / CANCELLED / REFUNDED\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#database-design","title":"Database Design","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#order-table","title":"Order Table","text":"<pre><code>orders\n-----\nid (UUID)\nuser_id\nstatus\ntotal_amount\ncreated_at\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#order-items","title":"Order Items","text":"<pre><code>order_items\n-----------\norder_id\nproduct_id\nquantity\nprice\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#payments","title":"Payments","text":"<pre><code>payments\n--------\nid\norder_id\npayment_gateway\nstatus\ntransaction_id\namount\ncreated_at\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#inventory","title":"Inventory","text":"<pre><code>inventory\n---------\nproduct_id\navailable_qty\nreserved_qty\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Applications/order_payment.html#tech-stack-example-aws-friendly","title":"Tech Stack (Example \u2013 AWS Friendly)","text":"<ul> <li>Backend: Python (FastAPI) / Java (Spring Boot)</li> <li>DB: PostgreSQL</li> <li>Queue: AWS SQS / Kafka</li> <li>Cache: Redis (locks, idempotency)</li> <li>Payments: Razorpay / Stripe</li> <li>Infra: ECS + ALB</li> <li>CI/CD: GitHub Actions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Caching/CDN%20Edge%20Caching.html","title":"CDN Edge Caching","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Caching/Cache%20Invalidation.html","title":"Cache Invalidation","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Caching/Caching%20Tools.html","title":"Caching Tools","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Caching/Eviction%20Policies.html","title":"Eviction Policies","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Client-Server%20Model.html","title":"Client Server Model","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Load%20Balancing.html","title":"Load Balancing","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Microservices_Monoliths.html","title":"Microservices Monoliths","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/REST_RPC_GraphQL.html","title":"REST RPC GraphQL","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Service%20Discovery.html","title":"Service Discovery","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Stateless_Stateful.html","title":"Stateless Stateful","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Core%20Concepts/Sync_Async%20Communication.html","title":"Sync Async Communication","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/CAP%20Theorem.html","title":"CAP Theorem","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/Caching%20Strategies.html","title":"Caching Strategies","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/Eventual%20Consistency.html","title":"Eventual Consistency","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/Indexing_Query%20Optimization.html","title":"Indexing Query Optimization","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/Replication.html","title":"Replication","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Data%20Management/SQL_NoSQL.html","title":"SQL NoSQL","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/12Factor%20App.html","title":"12Factor App","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/CQRS.html","title":"CQRS","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/Classic%20Patterns.html","title":"Classic Patterns","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/Dependency%20Injection.html","title":"Dependency Injection","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/Event-driven%20Architecture.html","title":"Event driven Architecture","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/Repository%20Pattern.html","title":"Repository Pattern","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Design%20Patterns%20%26%20Principles/Saga%20Pattern.html","title":"Saga Pattern","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Distributed%20Systems%20Concepts/Consensus%20Algorithms.html","title":"Consensus Algorithms","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Distributed%20Systems%20Concepts/Distributed%20Locks.html","title":"Distributed Locks","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Distributed%20Systems%20Concepts/Leader%20Election.html","title":"Leader Election","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Distributed%20Systems%20Concepts/Logical%20Clocks.html","title":"Logical Clocks","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Distributed%20Systems%20Concepts/Quorum.html","title":"Quorum","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/High%20Availability%20%26%20Fault%20Tolerance/Auto%20Healing.html","title":"Auto Healing","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/High%20Availability%20%26%20Fault%20Tolerance/Failover%20Mechanisms.html","title":"Failover Mechanisms","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/High%20Availability%20%26%20Fault%20Tolerance/Graceful%20Degradation.html","title":"Graceful Degradation","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/High%20Availability%20%26%20Fault%20Tolerance/Health%20Checks.html","title":"Health Checks","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/High%20Availability%20%26%20Fault%20Tolerance/Redundancy.html","title":"Redundancy","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Message%20Queues%20%26%20Async%20Processing/Dead%20Letter%20Queues%20%28DLQ%29.html","title":"Dead Letter Queues (DLQ)","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Message%20Queues%20%26%20Async%20Processing/Message%20Brokers.html","title":"Message Brokers","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Message%20Queues%20%26%20Async%20Processing/Pub_Sub.html","title":"Pub Sub","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Message%20Queues%20%26%20Async%20Processing/Task%20Queues.html","title":"Task Queues","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Observability/Alerting.html","title":"Alerting","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Observability/Logging.html","title":"Logging","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Observability/Monitoring.html","title":"Monitoring","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Observability/Tracing.html","title":"Tracing","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html","title":"WeamAI","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#system-design-concepts-used-in-weam-ai","title":"\u2705 System Design Concepts Used in Weam AI","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#api-design-architecture","title":"\ud83d\udd39 API Design &amp; Architecture","text":"<p>RESTful API design (fastapi) Stateless APIs (pydantic, starlette) Microservices architecture (fastapi, uvicorn, gunicorn)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#async-processing-concurrency","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Async Processing &amp; Concurrency","text":"<p>Async / Await (asyncio \u2013 built-in) Non-blocking I/O (httpx, aiofiles)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#background-processing","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Background Processing","text":"<p>Background tasks (fastapi \u2013 BackgroundTasks) Task queues (celery, redis) Async workers (celery, kombu)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#database-design","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Database Design","text":"<p>MongoDB integration (pymongo) Async MongoDB (motor) ODM / schema layer (beanie, odmantic)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#caching","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Caching","text":"<p>Distributed caching (redis) Async Redis client (redis) Response caching (fastapi-cache2)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#security-authentication","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Security &amp; Authentication","text":"<p>JWT authentication (python-jose) OAuth2 authentication (fastapi) Password hashing (passlib, bcrypt) CSRF protection (starlette) Encryption &amp; decryption (cryptography)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#rate-limiting-reliability","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Rate Limiting &amp; Reliability","text":"<p>Rate limiting (slowapi) Retry mechanism (tenacity) Error handling (fastapi)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#observability-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Observability &amp; Monitoring","text":"<p>Metrics (prometheus-fastapi-instrumentator) Structured logging (loguru, structlog) Distributed tracing (opentelemetry-sdk)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#file-storage-processing","title":"\ud83d\udd39 File Storage &amp; Processing","text":"<p>File uploads (python-multipart) AWS S3 integration (boto3) Async S3 operations (aioboto3) Async file handling (aiofiles)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#ai-llm-system-design","title":"\ud83d\udd39 AI / LLM System Design","text":"<p>Prompt engineering (langchain) Agent workflows (langgraph) Token counting (tiktoken) Vector DB client (pinecone-client) LLM APIs (openai)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#messaging-notifications","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Messaging &amp; Notifications","text":"<p>Email sending (fastapi-mail) SMTP support (aiosmtplib) Push notifications (firebase-admin)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#testing-quality","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Testing &amp; Quality","text":"<p>Unit testing (pytest) Async test support (pytest-asyncio) API testing (httpx) Load testing (locust)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#devops-deployment","title":"\ud83d\udd39 DevOps &amp; Deployment","text":"<p>ASGI server (uvicorn) Process manager (gunicorn) Environment config (python-dotenv) Containerization (docker) (platform) Orchestration (kubernetes) (platform)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#scalability","title":"\ud83d\udd39 Scalability","text":"<p>Horizontal scaling (uvicorn, gunicorn) Stateless scaling (fastapi, redis) Worker scaling (celery)</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Project/weam_design.html#one-line-interview-answer","title":"\ud83c\udfaf One-Line Interview Answer","text":"<p>\u201cWe use FastAPI with Pydantic and Starlette for stateless REST APIs, async processing with httpx and aiofiles, background jobs using Celery and Redis, JWT/OAuth security via python-jose and passlib, MongoDB via Motor/Beanie, and scalable deployment using Uvicorn, Gunicorn, Docker, and Kubernetes.\u201d</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/CDN.html","title":"CDN","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/CDN.html#workflow-create-use-cdn-url-from-s3","title":"\ud83d\udce6 Workflow: Create &amp; Use CDN URL from S3","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb DEVELOPER\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Upload File to S3\n             \u2502\n             \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Example: s3://my-bucket/images/photo.jpg\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Uploaded via SDK, CLI, web UI, or API\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f AWS SETUP (ONE-TIME CONFIG)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Create CloudFront Distribution\n   \u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 Origin: my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com\n   \u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 Restrict bucket access (optional)\n   \u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 Enable caching, compression\n   \u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 Get domain: https://d123abc456.cloudfront.net\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Configure S3 Permissions\n           \u251c\u2500\u2500 Make file public OR\n           \u2514\u2500\u2500 Use signed CloudFront URLs for private content\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udcc4 BACKEND SERVICE (API)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Receives request to fetch media URL\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Generates CDN URL using S3 object key\n   \u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 Example:\n   \u2502           key = \"images/photo.jpg\"\n   \u2502           cdn_url = \"https://d123abc456.cloudfront.net/\" + key\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Returns the CDN URL to frontend in API response\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f CLIENT (BROWSER / APP)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Loads CDN URL (e.g. image/video)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; CDN edge server checks cache\n   \u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 If cached \u2192 respond instantly\n   \u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 If not cached \u2192 fetch from S3 and cache it\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Media is displayed in UI instantly and globally optimized\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/CDN.html#why-use-a-cdn-url-instead-of-s3-url","title":"\u2705 Why Use a CDN URL Instead of S3 URL","text":"# Reason What Happens with CDN Benefit to You 1 \ud83d\ude80 Faster Loading Files are served from nearby locations Quick file load for all global users 2 \ud83d\udce6 Cached Content CDN stores file after first request No need to fetch again = faster &amp; lighter 3 \ud83d\udcb0 Save S3 Bandwidth CDN handles repeat traffic Lower AWS costs 4 \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f More Security (Optional) Can use signed URLs &amp; keep S3 private Protect sensitive files from public access 5 \u2696\ufe0f Scales Globally CDN handles millions of users with low latency Stable and fast even under heavy traffic"},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/Horizontal_Vertical%20Scaling.html","title":"Horizontal Vertical Scaling","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/Load%20Balancers.html","title":"Load Balancers","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/Sharding_Partitioning.html","title":"Sharding Partitioning","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Scalability/Sticky%20Sessions.html","title":"Sticky Sessions","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Authentication.html","title":"Authentication","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Authorization.html","title":"Authorization","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Common%20Threats.html","title":"Common Threats","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/DDoS%20Protection.html","title":"DDoS Protection","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Encryption.html","title":"Encryption","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html","title":"Pre SignedUrl","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html#what-is-a-pre-signed-url","title":"\ud83d\udd10 What is a Pre-Signed URL?","text":"<p>A pre-signed URL is a temporary, secure link to access a private file in S3 \u2014 without making the file public.</p> <p>\ud83d\udfe2 Anyone with the link can access the file \u2014 but only until it expires.</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html#real-world-example","title":"\u2705 Real-World Example","text":"<p>You have a private S3 file:</p> <pre><code>s3://my-bucket/reports/salary.pdf\n</code></pre> <p>You want to let User A download it for 5 minutes, without exposing your bucket to the public.</p> <p>\u2705 Use a pre-signed URL!</p> <p>It creates a temporary link like:</p> <pre><code>https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/reports/salary.pdf?AWSAccessKeyId=...&amp;Expires=...&amp;Signature=...\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html#how-pre-signed-urls-work-step-by-step","title":"\ud83d\udd01 How Pre-Signed URLs Work (Step-by-Step)","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Step 1: File is stored in S3 (Private)\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Only your AWS user/role can access it\n\n\ud83d\udd11 Step 2: Server Generates Pre-Signed URL\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Signed using IAM credentials\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Includes expiration time (e.g., 5 mins)\n\n\ud83d\udd17 Step 3: Server Sends Pre-Signed URL to User\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; e.g., in API response or email\n\n\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f Step 4: User Clicks the Link\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Can download or view the file directly\n\n\u23f3 Step 5: After Expiry, the Link Stops Working\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Protects your content from unauthorized reuse\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html#example-in-python-boto3","title":"\ud83e\uddea Example in Python (Boto3)","text":"<pre><code>import boto3\nimport os\n\ns3 = boto3.client(\"s3\")\n\ndef generate_presigned_url(bucket, key, expires_in=300):\n    return s3.generate_presigned_url(\n        'get_object',\n        Params={'Bucket': bucket, 'Key': key},\n        ExpiresIn=expires_in  # in seconds (e.g., 300 = 5 min)\n    )\n\nurl = generate_presigned_url(\"my-bucket\", \"reports/salary.pdf\")\nprint(\"Pre-signed URL:\", url)\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Pre-SignedUrl.html#why-pre-signed-urls-are-safe","title":"\ud83d\udd12 Why Pre-Signed URLs Are Safe","text":"Feature How It Helps \u23f1\ufe0f Expiration URL stops working after X minutes \ud83d\udd11 IAM Signer Only someone with AWS credentials can create it \ud83c\udf10 Private S3 File is still private (not public at all) \u2705 No Reuse Can\u2019t guess or reuse after expiry"},{"location":"System%20Design/Security/Transport%20Security.html","title":"Transport Security","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html","title":"30-Concepts-Cover-Project","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#full-project-plan-scalable-ai-powered-content-sharing-platform-fastapi","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Full Project Plan: Scalable AI-Powered Content Sharing Platform (FastAPI)","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#objective","title":"\ud83c\udfaf Objective","text":"<p>Design and build a production-ready content sharing platform using FastAPI, covering all 30+ key system design principles, from infrastructure to real-time APIs.</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-0-planning-scope-definition","title":"\ud83d\udcd8 Phase 0: Planning &amp; Scope Definition","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#what-the-app-will-do","title":"\u2705 What the App Will Do","text":"<ul> <li>Users upload files (images, videos, documents)</li> <li>Backend stores them in S3 (or MinIO locally)</li> <li>Generate CDN URL for file delivery</li> <li>AI service summarizes content in background (async)</li> <li>Notify users via WebSocket or webhook when ready</li> <li>Admins view analytics &amp; manage content</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#key-features","title":"\u2705 Key Features","text":"<ul> <li>Authentication (JWT)</li> <li>Upload + Serve via Pre-signed URL</li> <li>Background AI processing (via Celery)</li> <li>Notifications (WebSocket + Webhook)</li> <li>Caching + Rate Limiting</li> <li>Real-time analytics for Admin</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-1-project-setup-environment","title":"\u2699\ufe0f Phase 1: Project Setup &amp; Environment","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#11-initialize-project","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1.1 Initialize Project","text":"<ul> <li>GitHub repo setup with branch protections</li> <li>Create <code>.gitignore</code>, <code>README.md</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#12-setup-virtual-environment","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1.2 Setup Virtual Environment","text":"<ul> <li>Use <code>venv</code> or <code>poetry</code></li> <li>Install: FastAPI, Uvicorn, SQLAlchemy, Redis, Celery, boto3</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#13-define-folder-structure","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1.3 Define Folder Structure","text":"<pre><code>/app\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 api/               # API routes\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 services/          # Business logic\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 models/            # DB models\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 schemas/           # Pydantic schemas\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 websocket/         # WebSocket handlers\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 tasks/             # Celery workers\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 core/              # Config, rate limiting, logging\n  \u2514\u2500\u2500 main.py            # Entry point\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#14-local-docker-setup","title":"\ud83d\udd39 1.4 Local Docker Setup","text":"<ul> <li>PostgreSQL + Redis containers</li> <li>MinIO (for S3 emulation)</li> <li>docker-compose for orchestration</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-2-core-infrastructure","title":"\ud83e\uddf1 Phase 2: Core Infrastructure","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#21-postgresql-setup","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2.1 PostgreSQL Setup","text":"<ul> <li>Users, Files, Summary Logs tables</li> <li>Use Alembic for migrations</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#22-redis-setup","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2.2 Redis Setup","text":"<ul> <li>Caching layer</li> <li>Rate limiting store</li> <li>Task queue broker</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#23-minio-or-s3","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2.3 MinIO or S3","text":"<ul> <li>Bucket setup for file storage</li> <li>Permissions for pre-signed URL</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#24-cloudfront-cdn-if-live","title":"\ud83d\udd39 2.4 CloudFront / CDN (if live)","text":"<ul> <li>Setup public domain like <code>cdn.mysite.com</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-3-api-development","title":"\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbb Phase 3: API Development","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#31-auth-module","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3.1 Auth Module","text":"<ul> <li>Signup/Login endpoints</li> <li>JWT token generator + user middleware</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#32-file-upload-module","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3.2 File Upload Module","text":"<ul> <li>Endpoint to generate S3 upload pre-signed URL</li> <li>Endpoint to store metadata in DB</li> <li>Return <code>cdn_url</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#33-file-summary-status","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3.3 File Summary &amp; Status","text":"<ul> <li>Endpoint to fetch file + AI summary</li> <li>Endpoint to refresh status</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#34-admin-apis","title":"\ud83d\udd39 3.4 Admin APIs","text":"<ul> <li>Get analytics, top users, file counts</li> <li>GraphQL (optional)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-4-background-processing-ai","title":"\ud83e\udd16 Phase 4: Background Processing &amp; AI","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#41-setup-celery-worker","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4.1 Setup Celery Worker","text":"<ul> <li>Redis as broker</li> <li>Worker loads file \u2192 extracts summary \u2192 stores result</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#42-trigger-worker","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4.2 Trigger Worker","text":"<ul> <li>Upon file upload, enqueue task</li> <li>Include file ID for tracking</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#43-store-summary-results","title":"\ud83d\udd39 4.3 Store Summary Results","text":"<ul> <li>Store in PostgreSQL (indexed)</li> <li>Cache summary in Redis</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-5-real-time-webhooks","title":"\ud83d\udd14 Phase 5: Real-Time &amp; Webhooks","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#51-websocket-setup","title":"\ud83d\udd39 5.1 WebSocket Setup","text":"<ul> <li>User connects via <code>/ws/{user_id}</code></li> <li>Server pushes summary notification</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#52-webhook-callbacks","title":"\ud83d\udd39 5.2 Webhook Callbacks","text":"<ul> <li>Users can register a webhook URL</li> <li>Once summary is ready, send payload</li> <li>Retry failed webhooks</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-6-optimization-scaling","title":"\u26a1 Phase 6: Optimization &amp; Scaling","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#61-caching","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6.1 Caching","text":"<ul> <li>Redis for summary, profile caching</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#62-rate-limiting","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6.2 Rate Limiting","text":"<ul> <li>Middleware using Redis token bucket</li> <li>Limit by IP/user for upload &amp; download</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#63-indexing-query-optimization","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6.3 Indexing &amp; Query Optimization","text":"<ul> <li>Add indexes for user_id, file_type, created_at</li> <li>Materialized views (optional)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#64-sharding-partitioning","title":"\ud83d\udd39 6.4 Sharding / Partitioning","text":"<ul> <li>Partition file tables by month/user region</li> <li>Scale read replicas for summary logs</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#phase-7-deployment-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\ude80 Phase 7: Deployment &amp; Monitoring","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#71-production-docker-build","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7.1 Production Docker Build","text":"<ul> <li>Multi-stage Dockerfile</li> <li>Gunicorn with Uvicorn worker</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#72-deployment","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7.2 Deployment","text":"<ul> <li>Use EC2 / ECS / Kubernetes</li> <li>Set up CI/CD with GitHub Actions</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#73-https-cdn","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7.3 HTTPS + CDN","text":"<ul> <li>Certbot/Cloudflare for HTTPS</li> <li>CloudFront for CDN</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#74-logging-monitoring","title":"\ud83d\udd39 7.4 Logging &amp; Monitoring","text":"<ul> <li>ELK Stack or Grafana + Prometheus</li> <li>Log exceptions, slow queries, and traffic</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/30_Concept_Cover_Project.html#system-design-concepts-covered","title":"\u2705 System Design Concepts Covered","text":"Concept Covered By Client-Server Architecture API architecture IP, DNS Hosting, CDN setup HTTP/HTTPS REST endpoints + secure access REST, GraphQL, APIs FastAPI routes + optional GraphQL APIs SQL vs NoSQL PostgreSQL + Redis Caching Redis caching layer Rate Limiting Redis middleware Replication, Partitioning Read replica setup, partitioned logs Load Balancing NGINX or ALB Vertical/Horizontal Scaling Docker + K8s + ECS/EKS File Storage &amp; CDN S3, MinIO, CloudFront WebSockets Real-time user updates Webhooks External integrations Message Queues Celery + Redis Microservices Background processing with Celery CAP Theorem DB + Cache + Queue design trade-offs Idempotency Upload &amp; webhook retry design API Gateway NGINX proxy or Kong (optional) Database Indexing Performance tuning Denormalization Summary logs for analytics"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/E-Commerce.html","title":"E-Commerce","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/E-Commerce.html#e-commerce-system-design-workflow","title":"\ud83d\uded2 E-Commerce System Design Workflow","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udccc Step 1: Requirement Gathering\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Define Core Features: product listing, cart, checkout, order\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Define User Roles: guest, customer, admin\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Define Non-Functional Goals: high availability, low latency, security\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcd0 Step 2: High-Level Architecture Design\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Clients: Web (React/Next.js), Mobile (React Native)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; API Gateway / Backend for Frontend (optional)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Backend Services: Auth, Product, Cart, Order, Payment\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Databases: PostgreSQL, Redis, MongoDB (if needed)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; File Storage: AWS S3 / GCS\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; External Services: Stripe, Razorpay, SendGrid\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Communication Protocols: REST, WebSocket\n   \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddf1 Step 3: Low-Level Design (Service Internals)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Auth Module: JWT/OAuth2, password reset\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Product Module: CRUD, categories, search, filters\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Cart Module: add/remove/update cart items\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Order Module: order placement, status, history\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Payment Module: callback handling, success/failure\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Admin Panel: product/user management, dashboard\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcbe Step 4: Database Layer\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; PostgreSQL: users, orders, products, categories, inventory\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Redis: session tokens, rate limiting, cart cache\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; MongoDB (optional): product logs, audit trails\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; DB Relationships: users \u2194 orders \u2194 products \u2194 stock\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Optimization: indexes, normalization, sharding/partitioning\n   \u25bc\n\u267b\ufe0f Step 5: Communication &amp; Background Processing\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; REST APIs: CRUD endpoints, auth, search, order\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; WebSocket: real-time stock updates (optional)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Background Workers: payment retries, email confirmations\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Queue System: Redis Streams / Celery / Kafka\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udc33 Step 6: DevOps &amp; Deployment\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Dockerize backend, frontend, DB, cache\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Create docker-compose.yml (local, staging)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; CI/CD Pipeline: GitHub Actions \u2192 Docker Build &amp; Deploy\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Infrastructure: AWS/GCP + Kubernetes (EKS/GKE)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; IaC: Terraform / Pulumi for managing infrastructure\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd10 Step 7: Security Layer\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; HTTPS &amp; SSL (Let's Encrypt, Cloudflare)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; JWT/Refresh Token-based Auth\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Input validation (Pydantic, Joi)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; CSRF/XSS Protection + Secure Headers\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; RBAC: Role-Based Access Control (admin, user)\n   \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddea Step 8: Testing Strategy\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Unit Tests: functions, endpoints (Pytest, Jest)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Integration Tests: user \u2192 cart \u2192 order flow\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; E2E Tests: Playwright / Cypress (frontend + backend)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; docker-compose.test.yml: CI test execution with coverage\n   \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcca Step 9: Monitoring &amp; Observability\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Logs: Loki / ELK (ElasticSearch + Kibana)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Metrics: Prometheus + Grafana dashboards\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Error Tracking: Sentry (frontend + backend)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Health Checks: readiness/liveness probes (K8s)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Alerting: UptimeRobot / PagerDuty\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html","title":"Most Ask System Design","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#1-url-shortener-bitly-style","title":"1) URL Shortener (bit.ly style)","text":"<p>Goal / Requirements</p> <ul> <li>F: create short code for a long URL, redirect quickly.</li> <li>NF: very low latency redirect (&lt;50ms), high read throughput, high availability, eventual consistency ok for creation.</li> <li>Scale: ~millions of redirects/day.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; CDN / NGINX -&gt; API Gateway -&gt; Shortener Service -&gt; Cache(Redis) -&gt; DB(Postgres/Dynamo)\n                                         \u2198 Metrics/Logging\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>API (create, lookup)</li> <li>ID generator (Base62, incremental with sharding or hash + collision resolution)</li> <li>DB (primary store)</li> <li>Redis (hot cache)</li> <li>Worker for analytics &amp; cleanup</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <pre><code>urls (\n  short_code varchar PK,\n  long_url text,\n  user_id int,\n  created_at timestamp,\n  expire_at timestamp NULL,\n  clicks bigint\n)\n</code></pre> <p>Data flow</p> <ol> <li>Create: client POST <code>/shorten</code> \u2192 API validates \u2192 generate <code>short_code</code></li> <li>Persist to DB (transaction). Optionally write to cache.</li> <li> <p>Redirect: client hits <code>/s/{short_code}</code> \u2192 NGINX -&gt; service -&gt; check Redis</p> </li> <li> <p>Cache hit: return redirect (HTTP 301)</p> </li> <li>Cache miss: query DB, populate Redis, redirect</li> <li>Analytics: increment click counters in async worker (batch to DB)</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Read-heavy: horizontally scale stateless API + Redis cluster + read replicas for DB or use DynamoDB for infinite scale.</li> <li>Partition short codes by prefix to scale writes.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Redis miss OK.</li> <li>DB write failure: retry with idempotency (client token) and return 5xx.</li> <li>Short-code collisions: detect and regenerate.</li> </ul> <p>Caching &amp; Consistency</p> <ul> <li>TTL on cache; eventual consistency acceptable for analytics.</li> <li>Use cache aside pattern.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Latency (P95), cache hit ratio, error rate, create/sec, redirects/sec.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI, Uvicorn, aioredis, SQLAlchemy/asyncpg or boto3 for DynamoDB.</li> <li>Hashids/Base62 libs.</li> </ul> <p>Interview talking points</p> <ul> <li>Tradeoffs: fixed-length vs incremental IDs, using Redis vs CDN for redirects, supporting custom aliases, deletion/expiry policies, analytics pipeline.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#2-rate-limiter-api-level","title":"2) Rate Limiter (API-level)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Enforce limits per user/API key (e.g., 100 req/min), protect services.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API Gateway (Nginx/Envoy) -&gt; RateLimiter (Redis) -&gt; Service\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>API Gateway + middleware</li> <li>Rate limiter storage (Redis, ZK less common)</li> <li>Algorithms: Token bucket, Leaky bucket, Sliding window log/count</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <ul> <li>Redis keys: <code>rate:{user_id}</code> storing tokens or timestamped counters (sorted set or counter+TTL)</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Request arrives; gateway calls rate-limiter check.</li> <li> <p>Use LUA script in Redis to atomically:</p> </li> <li> <p>Check tokens; if available, decrement and allow.</p> </li> <li>Else deny (429).</li> <li>If allowed, forward request.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Use Redis Cluster; run limiter as middleware in API gateway for local short-circuit.</li> <li>For global limits across regions, use central Redis or approximate via local + periodic sync.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Redis down: either fail-open (not recommended) or fail-closed; prefer degrade to conservative limit or use local fallback with higher false positives.</li> </ul> <p>Caching &amp; Consistency</p> <ul> <li>Strict atomicity required \u2192 use Redis LUA scripts.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Rejections/sec, avg tokens, latency added by limiter.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI middleware, aioredis, implement Lua scripts via <code>redis-py</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Algorithm choice (token-bucket vs sliding window) and why (smooth bursts vs precise).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#3-notification-system-emailsmspush","title":"3) Notification System (Email/SMS/Push)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Deliver notifications reliably and scalably with retries.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Notification API -&gt; Queue(Kafka/RabbitMQ) -&gt; Worker Pool -&gt; 3rd-party (Twilio, SendGrid)\n                                     \u2193\n                                 DB (status)\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>API to enqueue notifications</li> <li>Message broker for decoupling and scaling</li> <li>Workers (Celery/async) for sending &amp; retry</li> <li>Provider adapters &amp; backoff</li> <li>DB for audit/status</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <pre><code>notifications(id, to, type, payload, status, attempts, next_retry_at, created_at)\n</code></pre> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Enqueue job (write DB + push to queue).</li> <li>Worker consumes: pick job, call provider API.</li> <li>On success: mark DB success, emit event.</li> <li>On failure: increment attempts, schedule retry (exponential backoff). On max attempts \u2192 mark failed.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Increase workers, partition queue by provider or tenant.</li> <li>Use idempotent send (dedupe via message id).</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Provider 5xx: retry with backoff.</li> <li>Provider rate limits: worker respects <code>Retry-After</code> header and pushes job to delayed queue.</li> </ul> <p>Caching</p> <ul> <li>Not needed for delivery; cache provider metadata or templates.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Delivery rate, failure rate, avg send latency, queue lag.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI + Celery/RabbitMQ or Faust for Kafka; <code>httpx</code> or <code>aiohttp</code> for provider calls.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Ensure idempotency, MVC of retry policy, and dead-letter queue design.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#4-e-commerce-small-amazon","title":"4) E-commerce (small Amazon)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Browse/catalog, add to cart, checkout, orders, inventory consistency.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API Gateway -&gt; {Search, Catalog, Cart, Order, Payment} \n                    \u2198 Event Bus (Kafka) -&gt; Inventory / Fulfillment / Notification\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Product catalog, search (ElasticSearch), cart (Redis), inventory (Postgres/NoSQL), order service, payment gateway, event bus.</li> </ul> <p>Schemas (samples)</p> <ul> <li><code>products(id, sku, name, desc, price, stock)</code></li> <li><code>orders(id, user_id, items[], status, created_at)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow (checkout)</p> <ol> <li>User initiates checkout.</li> <li>Order Service: create pending order, attempt inventory reservation (atomic or via distributed lock).</li> <li>Payment service: charge user (3rd-party).</li> <li>On success: finalize order \u2192 decrement stock, publish <code>order_placed</code> event.</li> <li>Workers: fulfillment, shipping, notifications.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Use read replicas for product reads, sharded order DB by user_id, Redis for carts.</li> <li>Inventory is critical: use optimistic locking (version columns) or a dedicated inventory service with strong consistency.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Payment success but DB failure: use two-phase commit-ish flow or rely on compensating transactions (refund).</li> <li>Inventory overcommit: use reservation layer + periodic reconciliation.</li> </ul> <p>Caching</p> <ul> <li>Product pages cached at CDN/Redis. Cart in Redis with TTL.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Checkout conversion, payment failure rates, inventory mismatch counts, queue lag.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI microservices, SQLAlchemy, Celery/Kafka, ElasticSearch client.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Strong vs eventual consistency on inventory, order idempotency, transactional outbox pattern for events.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#5-logging-system-elk-like","title":"5) Logging System (ELK-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Ingest, process, index, and query logs reliably.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>App -&gt; FluentBit -&gt; Kafka -&gt; Log Processor -&gt; ElasticSearch -&gt; Kibana\n                          \u2198 Cold Storage (S3)\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Agents (Fluentd/FluentBit), message queue (Kafka), processors (parsers/enrichers), indexer (Elasticsearch), long-term storage (S3).</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Agent collects logs from apps.</li> <li>Sends to Kafka for durability.</li> <li>Consumers parse/enrich and index into ES.</li> <li>Archive raw logs to S3.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Kafka partitions, multiple consumer groups, ES cluster with shard strategy.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Backpressure: Kafka retention protects ingestion; if ES slow, queue backs up; implement circuit breakers &amp; throttling.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Ingestion rate, indexing latency, query latency, storage size.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>Log collectors (not Python), processing pipelines can be Python workers (confluent-kafka + logstash-like processors).</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Indexing strategy and TTL, cost optimization (hot/warm/cold storage), schema-less vs structured logs.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#6-real-time-chat-whatsapp-like","title":"6) Real-time Chat (WhatsApp-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Real-time message delivery, at-least-once or exactly-once semantics for user experience.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client &lt;--&gt; WebSocket Gateway -&gt; Chat Service -&gt; Message Store (Cassandra) \n                            \u2198 Pub/Sub (Redis/Kafka) -&gt; Delivery Workers -&gt; Offline Queue\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>WebSocket gateway, message service, durable message store (Cassandra), pub/sub for routing, delivery workers, push notification service.</li> </ul> <p>Schema</p> <ul> <li><code>messages(id, chat_id, sender, content, ts, delivered_to[])</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Sender sends message over websocket.</li> <li>Gateway forwards to Chat Service which persist message (append-only).</li> <li>Chat Service publishes to pub/sub topic for recipient(s).</li> <li>Delivery worker pushes to connected clients; if offline, push via mobile push and keep as undelivered.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Partition chats by chat_id across nodes; use stateless gateways behind LB.</li> <li>Cassandra for high write throughput and predictable latency.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Gateway disconnects: client reconnect logic with exponential backoff and resume using last seen message ID.</li> <li>Message duplication: use sequence numbers for idempotency.</li> </ul> <p>Consistency</p> <ul> <li>Eventual consistency for read receipts; store authoritative in DB for reconciliation.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Message latency, delivery success %, connection count, consumer lag.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI or Sanic for websocket gateway; aioredis or confluent-kafka for pub/sub; use gRPC between services.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Choosing between PoD (push vs pull), persistent storage design (Cassandra vs DynamoDB), and end-to-end encryption considerations.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#7-file-storage-google-drive-like","title":"7) File Storage (Google Drive-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Upload/download large files, support resumable uploads, strong durability.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Upload API -&gt; Chunker -&gt; Object Store (S3/MinIO) \n                     -&gt; Metadata DB -&gt; CDN for downloads\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Upload service (handles chunking), object storage, metadata DB, background integrity checks, CDN for read.</li> </ul> <p>Schema</p> <ul> <li><code>files(id, owner, metadata, chunk_ids[], size, checksum)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Initiate upload \u2192 get upload-id.</li> <li>Client uploads chunks (each chunk PUT to pre-signed S3 URL).</li> <li>On final chunk, Upload service verifies checksums, writes metadata to DB.</li> <li>Download: generate pre-signed URL or serve via CDN.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Object storage scales out (S3).</li> <li>Metadata DB can be sharded.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Partial uploads: expire incomplete uploads, cleanup async.</li> <li>Corrupt chunk: retry chunk upload; verify checksum.</li> </ul> <p>Caching</p> <ul> <li>CDN for frequently downloaded files.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Upload success rate, storage cost, read throughput.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI for control plane, boto3 for S3 interactions, Celery for cleanup/verification.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Choosing chunk size tradeoffs, resumable upload semantics, deduplication, encryption at rest.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#8-payment-system-upibank-level","title":"8) Payment System (UPI/Bank-level)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Secure, ACID-like transactions, idempotent, strong auditing.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Payment API -&gt; Auth Service -&gt; Ledger DB (Postgres) -&gt; Bank/Network\n                                     \u2198 Event Bus -&gt; Reconciliation\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Auth, Payment Gateway, Ledger DB, Settlement subsystem, Fraud detection, Audit logs.</li> </ul> <p>Schema</p> <ul> <li><code>transactions(id, from_account, to_account, amount, status, created_at)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Initiate payment: authenticate, validate balance.</li> <li>Create transaction record (pending), attempt debit (reserve funds).</li> <li>Call external bank network (sync/async).</li> <li>On success: mark completed, publish event for settlement.</li> <li>On failure: rollback (release reservation) and notify user.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Use sharded ledger DB or logical partitioning; strong consistency needed \u2192 Postgres with SERIALIZABLE or two-phase commit patterns for cross-shard.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Network timeout after debit: implement idempotency, transaction reconciliation, and human ops tools.</li> </ul> <p>Security</p> <ul> <li>TLS, tokenization, HSM for keys, PCI compliance where required.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Failure rates, reconciliation mismatch, fraud signals.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI service layer, SQLAlchemy with Postgres, Celery for async settlement.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>ACID vs eventual consistency tradeoffs, reconciliations, dispute flows.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#9-redis-based-cache-system-generic","title":"9) Redis-based Cache System (generic)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Reduce DB load and latency with cache-aside pattern.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; App -&gt; Redis -&gt; DB\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Redis cluster, app cache logic, fallbacks, TTL policies.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Read: check Redis; miss \u2192 DB read \u2192 populate Redis.</li> <li>Write: write DB then invalidate cache or update cache (write-through or write-around patterns).</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Redis Cluster with key-based sharding; use consistent hashing for memcached style.</li> </ul> <p>Failure</p> <ul> <li>Redis failover: use Redis Sentinel or managed service.</li> <li>Cache stampede: use mutex locks or probabilistic early refresh.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Hit ratio, eviction rate, latency.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>aioredis, redis-py, use cachelib or custom decorators.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Invalidation strategies, cache warming, multi-layer caching.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#10-authentication-login-signup-jwt","title":"10) Authentication (Login / Signup / JWT)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Secure authentication with stateless tokens and optional revocation.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Auth Service -&gt; User DB (Postgres) \n                     \u2198 Token Store (Redis for blacklist)\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Signup, login, token issuance (JWT), refresh tokens, logout/blacklist, password reset.</li> </ul> <p>Schema</p> <ul> <li><code>users(id, email, password_hash, salt, created_at)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Signup: hash password (bcrypt/scrypt/argon2) + store salted hash.</li> <li>Login: validate credentials \u2192 issue JWT (short TTL) + refresh token (longer TTL saved in DB/Redis).</li> <li>Request: API validates JWT signature &amp; claims.</li> <li>Logout: revoke refresh token (delete) and optionally add to JWT blacklist until expiry.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Auth service stateless; store refresh tokens in Redis for revocation scalability.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Token compromise: rotate signing keys and provide key-rotation strategy (kid header).</li> <li>Password brute force: rate limit login attempts.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Failed logins, token issuance, blacklist size.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI Auth libs (fastapi-jwt-auth), passlib for hashing, PyJWT/cryptography.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Token revocation, refresh token security, biometric/MFA flows.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#11-task-queue-celery-like","title":"11) Task Queue (Celery-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Execute background jobs reliably and at scale.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API -&gt; Broker(RabbitMQ/Redis/Kafka) -&gt; Worker Pool -&gt; Results Store (DB/Redis)\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Broker, worker fleet, scheduler, results store, monitoring.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>API pushes job to broker.</li> <li>Worker consumes, executes task; updates status.</li> <li>For long jobs, use heartbeats and progress reporting.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Add workers, partition queues by task type/priority.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Worker crash: broker redelivers (use ack/nack).</li> <li>Idempotency required for replays.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Queue depth, worker count, task durations, failure rates.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>Celery (RabbitMQ/Redis), Dramatiq, or custom asyncio workers. Use <code>flower</code> or Prometheus exporters.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Exactly-once vs at-least-once, task ordering, scheduling, rate-limited workers.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#12-video-streaming-platform-youtube","title":"12) Video Streaming Platform (YouTube)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Upload, transcode, store, and stream with adaptive bitrate.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Uploader -&gt; Transcoder -&gt; Object Store (S3) -&gt; CDN -&gt; Client (HLS/DASH)\n                 \u2198 Metadata DB -&gt; Search/Recommendations\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Upload service, transcoder (FFmpeg workers), HLS/DASH packager, CDN, manifest generation.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Upload video \u2192 store raw.</li> <li>Trigger transcoding pipeline to generate multi-resolution renditions and HLS segments + manifests.</li> <li>Store outputs in S3, push to CDN.</li> <li>Client requests manifest and segments from CDN; ABR switches quality.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Transcoding autoscale; chunked uploads for parallelism.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Transcoder failure: retry job or mark for manual review; use idempotent job IDs.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Transcode success rate, CDN cache hit, segment latency.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>Control plane in FastAPI; Celery/Kubernetes jobs for transcoding; use boto3.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Offline vs live streaming differences, DRM support, storage cost and retention policies.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#13-ride-hailing-uber-like","title":"13) Ride-Hailing (Uber-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Real-time driver matching, low-latency geolocation updates.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API Gateway -&gt; Matching Service -&gt; Drivers (via WebSocket/PubSub)\n                 \u2198 Location Service -&gt; Spatial DB\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Location ingestion, matching engine, route service, pricing, billing.</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <ul> <li><code>drivers(id, location, status, rating)</code></li> <li><code>rides(id, rider, driver, status, route)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Driver publishes location every 1\u20135s.</li> <li>Rider requests ride: matching service queries nearby drivers via spatial index (geohash/R-tree).</li> <li>Assign driver, push notifications; handle cancellations and reassign.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Partition city by regions; run matching per region.</li> <li>Use in-memory geospatial indices (Redis GEO or PostGIS).</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Stale location: heartbeat and TTL.</li> <li>Split brain: ensure single assignment via distributed locks.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Match time, acceptance rate, ETA accuracy.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI, aioredis GEO, PostGIS for persistent geodata.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Real-time constraints, region sharding, surge pricing algorithms.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#14-feed-system-twitterinstagram","title":"14) Feed System (Twitter/Instagram)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Provide personalized feeds with low-latency reads.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>User -&gt; Feed API -&gt; (Fan-out on write OR Fan-out on read) -&gt; Storage (Redis / Cassandra) -&gt; Client\n</code></pre> <p>Options</p> <ul> <li>Fan-out-on-write: when a user posts, push to followers' feeds (fast reads, expensive writes).</li> <li>Fan-out-on-read: compute feed at read time (cheap writes, expensive reads).</li> </ul> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Timeline service, fan-out worker, storage for timelines, ranking service.</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <ul> <li><code>posts(post_id, author, content, ts)</code></li> <li><code>user_timeline(user_id) -&gt; sorted list</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow (fan-out-on-write)</p> <ol> <li>Post -&gt; persist -&gt; fetch followers -&gt; push post_id to each follower's timeline (Redis list).</li> <li>Read -&gt; serve from timeline (cached), apply ranking.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>For celebrities with millions of followers use hybrid approach: push to some, compute for others on read (special casing).</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Partial push failure: use retry and reconciliation batch jobs.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Feed freshness, fan-out throughput, read latency.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI, Redis for timelines, Celery for fan-out.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Tradeoffs between read vs write amplification; ranking model integration.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#15-search-system","title":"15) Search System","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Index documents and serve queries with relevance &amp; speed.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Crawler -&gt; Indexer -&gt; Search Engine (Elasticsearch) -&gt; API -&gt; Client\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Crawler, parser, indexer, search cluster, ranking &amp; scoring.</li> </ul> <p>Data model</p> <ul> <li>Inverted index (handled by ES).</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Crawl documents \u2192 extract text &amp; metadata.</li> <li>Index into ES with analyzers (tokenization, stemming).</li> <li>Query: parse user query, run multi-field matching, apply ranking &amp; filters.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>ES shard strategy by index/time/tenant; horizontal scaling for indexing &amp; query.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Index corruption: replicas &amp; snapshots.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Query latency, index refresh time, cluster health.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>Scrapy for crawling, <code>elasticsearch-py</code> for indexing.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Relevance tuning, stop words, synonyms, and near real-time indexing tradeoffs.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#16-online-code-runner-leetcode","title":"16) Online Code Runner (LeetCode)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Execute user code in sandboxed containers, return test results.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API -&gt; Judge Queue -&gt; Sandbox Workers (Docker) -&gt; Results Store\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Submission API, scheduler, sandbox (container/VM), security (resource limits), results DB.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Submit code \u2192 create job in queue with testcases.</li> <li>Scheduler assigns job to sandbox; container runs with time/mem limits.</li> <li>Capture stdout, stderr, exit code; compare against expected, return feedback.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Autoscale sandboxes; warm pool of containers for low latency.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Infinite loop/timeouts: enforce time limits and kill container.</li> <li>Malicious code: strict seccomp, network disabled.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Job throughput, avg run time, error rates.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI for submissions, use Docker SDK or Firecracker for light sandboxes.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Security and multi-tenant isolation, deterministic execution for reproducible tests.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#17-monitoring-system-prometheusgrafana","title":"17) Monitoring System (Prometheus/Grafana)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Collect metrics &amp; visualize them for alerting.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>App -&gt; /metrics -&gt; Prometheus (scrape) -&gt; Long-term storage -&gt; Grafana\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Instrumentation, Prometheus server, alertmanager, Grafana, remote-write storage.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Apps expose metrics endpoints.</li> <li>Prometheus scrapes at intervals, stores time-series.</li> <li>Alerts configured in Prometheus/Alertmanager.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Federated Prometheus or remote-write to Cortex/Thanos for scale.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Scrape overload: tune scrape intervals &amp; targets.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Scrape success/latency, alert error rates.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li><code>prometheus_client</code> for instrumentation.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Retention vs cost, high cardinality metrics concerns.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#18-pubsub-messaging-system","title":"18) Pub/Sub Messaging System","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Durable pub/sub with at-least-once delivery, multiple subscribers.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Publisher -&gt; Broker (Kafka) -&gt; Topic -&gt; Consumer Groups\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Brokers (Kafka), producers, consumer groups, retention &amp; compaction policies.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Producer publishes to topic; broker persists to partition log.</li> <li>Consumers poll by offset; commit offsets.</li> <li>Retries handled by consumer seeking to earlier offset.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Add brokers &amp; partitions.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Broker down -&gt; replication; need ISR for durability.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Consumer lag, broker throughput, partition leader availability.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li><code>confluent-kafka</code> or <code>kafka-python</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Exactly-once semantics using Kafka transactions, vs at-least-once with idempotent consumers.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#19-order-tracking-food-delivery","title":"19) Order Tracking (Food Delivery)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>End-to-end flow from order placement to delivery with live tracking.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Order Service -&gt; Restaurant -&gt; Rider Assignment -&gt; Rider App (GPS) -&gt; Tracking Service -&gt; Client\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Order service, dispatch (assign rider), route &amp; ETA service, tracking service, notifications.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Order placed -&gt; restaurant accepted -&gt; dispatch finds nearest rider -&gt; assign and notify.</li> <li>Rider updates location -&gt; tracking service updates order status &amp; ETA.</li> <li>Delivery completes -&gt; mark delivered and trigger rating.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Partition by region, scale dispatch per city.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Rider cancels: fallback to reassign with retry &amp; compensation (delay notice to user).</li> </ul> <p>Caching</p> <ul> <li>Use Redis for current order states.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Delivery time, cancellation rate, assignment latency.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI services, Redis, Postgres, geospatial libs.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>SLA guarantees, rider incentive algorithms, fallback strategies.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#20-cicd-pipeline","title":"20) CI/CD Pipeline","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Automate build, test, and deploy on code changes.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Git Push -&gt; CI Server -&gt; Build -&gt; Test -&gt; Artifact Store -&gt; CD -&gt; Deploy to Env\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>CI runner, artifact repo, test runners, deployment pipelines, rollback mechanisms.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Commit triggers pipeline.</li> <li>Pipeline runs unit/integration tests, builds Docker image, pushes to registry.</li> <li>CD triggers deployment to staging, runs smoke tests, then deploy to prod (canary/blue-green).</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Fleet of runners; isolated containers for concurrent builds.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Test flakiness: quarantine flaky tests, re-run policies.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Build success rate, deployment time, rollback occurrences.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, or self-hosted runner with Dockerized steps.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Canary vs blue-green deployments, artifact immutability, secret management.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#21-recommendation-system-netflix-like","title":"21) Recommendation System (Netflix-like)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Deliver personalized recommendations based on behavior.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Event Stream -&gt; Feature Store -&gt; Model Training -&gt; Ranking Service -&gt; API -&gt; Client\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Event ingestion, offline model training, online feature store, ranking API.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Collect user events (views, ratings).</li> <li>Offline: train collaborative filtering / matrix factorization / deep models.</li> <li>Online: compute candidate set (retrieval) and rank with model &amp; business rules.</li> <li>Serve personalized list via API.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Use feature caches, precompute top-k per user for fast-read.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Model serving outage: fallback to popularity-based recommendations.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>CTR of recommendations, model drift, latency.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>PySpark/Beam for offline, TensorFlow/PyTorch for models, FastAPI for serving with caching.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Cold start, diversity vs relevance tradeoff, A/B testing of models.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#22-multi-tenant-saas","title":"22) Multi-tenant SaaS","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Isolate tenants while sharing infrastructure.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; Auth -&gt; API -&gt; Tenant Router -&gt; Services -&gt; DB (shared or per-tenant)\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Tenant router, per-tenant configuration, storage isolation choice (shared schema, separate schema, separate DB).</li> </ul> <p>Data model choices</p> <ul> <li>Shared DB with <code>tenant_id</code> column (cheap), or separate DB per tenant (strong isolation).</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>On request, identify tenant via token/hostname.</li> <li>Apply tenant-specific features &amp; rate-limits.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Move heavy tenants to separate DBs; pool resources.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Noisy neighbor: monitor resource usage, enforce quotas, circuit breakers.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Per-tenant latency, CPU/memory usage.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI middleware for tenant resolution, SQLAlchemy multi-bind.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Tradeoffs of isolation levels, migration strategies between models.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#23-api-gateway","title":"23) API Gateway","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Single entrypoint for auth, routing, rate-limit, metrics, and aggregation.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; API Gateway (Auth, RateLimit, Routing, Logging) -&gt; Microservices\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Auth, routing, throttling, caching, circuit-breaker, analytics.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Gateway authenticates request, enforces policies, routes to backend service.</li> <li>Aggregation: coalesce multiple backend calls if needed.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Stateless gateways behind LB; use service mesh for deeper routing.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Gateway down -&gt; global outage: use multiple gateways in different AZs &amp; health checks.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Latency added by gateway, error percentage, throughput.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>Use Envoy/NGINX for production; Python for custom gateway if needed (FastAPI + uvicorn).</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Implementing cross-cutting concerns and where to place them (gateway vs service).</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#24-real-time-dashboard-stock-dashboard","title":"24) Real-time Dashboard (Stock Dashboard)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Stream high-frequency updates to many clients in real-time.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Market Feed -&gt; Ingest -&gt; Stream Processor (Kafka/Redis Streams) -&gt; WebSocket Server -&gt; Clients\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Ingest, processing (aggregate/minute), pub/sub, websocket servers, throttling.</li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>Ingest live ticks, push to stream platform.</li> <li>Process and emit updates to subscribers via WebSockets.</li> <li>Clients subscribe to subset of symbols.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Partition by symbol; scalable WebSocket gateways.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Data spikes: backpressure and aggregation to reduce bandwidth.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Update latency, message loss, connection counts.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI with websockets, aiokafka for stream consumption.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>How to handle huge fanout, rate limiting per connection, applying compression.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Most_Ask_System_Design.html#25-booking-system-movie-tickets","title":"25) Booking System (Movie Tickets)","text":"<p>Goal</p> <ul> <li>Seats selection with locking and final booking.</li> </ul> <p>ASCII</p> <pre><code>Client -&gt; SeatService -&gt; LockService (Redis) -&gt; Payment -&gt; Confirm -&gt; Persist\n</code></pre> <p>Components</p> <ul> <li>Seat inventory, lock service, payment, order finalizer.</li> </ul> <p>Schema</p> <ul> <li><code>seats(show_id, seat_no, status, lock_owner, lock_expiry)</code></li> </ul> <p>Flow</p> <ol> <li>User opens seat map; seats read from DB (cached).</li> <li>On select, lock seats in Redis with short TTL (60s).</li> <li>User pays; on payment success, persist seat status to DB (atomic) and release lock.</li> <li>If TTL expires, lock auto-releases.</li> </ol> <p>Scaling</p> <ul> <li>Partition shows/theaters; locks per show.</li> </ul> <p>Failures</p> <ul> <li>Payment timeouts: release locks to avoid deadlock.</li> <li>Double booking: transactional write with <code>WHERE status='available'</code> and rows affected check.</li> </ul> <p>Monitoring</p> <ul> <li>Lock contention, booking success rate, seat race conditions.</li> </ul> <p>Python tech</p> <ul> <li>FastAPI, Redis for locks, Postgres with transactions.</li> </ul> <p>Talking points</p> <ul> <li>Locking strategies, optimistic vs pessimistic, handling high concurrency for popular shows.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html","title":"Request-Response","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#full-workflow-from-request-to-response","title":"\ud83c\udf10 Full Workflow: From Request to Response","text":"<pre><code>\ud83d\udc68\u200d\ud83d\udcbb 1. User Opens Website (e.g., www.mysite.com)\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; DNS resolves domain to CDN edge server\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udce1 2. CDN (Content Delivery Network)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Checks cache for static assets (HTML, CSS, JS, images)\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 If cached \u2192 Return to user instantly\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u274c If not cached \u2192 Fetch from Blob Storage or origin server\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f 3. Client Browser Loads Page\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; For dynamic content (e.g., product details, user profile)\n         sends API request (AJAX/Fetch/XHR) to backend via public endpoint\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f 4. Load Balancer (NGINX / AWS ELB / HAProxy)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Accepts incoming API request\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Runs health checks on backend servers\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Forwards request to optimal backend server\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83e\udde0 5. Backend Server (e.g., FastAPI / Django / Node.js)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Parses request\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Authenticates user (JWT/session/cookies)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Business logic triggered (e.g., get product info)\n             \u25bc\n\n\u26a1 6. Cache Lookup (Redis / Memcached)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \ud83d\udd0d Check if the requested data is cached\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; \u2705 If found \u2192 Return data directly (faster)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; \u274c If not found \u2192 Query database\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f 7. Database Query (PostgreSQL / MySQL / MongoDB)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Fetch required data (e.g., product, user orders)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Return result to backend server\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83e\udde0 8. Backend Server (Continue)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Stores fresh result in cache for future\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; If needed, fetches media links from Blob Storage\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Prepares response (JSON, HTML, etc.)\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83e\udea3 9. Blob Storage Access (S3 / Azure Blob / GCP)\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Used only if image/video/file requested is not served via CDN\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Returns media URL or binary to server or CDN\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udce4 10. Response Sent to Load Balancer\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Load balancer passes it back to client\n             \u25bc\n\n\ud83d\udda5\ufe0f 11. Final Response Delivered to Client\n   \u2502\n   \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; User sees dynamic content (products, profile, dashboard)\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Media served via CDN/Blob \u2014 fast and global\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#request-response-workflow-cdn-load-balancer-cache-blob-storage","title":"\ud83d\udd04 Request - Response Workflow (CDN, Load Balancer, Cache, Blob Storage)","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#1-client-requests-a-page","title":"1. \ud83c\udf10 Client Requests a Page","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: A user opens the website (e.g., <code>www.amazon.com</code>) in a browser.</li> <li>In the diagram: The request starts from Clients.</li> <li>Example: User wants to view a product page.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#2-cdn-delivers-static-assets","title":"2. \ud83d\udce1 CDN Delivers Static Assets","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: The CDN (Content Delivery Network) serves static assets (images, CSS, JS) closest to the user for faster loading.</li> <li>In the diagram: Clients hit CDN, which is connected to Blob Storage.</li> <li>Example: Product image, site CSS, and JS are loaded quickly from nearby CDN edge server.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#3-request-sent-to-load-balancer","title":"3. \u21aa\ufe0f Request Sent to Load Balancer","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: The user's request for dynamic content (like product details) goes to a Load Balancer.</li> <li>In the diagram: Client \u2192 Load Balancer.</li> <li>Example: The request to fetch product description and price is sent to the load balancer.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#4-load-balancer-routes-to-servers","title":"4. \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f Load Balancer Routes to Servers","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: Load balancer distributes requests to the healthiest and least busy application server.</li> <li>In the diagram: Load Balancer \u2192 Servers.</li> <li>Example: The load balancer sends your request to Server 3, which is available.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#5-cache-layer-is-checked-first","title":"5. \u26a1 Cache Layer is Checked First","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: Server checks Cache (e.g., Redis) for recently accessed product details.</li> <li>In the diagram: Servers \u2194 Cache.</li> <li>Example: If someone recently viewed the same product, its info is in cache \u2192 served instantly.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#6-if-not-in-cache-query-the-database","title":"6. \ud83d\udcc2 If Not in Cache, Query the Database","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: If data isn\u2019t cached, server queries the main Database (e.g., MySQL/PostgreSQL).</li> <li>In the diagram: Servers \u2192 Database.</li> <li>Example: Server fetches the latest price, stock, reviews from the product table.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#7-store-data-in-cache-for-next-time","title":"7. \ud83d\udcdd Store Data in Cache for Next Time","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: After getting data from the database, server stores it in Cache to speed up future requests.</li> <li>Example: Product data is now cached for 10 mins \u2192 next user gets faster response.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#8-blob-storage-used-for-media","title":"8. \ud83d\udcbe Blob Storage Used for Media","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: Product images, videos, PDFs are stored in Blob Storage (e.g., AWS S3).</li> <li>In the diagram: Servers \u2190\u2192 Blob Storage, and CDN also pulls from Blob Storage.</li> <li>Example: Product video stored in S3 is linked in the product page.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/Request-Response.html#9-server-sends-response-back-to-client","title":"9. \ud83d\udce4 Server Sends Response Back to Client","text":"<ul> <li>What happens: Server compiles all data (HTML + media links) and sends it back to the user.</li> <li>In the diagram: Servers \u2192 Load Balancer \u2192 Clients.</li> <li>Example: User sees full product page, images, prices, stock, and reviews.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html","title":"System Design Process","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#system-design-from-scratch-complete-guide","title":"\ud83e\udded System Design from Scratch \u2013 Complete Guide","text":"<p>This guide will help you design any software system logically, structurally, and technically with modern tools and tech stacks.</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#system-design-workflow-from-requirement-to-deployment","title":"System Design Workflow (From Requirement to Deployment)","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#stakeholder-client-provide-business-requirements-product-thinking-requirements-gathering-identify-functional-features-mvp-roadmap-note-non-functional-needs-scalability-latency-security-define-user-roles-admin-user-guest-etc-high-level-system-design-draw-block-diagram-clients-gateway-backend-db-etc-identify-services-auth-product-cart-chat-etc-select-protocols-rest-grpc-websockets-database-storage-design-model-entities-and-relations-erd-normalize-schema-choose-nosql-if-needed-plan-indexes-partitions-sharding-add-file-storage-eg-s3-and-cache-redis-component-api-design-break-into-microservices-or-modular-monolith-design-apis-restopenapigraphql-define-data-contracts-validation-schemas-add-rate-limits-versioning-auth-middleware-real-time-async-design-if-needed-use-websockets-socketio-for-live-updates-background-jobs-via-celery-bullmq-messaging-using-kafka-redis-streams-rabbitmq-auth-security-design-oauth2-jwt-session-openid-rbac-or-abac-per-user-roles-sanitize-input-protect-endpoints-plan-ddos-protection-rate-limits-csrfxss-guards-tech-stack-selection-frontend-reactnextjs-tailwind-tanstack-backend-fastapi-node-spring-boot-go-db-postgresql-mongodb-redis-realtime-messaging-cicd-hosting-monitoring-tools-devops-infra-design-define-dockerfiles-docker-compose-setup-setup-github-actions-gitlab-ci-pipelines-plan-deployment-kubernetes-ecs-vms-add-logging-monitoring-alerts-grafana-sentry-testing-strategy-unit-tests-pytest-jest-integration-api-tests-e2e-testing-playwright-cypress-deploy-to-staging-production-use-infra-as-code-terraform-pulumi-enable-blue-green-canary-deployments-perform-staging-validation-rollout-to-production-ci-cd-monitor-scale-improve-sentry-prometheus-logs-user-feedback","title":"<pre><code>\ud83e\uddd1\u200d\ud83d\udcbc Stakeholder / Client\n   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Provide Business Requirements\n            \u2502\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde0 Product Thinking + Requirements Gathering\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Identify Functional Features (MVP + roadmap)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Note Non-Functional Needs (scalability, latency, security)\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Define User Roles (admin, user, guest, etc.)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcd0 High-Level System Design\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Draw block diagram (clients, gateway, backend, DB, etc.)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Identify services (auth, product, cart, chat, etc.)\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Select protocols (REST, gRPC, WebSockets)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddf1 Database &amp; Storage Design\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Model entities and relations (ERD)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Normalize schema / choose NoSQL if needed\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Plan indexes, partitions, sharding\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Add file storage (e.g., S3) and cache (Redis)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\udde9 Component &amp; API Design\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Break into microservices or modular monolith\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Design APIs (REST/OpenAPI/GraphQL)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Define data contracts + validation schemas\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Add rate limits, versioning, auth middleware\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udfe2 Real-Time + Async Design (if needed)\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Use WebSockets / Socket.IO for live updates\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Background jobs via Celery, BullMQ\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Messaging using Kafka / Redis Streams / RabbitMQ\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udd10 Auth &amp; Security Design\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; OAuth2 / JWT / Session / OpenID\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; RBAC or ABAC per user roles\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Sanitize input, protect endpoints\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Plan DDoS protection, rate limits, CSRF/XSS guards\n            \u25bc\n\u2699\ufe0f Tech Stack Selection\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Frontend (React/Next.js, Tailwind, TanStack)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Backend (FastAPI / Node / Spring Boot / Go)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; DB (PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Redis)\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Realtime, messaging, CI/CD, hosting, monitoring tools\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udce6 DevOps &amp; Infra Design\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Define Dockerfiles, docker-compose setup\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Setup GitHub Actions / GitLab CI pipelines\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Plan deployment (Kubernetes / ECS / VMs)\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Add logging, monitoring, alerts (Grafana, Sentry)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83e\uddea Testing Strategy\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Unit tests (Pytest, Jest)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Integration + API tests\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; E2E Testing (Playwright, Cypress)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\ude80 Deploy to Staging \u2192 Production\n            \u2502\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Use infra-as-code (Terraform, Pulumi)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Enable blue-green / canary deployments\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500&gt; Perform staging validation\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500&gt; Rollout to production (CI \u2192 CD)\n            \u25bc\n\ud83d\udcc8 Monitor, Scale, Improve\n      (Sentry, Prometheus, Logs, User Feedback)\n</code></pre>","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-1-requirement-understanding","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 1: Requirement Understanding","text":"Step Task \u2705 Understand business goals (functional + non-functional) \u2705 List core features (MVP + future scope) \u2705 Identify user types (e.g., admin, guest, authenticated user) \u2705 Define performance, scalability, and availability goals <p>\ud83e\udde0 Example: Chat App</p> <ul> <li>Functional: real-time messaging, group chat, file sharing</li> <li>Non-functional: low latency, high availability</li> <li>Users: guest, registered user</li> </ul>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-2-high-level-system-design","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 2: High-Level System Design","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#1-draw-high-level-block-diagram","title":"\ud83d\udd36 1. Draw High-Level Block Diagram","text":"<p>Include:</p> <ul> <li>Client (Web/Mobile)</li> <li>API Gateway / BFF (Backend for Frontend)</li> <li>Core Backend Services</li> <li>Database(s)</li> <li>Caching Layer</li> <li>Message Queues</li> <li>3rd Party Integrations</li> <li>File Storage</li> <li>Authentication</li> </ul> <p>\ud83e\udde9 Example Architecture (E-Commerce):</p> <pre><code>User \u2500\u25b6 Web/Mobile App\n     \u2514\u25b6 API Gateway\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Auth Service\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Product Service\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Cart Service\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Order Service\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Notification Service\n           \u251c\u2500\u25b6 Payment Gateway\n           \u2514\u2500\u25b6 DB/Redis/S3/Kafka etc.\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-3-database-design","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 3: Database Design","text":"Step Action 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Identify core entities (Users, Products, Messages, Orders, etc.) 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Define relationships (1-1, 1-many, many-many) 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Normalize schema (avoid duplication) 4\ufe0f\u20e3 Use NoSQL for scalable, unstructured data (chat, logs, feeds) 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Plan indexes, partitioning, sharding if needed <p>\ud83d\udcd8 Tools: dbdiagram.io, Lucidchart</p> <p>\ud83d\udce6 DBs: PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra, DynamoDB</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-4-component-design-api-design","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 4: Component Design &amp; API Design","text":"Step Action \ud83d\udd39 Break system into microservices or modular monoliths \ud83d\udd39 Define each service's responsibility \ud83d\udd39 Design REST/GraphQL APIs using OpenAPI (Swagger) \ud83d\udd39 Add validation, rate limiting, API versioning <p>\ud83d\udcd8 Tools: Postman, Swagger Editor, Insomnia</p> <p>\ud83d\udce6 Frameworks: FastAPI, Django, Express.js, Spring Boot</p>"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-5-real-time-async-background-tasks","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 5: Real-Time, Async &amp; Background Tasks","text":"Use Case Tech \ud83d\udfe2 Realtime updates (chat, dashboard) WebSockets, Socket.IO, WebRTC \ud83d\udfe0 Background processing Celery, BullMQ, Sidekiq \ud83d\udd01 Message queues Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis Streams"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-6-authentication-authorization","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 6: Authentication &amp; Authorization","text":"Feature Tech Stack Auth OAuth2, JWT, OpenID Connect Providers Google, GitHub, Auth0, Firebase Role-Based Access Middleware layer (admin/user/etc.)"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-7-scalability-performance","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 7: Scalability &amp; Performance","text":"Area Solution DB Reads Redis, Memcached API Caching CDN (Cloudflare, Akamai) Async Jobs Queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ) Rate Limiting Redis Token Bucket / Leaky Bucket Load Balancing NGINX, HAProxy, AWS ELB"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-8-devops-deployment","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 8: DevOps &amp; Deployment","text":"Stack Tool Infra Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform CI/CD GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins Cloud AWS, GCP, Azure, Render, Vercel Monitoring Prometheus, Grafana, ELK Stack, Sentry Secrets Vault, Doppler, AWS Secrets Manager"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-9-security-measures","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 9: Security Measures","text":"Concern Solutions API Security HTTPS, API Gateway, JWT Input Validation Pydantic, JOI, Marshmallow CSRF/XSS Use secure headers, CSRF tokens DDoS Protection Cloudflare, Rate Limiting DB Security ORM Injection protection, firewalls"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#phase-10-testing-observability","title":"\ud83d\udccc PHASE 10: Testing &amp; Observability","text":"Testing Observability Unit, Integration, E2E (Pytest, Jest, Playwright) Logs (ELK), Tracing (Jaeger), Metrics (Prometheus)"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#latest-tech-stack-suggestions","title":"\ud83d\udd27 Latest Tech Stack Suggestions","text":"Layer Recommended Stack Frontend Next.js, React, Tailwind, TanStack Query Mobile Flutter, React Native Backend FastAPI, Node.js (Express/NestJS), Go Realtime WebSocket, Pusher, Socket.IO DB (SQL) PostgreSQL, MySQL DB (NoSQL) MongoDB, DynamoDB Caching Redis, Memcached File Storage S3, GCS Messaging Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis Streams CI/CD GitHub Actions, Docker, K8s Monitoring Prometheus, Grafana, Sentry"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Design%20Guide/System%20Design%20Process.html#real-world-examples","title":"\ud83d\udca1 Real-World Examples","text":"App Type Key Features Chat App WebSocket, Redis pub/sub, MongoDB, token-based auth E-commerce Product catalog, search, payment gateway, user cart, order tracking Inventory System CRUD for items, suppliers, stock levels, PDF reports, alerts AI App (Gen AI) FastAPI + OpenAI SDK, LangChain/LangGraph, Redis VectorStore"},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Performance%20%26%20Optimization/API%20Profiling_Benchmarking.html","title":"API Profiling Benchmarking","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Performance%20%26%20Optimization/Backpressure%20Handling.html","title":"Backpressure Handling","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Performance%20%26%20Optimization/Bulkhead%20Pattern.html","title":"Bulkhead Pattern","text":""},{"location":"System%20Design/System%20Performance%20%26%20Optimization/Rate%20Limiting_Throttling.html","title":"Rate Limiting Throttling","text":""}]}